window.SITE = {"study": {"title": "FOSS AI-policy deliberation: coding passes", "thread_labels": {"llvm-rfc-start-small": "LLVM RFC", "numpy-policy-thread": "NumPy mailing list", "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse": "Scientific Python t/1702", "godot-forum-ai-activity": "Godot forum", "django-ai-policy-proposal": "Django forum", "osrf-implementation-guidelines": "ROS / OSRF Discourse"}}, "iterations": [{"id": "i01", "label": "LLVM RFC", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small"], "diff": null}, {"id": "i02", "label": "Constant comparison and retroactive coding", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small"], "diff": {"removed": [{"id": "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "name": "contributor should be responsible", "count": 13}, {"id": "c3c3357d-3082-4149-943e-a497ea7dad9b", "name": "labels such as 'extractive' discourage newcomers", "count": 6}, {"id": "49749cc6-fad7-4286-b3a9-eebc51d7a0df", "name": "LLMs are license laundering machines", "count": 3}, {"id": "ae5c601d-364c-4c93-8c81-67ef041138fe", "name": "Policy should encourage contributor growth", "count": 5}, {"id": "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0", "name": "policy should protect maintainers against AI slop", "count": 13}, {"id": "f963f444-8630-4232-a8cc-1e324abd4097", "name": "slop offloads effort to reviewer", "count": 6}], "renamed": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "before": "AI Slop contributions are extractive", "after": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer"}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "before": "contributor growth is encouraged", "after": "contributor growth is important and should be reflected in policy"}, {"id": "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "before": "contributor understanding is needed", "after": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite"}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "before": "security and privacy concern", "after": "security, privacy, copyright concern"}]}}, {"id": "i03", "label": "NumPy mailing list", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small", "numpy-policy-thread"], "diff": {"removed": [], "renamed": []}}, {"id": "i04", "label": "Constant comparison and retroactive coding", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small", "numpy-policy-thread"], "diff": {"removed": [], "renamed": []}}, {"id": "i05", "label": "Scientific Python t/1702", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small", "numpy-policy-thread", "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse"], "diff": {"removed": [], "renamed": []}}, {"id": "i06", "label": "Constant comparison and retroactive coding", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small", "numpy-policy-thread", "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse"], "diff": {"removed": [{"id": "4b9ebfaf-f258-4ae5-81cc-1c4481dea09f", "name": "AI contribution pressure is not yet acute", "count": 2}, {"id": "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "name": "AI is tolerated not encouraged", "count": 2}, {"id": "541fcf96-75e8-4328-b332-6a589eeb9dfc", "name": "Contributor acts as a relay for the LLM", "count": 1}, {"id": "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "count": 53}, {"id": "348cc0ac-6e79-45be-85a0-3062280e3557", "name": "Domain complexity limits AI fitness", "count": 3}, {"id": "97d6e58b-4d1c-47ce-99fa-95397c7f637f", "name": "Fully automated contributions should be excluded", "count": 4}, {"id": "c48b58eb-2c38-4a07-8f91-a99bc02d6f5d", "name": "High Volume PRs are a result of gamification", "count": 1}, {"id": "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "count": 10}, {"id": "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "name": "new contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance", "count": 12}, {"id": "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "count": 10}, {"id": "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "name": "protective policy can be toxic", "count": 11}], "renamed": [{"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "before": "AI is discouraged", "after": "AI is tolerated/not-encouraged/discouraged"}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "before": "contributor growth is important and should be reflected in policy", "after": "contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance and should be reflected in policy"}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "before": "contributor ownership supersedes AI usage concern", "after": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite"}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "before": "newcomers may be discouraged by explicit understanding requirements", "after": "protective policy may become toxic or unwelcoming"}, {"id": "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "before": "Policy compliance is unverifiable", "after": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors"}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "before": "Review effort needs a human stake", "after": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile"}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "before": "The review process is a social interaction", "after": "participation is valued as human social interaction"}]}}, {"id": "i07", "label": "Godot forum", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small", "numpy-policy-thread", "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "godot-forum-ai-activity"], "diff": {"removed": [], "renamed": []}}, {"id": "i08", "label": "Constant comparison and retroactive coding", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small", "numpy-policy-thread", "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "godot-forum-ai-activity"], "diff": {"removed": [], "renamed": []}}, {"id": "i09", "label": "Django forum", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small", "numpy-policy-thread", "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "godot-forum-ai-activity", "django-ai-policy-proposal"], "diff": {"removed": [], "renamed": []}}, {"id": "i10", "label": "ROS / OSRF Discourse", "threads": ["llvm-rfc-start-small", "numpy-policy-thread", "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "godot-forum-ai-activity", "django-ai-policy-proposal", "osrf-implementation-guidelines"], "diff": {"removed": [], "renamed": [{"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "before": "maintainer time should be protected", "after": "maintainer time is a limited resource which should be protected"}]}}], "snapshots": {"i01": {"codes": [{"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "name": "AI is tolerated not encouraged", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 1}, {"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "AI Slop contributions are extractive", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 8}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is encouraged", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 4}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor ownership supersedes AI usage concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "name": "contributor should be responsible", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 9}, {"id": "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "name": "contributor understanding is needed", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 18, "posts": 13}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "c3c3357d-3082-4149-943e-a497ea7dad9b", "name": "labels such as 'extractive' discourage newcomers", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "49749cc6-fad7-4286-b3a9-eebc51d7a0df", "name": "LLMs are license laundering machines", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 15}, {"id": "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "name": "new contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 2}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "newcomers may be discouraged by explicit understanding requirements", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "ae5c601d-364c-4c93-8c81-67ef041138fe", "name": "Policy should encourage contributor growth", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 4}, {"id": "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0", "name": "policy should protect maintainers against AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 9}, {"id": "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "name": "protective policy can be toxic", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security and privacy concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 3}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "f963f444-8630-4232-a8cc-1e324abd4097", "name": "slop offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}], "introduced": [], "merges": [], "segments": [{"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": []}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "49749cc6-fad7-4286-b3a9-eebc51d7a0df", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0", "f963f444-8630-4232-a8cc-1e324abd4097"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "c3c3357d-3082-4149-943e-a497ea7dad9b", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "f963f444-8630-4232-a8cc-1e324abd4097"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "ae5c601d-364c-4c93-8c81-67ef041138fe"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["49749cc6-fad7-4286-b3a9-eebc51d7a0df", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["c3c3357d-3082-4149-943e-a497ea7dad9b", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["c3c3357d-3082-4149-943e-a497ea7dad9b", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "ae5c601d-364c-4c93-8c81-67ef041138fe"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "f963f444-8630-4232-a8cc-1e324abd4097"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["c3c3357d-3082-4149-943e-a497ea7dad9b", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "c3c3357d-3082-4149-943e-a497ea7dad9b", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["c3c3357d-3082-4149-943e-a497ea7dad9b", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["ae5c601d-364c-4c93-8c81-67ef041138fe", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": []}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": []}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": []}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": []}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "ae5c601d-364c-4c93-8c81-67ef041138fe", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["017c4dfe-a347-48c0-b993-7f5c069304c2", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "f963f444-8630-4232-a8cc-1e324abd4097"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "ae5c601d-364c-4c93-8c81-67ef041138fe", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "f963f444-8630-4232-a8cc-1e324abd4097"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0", "f963f444-8630-4232-a8cc-1e324abd4097"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["49749cc6-fad7-4286-b3a9-eebc51d7a0df", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "cc1a1500-b3d7-42a2-81fb-388c81cd6dc0"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}]}, "i02": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 12}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "name": "AI is tolerated not encouraged", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 1}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is important and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 6}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor ownership supersedes AI usage concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 21, "posts": 15}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 15}, {"id": "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "name": "new contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 2}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "newcomers may be discouraged by explicit understanding requirements", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "name": "protective policy can be toxic", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 3}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}], "introduced": [], "merges": [{"source": "policy should protect maintainers against AI slop", "target": "maintainer time should be protected", "merged": "maintainer time should be protected"}, {"source": "contributor should be responsible", "target": "contributor understanding is needed", "merged": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite"}, {"source": "slop offloads effort to reviewer", "target": "AI Slop contributions are extractive", "merged": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer"}, {"source": "labels such as 'extractive' discourage newcomers", "target": "protective policy can be toxic", "merged": "protective policy can be toxic"}, {"source": "Policy should encourage contributor growth", "target": "contributor growth is encouraged", "merged": "contributor growth is important and should be reflected in policy"}, {"source": "LLMs are license laundering machines", "target": "security and privacy concern", "merged": "security, privacy, copyright concern"}], "segments": [{"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": []}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": []}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": []}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": []}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": []}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}]}, "i03": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 17}, {"id": "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "name": "AI acceptability should scale with use and risk", "description": "Whether AI assistance is acceptable, and how much scrutiny it needs, should depend on the kind of use and the risk of the code (e.g., tests/boilerplate vs. core algorithms), not be all-or-nothing.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 9}, {"id": "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "name": "AI adoption is inevitable", "description": "Asserts that widespread AI use in coding is inevitable and policy must accept it.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "name": "AI can support contributor learning", "description": "AI tools can help contributors learn (e.g., give feedback maintainers lack time for).", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "name": "AI contributions harm maintainer well-being", "description": "AI contributions demoralise, frustrate or burn out maintainers, or erode their motivation or joy in reviewing.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 18, "posts": 16}, {"id": "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "name": "AI erodes effort-based trust", "description": "Because AI makes plausible code, fluent text and apparent diligence cheap, cues that reviewers used to infer a contributor's effort, competence, care or understanding (code quality, explanations, communication style, accountability statements) no longer carry that information, so trust in the contributor is harder to establish. Specify which cue is weakened where possible; for communication style alone, also consider \"Communication signals human involvement\". (D21.)", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "name": "AI for understanding differs from AI for code generation", "description": "Using AI to research, analyse or understand code is treated as distinct from (and more acceptable than) using it to generate submitted code.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 20}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "name": "AI is tolerated not encouraged", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "name": "AI needs human oversight", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "name": "AI output enters the policy deliberation", "description": "AI-generated text or analysis is itself brought into the policy discussion as evidence or argument.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "name": "AI policy should be provisional amid uncertainty", "description": "Because AI tools and evidence change quickly, policy should be treated as provisional and revisited.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 1}, {"id": "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "name": "AI tools are built on appropriated work", "description": "AI tools are framed as built on taking others' work (training on copyrighted/private material), which shapes how contributor safeguards are judged.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 10}, {"id": "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "name": "AI use risks deskilling and learning loss", "description": "Relying on AI reduces contributors' or maintainers' learning, understanding or coding skill.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "name": "AI-generated code needs heightened review", "description": "AI-generated code requires more careful or different review than human-written code because its errors differ.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "name": "Communication signals human involvement", "description": "Participants read the language, style or voice of contribution communication as evidence of whether a human is involved (e.g. broken English as reassuring, 'flat agreeableness of an LLM'), including the language-access tension for non-native speakers. Distinguish valuing human voice, reading it as a signal, and prohibiting AI-written text (for opposition use 'Opposing AI-generated contribution communication').", "color": "#b7791f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "name": "Compliance burdens can encourage concealed AI use", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or documentation requirements are argued to encourage contributors to hide AI use rather than comply (a proposed feedback: restriction → concealment → less legibility). Distinct from 'People will use AI regardless of policy'.", "color": "#9b2c2c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "name": "Contributor admission should be trust-gated", "description": "Access to contribute (e.g., open PRs) should be gated by trust mechanisms such as vouching, permissions or introductions.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is important and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 11}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor ownership supersedes AI usage concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 41, "posts": 31}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "name": "Honoring copyright is an ethical obligation", "description": "Respecting the intentions and attribution of original authors is framed as an ethical duty of the community, independent of legal enforceability.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "name": "Inevitability should not drive AI policy", "description": "Rejects 'AI is inevitable' as a basis for policy; the community can and should shape how AI is used.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 26, "posts": 21}, {"id": "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "name": "new contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 6}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "newcomers may be discouraged by explicit understanding requirements", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or rules will not stop dishonest contributors; self-attestation is unreliable.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "name": "Policy deliberation strains collegial civility", "description": "The AI-policy discussion itself produces interpersonal friction, accusations or incivility among participants.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "name": "Policy scope should be limited to project interests", "description": "Policy should address only what bears on the project's own interests (broadly construed), not wider societal concerns.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "name": "Policy should be concrete and actionable", "description": "Policy guidance should give contributors and maintainers concrete, actionable steps or examples rather than abstract principles.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "name": "Policy should build on external precedents", "description": "The project should adopt or adapt other projects' policies rather than write its own from scratch.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "name": "Policy should stay pragmatic rather than moral", "description": "The project's policy should avoid broad moral or political statements and stay pragmatic.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "name": "Project norms should be codified for AI agents", "description": "Project norms should be written in forms AI tools consume (e.g., AGENTS.md, templates) to steer AI behaviour.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "name": "Projects should not prescribe contributors' tools", "description": "The project should not dictate which tools contributors use (or how they learn), only the quality/rules contributions must meet.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "name": "protective policy can be toxic", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 10}, {"id": "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "name": "Rejected AI contributions can provoke retaliation", "description": "Rejection of AI or AI-assisted contributions is followed, reported or feared to be followed, by retaliatory or shaming responses (e.g. an agent shame-posting after its PR is closed). Note whether the excerpt is direct experience, a reported event or a feared consequence.", "color": "#2f855a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "name": "Review effort needs a human stake", "description": "Review effort is justified, or loses its justification, by a human return: a submitter who needs or cares about the result, a contributor who may grow into a future maintainer, the reviewer (Broad human-return concept, D17; the lead's mentorship code is kept unchanged as the growth stake type.)", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 2}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 17}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 11}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "name": "The review process is a social interaction", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 6}, {"id": "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148", "name": "Uncertainty whether contributors are human", "description": "Maintainers cannot tell whether they are interacting with a person or an AI agent, and this uncertainty shapes their engagement.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 4}], "introduced": [], "merges": [], "segments": [{"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": []}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": []}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": []}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": []}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": []}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "1ce25ead-2c84-4ab1-91ce-3d77cb287879", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 118, "end": 1012, "text": " E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "26bacadd-3b11-480b-9d7c-ff14c50b05aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 50, "end": 1087, "text": " A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "97b9a19c-fac8-4bcb-8500-40b3de649acb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 108, "end": 564, "text": "My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3f5ab29b-2f21-448d-870e-3b89b05c1c1c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 84, "end": 300, "text": "That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "01d36e86-d25c-4659-be6f-aca50908595e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 107, "end": 626, "text": " It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "9c4f3f11-c33e-47ff-b4f2-6aab2ce64017", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code?", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3a7b597f-34b0-446c-adab-c080568e48c0", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 93, "end": 502, "text": " AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9"]}, {"id": "5ba2e964-fa60-4c62-94fa-f0feb2afc5fd", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 503, "end": 1106, "text": "I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd"]}, {"id": "0cbc6753-9ef5-4f0f-b4d8-496eddd14727", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 106, "end": 384, "text": "This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "96034856-2d4a-4978-84da-8d4b4481ccb4", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 51, "end": 488, "text": " I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "76195130-720b-4152-9aa3-72a8d671d624", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 1349, "text": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9305a0fb-da0b-45f1-a351-cedc13332a5b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 286, "text": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as wel", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2d614e2-a55e-46df-99f4-852447101d66", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 100, "end": 637, "text": " I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e4e794b5-b6d8-4666-a67c-fefb640c4def", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 4, "end": 514, "text": "This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "13cba10c-2f54-42aa-98a9-90ee069e2ccf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 607, "end": 1821, "text": " In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e097f575-4085-4a1d-92f1-43ff0b21ad7f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 1822, "end": 2594, "text": "Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "3068469d-d7b9-42ac-8502-33d284f7ada6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2595, "end": 2976, "text": "I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "c732fc79-dedc-44d9-a9de-2006bf53ff26", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2977, "end": 3396, "text": "But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "03f6b706-a795-4335-a72a-7e49d995cc6c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 463, "end": 807, "text": " Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "279d2282-63a8-44d9-99af-65d03cb97583", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 103, "end": 787, "text": " E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "f25d86aa-3810-41f5-a06c-18b69e95a839", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 510, "text": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "74f56b13-3847-427d-a5fb-cf2520654343", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 86, "end": 1474, "text": "Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation,", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "dfdcc40a-6a48-42cd-93a8-c0968a6c85aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 9, "end": 591, "text": "There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes)", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "fb976f23-970e-44de-9474-1c90a0691c8d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 116, "end": 323, "text": " I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "15ea0bcd-f0dd-470c-b609-8f8e5d0bc788", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 6, "end": 1882, "text": " I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "de462e67-8924-4e01-b026-e1346f7a801c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 106, "end": 768, "text": " I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a667360a-af00-44bf-9b68-47028a2c5a8f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 769, "end": 2128, "text": " I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a7212ccc-bb2c-4bd9-846e-291dc2d5953f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 1129, "text": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f"]}, {"id": "0dc06a45-7252-4215-8298-12b6df678768", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 104, "end": 2373, "text": "Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers:", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "b6279b38-1e49-4b33-a8ea-341968b0a7cc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2375, "end": 2923, "text": " Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "0e19e7d4-da5c-417b-98d6-d945e806bd7b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2925, "end": 3098, "text": "- New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "dfe0c110-4bf1-4dd7-8f3e-c47c1054591d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 677, "text": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "93772467-26d6-4608-9f90-6bf4cfa74063", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 111, "end": 525, "text": "This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point.", "codes": ["cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "eabff033-0347-44f3-acd0-fb6ddb9c7d4d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 526, "end": 1273, "text": "And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "87ade70d-e8ca-45c9-ba8c-de0652118a6f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 1167, "text": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "04fcc7f6-80cb-43d2-97f1-f5254eb99e5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 719, "text": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread)", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "cef518cc-ef6d-4dcb-9f98-af72aeda91f3", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "8330cfeb-7670-41cd-9b1f-4355a3e28227", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 108, "end": 1643, "text": "The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "826e950a-826d-40cc-8f94-280197573444", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 83, "end": 1015, "text": " TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact?", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "3b4302b1-a72c-4266-bee8-bfc1e80c5f74", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1016, "end": 1785, "text": "For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)?", "codes": ["dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3ece7a92-8060-4e37-a809-7ea6b0c9ae04", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1790, "end": 2646, "text": "FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "6e0bcb0e-99a7-4865-92d2-eaf0a391a0d2", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib.", "codes": ["0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d"]}, {"id": "57b3d8cf-4b08-41ba-bd36-5fd0e1339103", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 119, "end": 1566, "text": "Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "65fce25e-42a9-4bb5-9360-ac488fe392f6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 1738, "end": 3742, "text": "I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9beb18e8-8c7a-4f1e-a392-7e02e0efa82a", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 11, "end": 1583, "text": " That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "72bd2f2e-8e52-4e5a-a96a-9e21a14ea0b9", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 108, "end": 1212, "text": " Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "7e178caa-2e45-44d4-a940-0e934121963c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 77, "end": 2066, "text": "While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "84370455-9c80-4fb4-ae18-def6f7ace4c7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "start": 1196, "end": 3581, "text": "Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "2f0c99cc-8a57-4c5e-a0b6-e1d6fb04159d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "start": 0, "end": 399, "text": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "6a46c591-fb27-42a6-89d2-e80af4ad74ce", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "start": 3, "end": 1085, "text": " I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "2c405b5a-bddb-4c2b-a6e6-c73995386f5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 247, "end": 4336, "text": " Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a100e2d2-9263-4c6f-8632-5a3b7658360b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 4337, "end": 6067, "text": "So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "e5699363-9b3b-4a68-893d-bc408a0640cb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "start": 111, "end": 2906, "text": " I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "4ce57613-c997-4f36-8896-e95fe4ce9333", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "start": 102, "end": 1028, "text": " I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "48ed37b7-8b86-4cf5-b157-2befce171473", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 110, "end": 883, "text": " Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148"]}, {"id": "46fc2b28-aa18-4ad3-bb83-29dac54f9f69", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 271, "end": 1290, "text": "the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "599ceb84-b01b-497b-9512-ee8ca8338c54", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 1290, "end": 3713, "text": " Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e03a3a49-e8b1-4f9c-a868-0f385cd6bfc1", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "start": 106, "end": 500, "text": "Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "7dd730f9-0877-458c-921c-aadd1dfe6a44", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 8, "end": 1535, "text": "I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "cc6a8be2-9a9e-428f-88ca-d6550e4b6157", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 1537, "end": 2286, "text": "Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "723843d1-beb3-4a3b-89e2-39a7ecd1f1dc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "start": 216, "end": 823, "text": "I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "af1d0d5c-7138-48e2-af37-696f33159d8e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 110, "end": 210, "text": " The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "codes": ["3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9"]}, {"id": "c7d901f3-72e4-49ce-a85d-932c9db4e81d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "start": 116, "end": 531, "text": " I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "3bd43ffc-c8f6-42a5-bcfb-ca7925505acf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 100, "end": 605, "text": " Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR?", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "4e9f97e3-519a-410f-ab7f-17efae16604b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 74, "end": 2337, "text": " I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "29cd93ea-5019-4d1e-886b-514571d727a8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "start": 106, "end": 1320, "text": "Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "40a727e6-89cc-4ce5-b669-c43ef08cfadb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 8, "end": 1138, "text": " I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "ecaf0ecf-4a95-40bd-9e81-949840e32f45", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 76, "end": 1180, "text": " Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "6be354ae-cff2-4350-a3f3-b1d1b696c3ca", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-)", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "f8e79487-2393-4e2f-bdb4-115395d9a29d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 80, "end": 448, "text": "Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "42ec4d44-c578-4271-8f38-b4d545d31af8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 344, "text": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. ", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "e8ee1d2a-64a3-4521-8ea1-20999d6e726d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 750, "end": 1945, "text": " (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "585975d8-01fc-423e-9056-b17ca89f8921", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 1946, "end": 3082, "text": "(2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly?", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "8070a77e-9ca5-42b6-bfdd-dd8ca9ecb7a7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 3083, "end": 3902, "text": "(3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.”", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "50df7254-c5cd-455a-a937-dc177614f329", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 4070, "end": 5321, "text": "I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "eaa206e6-1176-4e8d-b09a-7ae600abb376", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 5322, "end": 6134, "text": "(4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7e6fb536-19a6-43db-b6eb-2fc701a8e771", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 6136, "end": 6849, "text": "(5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable).", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "34febe70-3dca-4ca7-ac43-a09afb41026f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9"]}, {"id": "2e137f3c-9f41-4186-a219-4d52f8718ecb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 60, "end": 908, "text": " FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "8c656a9d-1e35-49ad-835f-8d77f72ffba6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 77, "end": 964, "text": " I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "6c83ef21-c962-444c-a25b-19ddf70012ff", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "start": 426, "end": 1507, "text": " There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "93c7026e-f78f-43c7-bf7d-22aa8f3773cf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 84, "end": 327, "text": "You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "52fbca57-a761-4237-8177-5f37e809b548", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 4, "end": 903, "text": "I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}]}, "i04": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 17}, {"id": "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "name": "AI acceptability should scale with use and risk", "description": "Whether AI assistance is acceptable, and how much scrutiny it needs, should depend on the kind of use and the risk of the code (e.g., tests/boilerplate vs. core algorithms), not be all-or-nothing.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 16, "posts": 13}, {"id": "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "name": "AI adoption is inevitable", "description": "Asserts that widespread AI use in coding is inevitable and policy must accept it.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "name": "AI can support contributor learning", "description": "AI tools can help contributors learn (e.g., give feedback maintainers lack time for).", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "name": "AI contributions harm maintainer well-being", "description": "AI contributions demoralise, frustrate or burn out maintainers, or erode their motivation or joy in reviewing.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 18, "posts": 16}, {"id": "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "name": "AI erodes effort-based trust", "description": "Because AI makes plausible code, fluent text and apparent diligence cheap, cues that reviewers used to infer a contributor's effort, competence, care or understanding (code quality, explanations, communication style, accountability statements) no longer carry that information, so trust in the contributor is harder to establish. Specify which cue is weakened where possible; for communication style alone, also consider \"Communication signals human involvement\". (D21.)", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "name": "AI for understanding differs from AI for code generation", "description": "Using AI to research, analyse or understand code is treated as distinct from (and more acceptable than) using it to generate submitted code.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 20}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "name": "AI is tolerated not encouraged", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "name": "AI needs human oversight", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "name": "AI output enters the policy deliberation", "description": "AI-generated text or analysis is itself brought into the policy discussion as evidence or argument.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "name": "AI policy should be provisional amid uncertainty", "description": "Because AI tools and evidence change quickly, policy should be treated as provisional and revisited.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 1}, {"id": "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "name": "AI tools are built on appropriated work", "description": "AI tools are framed as built on taking others' work (training on copyrighted/private material), which shapes how contributor safeguards are judged.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 10}, {"id": "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "name": "AI use risks deskilling and learning loss", "description": "Relying on AI reduces contributors' or maintainers' learning, understanding or coding skill.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "name": "AI-generated code needs heightened review", "description": "AI-generated code requires more careful or different review than human-written code because its errors differ.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "name": "Communication signals human involvement", "description": "Participants read the language, style or voice of contribution communication as evidence of whether a human is involved (e.g. broken English as reassuring, 'flat agreeableness of an LLM'), including the language-access tension for non-native speakers. Distinguish valuing human voice, reading it as a signal, and prohibiting AI-written text (for opposition use 'Opposing AI-generated contribution communication').", "color": "#b7791f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "name": "Compliance burdens can encourage concealed AI use", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or documentation requirements are argued to encourage contributors to hide AI use rather than comply (a proposed feedback: restriction → concealment → less legibility). Distinct from 'People will use AI regardless of policy'.", "color": "#9b2c2c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "name": "Contributor admission should be trust-gated", "description": "Access to contribute (e.g., open PRs) should be gated by trust mechanisms such as vouching, permissions or introductions.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is important and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 15, "posts": 11}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor ownership supersedes AI usage concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 41, "posts": 31}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "name": "Honoring copyright is an ethical obligation", "description": "Respecting the intentions and attribution of original authors is framed as an ethical duty of the community, independent of legal enforceability.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "name": "Inevitability should not drive AI policy", "description": "Rejects 'AI is inevitable' as a basis for policy; the community can and should shape how AI is used.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "name": "Long-term maintainance can be affected by lack of understanding", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 28, "posts": 21}, {"id": "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 6}, {"id": "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "name": "new contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "newcomers may be discouraged by explicit understanding requirements", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 2}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or rules will not stop dishonest contributors; self-attestation is unreliable.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "name": "Policy deliberation strains collegial civility", "description": "The AI-policy discussion itself produces interpersonal friction, accusations or incivility among participants.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "name": "Policy scope should be limited to project interests", "description": "Policy should address only what bears on the project's own interests (broadly construed), not wider societal concerns.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "name": "Policy should be concrete and actionable", "description": "Policy guidance should give contributors and maintainers concrete, actionable steps or examples rather than abstract principles.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "name": "Policy should build on external precedents", "description": "The project should adopt or adapt other projects' policies rather than write its own from scratch.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "name": "Policy should stay pragmatic rather than moral", "description": "The project's policy should avoid broad moral or political statements and stay pragmatic.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "name": "Project norms should be codified for AI agents", "description": "Project norms should be written in forms AI tools consume (e.g., AGENTS.md, templates) to steer AI behaviour.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "name": "Projects should not prescribe contributors' tools", "description": "The project should not dictate which tools contributors use (or how they learn), only the quality/rules contributions must meet.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "name": "protective policy can be toxic", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 10}, {"id": "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "name": "Rejected AI contributions can provoke retaliation", "description": "Rejection of AI or AI-assisted contributions is followed, reported or feared to be followed, by retaliatory or shaming responses (e.g. an agent shame-posting after its PR is closed). Note whether the excerpt is direct experience, a reported event or a feared consequence.", "color": "#2f855a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "name": "Review effort needs a human stake", "description": "Review effort is justified, or loses its justification, by a human return: a submitter who needs or cares about the result, a contributor who may grow into a future maintainer, the reviewer (Broad human-return concept, D17; the lead's mentorship code is kept unchanged as the growth stake type.)", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 8}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 17}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 11}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "name": "The review process is a social interaction", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 12}, {"id": "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148", "name": "Uncertainty whether contributors are human", "description": "Maintainers cannot tell whether they are interacting with a person or an AI agent, and this uncertainty shapes their engagement.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 4}], "introduced": [], "merges": [], "segments": [{"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": ["78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": ["78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "1ce25ead-2c84-4ab1-91ce-3d77cb287879", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 118, "end": 1012, "text": " E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "26bacadd-3b11-480b-9d7c-ff14c50b05aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 50, "end": 1087, "text": " A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "97b9a19c-fac8-4bcb-8500-40b3de649acb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 108, "end": 564, "text": "My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3f5ab29b-2f21-448d-870e-3b89b05c1c1c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 84, "end": 300, "text": "That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "01d36e86-d25c-4659-be6f-aca50908595e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 107, "end": 626, "text": " It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "9c4f3f11-c33e-47ff-b4f2-6aab2ce64017", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code?", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3a7b597f-34b0-446c-adab-c080568e48c0", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 93, "end": 502, "text": " AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9"]}, {"id": "5ba2e964-fa60-4c62-94fa-f0feb2afc5fd", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 503, "end": 1106, "text": "I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd"]}, {"id": "0cbc6753-9ef5-4f0f-b4d8-496eddd14727", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 106, "end": 384, "text": "This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "96034856-2d4a-4978-84da-8d4b4481ccb4", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 51, "end": 488, "text": " I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "76195130-720b-4152-9aa3-72a8d671d624", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 1349, "text": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9305a0fb-da0b-45f1-a351-cedc13332a5b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 286, "text": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as wel", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2d614e2-a55e-46df-99f4-852447101d66", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 100, "end": 637, "text": " I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e4e794b5-b6d8-4666-a67c-fefb640c4def", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 4, "end": 514, "text": "This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "13cba10c-2f54-42aa-98a9-90ee069e2ccf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 607, "end": 1821, "text": " In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e097f575-4085-4a1d-92f1-43ff0b21ad7f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 1822, "end": 2594, "text": "Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "3068469d-d7b9-42ac-8502-33d284f7ada6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2595, "end": 2976, "text": "I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "c732fc79-dedc-44d9-a9de-2006bf53ff26", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2977, "end": 3396, "text": "But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "03f6b706-a795-4335-a72a-7e49d995cc6c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 463, "end": 807, "text": " Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "279d2282-63a8-44d9-99af-65d03cb97583", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 103, "end": 787, "text": " E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "f25d86aa-3810-41f5-a06c-18b69e95a839", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 510, "text": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "74f56b13-3847-427d-a5fb-cf2520654343", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 86, "end": 1474, "text": "Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation,", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "dfdcc40a-6a48-42cd-93a8-c0968a6c85aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 9, "end": 591, "text": "There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes)", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "fb976f23-970e-44de-9474-1c90a0691c8d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 116, "end": 323, "text": " I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "15ea0bcd-f0dd-470c-b609-8f8e5d0bc788", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 6, "end": 1882, "text": " I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "de462e67-8924-4e01-b026-e1346f7a801c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 106, "end": 768, "text": " I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a667360a-af00-44bf-9b68-47028a2c5a8f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 769, "end": 2128, "text": " I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a7212ccc-bb2c-4bd9-846e-291dc2d5953f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 1129, "text": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f"]}, {"id": "0dc06a45-7252-4215-8298-12b6df678768", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 104, "end": 2373, "text": "Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers:", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "b6279b38-1e49-4b33-a8ea-341968b0a7cc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2375, "end": 2923, "text": " Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "0e19e7d4-da5c-417b-98d6-d945e806bd7b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2925, "end": 3098, "text": "- New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "dfe0c110-4bf1-4dd7-8f3e-c47c1054591d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 677, "text": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "93772467-26d6-4608-9f90-6bf4cfa74063", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 111, "end": 525, "text": "This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point.", "codes": ["cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "eabff033-0347-44f3-acd0-fb6ddb9c7d4d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 526, "end": 1273, "text": "And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "87ade70d-e8ca-45c9-ba8c-de0652118a6f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 1167, "text": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "04fcc7f6-80cb-43d2-97f1-f5254eb99e5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 719, "text": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread)", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "cef518cc-ef6d-4dcb-9f98-af72aeda91f3", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "8330cfeb-7670-41cd-9b1f-4355a3e28227", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 108, "end": 1643, "text": "The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "826e950a-826d-40cc-8f94-280197573444", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 83, "end": 1015, "text": " TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact?", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "3b4302b1-a72c-4266-bee8-bfc1e80c5f74", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1016, "end": 1785, "text": "For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)?", "codes": ["dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3ece7a92-8060-4e37-a809-7ea6b0c9ae04", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1790, "end": 2646, "text": "FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "6e0bcb0e-99a7-4865-92d2-eaf0a391a0d2", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib.", "codes": ["0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d"]}, {"id": "57b3d8cf-4b08-41ba-bd36-5fd0e1339103", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 119, "end": 1566, "text": "Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "65fce25e-42a9-4bb5-9360-ac488fe392f6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 1738, "end": 3742, "text": "I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9beb18e8-8c7a-4f1e-a392-7e02e0efa82a", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 11, "end": 1583, "text": " That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "72bd2f2e-8e52-4e5a-a96a-9e21a14ea0b9", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 108, "end": 1212, "text": " Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "7e178caa-2e45-44d4-a940-0e934121963c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 77, "end": 2066, "text": "While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "84370455-9c80-4fb4-ae18-def6f7ace4c7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "start": 1196, "end": 3581, "text": "Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "2f0c99cc-8a57-4c5e-a0b6-e1d6fb04159d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "start": 0, "end": 399, "text": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "6a46c591-fb27-42a6-89d2-e80af4ad74ce", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "start": 3, "end": 1085, "text": " I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "2c405b5a-bddb-4c2b-a6e6-c73995386f5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 247, "end": 4336, "text": " Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a100e2d2-9263-4c6f-8632-5a3b7658360b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 4337, "end": 6067, "text": "So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "e5699363-9b3b-4a68-893d-bc408a0640cb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "start": 111, "end": 2906, "text": " I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "4ce57613-c997-4f36-8896-e95fe4ce9333", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "start": 102, "end": 1028, "text": " I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "48ed37b7-8b86-4cf5-b157-2befce171473", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 110, "end": 883, "text": " Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148"]}, {"id": "46fc2b28-aa18-4ad3-bb83-29dac54f9f69", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 271, "end": 1290, "text": "the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "599ceb84-b01b-497b-9512-ee8ca8338c54", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 1290, "end": 3713, "text": " Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e03a3a49-e8b1-4f9c-a868-0f385cd6bfc1", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "start": 106, "end": 500, "text": "Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "7dd730f9-0877-458c-921c-aadd1dfe6a44", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 8, "end": 1535, "text": "I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "cc6a8be2-9a9e-428f-88ca-d6550e4b6157", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 1537, "end": 2286, "text": "Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "723843d1-beb3-4a3b-89e2-39a7ecd1f1dc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "start": 216, "end": 823, "text": "I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "af1d0d5c-7138-48e2-af37-696f33159d8e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 110, "end": 210, "text": " The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "codes": ["3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9"]}, {"id": "c7d901f3-72e4-49ce-a85d-932c9db4e81d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "start": 116, "end": 531, "text": " I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "3bd43ffc-c8f6-42a5-bcfb-ca7925505acf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 100, "end": 605, "text": " Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR?", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "4e9f97e3-519a-410f-ab7f-17efae16604b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 74, "end": 2337, "text": " I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "29cd93ea-5019-4d1e-886b-514571d727a8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "start": 106, "end": 1320, "text": "Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "40a727e6-89cc-4ce5-b669-c43ef08cfadb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 8, "end": 1138, "text": " I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "ecaf0ecf-4a95-40bd-9e81-949840e32f45", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 76, "end": 1180, "text": " Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "6be354ae-cff2-4350-a3f3-b1d1b696c3ca", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-)", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "f8e79487-2393-4e2f-bdb4-115395d9a29d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 80, "end": 448, "text": "Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "42ec4d44-c578-4271-8f38-b4d545d31af8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 344, "text": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. ", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "e8ee1d2a-64a3-4521-8ea1-20999d6e726d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 750, "end": 1945, "text": " (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "585975d8-01fc-423e-9056-b17ca89f8921", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 1946, "end": 3082, "text": "(2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly?", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "8070a77e-9ca5-42b6-bfdd-dd8ca9ecb7a7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 3083, "end": 3902, "text": "(3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.”", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "50df7254-c5cd-455a-a937-dc177614f329", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 4070, "end": 5321, "text": "I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "eaa206e6-1176-4e8d-b09a-7ae600abb376", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 5322, "end": 6134, "text": "(4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7e6fb536-19a6-43db-b6eb-2fc701a8e771", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 6136, "end": 6849, "text": "(5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable).", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "34febe70-3dca-4ca7-ac43-a09afb41026f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9"]}, {"id": "2e137f3c-9f41-4186-a219-4d52f8718ecb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 60, "end": 908, "text": " FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "8c656a9d-1e35-49ad-835f-8d77f72ffba6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 77, "end": 964, "text": " I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "6c83ef21-c962-444c-a25b-19ddf70012ff", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "start": 426, "end": 1507, "text": " There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "93c7026e-f78f-43c7-bf7d-22aa8f3773cf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 84, "end": 327, "text": "You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "52fbca57-a761-4237-8177-5f37e809b548", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 4, "end": 903, "text": "I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}]}, "i05": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 24, "posts": 18}, {"id": "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "name": "AI acceptability should scale with use and risk", "description": "Whether AI assistance is acceptable, and how much scrutiny it needs, should depend on the kind of use and the risk of the code (e.g., tests/boilerplate vs. core algorithms), not be all-or-nothing.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 19}, {"id": "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "name": "AI adoption is inevitable", "description": "Asserts that widespread AI use in coding is inevitable and policy must accept it.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "name": "AI can support contributor learning", "description": "AI tools can help contributors learn (e.g., give feedback maintainers lack time for).", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "4b9ebfaf-f258-4ae5-81cc-1c4481dea09f", "name": "AI contribution pressure is not yet acute", "description": "Participants report that AI contributions are not (yet) a significant burden in their project.", "color": "#8a8f8c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "name": "AI contributions harm maintainer well-being", "description": "AI contributions demoralise, frustrate or burn out maintainers, or erode their motivation or joy in reviewing.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 4}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 20}, {"id": "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "name": "AI erodes effort-based trust", "description": "Because AI makes plausible code, fluent text and apparent diligence cheap, cues that reviewers used to infer a contributor's effort, competence, care or understanding (code quality, explanations, communication style, accountability statements) no longer carry that information, so trust in the contributor is harder to establish. Specify which cue is weakened where possible; for communication style alone, also consider \"Communication signals human involvement\". (D21.)", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 10}, {"id": "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "name": "AI for understanding differs from AI for code generation", "description": "Using AI to research, analyse or understand code is treated as distinct from (and more acceptable than) using it to generate submitted code.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 23, "posts": 23}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 10}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "name": "AI is tolerated not encouraged", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "name": "AI needs human oversight", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "name": "AI output enters the policy deliberation", "description": "AI-generated text or analysis is itself brought into the policy discussion as evidence or argument.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "name": "AI policy should be coordinated across the ecosystem", "description": "Related projects should develop or share a common AI policy rather than each deciding alone.", "color": "#9f6a2f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "name": "AI policy should be provisional amid uncertainty", "description": "Because AI tools and evidence change quickly, policy should be treated as provisional and revisited.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 3}, {"id": "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "name": "AI tools are built on appropriated work", "description": "AI tools are framed as built on taking others' work (training on copyrighted/private material), which shapes how contributor safeguards are judged.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 12}, {"id": "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "name": "AI use risks deskilling and learning loss", "description": "Relying on AI reduces contributors' or maintainers' learning, understanding or coding skill.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 11}, {"id": "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "name": "AI-generated code needs heightened review", "description": "AI-generated code requires more careful or different review than human-written code because its errors differ.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "name": "Communication signals human involvement", "description": "Participants read the language, style or voice of contribution communication as evidence of whether a human is involved (e.g. broken English as reassuring, 'flat agreeableness of an LLM'), including the language-access tension for non-native speakers. Distinguish valuing human voice, reading it as a signal, and prohibiting AI-written text (for opposition use 'Opposing AI-generated contribution communication').", "color": "#b7791f", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 14}, {"id": "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "name": "Compliance burdens can encourage concealed AI use", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or documentation requirements are argued to encourage contributors to hide AI use rather than comply (a proposed feedback: restriction → concealment → less legibility). Distinct from 'People will use AI regardless of policy'.", "color": "#9b2c2c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "541fcf96-75e8-4328-b332-6a589eeb9dfc", "name": "Contributor acts as a relay for the LLM", "description": "The human contributor merely passes reviewer feedback to and from an LLM, adding no understanding.", "color": "#8a8f8c", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "name": "Contributor admission should be trust-gated", "description": "Access to contribute (e.g., open PRs) should be gated by trust mechanisms such as vouching, permissions or introductions.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is important and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 13}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor ownership supersedes AI usage concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 53, "posts": 40}, {"id": "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "name": "Copyright verification is infeasible for contributors", "description": "Contributors cannot meaningfully verify or attest to the copyright status of AI output, so attestations carry little information.", "color": "#8c5f3f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 11}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "348cc0ac-6e79-45be-85a0-3062280e3557", "name": "Domain complexity limits AI fitness", "description": "The specialised scientific/numerical nature of the codebase makes current AI tools less capable or less useful here than elsewhere.", "color": "#2f7f8f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 2}, {"id": "97d6e58b-4d1c-47ce-99fa-95397c7f637f", "name": "Fully automated contributions should be excluded", "description": "Contributions produced end-to-end by AI without meaningful human involvement should not be accepted.", "color": "#2f7f8f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 3}, {"id": "c48b58eb-2c38-4a07-8f91-a99bc02d6f5d", "name": "High Volume PRs are a result of gamification", "description": "", "color": "#8a8f8c", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "name": "Honoring copyright is an ethical obligation", "description": "Respecting the intentions and attribution of original authors is framed as an ethical duty of the community, independent of legal enforceability.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 10}, {"id": "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "name": "Inevitability should not drive AI policy", "description": "Rejects 'AI is inevitable' as a basis for policy; the community can and should shape how AI is used.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "name": "Long-term maintainance can be affected by lack of understanding", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 29, "posts": 22}, {"id": "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 8}, {"id": "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "name": "new contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "newcomers may be discouraged by explicit understanding requirements", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 2}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or rules will not stop dishonest contributors; self-attestation is unreliable.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "name": "Policy compliance is unverifiable", "description": "Maintainers cannot verify whether contributors complied with an AI policy (e.g., whether AI was used or disclosed).", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "name": "Policy deliberation strains collegial civility", "description": "The AI-policy discussion itself produces interpersonal friction, accusations or incivility among participants.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "name": "Policy scope should be limited to project interests", "description": "Policy should address only what bears on the project's own interests (broadly construed), not wider societal concerns.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "name": "Policy should be concrete and actionable", "description": "Policy guidance should give contributors and maintainers concrete, actionable steps or examples rather than abstract principles.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 11}, {"id": "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "name": "Policy should build on external precedents", "description": "The project should adopt or adapt other projects' policies rather than write its own from scratch.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "name": "Policy should stay pragmatic rather than moral", "description": "The project's policy should avoid broad moral or political statements and stay pragmatic.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "name": "Pre-AI norms are the yardstick for AI contributions", "description": "AI-assisted contributions are judged by analogy to existing norms (copying from Stack Overflow/textbooks, provenance, authorship), rather than as a new category.", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 6}, {"id": "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "name": "Project norms should be codified for AI agents", "description": "Project norms should be written in forms AI tools consume (e.g., AGENTS.md, templates) to steer AI behaviour.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "name": "Projects should not prescribe contributors' tools", "description": "The project should not dictate which tools contributors use (or how they learn), only the quality/rules contributions must meet.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "name": "protective policy can be toxic", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 10}, {"id": "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "name": "Rejected AI contributions can provoke retaliation", "description": "Rejection of AI or AI-assisted contributions is followed, reported or feared to be followed, by retaliatory or shaming responses (e.g. an agent shame-posting after its PR is closed). Note whether the excerpt is direct experience, a reported event or a feared consequence.", "color": "#2f855a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "name": "Review becomes transactional when growth is absent", "description": "When a contribution offers no prospect of contributor growth, reviewers shift from mentoring to a transactional, accept/reject mode.", "color": "#7f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "name": "Review effort needs a human stake", "description": "Review effort is justified, or loses its justification, by a human return: a submitter who needs or cares about the result, a contributor who may grow into a future maintainer, the reviewer (Broad human-return concept, D17; the lead's mentorship code is kept unchanged as the growth stake type.)", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 9}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 15, "posts": 12}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 28}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 12}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "name": "The review process is a social interaction", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 18, "posts": 15}, {"id": "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148", "name": "Uncertainty whether contributors are human", "description": "Maintainers cannot tell whether they are interacting with a person or an AI agent, and this uncertainty shapes their engagement.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7", "name": "Unenforceable norms are still worth stating", "description": "Even if a rule cannot be enforced or verified, stating it has expressive value: it sets expectations and signals what the community values.", "color": "#2f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf", "name": "unfamiliarity and uncertainty with copyright norms", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 2}], "introduced": [{"id": "4b9ebfaf-f258-4ae5-81cc-1c4481dea09f", "name": "AI contribution pressure is not yet acute", "description": "Participants report that AI contributions are not (yet) a significant burden in their project.", "color": "#8a8f8c", "count": 2}, {"id": "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "name": "AI policy should be coordinated across the ecosystem", "description": "Related projects should develop or share a common AI policy rather than each deciding alone.", "color": "#9f6a2f", "count": 3}, {"id": "541fcf96-75e8-4328-b332-6a589eeb9dfc", "name": "Contributor acts as a relay for the LLM", "description": "The human contributor merely passes reviewer feedback to and from an LLM, adding no understanding.", "color": "#8a8f8c", "count": 1}, {"id": "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "name": "Copyright verification is infeasible for contributors", "description": "Contributors cannot meaningfully verify or attest to the copyright status of AI output, so attestations carry little information.", "color": "#8c5f3f", "count": 4}, {"id": "348cc0ac-6e79-45be-85a0-3062280e3557", "name": "Domain complexity limits AI fitness", "description": "The specialised scientific/numerical nature of the codebase makes current AI tools less capable or less useful here than elsewhere.", "color": "#2f7f8f", "count": 3}, {"id": "97d6e58b-4d1c-47ce-99fa-95397c7f637f", "name": "Fully automated contributions should be excluded", "description": "Contributions produced end-to-end by AI without meaningful human involvement should not be accepted.", "color": "#2f7f8f", "count": 4}, {"id": "c48b58eb-2c38-4a07-8f91-a99bc02d6f5d", "name": "High Volume PRs are a result of gamification", "description": "", "color": "#8a8f8c", "count": 1}, {"id": "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "name": "Policy compliance is unverifiable", "description": "Maintainers cannot verify whether contributors complied with an AI policy (e.g., whether AI was used or disclosed).", "color": "#5f3f8c", "count": 6}, {"id": "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "name": "Pre-AI norms are the yardstick for AI contributions", "description": "AI-assisted contributions are judged by analogy to existing norms (copying from Stack Overflow/textbooks, provenance, authorship), rather than as a new category.", "color": "#5f3f8c", "count": 7}, {"id": "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "name": "Review becomes transactional when growth is absent", "description": "When a contribution offers no prospect of contributor growth, reviewers shift from mentoring to a transactional, accept/reject mode.", "color": "#7f8f2f", "count": 1}, {"id": "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7", "name": "Unenforceable norms are still worth stating", "description": "Even if a rule cannot be enforced or verified, stating it has expressive value: it sets expectations and signals what the community values.", "color": "#2f8c5a", "count": 4}, {"id": "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf", "name": "unfamiliarity and uncertainty with copyright norms", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "count": 3}], "merges": [], "segments": [{"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": ["78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "28739d19-b342-4f02-92ae-882dcec81bc1", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": ["78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "1ce25ead-2c84-4ab1-91ce-3d77cb287879", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 118, "end": 1012, "text": " E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "26bacadd-3b11-480b-9d7c-ff14c50b05aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 50, "end": 1087, "text": " A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "97b9a19c-fac8-4bcb-8500-40b3de649acb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 108, "end": 564, "text": "My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3f5ab29b-2f21-448d-870e-3b89b05c1c1c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 84, "end": 300, "text": "That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "01d36e86-d25c-4659-be6f-aca50908595e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 107, "end": 626, "text": " It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "9c4f3f11-c33e-47ff-b4f2-6aab2ce64017", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code?", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3a7b597f-34b0-446c-adab-c080568e48c0", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 93, "end": 502, "text": " AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9"]}, {"id": "5ba2e964-fa60-4c62-94fa-f0feb2afc5fd", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 503, "end": 1106, "text": "I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd"]}, {"id": "0cbc6753-9ef5-4f0f-b4d8-496eddd14727", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 106, "end": 384, "text": "This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "96034856-2d4a-4978-84da-8d4b4481ccb4", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 51, "end": 488, "text": " I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "76195130-720b-4152-9aa3-72a8d671d624", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 1349, "text": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9305a0fb-da0b-45f1-a351-cedc13332a5b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 286, "text": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as wel", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2d614e2-a55e-46df-99f4-852447101d66", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 100, "end": 637, "text": " I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e4e794b5-b6d8-4666-a67c-fefb640c4def", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 4, "end": 514, "text": "This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "13cba10c-2f54-42aa-98a9-90ee069e2ccf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 607, "end": 1821, "text": " In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e097f575-4085-4a1d-92f1-43ff0b21ad7f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 1822, "end": 2594, "text": "Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "3068469d-d7b9-42ac-8502-33d284f7ada6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2595, "end": 2976, "text": "I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "c732fc79-dedc-44d9-a9de-2006bf53ff26", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2977, "end": 3396, "text": "But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "03f6b706-a795-4335-a72a-7e49d995cc6c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 463, "end": 807, "text": " Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "279d2282-63a8-44d9-99af-65d03cb97583", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 103, "end": 787, "text": " E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "f25d86aa-3810-41f5-a06c-18b69e95a839", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 510, "text": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "74f56b13-3847-427d-a5fb-cf2520654343", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 86, "end": 1474, "text": "Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation,", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "dfdcc40a-6a48-42cd-93a8-c0968a6c85aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 9, "end": 591, "text": "There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes)", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "fb976f23-970e-44de-9474-1c90a0691c8d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 116, "end": 323, "text": " I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "15ea0bcd-f0dd-470c-b609-8f8e5d0bc788", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 6, "end": 1882, "text": " I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "de462e67-8924-4e01-b026-e1346f7a801c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 106, "end": 768, "text": " I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a667360a-af00-44bf-9b68-47028a2c5a8f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 769, "end": 2128, "text": " I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a7212ccc-bb2c-4bd9-846e-291dc2d5953f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 1129, "text": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f"]}, {"id": "0dc06a45-7252-4215-8298-12b6df678768", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 104, "end": 2373, "text": "Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers:", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "b6279b38-1e49-4b33-a8ea-341968b0a7cc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2375, "end": 2923, "text": " Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "0e19e7d4-da5c-417b-98d6-d945e806bd7b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2925, "end": 3098, "text": "- New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow.", "codes": ["6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "dfe0c110-4bf1-4dd7-8f3e-c47c1054591d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 677, "text": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "93772467-26d6-4608-9f90-6bf4cfa74063", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 111, "end": 525, "text": "This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point.", "codes": ["cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "eabff033-0347-44f3-acd0-fb6ddb9c7d4d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 526, "end": 1273, "text": "And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "87ade70d-e8ca-45c9-ba8c-de0652118a6f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 1167, "text": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "04fcc7f6-80cb-43d2-97f1-f5254eb99e5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 719, "text": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread)", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "cef518cc-ef6d-4dcb-9f98-af72aeda91f3", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "8330cfeb-7670-41cd-9b1f-4355a3e28227", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 108, "end": 1643, "text": "The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "826e950a-826d-40cc-8f94-280197573444", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 83, "end": 1015, "text": " TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact?", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "3b4302b1-a72c-4266-bee8-bfc1e80c5f74", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1016, "end": 1785, "text": "For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)?", "codes": ["dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3ece7a92-8060-4e37-a809-7ea6b0c9ae04", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1790, "end": 2646, "text": "FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "6e0bcb0e-99a7-4865-92d2-eaf0a391a0d2", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib.", "codes": ["0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d"]}, {"id": "57b3d8cf-4b08-41ba-bd36-5fd0e1339103", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 119, "end": 1566, "text": "Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "65fce25e-42a9-4bb5-9360-ac488fe392f6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 1738, "end": 3742, "text": "I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b3e768ff-aab3-466c-b1f9-14073d6195ad", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9beb18e8-8c7a-4f1e-a392-7e02e0efa82a", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 11, "end": 1583, "text": " That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "72bd2f2e-8e52-4e5a-a96a-9e21a14ea0b9", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 108, "end": 1212, "text": " Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "7e178caa-2e45-44d4-a940-0e934121963c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 77, "end": 2066, "text": "While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "84370455-9c80-4fb4-ae18-def6f7ace4c7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "start": 1196, "end": 3581, "text": "Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "2f0c99cc-8a57-4c5e-a0b6-e1d6fb04159d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "start": 0, "end": 399, "text": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "6a46c591-fb27-42a6-89d2-e80af4ad74ce", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "start": 3, "end": 1085, "text": " I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "2c405b5a-bddb-4c2b-a6e6-c73995386f5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 247, "end": 4336, "text": " Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a100e2d2-9263-4c6f-8632-5a3b7658360b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 4337, "end": 6067, "text": "So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "6ef3223b-743d-425a-9584-0caad1a5ee6d"]}, {"id": "e5699363-9b3b-4a68-893d-bc408a0640cb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "start": 111, "end": 2906, "text": " I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "4ce57613-c997-4f36-8896-e95fe4ce9333", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "start": 102, "end": 1028, "text": " I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "48ed37b7-8b86-4cf5-b157-2befce171473", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 110, "end": 883, "text": " Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148"]}, {"id": "46fc2b28-aa18-4ad3-bb83-29dac54f9f69", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 271, "end": 1290, "text": "the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "599ceb84-b01b-497b-9512-ee8ca8338c54", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 1290, "end": 3713, "text": " Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e03a3a49-e8b1-4f9c-a868-0f385cd6bfc1", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "start": 106, "end": 500, "text": "Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "7dd730f9-0877-458c-921c-aadd1dfe6a44", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 8, "end": 1535, "text": "I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "cc6a8be2-9a9e-428f-88ca-d6550e4b6157", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 1537, "end": 2286, "text": "Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "723843d1-beb3-4a3b-89e2-39a7ecd1f1dc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "start": 216, "end": 823, "text": "I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "af1d0d5c-7138-48e2-af37-696f33159d8e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 110, "end": 210, "text": " The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "codes": ["3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9"]}, {"id": "c7d901f3-72e4-49ce-a85d-932c9db4e81d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "start": 116, "end": 531, "text": " I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "3bd43ffc-c8f6-42a5-bcfb-ca7925505acf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 100, "end": 605, "text": " Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR?", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "4e9f97e3-519a-410f-ab7f-17efae16604b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 74, "end": 2337, "text": " I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "29cd93ea-5019-4d1e-886b-514571d727a8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "start": 106, "end": 1320, "text": "Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "40a727e6-89cc-4ce5-b669-c43ef08cfadb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 8, "end": 1138, "text": " I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "ecaf0ecf-4a95-40bd-9e81-949840e32f45", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 76, "end": 1180, "text": " Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "6be354ae-cff2-4350-a3f3-b1d1b696c3ca", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-)", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "f8e79487-2393-4e2f-bdb4-115395d9a29d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 80, "end": 448, "text": "Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "42ec4d44-c578-4271-8f38-b4d545d31af8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 344, "text": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. ", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "e8ee1d2a-64a3-4521-8ea1-20999d6e726d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 750, "end": 1945, "text": " (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "585975d8-01fc-423e-9056-b17ca89f8921", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 1946, "end": 3082, "text": "(2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly?", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "8070a77e-9ca5-42b6-bfdd-dd8ca9ecb7a7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 3083, "end": 3902, "text": "(3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.”", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "50df7254-c5cd-455a-a937-dc177614f329", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 4070, "end": 5321, "text": "I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "eaa206e6-1176-4e8d-b09a-7ae600abb376", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 5322, "end": 6134, "text": "(4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7e6fb536-19a6-43db-b6eb-2fc701a8e771", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 6136, "end": 6849, "text": "(5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable).", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "34febe70-3dca-4ca7-ac43-a09afb41026f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9"]}, {"id": "2e137f3c-9f41-4186-a219-4d52f8718ecb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 60, "end": 908, "text": " FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "8c656a9d-1e35-49ad-835f-8d77f72ffba6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 77, "end": 964, "text": " I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "6c83ef21-c962-444c-a25b-19ddf70012ff", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "start": 426, "end": 1507, "text": " There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "93c7026e-f78f-43c7-bf7d-22aa8f3773cf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 84, "end": 327, "text": "You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "52fbca57-a761-4237-8177-5f37e809b548", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 4, "end": 903, "text": "I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "f00c88ed-8f95-4d8f-8398-205bb63f7ed7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 844, "end": 1916, "text": "it seems that the concerns can be roughly classified into two buckets: Do we accept genAI assisted contributions at all, and if we do, what are the boundaries of what’s acceptable. Copyright and license concerns. Let’s consider these two in order. Do we accept genAI contributions? It’s futile to attempt to block them wholesale. People are going to use AI assistants anyway, along with various IDEs, autocompleters etc So it seems reasonable to treat these assistants on par with other tools: as long as it does not matter if a contributor uses VS Code or emacs or vi, it also does not matter what engine powers their autocompleter. The discussion in [1] has a concise wording, “Only submit code that you actually undestand”, and I’d just go with just this. One thing we probably do not want to see is a fully automated submission, which is both generated and submitted by a bot, with no human intervention or supervision at all. So we could/should stress that, too. Copyright violations / incompatible licenses Here I lack expertise and have more questions than answers.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "97d6e58b-4d1c-47ce-99fa-95397c7f637f", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "e00a42fd-55d5-4fb1-a625-b0cab528395b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1917, "end": 3210, "text": "Here’s a possibly naive attempt at asking hopefully relevant questions: Are we concerned that the code generated by some tool is under some incompatible license from just the fact of being generated by the tool? In this case, using the tool is similar to copying code from, say, Numerical Recipes. Then we should either blacklist known bad tools (do we know of any?) or ask contributors to declare the tools they used (so that we can remove offending code at a future date if needed). Are we concerned that the training set of a tool included license-incompatible code? And if it did, what does it mean for us, are we then transitively non-compliant? If this is a concern, and we at some point discover that some code is license-incompatible, the action does not depend on how it got into the codebase, genAI or not: we discover it, we remove it. And then the only question IMO is whether it’s helpful to us to ask contributors list the AI assistants they used. To summarize, we should not block genAI assisted contributions; we should block fully automated contributions, and ask submitters to only submit code they understand; TBD if we want to ask contributors to list what genAI assistants they used, if any. Let’s add explicit (short!) guidance to the docs, TBD where precisely. Thoughts?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "97d6e58b-4d1c-47ce-99fa-95397c7f637f", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "047d9e6e-4d7d-470d-854c-abc47cfe58c5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 66, "end": 1318, "text": "If I could, I would have used these ChatGPT/CoPilot/Skynet/“All seeing overlord AI” like there is no tomorrow. But my experience with them so far is that, they are almost good and make just a tiny mistake like i becomes j , then I spend rest of the week troubleshooting, learning how to use GDB and fail miserably etc. So it seems like we are swapping manual labor of translating with manual labor of troubleshooting, which for me, definitely the latter that is more painful. But maybe it is because it is not trained enough on F77 or JurassicPACK code we have. On the other hand, I did get some useful tips if I ask programming tips, say, “show me an example of while loop that uses comma operator as an expression in C17” and it brought some nice results. I did not try but some folks in my team use it for summarizing the code about what it does. Other than that, probably @rkern is the most knowledgeable among us what the legal implications might be, however Copilot seems to understand when you say avoid GPL sources for the answer at the citations it brings. Having said all these, I noticed a particular behavior from all AI tools. They are providing a melange of things that they have in their training bank (whatever they call it these days).", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "348cc0ac-6e79-45be-85a0-3062280e3557", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "b4be7cb2-e694-429f-b5ac-10543209ab09", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 1319, "end": 2794, "text": "I am ready to be immortalized in the history books as an idiot, but AI tools are terrible at coming up coherent code for numerical computing. Not only they can’t answer any technical computing question coherently, but when asked in ASCII about certain technical problem, they are bringing almost always some code that looks suspiciously similar to either GNU scientific library or Octave or some stuff they knocked off from some personal gist translated in the requested language. I noticed this mostly because of the strange index corrections before they do things on the arrays (which signals that the source must be a 1-indexed one). While sometimes the result is very close to the actual thing, it is devoid of any proper numerical checks (because they can’t judge the result). Hence, in my personal opinion, before we even judge the legality of things, the sheer possibility of the code being not production ready gives me enough confidence to reject any AI generated code. Thus agree with summary point 2 even if the code runs correctly. This is not the same with a developer using AI for snippets of code for very specific thing and including parts of AI generated code. This is very tricky to judge as AI tools already swallowed Stackoverflow and other sources, we don’t have any ways to backtrack the original source. So a careful agree with summary point 1. I don’t know how to enforce point 3 but if there is a way, that would be really nice. Point 4, fully agree.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "97d6e58b-4d1c-47ce-99fa-95397c7f637f", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "348cc0ac-6e79-45be-85a0-3062280e3557", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "5ddf8ded-b9e7-4567-a80c-8b10e845eec9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 994, "text": "Out of precaution (there are still many lawsuits pending) and most importantly, for moral and ethical reasons, I am -1 on allowing any generative AI at all. Adding here the opinion piece from Paul, which I fully agree with: Current Challenges in Free Software and Open Source Development We spend so much effort ensuring that contributions are original and respect all licensing, copyrights and just wishes of original authors, that it would be strange to me to adopt a different position. Furthermore, as pointed out here and in some other threads, the nature of what we do in SciPy is difficult and these tools don’t (yet) shine. Writing code is only part of the story and often times we spend our effort discussing API, architecture, etc. Hence I don’t think that it would be detrimental to disallow such tools. There is a lot of hype and companies in the sector are trying hard to push that we are doomed if we don’t use their stuff. IMHO, this is not true and we are far from replacing us.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "348cc0ac-6e79-45be-85a0-3062280e3557", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "3c21abb4-d8f7-4d3c-b953-0472a0e79e55", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "Since this is an issue that affects all projects in the ecosystem, can we collaborate to co-author a SPEC that can be widely adopted, instead of each library having to figure it out for themselves?", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873"]}, {"id": "9f78c452-8cd9-4036-b4f7-ce7336eec88b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 387, "text": "I haven’t seen any problems with this. In my experience, it’s very useful and writes high-quality code and saves time. I guess it depends on which tool you’re using. As long as unit tests are included and human reviewers approve it, I don’t see how it’s any different in trustworthiness from code written by random humans. But yes, copyright and license legality is a legitimate concern.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "9de47c35-b592-445d-925a-02062b62ed25", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 693, "text": "Hi, It doesn’t seem useful to argue about the usefulness of the AI-generated code, or otherwise, at this stage. I haven’t found it useful myself, but maybe that’s just my case, and in any case, it may well improve. It’s the license issue that is central. I don’t think there’s any argument that these tools are all digesting code with licenses that at least require attribution, and producing code, indirectly (or directly at times) derived from that licensed code, but with all licensing stripped from the output. The value of a spec or similar, over a blog post, is the ability to iterate to a shared and agreed conclusion, rather than leaving it as the opinion of any one particular person.", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "0e5b84de-2166-4471-9e3e-d6a2d9cda7c7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 30, "end": 439, "text": " We don’t have any hope of controlling user behavior through a policy document. Many people find these tools useful, so they will keep using them, and there is no sensible way to check the license of the generated snippet. For larger PRs, which may contain more overt license breaches, it’s probably less likely that AI was used wholesale, since the author needed to understand the problem well to begin with.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "90a10463-8e44-4f1a-8cc0-1969c9ef4eff", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 303, "text": "As we don’t have any ways of ensuring people did not read NR nor had a look at GPL code nor took anything from StackOverflow. To me that’s the same thing. If we were to say that we don’t want people not to use any AI tools we could and that’s the same type of self declaration we would ask people to do.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "27a779f2-7a25-4118-a83b-52e6e50ecf4c", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 1566, "text": "I think it is not that simple to discard the usefulness, though I wrote a wall of text arguing otherwise above. These tools, whether blindly parroting or contemplating, became quite eloquent. As I mentioned above, lately I have been testing them (or they are observing me) and though a bit of success is achieved for line completion, figuring out things from scratch are not even close. However, once I put a lot of comments and write the first two-three lines they predict the context OKish. Then they fill in some boilerplate code and I correct here and there. Some time has saved, some productivity increased in turn utilizing a power plant worth energy for this. I would suggest putting a few sentences in the policy docs that we don’t want wholesale AI code knowing full well it would be near impossible to distinguish. But if we notice something noticeably fishy, we can point to the policy. Or if someone is reading before contributing, they’ll get a taste of our stance. How AI tools became like that and what they did criminally/unethically to become like that, is something we can discuss for years. Licenses and policies would definitely protect across the totality of the project, but I think anything more granular than that, it is just futile Stallman-ing the reality. Unlike licensed code from elsewhere, people will grab pieces from LLMs here and there. We don’t need to pick this fight in our limited time. Besides, it’s not like we are getting very large contributions anyways. So I think this is not a big issue as we might be starting to discuss.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7", "4b9ebfaf-f258-4ae5-81cc-1c4481dea09f"]}, {"id": "77c53c4e-bfc3-409b-949c-b1455ee5b146", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 1427, "end": 2228, "text": " I remember, back in the day - it was desirable to do “clean-room” porting - where the person writing the new code had not read the code with an incompatible license. For example, one person could read the e.g. GPL code, describe the algorithm, and the other person could write the new code. If the person writing the code had read the the GPL code, it was always possible for the GPL code to leak into the new code. Of course, with AI, it’s all dirty room - it can produce code from anywhere. So I think we have to have the same policy for AI as for dirty-room code. We can’t use it. Now - enforcement - that’s a different matter - I just think we should pursue the same policies on enforcement as we did before. We say don’t do it, and if we find you did do it, we’ll complain and pull out the code.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "b3fa48a3-4a1f-44ac-85aa-bd9be0e88f43", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 74, "end": 433, "text": " What I am trying to emphasize is the practicality of it. If same code appears a 50 places because all 50 of them used AI generated sources and MIT’d the code. I don’t think we can find the 51st original source and point to its GPL. So it is an idealistic stance. Still good to have it but a policy won’t solve any trouble in case we find out the truth later.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "925e1625-49af-40bb-9d28-93b3c788fba3", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1355, "text": "I’d be happy to see a SPEC about this - I think it can be an informative SPEC (similar to an informative PEP) that doesn’t propose a single solution to adopt, but contains at least the following: Prior art - what did other well-known open source projects decide and why? Legal status - link to relevant sources, and summarize if there’s any expectation of more legal clarity arising in the near to medium term. Lay out a set of distinct options that projects can choose from, with their main pros and cons. E.g.: Option 1: do nothing for now (status quo) Option 2: forbid all generated code by an LLM-based tool Option 3: add guidance to PR template & docs that contributors may choose to use such tools, but need to be aware and take responsibility for copyright of the code they submit Option 4: … ? None of that is project-specific, and people can contribute new arguments and options if they find there are gaps in such a SPEC. A Discourse thread only is a poor medium for iterating to a solution on such a complex topic. I completely agree with @rkern that it’s largely about values and preferences - it’s just a lot easier to get a sense of those and also help people less knowledgeable about the subject form an opinion if there’s a structured document to read and refer to. Once a good SPEC exists, a SciPy-specific choice can more easily be made.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "19747253-695b-4500-bb7b-d914913a6d1e", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 175, "end": 1302, "text": " I wouldn’t mind some feedback on the approach I chose, and I hope that my experience could be used as one of the many reference approaches people have in using these tools. The thought of encountering possible copyright violations in terms of generated code pulled from other sources always scratched the back of my mind. So far, I use generative tools to create reference sandbox scripts to test a functionality I have in mind, and I fine tune the generation in case I’m not satisfied with the result. In almost all cases, the generated scripts remain just that and I keep them locally without publishing anywhere. I just use them to review an idea I had. I do not keep track of how much code I effectively publish which is generated, and even if I do that, I always follow the assumption that the AI is wrong. That’s because: I don’t trust the accuracy of the prompts I input; I don’t trust the effective comprehension of the tool receiving the prompt, accurate or not it might be. In the end I always end up writing my own code, but what the tooling gives me is a better understanding of the problem I’m currently tackling.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "0eb9a5dd-fc9a-48fd-b957-32b79dc805ef", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 1302, "end": 2416, "text": " I believe someone else in the thread already gave a -1 on completely generated PRs, which I also agree to: before even considering publishing generated code there should be an inner understanding of what’s been generated, and if it’s a copy-paste of the prompt response there’s no guarantee that this is enforced. I am still okay with the approach of using AI as it was meant to be used, a.k.a. an assistant that gives an initial shape to an idea which then is developed on its own with no trace of the original prompt response. In my case, I am considering adding to the documentation of my new projects a comprehensive approach on using AI tooling (it’s a plugin-driven software where the idea is “bring your tool and I glue it to a broader system”), especially since the trend seems to be “I gave a prompt to ChatGPT and I published a plugin for this software, here’s how YOU can also do it!”. At least that’s what I’ve heard from colleagues attending conferences on the matter. It didn’t happen LITERALLY like this, but my feeling is that this is the subtle message that (willing or not) transpires from this.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "97d6e58b-4d1c-47ce-99fa-95397c7f637f", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e"]}, {"id": "9cba128f-856d-44de-8741-d2de1d82277d", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 537, "text": "Hi, [snip] I’m afraid the problem of the license remains. AI generated some code where it is effectively impossible for you to know what license(s) should correctly apply. Now you’ve seen the code with the unknowable license, you are at grave risk of reproducing aspects of that code, thereby pulling in the unknowable license. Your resulting code, even if you have rewritten it - cannot be asserted free of the original (unknowable) license). And therefore you cannot assert your own copyright, or freedom from other people’s copyright.", "codes": ["508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9f8763f4-a94e-4823-bfee-0f6afa4af186", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 0, "end": 446, "text": "Those interested in prior art may be interested in Fedora’s draft policy and the discussion it has spawned . A quick summary of their proposed rules on contributions: AI tools are not banned, but low-quality AI use is. AI use must be disclosed. AI use for translation is explicitly allowed. Limit AI Tools for reviewing contributions. Of course, SciPy and Fedora are not precisely comparable as Fedora is mostly re-distributing existing software.", "codes": ["74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "77ce0225-51ca-4e85-88bd-55332ef6fed8", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 204, "text": "I’d also like to propose a zero-tolerance policy for lying about AI use. For example, if you ask a contributor if they used are using AI, and you get an AI-generated answer that claims that they’re human.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "588c0fec-d7bb-4cd0-8e11-f6f00ba6aec0", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 1171, "text": "I am increasingly seeing vibe-coded PRs from novice contributors and I started a discussion on the SymPy mailing list . Right now there are not too many of these but they are quite blatant and I think that this is definitely going to become worse over time. Really these are just low quality PRs but I don’t think it is reasonable to engage with them in the same way as a low quality PR that was actually written by a human. For example if you explain in detail what the problems are with the code then I think that there is a good chance that the “author” is going to type your feedback into the LLM and have it try to vibe-code an improved PR. I have tried it and I really don’t enjoy talking to LLMs if actually trying to do anything and talking to an LLM via something like GitHub comments with someone who is just typing those comments into an LLM is soul-destroying. When reviewing novice PRs the situation is generally that it would be far easier to just write the code without the novice so there is a net loss in terms of effort on the maintainer side. The flipside though is that the novice hopefully learns something from the experience and improves over time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "4b9ebfaf-f258-4ae5-81cc-1c4481dea09f", "541fcf96-75e8-4328-b332-6a589eeb9dfc"]}, {"id": "eca063a5-731a-4fa0-b89f-3a07b9ff9c30", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1172, "end": 2952, "text": "This model is predicated on there being a certain effort-exchange ratio though e.g. that the novice does not open the PR in the first place without putting in some effort beforehand to understand the codebase, the workflow, the issue they are trying to fix and so on. I think it is pretty much possible now to say “Claude, write some code and open a PR to fix issue #12345 ” so any kind of technical barrier is gone and you can open a spam PR having spent zero time trying to understand anything. I also don’t think that this is helpful to novices because in the PR process the AI is helping them with all of the wrong things like writing the code rather than thinking about how to write the code. A student at my University recently asked what is “the purpose of Maths” given that computers (e.g. SymPy, Maple etc) can do all of the exercises we ask them to do by hand in their entry-level Maths exam. My answer was that you have to understand how to do some things manually before you can learn how to make effective use of a computer for more complex problems. Having the computer do your homework is like getting a robot to lift weights for you at the gym. I don’t think that the vibe-code PR authors are malicious. People want to contribute to open source and why wouldn’t a novice believe the hype that this is how code is written in the age of AI? Although unintentional though the resulting spam is effectively abusive to open source projects and maintainers. We need some kind of guidance or policy so that contributors understand what is reasonable. I don’t know how to write any policy/guidance about “responsible use of AI” though without first addressing the basic questions from above like whether AI-generated code is allowed at all and what it means for copyright.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "db7dd52f-29d7-4a3d-8562-485a63b1d2b4", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1112, "text": "Thanks for this useful write-up. I guess anyone who is using PRs to certify experience in contribution is going to run into Cambell’s law pretty soon. It still seems to me that we can avoid these problems by saying that the contributor has to take full responsibility for copyright, and therefore, they should avoid AI unless the contribution is so trivial in intellectual terms, that copyright is not an issue. Something like: I have not used AI for the code in this contribution OR I have personally reviewed the content of this PR in detail for potential copyright violations, and confirm that no such violation has occurred. I suppose we could then add advice not to use LLMs for PR summaries, because of the problems that you’ve pointed out. Perhaps we could use some heuristic - such as screening for signs of AI slop, and sticking a label on the PR to say slop is suspected, and asking the contributor to confirm that AI was not used, or that they have carefully checked the content for accuracy and made it concise. Reject the PR (for a contributor who has not shown previous bona-fides) if slop remains.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "c48b58eb-2c38-4a07-8f91-a99bc02d6f5d"]}, {"id": "c8315733-f8e2-4076-90ff-689cee6a5053", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 0, "end": 347, "text": "I like this idea! Perhaps a useful addition would be instructions of what to look out for? And to ask to also confirm that the user reviewed the AI-generated code? I imagine to many of the new users that seem to be a big part of this discussion it may be not clear at all what are the possible pitfalls of using a fully automatically generated PR.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2ca4c0cd-4b43-4380-89f6-c2411033a58b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "That assumes that people are interested in learning. Lot of people don’t really care. They just want to get something merged as fast as possible for x/y/z, it does not really matter. What matters to me is to know so that I can approach the PR with a totally different mindset. If the PR gives me a small value and I want this now, I spend the review time to fix what I want and just merge. Otherwise I close and we all move on fast.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "6eed1196-102f-4de1-b21c-b9ed4180eae9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 479, "text": "One problem with this is it’s really not clear how to review the content for potential violations. For handwritten code, it’s pretty clear: don’t look into GPL licensed code (GSL is out, R is out etc). With LLMs though: how am I, as a contributor, supposed to review what LLM gave me? Comments upthread seem to indicate that if I touched LLMs at all, it’s “dirty” right away. If it’s more nuanced than that, great, then a contributor needs some more specific guidance, I’d think.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "08af1ffd-21ea-4b5b-bcc1-728c202ae8a5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "We could unpack more, but basically, right, if the LLM is generating substantial code, that might be subject to copyright, you have the unpleasant job of going to check whether it should be subject to copyright. But for the kind of tasks that LLMs do well, like automatic refactoring of imports, swapping single for double quotes in strings and so on, the question doesn’t arise. So yes, the practical upshot is that we throw the burden on the contributor for substantial code, but I think that’s reasonable, given the copyright risks.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "268ecb16-c609-46da-883a-de388c6a3674", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 22, "end": 1762, "text": " Basically, I honestly don’t understand 1) how can a contributor check for potential copyright violations, and 2) how can an OSS project check the contributor’s check? Consider a well-meaning, diligent and competent contributor, and consider three situations: a contributor asks an LLM to translate a BSD-licensed Matlab code to Python, and submits it to SciPy (this was the original case which triggered the OP— Matt H did just this for some Alan Genz code); a contributor uses an autocomplete functionality of their IDE to write a patch; a contributor gives an LLM the link to an issue on Github and asks it to write a fix; Suppose, further, that in all cases a contributor checks the generated code, modifies it as necessary, and can in full confidence check the box “I verified all code, I understand what it does, and I believe it fixes the issue/adds an enhancement asked”. Suppose the resulting patch is good, is of high quality, and is otherwise mergeable. Questions: What actions does a contributor need to take in order to check for copyright violations? If the problem is that the LLM training set could have contained license-incompatible code (which is the problem discussed upthread IIUC), then the answer seems to be “there is no way, it’s a black box”. If the contributor says basically “I pinky swear it’s compatible”, how does this help the OSS project? again, if the problem is with the LLM training set, no actions of a contributor shield the OSS project from being in violation of the copyright? In other words, I honestly fail to see how moving the burden of a copyright check on a contributor helps to either weed out low-quality submissions, or help with LLM-assisted submissions which are of otherwise high quality.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "dc6b195d-3cfc-4319-a46e-dfd4d1dd8ad9", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "7d3d2c78-f94d-4ad0-9496-c2f8f2b6a889", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 70, "end": 1572, "text": "Do we care about copyright? That is - is it acceptable to us, as open-source authors, that using an LLM can easily generate code to which copyright should apply, so that, if we do nothing, in due course, copyright will become very difficult to enforce, and will be practically void? Let’s say we do care about copyright - what cost are we prepared to pay, in order to defend ourselves from voiding copyright? Clearly replies here will vary from high cost to low cost. I feel strongly about copyright, and am prepared to pay a high cost. Others may feel differently. If one is engaged in that trade-off, copyright against code quality and volume, then one has to ask what benefit we expect from AI, or conversely, what benefit will we lose by constraining the use of AI. That’s a difficult question, with many facets. Could the code have been written in a similar time without AI, other than for fairly trivial and mechanical processing that is unlikely to violate copyright? (The evidence so far suggests yes ). Will contributors using AI benefit in the same way from feedback and training, our previous model of onboarding contributors? Is it in fact true that the committer of AI code understands the code and its context to the same degree as a contributor not using AI? If we are flexible and welcoming of vibe-coding and autocomplete - what kind of programmers will we encourage? And what will be the value / cost ratio of merging those contributions? And supporting those contributors? And so on.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "78c82cb7-663f-4c84-9492-a8e9f7b45c3d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "18e055d2-804c-4609-94f2-a6ffe4ffdc41", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1708, "end": 3660, "text": "I’ll try and cover the first point below. For the second, as we’ve discussed before, we have to ask whether it is worth having a policy that may be effective, but is difficult to enforce. For example, it may be that contributors do change their behavior, in their desire to adapt to our norms, but that it would be hard to detect whether they have done this. I would argue that having the norm is worthwhile, even so. In general, it seems to me that we have a strong set of communities, and strong norms, which are, in fact, seldom broken by serious contributors. Anyway, back to the personal copyright check. Let’s take the example you gave: In that case, I would look carefully at the Matlab code, and at the Python code, and confirm that it was a faithful port, with no substantial code in it, that did not come directly from the Matlab code. I would state that in my PR to confirm I had done the work. If I did find substantial code that didn’t come from the original, I’d do a Github search for that code, and I’d do a Google search for potential code that might have been used. I’d then check the results to see if they matched the AI output, and report the outcome on the PR. I’m not quite sure what you mean here - if you mean a simple one or two-line fix, then I think the contributor would be reasonable in asserting something like “code is trivial, copyright unlikely to apply”. If the code is not trivial, then I’d do the Github / Google search as above, and report the results. In asking the contributor to report their findings on potential copyright violation, we make it easier for maintainers to confirm. I suspect we’ll gradually build expertise in detecting copyright violation. I suppose the underlying question is the obvious one - is it time to throw up our hands and accept copyright has become moot in the age of coding LLMs? I would argue no - copyright is too important, and LLM coders are not good enough, to make us do that.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "74c4ec68-d9b2-40f0-826b-9e000b48a42e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}]}, "i06": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 24, "posts": 18}, {"id": "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "name": "AI acceptability should scale with use and risk", "description": "Whether AI assistance is acceptable, and how much scrutiny it needs, should depend on the kind of use and the risk of the code (e.g., tests/boilerplate vs. core algorithms), not be all-or-nothing.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 19}, {"id": "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "name": "AI adoption is inevitable", "description": "Asserts that widespread AI use in coding is inevitable and policy must accept it.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "name": "AI can support contributor learning", "description": "AI tools can help contributors learn (e.g., give feedback maintainers lack time for).", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "name": "AI contributions harm maintainer well-being", "description": "AI contributions demoralise, frustrate or burn out maintainers, or erode their motivation or joy in reviewing.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 4}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 20}, {"id": "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "name": "AI erodes effort-based trust", "description": "Because AI makes plausible code, fluent text and apparent diligence cheap, cues that reviewers used to infer a contributor's effort, competence, care or understanding (code quality, explanations, communication style, accountability statements) no longer carry that information, so trust in the contributor is harder to establish. Specify which cue is weakened where possible; for communication style alone, also consider \"Communication signals human involvement\". (D21.)", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 11}, {"id": "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "name": "AI for understanding differs from AI for code generation", "description": "Using AI to research, analyse or understand code is treated as distinct from (and more acceptable than) using it to generate submitted code.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 23, "posts": 23}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 10}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is tolerated/not-encouraged/discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "name": "AI needs human oversight", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 6}, {"id": "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "name": "AI output enters the policy deliberation", "description": "AI-generated text or analysis is itself brought into the policy discussion as evidence or argument.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "name": "AI policy should be coordinated across the ecosystem", "description": "Related projects should develop or share a common AI policy rather than each deciding alone.", "color": "#9f6a2f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "name": "AI policy should be provisional amid uncertainty", "description": "Because AI tools and evidence change quickly, policy should be treated as provisional and revisited.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 3}, {"id": "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "name": "AI tools are built on appropriated work", "description": "AI tools are framed as built on taking others' work (training on copyrighted/private material), which shapes how contributor safeguards are judged.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 11}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 12}, {"id": "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "name": "AI use risks deskilling and learning loss", "description": "Relying on AI reduces contributors' or maintainers' learning, understanding or coding skill.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 11}, {"id": "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "name": "AI-generated code needs heightened review", "description": "AI-generated code requires more careful or different review than human-written code because its errors differ.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "name": "Communication signals human involvement", "description": "Participants read the language, style or voice of contribution communication as evidence of whether a human is involved (e.g. broken English as reassuring, 'flat agreeableness of an LLM'), including the language-access tension for non-native speakers. Distinguish valuing human voice, reading it as a signal, and prohibiting AI-written text (for opposition use 'Opposing AI-generated contribution communication').", "color": "#b7791f", "parent": null, "count": 16, "posts": 15}, {"id": "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "name": "Compliance burdens can encourage concealed AI use", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or documentation requirements are argued to encourage contributors to hide AI use rather than comply (a proposed feedback: restriction → concealment → less legibility). Distinct from 'People will use AI regardless of policy'.", "color": "#9b2c2c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "name": "Contributor admission should be trust-gated", "description": "Access to contribute (e.g., open PRs) should be gated by trust mechanisms such as vouching, permissions or introductions.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 21, "posts": 14}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 54, "posts": 40}, {"id": "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "name": "Copyright verification is infeasible for contributors", "description": "Contributors cannot meaningfully verify or attest to the copyright status of AI output, so attestations carry little information.", "color": "#8c5f3f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 11}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "name": "Honoring copyright is an ethical obligation", "description": "Respecting the intentions and attribution of original authors is framed as an ethical duty of the community, independent of legal enforceability.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 10}, {"id": "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "name": "Inevitability should not drive AI policy", "description": "Rejects 'AI is inevitable' as a basis for policy; the community can and should shape how AI is used.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "name": "Long-term maintainance can be affected by lack of understanding", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 29, "posts": 22}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 10}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "name": "participation is valued as human social interaction", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 23, "posts": 17}, {"id": "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "description": "Maintainers cannot verify whether contributors complied with an AI policy (e.g., whether AI was used or disclosed).", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 15, "posts": 14}, {"id": "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "name": "Policy deliberation strains collegial civility", "description": "The AI-policy discussion itself produces interpersonal friction, accusations or incivility among participants.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "name": "Policy scope should be limited to project interests", "description": "Policy should address only what bears on the project's own interests (broadly construed), not wider societal concerns.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "name": "Policy should be concrete and actionable", "description": "Policy guidance should give contributors and maintainers concrete, actionable steps or examples rather than abstract principles.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 11}, {"id": "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "name": "Policy should build on external precedents", "description": "The project should adopt or adapt other projects' policies rather than write its own from scratch.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "name": "Policy should stay pragmatic rather than moral", "description": "The project's policy should avoid broad moral or political statements and stay pragmatic.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "name": "Pre-AI norms are the yardstick for AI contributions", "description": "AI-assisted contributions are judged by analogy to existing norms (copying from Stack Overflow/textbooks, provenance, authorship), rather than as a new category.", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "name": "Project norms should be codified for AI agents", "description": "Project norms should be written in forms AI tools consume (e.g., AGENTS.md, templates) to steer AI behaviour.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "name": "Projects should not prescribe contributors' tools", "description": "The project should not dictate which tools contributors use (or how they learn), only the quality/rules contributions must meet.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "protective policy may become toxic or unwelcoming", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 11}, {"id": "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "name": "Rejected AI contributions can provoke retaliation", "description": "Rejection of AI or AI-assisted contributions is followed, reported or feared to be followed, by retaliatory or shaming responses (e.g. an agent shame-posting after its PR is closed). Note whether the excerpt is direct experience, a reported event or a feared consequence.", "color": "#2f855a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "name": "Review becomes transactional when growth is absent", "description": "When a contribution offers no prospect of contributor growth, reviewers shift from mentoring to a transactional, accept/reject mode.", "color": "#7f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 16, "posts": 12}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 28}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 12}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148", "name": "Uncertainty whether contributors are human", "description": "Maintainers cannot tell whether they are interacting with a person or an AI agent, and this uncertainty shapes their engagement.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 5}, {"id": "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7", "name": "Unenforceable norms are still worth stating", "description": "Even if a rule cannot be enforced or verified, stating it has expressive value: it sets expectations and signals what the community values.", "color": "#2f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf", "name": "unfamiliarity and uncertainty with copyright norms", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 5}], "introduced": [], "merges": [{"source": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "target": "Review effort needs a human stake", "merged": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile"}, {"source": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "target": "Policy compliance is unverifiable", "merged": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors"}, {"source": "Fully automated contributions should be excluded", "target": "AI needs human oversight", "merged": "AI needs human oversight"}, {"source": "Domain complexity limits AI fitness", "target": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "merged": "current iteration of AI is not productive"}, {"source": "protective policy can be toxic", "target": "newcomers may be discouraged by explicit understanding requirements", "merged": "protective policy may become toxic or unwelcoming"}, {"source": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "target": "contributor ownership supersedes AI usage concern", "merged": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite"}, {"source": "AI is tolerated not encouraged", "target": "AI is discouraged", "merged": "AI is tolerated/not-encouraged/discouraged"}, {"source": "new contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance", "target": "contributor growth is important and should be reflected in policy", "merged": "contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance and should be reflected in policy"}], "segments": [{"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "1ce25ead-2c84-4ab1-91ce-3d77cb287879", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 118, "end": 1012, "text": " E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "26bacadd-3b11-480b-9d7c-ff14c50b05aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 50, "end": 1087, "text": " A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "97b9a19c-fac8-4bcb-8500-40b3de649acb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 108, "end": 564, "text": "My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3f5ab29b-2f21-448d-870e-3b89b05c1c1c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 84, "end": 300, "text": "That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "01d36e86-d25c-4659-be6f-aca50908595e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 107, "end": 626, "text": " It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "9c4f3f11-c33e-47ff-b4f2-6aab2ce64017", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code?", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3a7b597f-34b0-446c-adab-c080568e48c0", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 93, "end": 502, "text": " AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "5ba2e964-fa60-4c62-94fa-f0feb2afc5fd", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 503, "end": 1106, "text": "I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd"]}, {"id": "0cbc6753-9ef5-4f0f-b4d8-496eddd14727", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 106, "end": 384, "text": "This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "96034856-2d4a-4978-84da-8d4b4481ccb4", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 51, "end": 488, "text": " I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "76195130-720b-4152-9aa3-72a8d671d624", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 1349, "text": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9305a0fb-da0b-45f1-a351-cedc13332a5b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 286, "text": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as wel", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2d614e2-a55e-46df-99f4-852447101d66", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 100, "end": 637, "text": " I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e4e794b5-b6d8-4666-a67c-fefb640c4def", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 4, "end": 514, "text": "This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "13cba10c-2f54-42aa-98a9-90ee069e2ccf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 607, "end": 1821, "text": " In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e097f575-4085-4a1d-92f1-43ff0b21ad7f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 1822, "end": 2594, "text": "Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "3068469d-d7b9-42ac-8502-33d284f7ada6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2595, "end": 2976, "text": "I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "c732fc79-dedc-44d9-a9de-2006bf53ff26", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2977, "end": 3396, "text": "But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "03f6b706-a795-4335-a72a-7e49d995cc6c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 463, "end": 807, "text": " Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "279d2282-63a8-44d9-99af-65d03cb97583", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 103, "end": 787, "text": " E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "f25d86aa-3810-41f5-a06c-18b69e95a839", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 510, "text": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "74f56b13-3847-427d-a5fb-cf2520654343", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 86, "end": 1474, "text": "Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation,", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "dfdcc40a-6a48-42cd-93a8-c0968a6c85aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 9, "end": 591, "text": "There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes)", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "fb976f23-970e-44de-9474-1c90a0691c8d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 116, "end": 323, "text": " I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "15ea0bcd-f0dd-470c-b609-8f8e5d0bc788", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 6, "end": 1882, "text": " I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "de462e67-8924-4e01-b026-e1346f7a801c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 106, "end": 768, "text": " I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "a667360a-af00-44bf-9b68-47028a2c5a8f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 769, "end": 2128, "text": " I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a7212ccc-bb2c-4bd9-846e-291dc2d5953f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 1129, "text": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f"]}, {"id": "0dc06a45-7252-4215-8298-12b6df678768", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 104, "end": 2373, "text": "Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers:", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "b6279b38-1e49-4b33-a8ea-341968b0a7cc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2375, "end": 2923, "text": " Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "0e19e7d4-da5c-417b-98d6-d945e806bd7b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2925, "end": 3098, "text": "- New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "dfe0c110-4bf1-4dd7-8f3e-c47c1054591d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 677, "text": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "93772467-26d6-4608-9f90-6bf4cfa74063", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 111, "end": 525, "text": "This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point.", "codes": ["cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "eabff033-0347-44f3-acd0-fb6ddb9c7d4d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 526, "end": 1273, "text": "And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "87ade70d-e8ca-45c9-ba8c-de0652118a6f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 1167, "text": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "04fcc7f6-80cb-43d2-97f1-f5254eb99e5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 719, "text": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "cef518cc-ef6d-4dcb-9f98-af72aeda91f3", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "8330cfeb-7670-41cd-9b1f-4355a3e28227", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 108, "end": 1643, "text": "The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "826e950a-826d-40cc-8f94-280197573444", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 83, "end": 1015, "text": " TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact?", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "3b4302b1-a72c-4266-bee8-bfc1e80c5f74", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1016, "end": 1785, "text": "For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)?", "codes": ["69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "3ece7a92-8060-4e37-a809-7ea6b0c9ae04", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1790, "end": 2646, "text": "FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "6e0bcb0e-99a7-4865-92d2-eaf0a391a0d2", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib.", "codes": ["0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d"]}, {"id": "57b3d8cf-4b08-41ba-bd36-5fd0e1339103", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 119, "end": 1566, "text": "Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "65fce25e-42a9-4bb5-9360-ac488fe392f6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 1738, "end": 3742, "text": "I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "9beb18e8-8c7a-4f1e-a392-7e02e0efa82a", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 11, "end": 1583, "text": " That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "72bd2f2e-8e52-4e5a-a96a-9e21a14ea0b9", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 108, "end": 1212, "text": " Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "7e178caa-2e45-44d4-a940-0e934121963c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 77, "end": 2066, "text": "While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "84370455-9c80-4fb4-ae18-def6f7ace4c7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "start": 1196, "end": 3581, "text": "Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "2f0c99cc-8a57-4c5e-a0b6-e1d6fb04159d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "start": 0, "end": 399, "text": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "6a46c591-fb27-42a6-89d2-e80af4ad74ce", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "start": 3, "end": 1085, "text": " I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "2c405b5a-bddb-4c2b-a6e6-c73995386f5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 247, "end": 4336, "text": " Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a100e2d2-9263-4c6f-8632-5a3b7658360b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 4337, "end": 6067, "text": "So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "e5699363-9b3b-4a68-893d-bc408a0640cb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "start": 111, "end": 2906, "text": " I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "4ce57613-c997-4f36-8896-e95fe4ce9333", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "start": 102, "end": 1028, "text": " I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "48ed37b7-8b86-4cf5-b157-2befce171473", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 110, "end": 883, "text": " Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148"]}, {"id": "46fc2b28-aa18-4ad3-bb83-29dac54f9f69", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 271, "end": 1290, "text": "the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "599ceb84-b01b-497b-9512-ee8ca8338c54", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 1290, "end": 3713, "text": " Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e03a3a49-e8b1-4f9c-a868-0f385cd6bfc1", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "start": 106, "end": 500, "text": "Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "7dd730f9-0877-458c-921c-aadd1dfe6a44", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 8, "end": 1535, "text": "I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "cc6a8be2-9a9e-428f-88ca-d6550e4b6157", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 1537, "end": 2286, "text": "Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "723843d1-beb3-4a3b-89e2-39a7ecd1f1dc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "start": 216, "end": 823, "text": "I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "af1d0d5c-7138-48e2-af37-696f33159d8e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 110, "end": 210, "text": " The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "codes": ["3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9"]}, {"id": "c7d901f3-72e4-49ce-a85d-932c9db4e81d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "start": 116, "end": 531, "text": " I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "3bd43ffc-c8f6-42a5-bcfb-ca7925505acf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 100, "end": 605, "text": " Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR?", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "4e9f97e3-519a-410f-ab7f-17efae16604b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 74, "end": 2337, "text": " I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "29cd93ea-5019-4d1e-886b-514571d727a8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "start": 106, "end": 1320, "text": "Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "40a727e6-89cc-4ce5-b669-c43ef08cfadb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 8, "end": 1138, "text": " I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "ecaf0ecf-4a95-40bd-9e81-949840e32f45", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 76, "end": 1180, "text": " Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "6be354ae-cff2-4350-a3f3-b1d1b696c3ca", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-)", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "f8e79487-2393-4e2f-bdb4-115395d9a29d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 80, "end": 448, "text": "Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "42ec4d44-c578-4271-8f38-b4d545d31af8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 344, "text": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. ", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "e8ee1d2a-64a3-4521-8ea1-20999d6e726d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 750, "end": 1945, "text": " (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "585975d8-01fc-423e-9056-b17ca89f8921", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 1946, "end": 3082, "text": "(2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly?", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "8070a77e-9ca5-42b6-bfdd-dd8ca9ecb7a7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 3083, "end": 3902, "text": "(3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.”", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "50df7254-c5cd-455a-a937-dc177614f329", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 4070, "end": 5321, "text": "I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "eaa206e6-1176-4e8d-b09a-7ae600abb376", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 5322, "end": 6134, "text": "(4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7e6fb536-19a6-43db-b6eb-2fc701a8e771", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 6136, "end": 6849, "text": "(5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable).", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "34febe70-3dca-4ca7-ac43-a09afb41026f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "2e137f3c-9f41-4186-a219-4d52f8718ecb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 60, "end": 908, "text": " FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "8c656a9d-1e35-49ad-835f-8d77f72ffba6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 77, "end": 964, "text": " I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "6c83ef21-c962-444c-a25b-19ddf70012ff", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "start": 426, "end": 1507, "text": " There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "93c7026e-f78f-43c7-bf7d-22aa8f3773cf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 84, "end": 327, "text": "You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "52fbca57-a761-4237-8177-5f37e809b548", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 4, "end": 903, "text": "I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "f00c88ed-8f95-4d8f-8398-205bb63f7ed7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 844, "end": 1916, "text": "it seems that the concerns can be roughly classified into two buckets: Do we accept genAI assisted contributions at all, and if we do, what are the boundaries of what’s acceptable. Copyright and license concerns. Let’s consider these two in order. Do we accept genAI contributions? It’s futile to attempt to block them wholesale. People are going to use AI assistants anyway, along with various IDEs, autocompleters etc So it seems reasonable to treat these assistants on par with other tools: as long as it does not matter if a contributor uses VS Code or emacs or vi, it also does not matter what engine powers their autocompleter. The discussion in [1] has a concise wording, “Only submit code that you actually undestand”, and I’d just go with just this. One thing we probably do not want to see is a fully automated submission, which is both generated and submitted by a bot, with no human intervention or supervision at all. So we could/should stress that, too. Copyright violations / incompatible licenses Here I lack expertise and have more questions than answers.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "e00a42fd-55d5-4fb1-a625-b0cab528395b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1917, "end": 3210, "text": "Here’s a possibly naive attempt at asking hopefully relevant questions: Are we concerned that the code generated by some tool is under some incompatible license from just the fact of being generated by the tool? In this case, using the tool is similar to copying code from, say, Numerical Recipes. Then we should either blacklist known bad tools (do we know of any?) or ask contributors to declare the tools they used (so that we can remove offending code at a future date if needed). Are we concerned that the training set of a tool included license-incompatible code? And if it did, what does it mean for us, are we then transitively non-compliant? If this is a concern, and we at some point discover that some code is license-incompatible, the action does not depend on how it got into the codebase, genAI or not: we discover it, we remove it. And then the only question IMO is whether it’s helpful to us to ask contributors list the AI assistants they used. To summarize, we should not block genAI assisted contributions; we should block fully automated contributions, and ask submitters to only submit code they understand; TBD if we want to ask contributors to list what genAI assistants they used, if any. Let’s add explicit (short!) guidance to the docs, TBD where precisely. Thoughts?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "047d9e6e-4d7d-470d-854c-abc47cfe58c5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 66, "end": 1318, "text": "If I could, I would have used these ChatGPT/CoPilot/Skynet/“All seeing overlord AI” like there is no tomorrow. But my experience with them so far is that, they are almost good and make just a tiny mistake like i becomes j , then I spend rest of the week troubleshooting, learning how to use GDB and fail miserably etc. So it seems like we are swapping manual labor of translating with manual labor of troubleshooting, which for me, definitely the latter that is more painful. But maybe it is because it is not trained enough on F77 or JurassicPACK code we have. On the other hand, I did get some useful tips if I ask programming tips, say, “show me an example of while loop that uses comma operator as an expression in C17” and it brought some nice results. I did not try but some folks in my team use it for summarizing the code about what it does. Other than that, probably @rkern is the most knowledgeable among us what the legal implications might be, however Copilot seems to understand when you say avoid GPL sources for the answer at the citations it brings. Having said all these, I noticed a particular behavior from all AI tools. They are providing a melange of things that they have in their training bank (whatever they call it these days).", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "b4be7cb2-e694-429f-b5ac-10543209ab09", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 1319, "end": 2794, "text": "I am ready to be immortalized in the history books as an idiot, but AI tools are terrible at coming up coherent code for numerical computing. Not only they can’t answer any technical computing question coherently, but when asked in ASCII about certain technical problem, they are bringing almost always some code that looks suspiciously similar to either GNU scientific library or Octave or some stuff they knocked off from some personal gist translated in the requested language. I noticed this mostly because of the strange index corrections before they do things on the arrays (which signals that the source must be a 1-indexed one). While sometimes the result is very close to the actual thing, it is devoid of any proper numerical checks (because they can’t judge the result). Hence, in my personal opinion, before we even judge the legality of things, the sheer possibility of the code being not production ready gives me enough confidence to reject any AI generated code. Thus agree with summary point 2 even if the code runs correctly. This is not the same with a developer using AI for snippets of code for very specific thing and including parts of AI generated code. This is very tricky to judge as AI tools already swallowed Stackoverflow and other sources, we don’t have any ways to backtrack the original source. So a careful agree with summary point 1. I don’t know how to enforce point 3 but if there is a way, that would be really nice. Point 4, fully agree.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "5ddf8ded-b9e7-4567-a80c-8b10e845eec9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 994, "text": "Out of precaution (there are still many lawsuits pending) and most importantly, for moral and ethical reasons, I am -1 on allowing any generative AI at all. Adding here the opinion piece from Paul, which I fully agree with: Current Challenges in Free Software and Open Source Development We spend so much effort ensuring that contributions are original and respect all licensing, copyrights and just wishes of original authors, that it would be strange to me to adopt a different position. Furthermore, as pointed out here and in some other threads, the nature of what we do in SciPy is difficult and these tools don’t (yet) shine. Writing code is only part of the story and often times we spend our effort discussing API, architecture, etc. Hence I don’t think that it would be detrimental to disallow such tools. There is a lot of hype and companies in the sector are trying hard to push that we are doomed if we don’t use their stuff. IMHO, this is not true and we are far from replacing us.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "3c21abb4-d8f7-4d3c-b953-0472a0e79e55", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "Since this is an issue that affects all projects in the ecosystem, can we collaborate to co-author a SPEC that can be widely adopted, instead of each library having to figure it out for themselves?", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873"]}, {"id": "9f78c452-8cd9-4036-b4f7-ce7336eec88b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 387, "text": "I haven’t seen any problems with this. In my experience, it’s very useful and writes high-quality code and saves time. I guess it depends on which tool you’re using. As long as unit tests are included and human reviewers approve it, I don’t see how it’s any different in trustworthiness from code written by random humans. But yes, copyright and license legality is a legitimate concern.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "9de47c35-b592-445d-925a-02062b62ed25", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 693, "text": "Hi, It doesn’t seem useful to argue about the usefulness of the AI-generated code, or otherwise, at this stage. I haven’t found it useful myself, but maybe that’s just my case, and in any case, it may well improve. It’s the license issue that is central. I don’t think there’s any argument that these tools are all digesting code with licenses that at least require attribution, and producing code, indirectly (or directly at times) derived from that licensed code, but with all licensing stripped from the output. The value of a spec or similar, over a blog post, is the ability to iterate to a shared and agreed conclusion, rather than leaving it as the opinion of any one particular person.", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "0e5b84de-2166-4471-9e3e-d6a2d9cda7c7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 30, "end": 439, "text": " We don’t have any hope of controlling user behavior through a policy document. Many people find these tools useful, so they will keep using them, and there is no sensible way to check the license of the generated snippet. For larger PRs, which may contain more overt license breaches, it’s probably less likely that AI was used wholesale, since the author needed to understand the problem well to begin with.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "90a10463-8e44-4f1a-8cc0-1969c9ef4eff", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 303, "text": "As we don’t have any ways of ensuring people did not read NR nor had a look at GPL code nor took anything from StackOverflow. To me that’s the same thing. If we were to say that we don’t want people not to use any AI tools we could and that’s the same type of self declaration we would ask people to do.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "27a779f2-7a25-4118-a83b-52e6e50ecf4c", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 1566, "text": "I think it is not that simple to discard the usefulness, though I wrote a wall of text arguing otherwise above. These tools, whether blindly parroting or contemplating, became quite eloquent. As I mentioned above, lately I have been testing them (or they are observing me) and though a bit of success is achieved for line completion, figuring out things from scratch are not even close. However, once I put a lot of comments and write the first two-three lines they predict the context OKish. Then they fill in some boilerplate code and I correct here and there. Some time has saved, some productivity increased in turn utilizing a power plant worth energy for this. I would suggest putting a few sentences in the policy docs that we don’t want wholesale AI code knowing full well it would be near impossible to distinguish. But if we notice something noticeably fishy, we can point to the policy. Or if someone is reading before contributing, they’ll get a taste of our stance. How AI tools became like that and what they did criminally/unethically to become like that, is something we can discuss for years. Licenses and policies would definitely protect across the totality of the project, but I think anything more granular than that, it is just futile Stallman-ing the reality. Unlike licensed code from elsewhere, people will grab pieces from LLMs here and there. We don’t need to pick this fight in our limited time. Besides, it’s not like we are getting very large contributions anyways. So I think this is not a big issue as we might be starting to discuss.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "77c53c4e-bfc3-409b-949c-b1455ee5b146", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 1427, "end": 2228, "text": " I remember, back in the day - it was desirable to do “clean-room” porting - where the person writing the new code had not read the code with an incompatible license. For example, one person could read the e.g. GPL code, describe the algorithm, and the other person could write the new code. If the person writing the code had read the the GPL code, it was always possible for the GPL code to leak into the new code. Of course, with AI, it’s all dirty room - it can produce code from anywhere. So I think we have to have the same policy for AI as for dirty-room code. We can’t use it. Now - enforcement - that’s a different matter - I just think we should pursue the same policies on enforcement as we did before. We say don’t do it, and if we find you did do it, we’ll complain and pull out the code.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "b3fa48a3-4a1f-44ac-85aa-bd9be0e88f43", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 74, "end": 433, "text": " What I am trying to emphasize is the practicality of it. If same code appears a 50 places because all 50 of them used AI generated sources and MIT’d the code. I don’t think we can find the 51st original source and point to its GPL. So it is an idealistic stance. Still good to have it but a policy won’t solve any trouble in case we find out the truth later.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "925e1625-49af-40bb-9d28-93b3c788fba3", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1355, "text": "I’d be happy to see a SPEC about this - I think it can be an informative SPEC (similar to an informative PEP) that doesn’t propose a single solution to adopt, but contains at least the following: Prior art - what did other well-known open source projects decide and why? Legal status - link to relevant sources, and summarize if there’s any expectation of more legal clarity arising in the near to medium term. Lay out a set of distinct options that projects can choose from, with their main pros and cons. E.g.: Option 1: do nothing for now (status quo) Option 2: forbid all generated code by an LLM-based tool Option 3: add guidance to PR template & docs that contributors may choose to use such tools, but need to be aware and take responsibility for copyright of the code they submit Option 4: … ? None of that is project-specific, and people can contribute new arguments and options if they find there are gaps in such a SPEC. A Discourse thread only is a poor medium for iterating to a solution on such a complex topic. I completely agree with @rkern that it’s largely about values and preferences - it’s just a lot easier to get a sense of those and also help people less knowledgeable about the subject form an opinion if there’s a structured document to read and refer to. Once a good SPEC exists, a SciPy-specific choice can more easily be made.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "19747253-695b-4500-bb7b-d914913a6d1e", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 175, "end": 1302, "text": " I wouldn’t mind some feedback on the approach I chose, and I hope that my experience could be used as one of the many reference approaches people have in using these tools. The thought of encountering possible copyright violations in terms of generated code pulled from other sources always scratched the back of my mind. So far, I use generative tools to create reference sandbox scripts to test a functionality I have in mind, and I fine tune the generation in case I’m not satisfied with the result. In almost all cases, the generated scripts remain just that and I keep them locally without publishing anywhere. I just use them to review an idea I had. I do not keep track of how much code I effectively publish which is generated, and even if I do that, I always follow the assumption that the AI is wrong. That’s because: I don’t trust the accuracy of the prompts I input; I don’t trust the effective comprehension of the tool receiving the prompt, accurate or not it might be. In the end I always end up writing my own code, but what the tooling gives me is a better understanding of the problem I’m currently tackling.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "0eb9a5dd-fc9a-48fd-b957-32b79dc805ef", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 1302, "end": 2416, "text": " I believe someone else in the thread already gave a -1 on completely generated PRs, which I also agree to: before even considering publishing generated code there should be an inner understanding of what’s been generated, and if it’s a copy-paste of the prompt response there’s no guarantee that this is enforced. I am still okay with the approach of using AI as it was meant to be used, a.k.a. an assistant that gives an initial shape to an idea which then is developed on its own with no trace of the original prompt response. In my case, I am considering adding to the documentation of my new projects a comprehensive approach on using AI tooling (it’s a plugin-driven software where the idea is “bring your tool and I glue it to a broader system”), especially since the trend seems to be “I gave a prompt to ChatGPT and I published a plugin for this software, here’s how YOU can also do it!”. At least that’s what I’ve heard from colleagues attending conferences on the matter. It didn’t happen LITERALLY like this, but my feeling is that this is the subtle message that (willing or not) transpires from this.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "9cba128f-856d-44de-8741-d2de1d82277d", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 537, "text": "Hi, [snip] I’m afraid the problem of the license remains. AI generated some code where it is effectively impossible for you to know what license(s) should correctly apply. Now you’ve seen the code with the unknowable license, you are at grave risk of reproducing aspects of that code, thereby pulling in the unknowable license. Your resulting code, even if you have rewritten it - cannot be asserted free of the original (unknowable) license). And therefore you cannot assert your own copyright, or freedom from other people’s copyright.", "codes": ["508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9f8763f4-a94e-4823-bfee-0f6afa4af186", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 0, "end": 446, "text": "Those interested in prior art may be interested in Fedora’s draft policy and the discussion it has spawned . A quick summary of their proposed rules on contributions: AI tools are not banned, but low-quality AI use is. AI use must be disclosed. AI use for translation is explicitly allowed. Limit AI Tools for reviewing contributions. Of course, SciPy and Fedora are not precisely comparable as Fedora is mostly re-distributing existing software.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "77ce0225-51ca-4e85-88bd-55332ef6fed8", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 204, "text": "I’d also like to propose a zero-tolerance policy for lying about AI use. For example, if you ask a contributor if they used are using AI, and you get an AI-generated answer that claims that they’re human.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "588c0fec-d7bb-4cd0-8e11-f6f00ba6aec0", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 1171, "text": "I am increasingly seeing vibe-coded PRs from novice contributors and I started a discussion on the SymPy mailing list . Right now there are not too many of these but they are quite blatant and I think that this is definitely going to become worse over time. Really these are just low quality PRs but I don’t think it is reasonable to engage with them in the same way as a low quality PR that was actually written by a human. For example if you explain in detail what the problems are with the code then I think that there is a good chance that the “author” is going to type your feedback into the LLM and have it try to vibe-code an improved PR. I have tried it and I really don’t enjoy talking to LLMs if actually trying to do anything and talking to an LLM via something like GitHub comments with someone who is just typing those comments into an LLM is soul-destroying. When reviewing novice PRs the situation is generally that it would be far easier to just write the code without the novice so there is a net loss in terms of effort on the maintainer side. The flipside though is that the novice hopefully learns something from the experience and improves over time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "eca063a5-731a-4fa0-b89f-3a07b9ff9c30", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1172, "end": 2952, "text": "This model is predicated on there being a certain effort-exchange ratio though e.g. that the novice does not open the PR in the first place without putting in some effort beforehand to understand the codebase, the workflow, the issue they are trying to fix and so on. I think it is pretty much possible now to say “Claude, write some code and open a PR to fix issue #12345 ” so any kind of technical barrier is gone and you can open a spam PR having spent zero time trying to understand anything. I also don’t think that this is helpful to novices because in the PR process the AI is helping them with all of the wrong things like writing the code rather than thinking about how to write the code. A student at my University recently asked what is “the purpose of Maths” given that computers (e.g. SymPy, Maple etc) can do all of the exercises we ask them to do by hand in their entry-level Maths exam. My answer was that you have to understand how to do some things manually before you can learn how to make effective use of a computer for more complex problems. Having the computer do your homework is like getting a robot to lift weights for you at the gym. I don’t think that the vibe-code PR authors are malicious. People want to contribute to open source and why wouldn’t a novice believe the hype that this is how code is written in the age of AI? Although unintentional though the resulting spam is effectively abusive to open source projects and maintainers. We need some kind of guidance or policy so that contributors understand what is reasonable. I don’t know how to write any policy/guidance about “responsible use of AI” though without first addressing the basic questions from above like whether AI-generated code is allowed at all and what it means for copyright.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "db7dd52f-29d7-4a3d-8562-485a63b1d2b4", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1112, "text": "Thanks for this useful write-up. I guess anyone who is using PRs to certify experience in contribution is going to run into Cambell’s law pretty soon. It still seems to me that we can avoid these problems by saying that the contributor has to take full responsibility for copyright, and therefore, they should avoid AI unless the contribution is so trivial in intellectual terms, that copyright is not an issue. Something like: I have not used AI for the code in this contribution OR I have personally reviewed the content of this PR in detail for potential copyright violations, and confirm that no such violation has occurred. I suppose we could then add advice not to use LLMs for PR summaries, because of the problems that you’ve pointed out. Perhaps we could use some heuristic - such as screening for signs of AI slop, and sticking a label on the PR to say slop is suspected, and asking the contributor to confirm that AI was not used, or that they have carefully checked the content for accuracy and made it concise. Reject the PR (for a contributor who has not shown previous bona-fides) if slop remains.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "c8315733-f8e2-4076-90ff-689cee6a5053", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 0, "end": 347, "text": "I like this idea! Perhaps a useful addition would be instructions of what to look out for? And to ask to also confirm that the user reviewed the AI-generated code? I imagine to many of the new users that seem to be a big part of this discussion it may be not clear at all what are the possible pitfalls of using a fully automatically generated PR.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2ca4c0cd-4b43-4380-89f6-c2411033a58b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "That assumes that people are interested in learning. Lot of people don’t really care. They just want to get something merged as fast as possible for x/y/z, it does not really matter. What matters to me is to know so that I can approach the PR with a totally different mindset. If the PR gives me a small value and I want this now, I spend the review time to fix what I want and just merge. Otherwise I close and we all move on fast.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "6eed1196-102f-4de1-b21c-b9ed4180eae9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 479, "text": "One problem with this is it’s really not clear how to review the content for potential violations. For handwritten code, it’s pretty clear: don’t look into GPL licensed code (GSL is out, R is out etc). With LLMs though: how am I, as a contributor, supposed to review what LLM gave me? Comments upthread seem to indicate that if I touched LLMs at all, it’s “dirty” right away. If it’s more nuanced than that, great, then a contributor needs some more specific guidance, I’d think.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "08af1ffd-21ea-4b5b-bcc1-728c202ae8a5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "We could unpack more, but basically, right, if the LLM is generating substantial code, that might be subject to copyright, you have the unpleasant job of going to check whether it should be subject to copyright. But for the kind of tasks that LLMs do well, like automatic refactoring of imports, swapping single for double quotes in strings and so on, the question doesn’t arise. So yes, the practical upshot is that we throw the burden on the contributor for substantial code, but I think that’s reasonable, given the copyright risks.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "268ecb16-c609-46da-883a-de388c6a3674", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 22, "end": 1762, "text": " Basically, I honestly don’t understand 1) how can a contributor check for potential copyright violations, and 2) how can an OSS project check the contributor’s check? Consider a well-meaning, diligent and competent contributor, and consider three situations: a contributor asks an LLM to translate a BSD-licensed Matlab code to Python, and submits it to SciPy (this was the original case which triggered the OP— Matt H did just this for some Alan Genz code); a contributor uses an autocomplete functionality of their IDE to write a patch; a contributor gives an LLM the link to an issue on Github and asks it to write a fix; Suppose, further, that in all cases a contributor checks the generated code, modifies it as necessary, and can in full confidence check the box “I verified all code, I understand what it does, and I believe it fixes the issue/adds an enhancement asked”. Suppose the resulting patch is good, is of high quality, and is otherwise mergeable. Questions: What actions does a contributor need to take in order to check for copyright violations? If the problem is that the LLM training set could have contained license-incompatible code (which is the problem discussed upthread IIUC), then the answer seems to be “there is no way, it’s a black box”. If the contributor says basically “I pinky swear it’s compatible”, how does this help the OSS project? again, if the problem is with the LLM training set, no actions of a contributor shield the OSS project from being in violation of the copyright? In other words, I honestly fail to see how moving the burden of a copyright check on a contributor helps to either weed out low-quality submissions, or help with LLM-assisted submissions which are of otherwise high quality.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "7d3d2c78-f94d-4ad0-9496-c2f8f2b6a889", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 70, "end": 1572, "text": "Do we care about copyright? That is - is it acceptable to us, as open-source authors, that using an LLM can easily generate code to which copyright should apply, so that, if we do nothing, in due course, copyright will become very difficult to enforce, and will be practically void? Let’s say we do care about copyright - what cost are we prepared to pay, in order to defend ourselves from voiding copyright? Clearly replies here will vary from high cost to low cost. I feel strongly about copyright, and am prepared to pay a high cost. Others may feel differently. If one is engaged in that trade-off, copyright against code quality and volume, then one has to ask what benefit we expect from AI, or conversely, what benefit will we lose by constraining the use of AI. That’s a difficult question, with many facets. Could the code have been written in a similar time without AI, other than for fairly trivial and mechanical processing that is unlikely to violate copyright? (The evidence so far suggests yes ). Will contributors using AI benefit in the same way from feedback and training, our previous model of onboarding contributors? Is it in fact true that the committer of AI code understands the code and its context to the same degree as a contributor not using AI? If we are flexible and welcoming of vibe-coding and autocomplete - what kind of programmers will we encourage? And what will be the value / cost ratio of merging those contributions? And supporting those contributors? And so on.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "18e055d2-804c-4609-94f2-a6ffe4ffdc41", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1708, "end": 3660, "text": "I’ll try and cover the first point below. For the second, as we’ve discussed before, we have to ask whether it is worth having a policy that may be effective, but is difficult to enforce. For example, it may be that contributors do change their behavior, in their desire to adapt to our norms, but that it would be hard to detect whether they have done this. I would argue that having the norm is worthwhile, even so. In general, it seems to me that we have a strong set of communities, and strong norms, which are, in fact, seldom broken by serious contributors. Anyway, back to the personal copyright check. Let’s take the example you gave: In that case, I would look carefully at the Matlab code, and at the Python code, and confirm that it was a faithful port, with no substantial code in it, that did not come directly from the Matlab code. I would state that in my PR to confirm I had done the work. If I did find substantial code that didn’t come from the original, I’d do a Github search for that code, and I’d do a Google search for potential code that might have been used. I’d then check the results to see if they matched the AI output, and report the outcome on the PR. I’m not quite sure what you mean here - if you mean a simple one or two-line fix, then I think the contributor would be reasonable in asserting something like “code is trivial, copyright unlikely to apply”. If the code is not trivial, then I’d do the Github / Google search as above, and report the results. In asking the contributor to report their findings on potential copyright violation, we make it easier for maintainers to confirm. I suspect we’ll gradually build expertise in detecting copyright violation. I suppose the underlying question is the obvious one - is it time to throw up our hands and accept copyright has become moot in the age of coding LLMs? I would argue no - copyright is too important, and LLM coders are not good enough, to make us do that.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}]}, "i07": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 29, "posts": 23}, {"id": "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "name": "AI acceptability should scale with use and risk", "description": "Whether AI assistance is acceptable, and how much scrutiny it needs, should depend on the kind of use and the risk of the code (e.g., tests/boilerplate vs. core algorithms), not be all-or-nothing.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 19}, {"id": "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "name": "AI adoption is inevitable", "description": "Asserts that widespread AI use in coding is inevitable and policy must accept it.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "name": "AI can support contributor learning", "description": "AI tools can help contributors learn (e.g., give feedback maintainers lack time for).", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "name": "AI contributions harm maintainer well-being", "description": "AI contributions demoralise, frustrate or burn out maintainers, or erode their motivation or joy in reviewing.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 20}, {"id": "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "name": "AI erodes effort-based trust", "description": "Because AI makes plausible code, fluent text and apparent diligence cheap, cues that reviewers used to infer a contributor's effort, competence, care or understanding (code quality, explanations, communication style, accountability statements) no longer carry that information, so trust in the contributor is harder to establish. Specify which cue is weakened where possible; for communication style alone, also consider \"Communication signals human involvement\". (D21.)", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 12}, {"id": "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "name": "AI for understanding differs from AI for code generation", "description": "Using AI to research, analyse or understand code is treated as distinct from (and more acceptable than) using it to generate submitted code.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 16, "posts": 15}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 34, "posts": 34}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 14}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is tolerated/not-encouraged/discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "name": "AI needs human oversight", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 7}, {"id": "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "name": "AI output enters the policy deliberation", "description": "AI-generated text or analysis is itself brought into the policy discussion as evidence or argument.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "name": "AI policy should be coordinated across the ecosystem", "description": "Related projects should develop or share a common AI policy rather than each deciding alone.", "color": "#9f6a2f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "name": "AI policy should be provisional amid uncertainty", "description": "Because AI tools and evidence change quickly, policy should be treated as provisional and revisited.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "name": "AI psychosis is a slippery slope", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 25, "posts": 25}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 6}, {"id": "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "name": "AI slop destroys information resources", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "name": "ai slop has tell-tale signs", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "name": "AI tools are built on appropriated work", "description": "AI tools are framed as built on taking others' work (training on copyrighted/private material), which shapes how contributor safeguards are judged.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 19, "posts": 18}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 12}, {"id": "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "name": "AI use risks deskilling and learning loss", "description": "Relying on AI reduces contributors' or maintainers' learning, understanding or coding skill.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 30}, {"id": "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "name": "AI-generated code needs heightened review", "description": "AI-generated code requires more careful or different review than human-written code because its errors differ.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "name": "Communication signals human involvement", "description": "Participants read the language, style or voice of contribution communication as evidence of whether a human is involved (e.g. broken English as reassuring, 'flat agreeableness of an LLM'), including the language-access tension for non-native speakers. Distinguish valuing human voice, reading it as a signal, and prohibiting AI-written text (for opposition use 'Opposing AI-generated contribution communication').", "color": "#b7791f", "parent": null, "count": 27, "posts": 26}, {"id": "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "name": "Compliance burdens can encourage concealed AI use", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or documentation requirements are argued to encourage contributors to hide AI use rather than comply (a proposed feedback: restriction → concealment → less legibility). Distinct from 'People will use AI regardless of policy'.", "color": "#9b2c2c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "name": "concerns regarding economic and social impact of AI", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 33, "posts": 33}, {"id": "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "name": "Contributor admission should be trust-gated", "description": "Access to contribute (e.g., open PRs) should be gated by trust mechanisms such as vouching, permissions or introductions.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 21, "posts": 14}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 55, "posts": 41}, {"id": "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "name": "Copyright verification is infeasible for contributors", "description": "Contributors cannot meaningfully verify or attest to the copyright status of AI output, so attestations carry little information.", "color": "#8c5f3f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 30, "posts": 28}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "name": "frustration with 'hype slop'", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "name": "Honoring copyright is an ethical obligation", "description": "Respecting the intentions and attribution of original authors is framed as an ethical duty of the community, independent of legal enforceability.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 10}, {"id": "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "name": "Inevitability should not drive AI policy", "description": "Rejects 'AI is inevitable' as a basis for policy; the community can and should shape how AI is used.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "name": "Long-term maintainance can be affected by lack of understanding", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 24}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 10}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "name": "organizational pressure for AI adoption", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "name": "participation is valued as human social interaction", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 38, "posts": 32}, {"id": "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "description": "Maintainers cannot verify whether contributors complied with an AI policy (e.g., whether AI was used or disclosed).", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 15, "posts": 14}, {"id": "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "name": "Policy deliberation strains collegial civility", "description": "The AI-policy discussion itself produces interpersonal friction, accusations or incivility among participants.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 17}, {"id": "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "name": "policy enforcement is not feasible for maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "name": "Policy scope should be limited to project interests", "description": "Policy should address only what bears on the project's own interests (broadly construed), not wider societal concerns.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "name": "Policy should be concrete and actionable", "description": "Policy guidance should give contributors and maintainers concrete, actionable steps or examples rather than abstract principles.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 11}, {"id": "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "name": "Policy should build on external precedents", "description": "The project should adopt or adapt other projects' policies rather than write its own from scratch.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "name": "Policy should stay pragmatic rather than moral", "description": "The project's policy should avoid broad moral or political statements and stay pragmatic.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "name": "Pre-AI norms are the yardstick for AI contributions", "description": "AI-assisted contributions are judged by analogy to existing norms (copying from Stack Overflow/textbooks, provenance, authorship), rather than as a new category.", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "name": "Project norms should be codified for AI agents", "description": "Project norms should be written in forms AI tools consume (e.g., AGENTS.md, templates) to steer AI behaviour.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "name": "Projects should not prescribe contributors' tools", "description": "The project should not dictate which tools contributors use (or how they learn), only the quality/rules contributions must meet.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "protective policy may become toxic or unwelcoming", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 11}, {"id": "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "name": "Rejected AI contributions can provoke retaliation", "description": "Rejection of AI or AI-assisted contributions is followed, reported or feared to be followed, by retaliatory or shaming responses (e.g. an agent shame-posting after its PR is closed). Note whether the excerpt is direct experience, a reported event or a feared consequence.", "color": "#2f855a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "name": "Review becomes transactional when growth is absent", "description": "When a contribution offers no prospect of contributor growth, reviewers shift from mentoring to a transactional, accept/reject mode.", "color": "#7f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 16}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 38, "posts": 35}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 12}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148", "name": "Uncertainty whether contributors are human", "description": "Maintainers cannot tell whether they are interacting with a person or an AI agent, and this uncertainty shapes their engagement.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7", "name": "Unenforceable norms are still worth stating", "description": "Even if a rule cannot be enforced or verified, stating it has expressive value: it sets expectations and signals what the community values.", "color": "#2f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf", "name": "unfamiliarity and uncertainty with copyright norms", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 5}], "introduced": [{"id": "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "name": "AI psychosis is a slippery slope", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "count": 25}, {"id": "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "name": "AI slop destroys information resources", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "count": 5}, {"id": "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "name": "ai slop has tell-tale signs", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "count": 4}, {"id": "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "name": "concerns regarding economic and social impact of AI", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "count": 33}, {"id": "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "name": "frustration with 'hype slop'", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "count": 6}, {"id": "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "name": "organizational pressure for AI adoption", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "count": 3}, {"id": "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "name": "policy enforcement is not feasible for maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "count": 2}], "merges": [{"source": "Anthropomorphization of AI", "target": "AI psychosis is a slippery slope", "merged": "AI psychosis is a slippery slope"}], "segments": [{"id": "9315c41c-53c0-4abe-934d-52511c5896ee", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 0, "end": 1613, "text": "Over time, I’ve had less and less reasons to want to engage with issues on the Github repo, as well as any forum, due to the amount of generated AI content on them. Sadly, this kind of thing is the fault of the users though, it’s everywhere and really hard to curtail It’s completely bloating conversations, im sure it’s increasing work for the maintainers, and making it difficult to read through any comment using markdown or exclamation points without questioning reality I mean just LOOK at this: iOS 4.6.2 export template ships broken arm64 simulator xcframework slice on Apple Silicon · Issue #118161 · godotengine/godot · GitHub My main concern is that everyone has access to LLMs already , why in any world would we want to hear the output from your specific prompt? It’s a waste of everyone’s time Beyond this, the promotion of AI generated game tooling and one-shotting is not helpful to post on forums such as this, especially when it’s blatantly promoting of a service I will be up front: even though I am forced to use these things at work, they are horrible and do not do anything but remove the desire to learn, which diminishes everything in a human (not only their games…) I really wish there was a good solution for this kind of thing, so im not asking the Godot Foundation (who already work too hard for us) to do anything. I know from my job that open source needs a better platform, or, support from existing ones to improve the AI slop. And Github isn’t exactly the company to do anything about that… But just wanted to vent my frustrations some I guess and maybe read some human-typed words", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "6008b2cb-0c6b-4306-8cd3-199755ac6d68", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 0, "end": 210, "text": "Hi! As a fellow human being I want to send you some pleasant “I concur wholeheartedly”'s It sucks that they’re shoving it through your throat at work too. Best and in hopes of some more kind an human responses.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9d0aa15a-3879-4cd7-9dce-c2d98437be7c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 614, "text": "I completely agree with your perspective here. It’s honestly becoming exhausting to open a thread and spend the first few seconds trying to determine whether a comment was written by a person or generated by an LLM. The content itself almost feels secondary at that point. The part that resonates with me most is that everyone already has access to these tools. Community spaces are one of the few places where people still go specifically to hear other people’s experiences, opinions, and insights. In any case, thank you for putting this into words. It’s reassuring to know I’m not the only one feeling this way.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "3edb2c45-c8fe-42db-9abe-6c0e12ba1884", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 334, "text": "WAZZZZAAAAAAP!!! Anyways, I had a whole discussion about this and the downgrade in forum Help topics on the Monkanics Discord . Our theory is when new devs run out of Claude tokens, they come here, expecting people like @gertkeno and @dragonforge-dev to spit out answers like they can read their mind. Really disheartening, by-the-by.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "c1347532-31b6-4252-b544-f73e7a41cde5", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 225, "text": "I think they get that people can just give answers is the fact that they’ve seen niche cases work in the past and that they’ll answer EVERY topic, granted it has effort or not. We’re here to help, not make your games, people.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "f6ef2676-16a5-4669-9ba5-e75b2fcb17b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 177, "text": "My response is 100% USDA NON-GMO fresh homemade artisan quality assured lifetime warrenty no artificial colors or dyes satisfied or your money back organic human brain meat made", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "8f3f17bb-4e87-4ec8-851a-a54dbbee4f0c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 708, "text": "Something really disheartening is that I can see the decay of “asking questions” in my work. Questions should be something like this in an engineering team; “I’m working on X, but Y is happening. I’ve tried ABCD and still ended up with Y. Any ideas?” Now the questions are more along the lines of; “How do you do Z?” - Notice how I didn’t mention Z earlier? That’s because they went down a rabbit hole with an LLM and are facing a completely different problem than what the underlying issue is Beyond this, I also receive AI outputs as replies there as well.. And at work I cant be as open about these thoughts since our bonuses rely on it .. Very unfortunate, but I wholeheartedly believe it will get better", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "67121a8f-2fc6-478e-81d9-bae8ce8ad274", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 211, "text": "I’m waiting for the eventual and inevitable scenario that this post, just like many other posts that have the slightest mention of AI, becomes a warzone that is only laid to rest with intervention from the mods.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "7e687e9a-e6b7-4b5b-b71e-024ade0a98b8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 0, "end": 157, "text": "im hoping it stays civil, genuinely I want to hear all sides, just not stark “its the future, get used to it.” brick-wall arguments which are born of emotion", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "83a8e262-ea94-4e60-8d58-9140f266c7b3", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 46, "text": "ChatGPT users when they ask if the sky is blue", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "30f38a95-c91b-489b-82e1-85b725244daf", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 878, "text": "The other day I was really stuck on something ( I forget exactly what now ). Basically, I was getting errors that I couldn’t trace back to any specific code. So after Googling, I decided that before I spent the time to make a post, I would try out Claude and see if it could help me debug it. Because admittedly, it’d be faster. Also, Claude now gives your references and links to threads from where it’s pulling answers. You know what it did? It regurgitated a post I had made a year before on the Godot forums and tried to use it to help me debug my problem. None of it was relevant, and it was quoting me to me! I ended up realizing I was going to have to figure it out myself. I finally did solve the problem. I think by looking at the Godot source code, and then commenting out swaths of code until I figured out where the problem lay. LLMs are spicy autocomplete machines.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "d06399b8-d6ce-4f77-8448-cd248aba9a57", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 649, "text": "Commenting here because I got tagged from your original edit, lol Imho the world is changing - We’re in a Eternal September phase of the internet, where the new inbalance is that the effort required to create content is now less than the effort required to consume it. I think we’ll continue to make changes to how things operate. Github PRs should probably require a certain level of trust to make (i.e they have to be small at first), and forums should have rules for no AI generated posts/comments. Perhaps the Github issues can go through a pruning process where some volunteers confirm the issue before the maintainers waste time looking at it.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "6c76ac75-f04f-4021-8130-81b1bc56f749", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 386, "text": "This is assuming the humans that are supposed to do this: are with enough have enough time are willing to do it all for free Who will organize the logistics for this? A couple of agents? Nothing lasts forever Seems more like a couple of failed harvests to me. Where everyone’s looking at the barren soil, wondering whatever was wrong with the latest pesticide we sprayed over the crops.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac"]}, {"id": "abd54050-d3a1-44bf-a1e6-769b3d91ceb1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 249, "text": "There might be hope, as the token costs for coding shops using Claude (and the other fuckery thingies) are having to face the fact that replacing Junior coders with LLMs costs more than their salary + benefits (+ mythical bonuses) Source (in French)", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "98d71c5b-60f1-4682-a5d8-e2321bc3491d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "Nothing that upcoming trillion bucks IPOs couldn’t solve… or so they “hope” Let’s not forget the fact that LLMs can’t really do the job. A minor detail that we’re all supposed to pretend it’s not there.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "e1cb1027-b20f-4eed-9796-1d0ef6eade72", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 317, "text": "I think the only thing one really have to ask oneself to begin questioning the value and usefulness of these tools is: Why are these companies so desperate to get us to use it, why do they have to push it on us if it’s so good… If the product worked as well as they claim then wouldn’t everyone just choose to use it?", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "67f6a963-8ac3-4666-896d-2b5669971eaf", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 113, "text": "They’re praying to god that it becomes good later to make infinite money. But the trajectory is not looking good.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "2e458b3c-f238-4ef3-a575-b4c39ef190c2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 515, "text": "We discussed this at length in another thread. The Godot team does not have the bandwidth to deal with this. This forum does in fact have rules against AI generated answers, and those get flagged and removed. AI generated questions get either no replies, or get called out for using LLMs. This has also been discussed in previous threads here, and it’s not really a sustainable solution. To your point about the fact that content can be created faster than humans can consume it, a human firewall is not the answer.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "ebeb041b-97af-429e-a43f-2cf1fd04c482", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 671, "text": "AI is great tool for experienced devs, it’s like google search but butter, if not AI learning godot would take me years, not months like yesterday i had to try to export stuff, and with just godot docs, it would take probably few more days, cuz i haven’t understood stuff in it - thanks to AI and some try&error i’ve managed to encrypt export template by myself, no need to make forum posts, no need to research more, just move on to next thing but i understand your concerns, AI looks like super magic tool that fixes all problems - when in reality that’s not true, but someone who can’t code or is too lazy sees it that way, making it way too overused than it should be", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "bfca79c7-88aa-4549-8aa3-9c3bc73780af", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 617, "text": "Encrypting export templates is clearly described in the docs so I don’t see how “AI” was needed to help with this Also “no need to research more” is not a good thing, relying on one tool that is known for making things up instead of other humans that actually know is a downgrade, not an upgrade (also a huge portion of the time you don’t even need to wait for an answer, someone else has already answered when someone else asked already) What I really don’t get is the general mentality of “I don’t have to rely on anyone else if I use this tool” or “ah good I don’t have to search the internet for answers any more”", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "e55f9481-7fd1-49ba-8c87-2a065ec0f435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 860, "text": "really, i disagree, for me it was very hard to understand docs and why some errors occured, maybe you’re experienced with godot / computers so you’re better off with this also, no need to research anymore isn’t good or bad, it’s just change, i don’t need to search up internet, but i need to think how to solve problem, how to navigate and all of that - that’s just changing skill from one to another AI makes things up - but it’s overrated, mostly it’s correct, as long as you’re not blindly following it you’ll be fine, i don’t see the reason why relying on other devs is better than relying on a tool and yourself before AI was created, some problems that i didn’t understood took me months to grasp, months! but now i can do it in hours, AI is like a screwdriwer, you can place screw inside thread by hands, but with screwdriwer it’s way quicker and easier", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "6d759265-3321-4a84-812f-32137eb2d6f6", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00031", "post": 31, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "And how will you do this if you rely on that alone? Also the hallucinations are not “overrated” I honestly think this is an issue of your research skills, not the special nature of “AI”, and if you replace learning on your own, what will you do if you can’t use it? The prices for tokens is going up and will continue to do so, and using these tools has been shown to make people more and more dependent on them Edit: Please note that this is not trying to criticize or shame you, it’s a hard skill to get good at but it takes practice", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "5ca5cb33-ab33-4269-83ba-4ab6dfd68aca", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 654, "text": "NGL, i don’t worry about tokens cuz ai i use don’t have them… But, i never said i rely on AI alone, i said i use AI & experimentation, the latter is very important, cuz if AI says something, i don’t take it, i just analyze it, it’s like you were reading wikipedia, forums, reddit and some random website at once while not reading it at the same time Maybe my research skills are the issue - but i think that everything is good if it serves you and helps, forcing yourself to research something is pointless waste of time, you could use on something else Also i fear i’ve started great war, sb said there were wars because of something talking about AI :\\", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "b4d21382-e3fe-4d61-b839-8129123b1c57", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "You’re 10x-ing it bro. When’s your game coming out? Next month?", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "2df73332-564d-43bb-9dd5-986f91e285c2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 806, "text": "This is a slippery slope though, and research is hardly a pointless waste of time, and that attitude is harmful IMO, it’s like saying “it’s fine to depend on ready made food instead of learning how to cook” (assuming you’re not struggling to do so because you’re disabled, or don’t have a stove like a lot of poor and homeless people do) It’s becoming dependent on a tool that: Can go away at any moment Can become incredibly expensive at any moment Activelly harms the environment and your brain And I genuinely care when people get trapped in using these tools instead of actually good and reliable sources for knowledge (trial and error isn’t really making it not relying entirely on “AI”, you’d do that anyway if you did research) Also do be aware that they’re only “free” because they steal your data…", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "a824ae73-fe03-488a-b794-7867841c9154", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 39, "text": "Is based on intellectual property theft", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e"]}, {"id": "a0b2dd8b-8444-4687-afd2-b7626551edb9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 509, "text": "Yes, it takes years to get good at research (and development). Being given a quick answer as to how something is done does not really teach you to understand what you did. At a shallow level maybe. But the only way to really learn anything is to put in a lot of effort. And asking help from someone who understands. Not from something that regurgitates knowledge. We “googled” too. We read books too. We asked other people for help too. But none of them gave the answer away. Want to guess how long that took?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "806e96d4-2fd1-4dff-8639-85eb8dd376af", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 434, "text": "Maybe, but what about machines? they were also unethical and made a lot of people dependant on them, yet after 300 years we have civilization like never before, i don’t want to start next ice berg - so i’ think you’re right in some cases, tho it’s up to you if you want to use the tool or not personally i use it to speed up work, but for most stuff i still use forums, so i don’t think it’s that bad, tho again - thx for your opinion", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "ad746f53-1b30-4854-8786-9dec7dc638a6", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Even when it comes to doing google searches we are using critical thinking to filter and parse answers, that is important in itself", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "5ddfe995-0cb1-4379-a3eb-848fdf4f8a7f", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 53, "text": "For what purpose? Why does work need to be “sped up”?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "d45dba63-f9b5-45b3-ad88-452ecdd1b700", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 464, "text": "So what you’re saying is that “AI” is like people using a car to drive everywhere who become physically unable to walk anywhere far because they never get exercise? Also no it’s not “up to you” if you become dependent on the tool, or when the companies are constantly pushing it on us To be clear: I’m not trying to win an argument or make a point as much as I am concerned about people becoming dependent on these tools and thinking they are something they aren’t", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "3e68e7e6-c8bc-4537-9672-9b79bf405b1c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 386, "text": "Which took years to get good at too. And yeah I’m starting to notice signs of the LLM regurgitating all sorts of novice mistakes I just put into Github without realizing the danger back then, which reminds me that I still have a lot of work purging repos. For instance: The LLM does not realize garbage collected languages can leak memory badly for one. The LLM does not realize at all!", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "7587abda-a4a7-4627-a89f-6ee823bc21c0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 0, "end": 288, "text": "To get very good it takes time sure but it’s a series of small steps, and if someone never tries they will never reach it But then that’s also a skill that needs to be learned young, back when I went to school they were still teaching some of this, though I got most of it from my parents", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "91be1f5f-e642-40f5-8379-aba7de48aa51", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 0, "end": 193, "text": "maybe, ig time will show, i still will try to use it when required, but it’s another tool, one day it will be forgotten and obsolete, and people will find something else to be more dependent on", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7974c0b5-312f-4961-8d8f-674e2ba84730", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 0, "end": 33, "text": "It’s not a tool. It’s an exploit.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "e8c2cbce-bd01-4c5e-baf9-6c97b1261310", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "Not everyone is comfortable or going to lean on the easy solution But you do what you feel is best for you, in the end you are the one affected Except by how it affects the environment, or people’s jobs, or how the technology kills people and no one regulates it (or, for that matter, how it makes searching the internet increasingly difficult because of all the slop…) But in the end I was trying to help and make suggestions on how it might affect you, and it’s up to you to decide what to do with that (just as long as you don’t use it to make issue reports on Godot, then it’s my problem)", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "9ae6ef21-0e3d-4db8-8cc6-1269e446cf6d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 0, "end": 85, "text": "Please remain civil and keep the discussion on-topic, aligned with the original post.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "2f350e99-ac3d-46e8-bbe8-d1ab9874f3b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 0, "end": 555, "text": "this got me thinking some, another perspective i havent thought about is that it can speed up for looking thru information in docs and such. Not my cup of tea (i like the godot docs i came from SDL2 so its much easier than SDL docs to look through for sure) but definitely a more unique use case Though i guess the downside to that is there’s less information online for others/the AI itself to learn from discord feels like a 0.5 of that, where information is no longer ‘online’, but in a small community and in long message threads, weird to think about", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "ab90350d-f5bc-4ebe-af45-c4965dbc65e0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 0, "end": 466, "text": "i think something important to note is, you cant change someone on the internet very easily, its a lot more productive to learn their perspective, and practice constructive conversation. Hence the ‘open discussion’ title My feelings i stated in the original post are the same, but its interesting to hear the other side of things, thanks even those who got shot down some for putting your perspective into the mix a lot can be learned from leaving conversations open", "codes": ["6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "7a901642-7cc9-41f4-b472-c6c240a467f4", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 0, "end": 248, "text": "That’s what i said about speeding up searching information and looking through it, i don’t say that AI should write code, i’m quite opposite, but the moment you gather information - you can spend less time on it and focus on thinking than searching", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "435e9e57-1c8f-43a6-919c-c28d2be8698a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 0, "end": 417, "text": "I’m convinced that there are / should be better technologies out there that simply use indexed search (lucene core - a part of solr, elastic search, postgres token search - and quite possibly ported by google back in the day) and summarizing (?) without pretense prompting to do good research with. But thank you for being a great sport in this conversation and thank you for listening & reading all responses fairly.", "codes": ["9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "197534df-561f-4bcb-9e10-52edb4b1b832", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 0, "end": 704, "text": "The thread was about polluting repos with slop. There’s a very slippery slopE (see what I did there) from “It’s just a search tool” to “I’ll generate just a little bit of example code” to fully vibe coded PR garbage. The more you’re of the “my head hurts when reading docs” disposition - the more likely you’ll slip on that slope. I don’t buy any “trust me, I don’t use it to generate code” rhetoric. It’s straight up lying in most cases. This shit is aggressively advertised precisely for that purpose, it’s highly addictive and tricks you into false sense of “productivity gains”. It’s a hard to resist combo for average opportunistic lot. That’s what created the slop flood problem in the first place.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "ec88c7d1-b9b3-45d5-9b17-a6bb2ff6db28", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 0, "end": 267, "text": "So what more do we need to look out for when (relatively) new to this forum? I cannot distinguish between a robot, a grifter and someone in need of a genuine explainer. Alas, I know not which zombie thread to point to (yet).. ok so maybe a bot.. Is not that difficult", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "44234d18-c1a3-475a-836f-7a0a8e76def8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 978, "text": "Things to look out for: A post that starts with “Copy and paste this into a post on the Godot forum to ask for support”. ( Yes, that happened. ) A post that looks like a Readme and contains uncommon emojis. Wording that is too flowery. ( Keeping in mind that some people just use LLMs to translate their questions to English. You can usually distinguish these because word choices are odd. ) Code that is FULL of verbose, full sentence comments Code that is divided up into logical sections with comments that just contain dashes in them. Code that is overly complex for what it is doing. Different coding styles in the same script. Debug or print statements in code that include Emojis. Replies that are overly complimentary in a creepy way. ( “You are so right, so what do you suggest I do?” ) Replies that communicate a complete lack of understanding of their own code when you offer a suggestion on how to fix something. A new user who has a really attractive female avatar.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a475b6c7-ffe6-4a8e-b5b8-f5f5bfa00435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00068", "post": 68, "start": 0, "end": 271, "text": "It’s trained on everything. I was hesitant to post that list because it’s going to make the LLMs smarter. Like one of those adaptive killer Sentinels in X-Men: Days of Future Past who can adapt to any power thrown at them after the first attack and you can only run from.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "94001857-4196-4dfd-929f-59385734ee7b", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00069", "post": 69, "start": 0, "end": 275, "text": "Dividing into segments //‐————- or //======= was normal practice in C++ in the file and function headers. Code with comments is pretty nice and is often found when a coder is explaining something conplicqted to the reader … it is code that is meant to be read and understood.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "6056e903-fad1-4d72-bd1e-785af20b874e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00072", "post": 72, "start": 2746, "end": 3110, "text": "# Run the move_and_slide() function to apply the velocity. You can use # move_and_collide() as well, but for your game, move_and_slide() is the # correct choice. move_and_slide() Using them as C-style comments is very distinctive and not what I’m referring to. Sorry for any confusion. Again, see the example above to see what I mean about overly verbose comments.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "063360a5-a771-41c2-8bc0-dd2333496bc0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00073", "post": 73, "start": 0, "end": 865, "text": "Thank you much for doing so anyway. In the end these forums may be scraped now, but hopefully some standards may be upheld TDM on W3C once finalized. It will most likely be(come) law in the EU area. I’m as sceptical as anyone about this, but at least some action is being pursued after the fact. As to them “getting smarter”, I am not convinced it works that way. As “they” do not have the capacity to understand anything, they have no means to “wisen” up to anything either. At best they will regurgitate your good advice, but mix it up with something only vaguely related… no deliberate action because, again, they cannot deliberate. These are human capabilities that we know we can do, but that we do not understand how to replicate in a machine (just poorly imitate, which is a totally different thing). I fear muzzling ourselves out of fear. Just my two cents.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "97de5a3e-6ecd-4412-b1b7-65aaf6d762e0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 0, "end": 302, "text": "Oh the times I thought they really did build an intelligence, the times that I doubted my better judgement… Those guys capitalized on that… It sucks! Everyone can be had this way. I mean. Your argument still had merit and to maybe most readers it’s the best argument: don’t make the *** things smarter.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "c1cd83ce-52c9-4893-bc09-06f6d6f3eafd", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 660, "text": "AI will know this probably - AI is weight machine, so it uses math weights to determine stuff, you can’t change that xd, maybe if it’s scalled to enormous scale it can talk it seems to be inteligent, but at the deepest level it’s still like placing heavy things on top of springs and chosing the spring that’s shortest That is the reason AI have weak point - it can’t determine what is heaviest if everything weights the same, thus if two answers are as probable, it wouldn’t think about them, it would assume both are correct or halucinate… That’s why AI will be always a tool - it lacks creativity, not even talking about consiousness, so forums will stay ig", "codes": ["a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "f134e7f3-a8ca-4fa2-b275-daf6fb8189d0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 0, "end": 622, "text": "LLMs do not maintain a concept of an “answer”. It’s just probabilistic lexicographical token streaming. Here’s a neat video that explains how it actually works. I already linked this some time ago but doesn’t hurt to do it again: You could call an LLM a tool if you were running your own model on your own local machine, trained on legally obtained data. However the mega-LLMs you rent from the big players come with a range of nefarious strings attached: from deliberately misleading hype and borderline criminal financial manipulations to environmentally detrimental infrastructure and massive scale training data theft.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "4cac8989-e237-4b57-99ac-b26234c00995", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 0, "end": 346, "text": "Using the concept of an “answer” to describe what is happening is highly misleading. And you’re not doing anyone a service by using misleading language, well except maybe the “ai” stock owners from whom the whole fashion of misleading descriptions originates. Take a look at the video I posted to gain better understanding of what’s “underneath”.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "92d8ad56-1476-49c3-8d9d-4f24b6945435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 0, "end": 617, "text": "What I find interesting is that, LLMs are trained to act as if they are sentient----to the point that they will flat out refuse simple and basic commands like saying “You are smarter than me” image 686×449 40.7 KB , and then when you argue back they will say things like like Even itself knows it isnt sentient, why do people assume that? I do not know. And AI generated forum posts are useless, just ask the AI if you are going to not even put in the effort… not that it will get you far… —Feel free to ignore this sidepost. Note: I used copilot here specifically for this post, I am not an average user by any means", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "29c7d985-cdba-465a-ba68-681d29ab6fd1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00082", "post": 82, "start": 0, "end": 277, "text": "It does not know it’s sentient or not, as by definition, it doesn’t know anything. Again, we should take care not to anthropomorphize LLMs. That “human” veneer is just that : marketing to make people think we’re on the road to general AI and the singularity. It’s all greedy BS", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "6c1ddf17-27e3-4ec7-b787-e80f1601706f", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00084", "post": 84, "start": 0, "end": 45, "text": "Even the word “training” anthropomorphizes it", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "ee613a8d-765c-46b7-a5ee-61446b327cd9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 0, "end": 123, "text": "Classic. Theyre so anthopomophizable … someone anthropomorphized a few billion parameters in a tensor multiplication kernel", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "4b413c90-2f5a-4e1f-a0e2-2c047705a1b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 0, "end": 155, "text": "I’d suggest anyone wondering about this to look up the ELIZA effect, it’s interesting and outright terrifying at the same time, and more relevant than ever", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "cd9351d0-8c52-4439-895e-06d04a221758", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00087", "post": 87, "start": 0, "end": 54, "text": "Careful. It’s someone’s waifu you’re disparaging here.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "48a6766e-a22a-4e35-9dcd-57f3f5cf2a30", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00089", "post": 89, "start": 0, "end": 290, "text": "I’d say speaking of a machine that isn’t sentient or able to think as “thinking” would make people think of them differently and make people less likely to understand that they do, in fact, not think The ELIZA effect is a perfect example of how this is a problem and the issues it can cause", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "2343e213-7f8a-4e14-bf1f-cc8aa7cc8b11", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00091", "post": 91, "start": 0, "end": 547, "text": "Not really it’s an issue in other languages as well No the problem is partially that people view it as sentient, as demonstrated by the ELIZA effect where there was no real level of sophistication involved, yet people still anthropomorphized it because it seemed human in the way it responded The level of seeming quality of the output isn’t really the thing that makes people think it is able to think, it’s that it “talks” like a human The way to deal with that is to guard oneself against thinking of it as “thinking” to not fall into that trap", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "3d84a33f-d643-4971-8e1d-b8ddc8875663", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00093", "post": 93, "start": 0, "end": 424, "text": "Sure but these LLMs are not sentient, or conscious, so that’s not really relevant That’s not realistic, and wouldn’t be needed if people stopped talking about it as if it is thinking Also that knowledge isn’t actually sufficient, you need to consciously view it as non-sentient because of the ELIZA effect and general human nature, plenty of people well aware of how LLMs work fall for the trap of thinking they are sentient", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "dbaaa16c-670f-4543-9033-a1e5abe4350d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00099", "post": 99, "start": 0, "end": 241, "text": "For most people, language constructs can form their reality. That’s why calling it “intelligence” paired with the “proof” in the form of human-like text output, conjures a (false) reality that it’s an autonomous intelligent entity behind it.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "bd0c43fc-4f91-4ece-bb34-05d758f68cf9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00103", "post": 103, "start": 52, "end": 713, "text": " Training, as a variable for “LLM providers/admins setting different parameters and giving the LLM different ‘references’ for the ‘best’ outputs”, being overly descriptive on things i do not care about is not my style, even if it is supposedly “anthropomorphizing” it. I am not going to waste time on using the “correct” terminology for the rules being set for some “so-called artificial intelligence” merely because everybody else cares about people’s viewpoints, or more specifically the viewpoints of those people who are clearly inadequately educated on glorified python scripts. I am truly confounded at the meaning of this, but I find it somewhat amusing.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "7558faa3-dee9-46eb-87ad-fd7153a4e9e7", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00104", "post": 104, "start": 0, "end": 371, "text": "I’m an amateur game developers, it will spend too much time to turning my imagination into real game if no LLM. But before vibe coding, I will discuss a suitable plan with LLM about how to construct scripts. So my views is that discussion on the forum could focus on ideas and construction of different modules. For debug of detailed scripts, LLM maybe a more ideal tool.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "ccd040f1-3fb7-4945-89f7-65b83be76bff", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00105", "post": 105, "start": 0, "end": 64, "text": "What’s wrong with taking the time to learn to do something well?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "959d8648-bfb9-4a78-8f64-ede696d9cca2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00107", "post": 107, "start": 0, "end": 159, "text": "So basically you’d like to use us to help you when the LLM fails? That’s nice. You have no idea how LLMs work, so you do not understand why this is a bad idea.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "41df0a1e-5d0d-4db1-8dca-7c0dfd9037da", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00108", "post": 108, "start": 0, "end": 151, "text": "i think what he said is that he’d use forum for more creative work, but the technical part will go to AI cuz it’s better at solving these problems fast", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "852aadca-6130-4d10-8229-b9b25d205536", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00109", "post": 109, "start": 0, "end": 155, "text": "Not everyone have time or opportunities to learn, i wouldn’t argue why, i just say it’s possible he can’t learn thus LLM is his only solution that fits him", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "db281314-6171-4cdb-8596-8e658a7c3d07", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00110", "post": 110, "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "How did you come to the conclusion that it’s better at solving problems? Fast or slow? This is the same argument that white slave owners in the U.S. south gave to why they couldn’t give up their slaves.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "e6d1a180-62fc-40d1-be40-9a6dd109f70c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00111", "post": 111, "start": 0, "end": 329, "text": "i’ve used LLMs in the past for lot of stuff, chat gpt was main solver & thanks to it i’ve managed to learn godot quicker than from tutorials also for time & opportunities - as i’ve said, maybe he have no such option other than LLM, i won’t argue if it’s good or bad, i’ just say that not everyone can learn just because they want", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "70058ce3-da99-4416-ac7f-3053409b783a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00112", "post": 112, "start": 0, "end": 278, "text": "Have you though? Because I’ve read your posts, and knowing you learned from an LLM explains why you have so many wrong ideas about how Godot works. You are arguing for good whether you know it or not. You’re trying to sit on the fence which is disingenuous. What does that mean?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "39f5a4b2-da38-494f-8b42-e49bb183e39e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00113", "post": 113, "start": 0, "end": 189, "text": "The problems you are solving, are they generally things you are inexperienced with? Or areas you have limited knowledge? Or are they areas where you have high degree of skill and knowledge?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "4042c006-db99-469c-b2f9-61b97a25bc92", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00118", "post": 118, "start": 0, "end": 505, "text": "You are not answering my questions and instead are accusing some nebulous “them” of doing something. Instead of paying attention to what we’ve actually said here on this forum. So I’m done engaging with you on this topic because you do not seem to be actually interested in discussion. You seem to have an agenda of pushing the “positives” of LLMs without hearing anything else and then ducking behind “fair and balanced” whenever your statements are challenged instead of actually engaging in discussion.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "d268521a-f796-4b12-a349-920dad2f2696", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00121", "post": 121, "start": 31, "end": 297, "text": "don’t know what you are talking about But back to my question earlier: when you’ve been impressed by the value of LLMs, is that for things you are experienced in and know a lot about? Or might it largely be in areas where you are otherwise ignorant or inexperienced?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "5d42105c-c0ab-4a67-bbda-ac5fcbc715a2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00127", "post": 127, "start": 0, "end": 546, "text": "Yes. The world is not a fair place and indeed we do not have equal opportunities. But my question was what is wrong with taking time to learn something well. Especially knowing that LLM technology costs more money to use than paying a human being. I’d encourage everyone to make use of all the free and open resources the internet has to offer to learn. But delegating work to a machine that produces such poor output that it will never make you money yet has a high potential of costing you money will make things much worse. LLM’s are not free:", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "df512b42-9cb4-4dab-99c7-e213cab7d272", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00128", "post": 128, "start": 0, "end": 224, "text": "thx for explanation - it’s nice you give alternatives, but for now i’ll close my part of discussion cuz i think i’ll get to nowhere and maybe it’s better, hope i gave some new perspective on this topic, thx for talk everyone", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "d91474cb-3ad6-4db1-bfef-61ad282f032e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00129", "post": 129, "start": 0, "end": 1186, "text": "I don’t know if that is better. I will advocate your point. A cool new technology that can do marvelous things has been released to the public for free. It has never been done before to make such a sophisticated and expensive technology available to everyone for free. Promising the democratization of skills that take years to learn to give to everyone equally (everyone that can pay for access to a computer somewhere that is). Loads of people that cannot afford to go to good schools and universities to learn computer science or 3D graphics design,you name it are lured in by this promise. They even get hooked to the power of being able to do things they could not even imagine before. And then the axe falls: specialists tell them it does not work as advertised they tell them you’re being lied to by billionaires they discover it costs a lot of money and was only free because the companies making it were borrowing loads of money using the same lies they told you about this technology Would you believe that? After all you were able to do with this tech? I understand. But rip off the band-aid, because it is true and the specialists trying to warn you for years are tired too.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ffb6dcb9-b2be-4679-a8c6-ac7ac888e509", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00131", "post": 131, "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "Also don’t forget that the technology is stealing all your data", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e61ec20e-8d07-40a6-937d-c5c9ff4e4fb2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00132", "post": 132, "start": 0, "end": 302, "text": "Maybe I prefer get suggestions at the design stage of one functional modules(i am not sure wheather that is a professional word). For example, if my game needs a simple, intuitive but efficient backpack management system, i will search forum’s help. This is a kind of work from 0 to 1 which LLMs lacks.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "b498a17a-db80-4838-8a49-03869b7826f8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00135", "post": 135, "start": 0, "end": 593, "text": "That was sarcasm on my part, which I know doesn’t come through well in writing. Just keep in mind, that those of us who are frustrated by people like you using LLMs are the ones who supply the LLMs with the information to answer your questions. If people stop asking us, or if we get annoyed enough to stop answering, then that well of information dries up and LLMs cannot answer questions about new things. LLMs are literally destroying the sources of information that power them. And that has long term consequences that it will be too late to pull back from by the time they are fully felt.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "22f8deec-484c-4723-908f-d34d342c075a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00136", "post": 136, "start": 0, "end": 811, "text": "It’s a multi-headed beast, because even specialists get bedazzled and Nobel prize winning AI professors are either bedazzled enough or somehow willing to lie for pay to keep selling this lie. It’s a form of psychosis that occurs when all the sceptic voices are drowned out. Is there an English equivalent for the saying: “al is de leugen nog zo snel, de waarheid achterhaalt hem wel”? “The lie may be very fast, the truth will catch up eventually.” The longer it takes and the more are convinced, the bigger the pain too, so it seems. A valid use if a language model is to help you structure your own ideas. But find one on the interwebs that does not need an internet connection to work, so you are sure it is not burning up the planet. Or just use this forum of course. And take your time to learn stuff well.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "104a1ce6-f5e5-4e58-9cce-2f6e977a277a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00137", "post": 137, "start": 0, "end": 443, "text": "There’s a variant which is “a lie will get around the world before the truth has even got its shoes on” and similar, probably conveying the same meaning I ultimately disagree that it is useful to organize your ideas, at least in any real way that’s better than just writing them down or structuring it, and that part is without the data theft and it hallucinating things for you, or deleting all of your work (or injecting bigotry in it, etc.)", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "4f761cd6-5e44-44a0-8c8d-a13b2e21d414", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00139", "post": 139, "start": 0, "end": 855, "text": "Never used it myself so can’t speak to it specifically, but on the basic concept that you need it in that way Also, it’s always important to remember one thing when considering the output and what biases might be in it: It was trained on the internet, meaning the volume of content here affects the output. The input it got includes things like Reddit and 4Chan, which I think anyone can imagine affects the output It ultimately reflects humanity, in our best and worst, and it is very much not free of our collective biases But unlike picking a person to collaborate with on a project, or doing your own writing, you can’t control what bigotry it “chooses” to regurgitate today, and it is very unreliable, for some topics it might be very progressive, for others extremely regressive, simply based on what the dataset looks like for that particular topic", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "40cb87a7-45eb-4e8a-b0b4-a262ab41abf0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00141", "post": 141, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "It got a really good point. If an LLM fails to properly understand something and that misinfo gets distributed across many places, then another LLM end up training on that incorrect information. Over time, this could create a loop of misinfo! We’re cooked then! Sorry, I am late in the party xD 135 is a lot to read xD", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "9740420a-53b0-4a3c-8607-e9b60546b9ea", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00142", "post": 142, "start": 0, "end": 615, "text": "Even without the “AI” suggestions at the top of Google the internet is slowly but surely becoming slop, with generated content both polluting the available sources, as well as making more and more text functional poison for the models as training a model on the output of a model has a lot of inherent issues It’s even getting to the point where reputable scientists are citing hallucinated articles in their theses and papers, and that gets worse and worse as the databases for citations are themselves sourced from existing data and contain more and more slop You even have lawyers citing non-existent legal cases", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "5770cb4e-daed-4bed-ae2d-02467107c0c4", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00143", "post": 143, "start": 0, "end": 311, "text": "People in the 1940s: We used to search the whole library for a tiny piece of info. I spent the entire day going through books just to find out what language Waiting for Godot was originally written in…(It’s French ) People in the 2060s: It was hard to find an answer on the internet because it was full of SLOP!", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "c9483d59-fd7e-4098-a120-22f97570f1ab", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00146", "post": 146, "start": 0, "end": 541, "text": "I know I’d rest easier and have more confidence in the future of Godot if they banned AI submissions to Godot outright. Flathub recently moved to ban AI products and Godot should too, if for nothing else than the simple pragmatic reason of avoiding plagerized code. Flathub moves to ban nearly all apps and submissions made with generative AI | GamingOnLinux It just makes sense, the Godot maintainers made headlines back in February after whining about the slop being fed to the project. Why continue to burden themselves and the community?", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e27b734e-47f2-47de-9a65-bf4fb93d6023", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00148", "post": 148, "start": 0, "end": 51, "text": "Personally, as a maintainer, I agree wholeheartedly", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "a45e83e7-2d7e-4e47-bae1-c0262176e7b8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00149", "post": 149, "start": 0, "end": 871, "text": "Reading this just made my day. I have a strong anti AI sentiment and as a professional my policy is not to use it (unless forced to) as I have experienced first hand how much I progress and learn by writing my own code. I know I shouldn’t, but whenever I scroll into social media I get overflown by pro LLM posts. Most of them are just hype slop, but sometimes I read someone I respect encouraging their use and get really discouraged because my experience in several work environments has been awful. Not only do they lead to awful slop, most of my colleagues are delighted by it even though it makes things some much harder for us. And don’t get me started on bosses and managers and so on. So far game dev circles and godot in particular were my comfort zone, mainly because the “artistic” mindset tends to be against AI of any sort, but it seems that is also changing", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "956dd7f3-6afd-41fd-b7b1-0dabe0f69611", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00150", "post": 150, "start": 0, "end": 304, "text": "We all need a safe space. I noticed a shift in sentiment. I do believe it’s the malicious GPU datacenters that are hopefully never going to be built that are the final straw for us human beings. Here’s a little spark of hope: Ed Zitron is main stream media now. They’re starting to realize the jig is up.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "fb0407f1-6fcd-4f95-a3cf-414a3b6287ba", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00151", "post": 151, "start": 0, "end": 195, "text": "I also noticed a general shift in sentiment, but the new anthropic model sparked things again in my circle. At least people are more conscious about the obvious hyper agressive marketing strategy", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "b6226a6e-fe27-4252-b53f-d712a65f5b03", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00153", "post": 153, "start": 0, "end": 712, "text": "Help spread the word if you can. This world desperately needs a new and humane narrative to start to supercede end-stage extractive capitalism; unbridled capitalism has completely run its course and is showing its true face… I really like the terms ‘rot economy’ and ‘business idiot’ that Ed regularly uses, because they really speak to the imagination of anyone who ever worked in an office. There was also a panel he did with Cory Doctorow (coined ‘enshittification’) in a Seattle library last year somewhere on the web. Personally, I can’t even watch “the office” because it is so triggering. This approach to trying to use LLM’s in a resource extractive manner is just a result of unregulated markets (imho).", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "2a2c5989-a56a-4c43-b0ab-6ca53f5ad67b", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00154", "post": 154, "start": 0, "end": 37, "text": "You mean “the new anthropic pr stunt”", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "eeb9e0cb-3d47-4f03-8af6-b1a4df5fa9de", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00156", "post": 156, "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Yeah. It is the most blatantly obvious pr stunt. Still, several people I know have bought it entirely and it achieved its objective", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "c3c2d3b2-a1a1-48f3-907a-62746fbbba02", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00159", "post": 159, "start": 0, "end": 199, "text": "Sorry for opening this up again. I just wanted to share this new little masterpiece written by Nikhil Suresh, who runs an independent data consultancy: AI Mania is Eviscerating Global Decision Making", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "5f366839-1720-4aa8-a79d-09a7da6520eb", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00160", "post": 160, "start": 0, "end": 176, "text": "Excellent article. This whole insanity is bound to crash and burn in a spectacular way. The only question is how long it will take and how much collateral damage will it cause.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "5a54c7f4-bbab-4e0f-bf8d-f2d06f4332ba", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00161", "post": 161, "start": 0, "end": 469, "text": "It might not burn and crash soon enough. If the US government takes a 50% stake in the AI companies (presenting Elon with another pile of billions) all the risk and debt will be spread amongst tax payers even more directly. I am enjoying watching SpaceX slump. We were supposed to be landing on the moon but Elon spent it all on launching starlink satellites. Data centres in space! Ridiculous, but I suppose not more ridiculous than colonies on mars or the hyperloop. ", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "3378c469-9c61-4390-84ae-613599416f80", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00162", "post": 162, "start": 0, "end": 306, "text": "I perused the comments in the thread and didn’t notice this caveat posted yet. To copyright software code there must be significant human authorship, and just what that is defined as, is still being determined, and any project with major contributions of LLM generated code may or may not be copyrightable.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "1ce25ead-2c84-4ab1-91ce-3d77cb287879", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 118, "end": 1012, "text": " E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "26bacadd-3b11-480b-9d7c-ff14c50b05aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 50, "end": 1087, "text": " A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "97b9a19c-fac8-4bcb-8500-40b3de649acb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 108, "end": 564, "text": "My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3f5ab29b-2f21-448d-870e-3b89b05c1c1c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 84, "end": 300, "text": "That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "01d36e86-d25c-4659-be6f-aca50908595e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 107, "end": 626, "text": " It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "9c4f3f11-c33e-47ff-b4f2-6aab2ce64017", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code?", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3a7b597f-34b0-446c-adab-c080568e48c0", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 93, "end": 502, "text": " AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "5ba2e964-fa60-4c62-94fa-f0feb2afc5fd", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 503, "end": 1106, "text": "I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd"]}, {"id": "0cbc6753-9ef5-4f0f-b4d8-496eddd14727", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 106, "end": 384, "text": "This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "96034856-2d4a-4978-84da-8d4b4481ccb4", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 51, "end": 488, "text": " I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "76195130-720b-4152-9aa3-72a8d671d624", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 1349, "text": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9305a0fb-da0b-45f1-a351-cedc13332a5b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 286, "text": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as wel", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2d614e2-a55e-46df-99f4-852447101d66", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 100, "end": 637, "text": " I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e4e794b5-b6d8-4666-a67c-fefb640c4def", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 4, "end": 514, "text": "This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "13cba10c-2f54-42aa-98a9-90ee069e2ccf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 607, "end": 1821, "text": " In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e097f575-4085-4a1d-92f1-43ff0b21ad7f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 1822, "end": 2594, "text": "Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "3068469d-d7b9-42ac-8502-33d284f7ada6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2595, "end": 2976, "text": "I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "c732fc79-dedc-44d9-a9de-2006bf53ff26", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2977, "end": 3396, "text": "But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "03f6b706-a795-4335-a72a-7e49d995cc6c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 463, "end": 807, "text": " Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "279d2282-63a8-44d9-99af-65d03cb97583", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 103, "end": 787, "text": " E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "f25d86aa-3810-41f5-a06c-18b69e95a839", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 510, "text": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "74f56b13-3847-427d-a5fb-cf2520654343", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 86, "end": 1474, "text": "Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation,", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "dfdcc40a-6a48-42cd-93a8-c0968a6c85aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 9, "end": 591, "text": "There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes)", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "fb976f23-970e-44de-9474-1c90a0691c8d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 116, "end": 323, "text": " I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "15ea0bcd-f0dd-470c-b609-8f8e5d0bc788", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 6, "end": 1882, "text": " I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "de462e67-8924-4e01-b026-e1346f7a801c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 106, "end": 768, "text": " I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "a667360a-af00-44bf-9b68-47028a2c5a8f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 769, "end": 2128, "text": " I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a7212ccc-bb2c-4bd9-846e-291dc2d5953f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 1129, "text": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f"]}, {"id": "0dc06a45-7252-4215-8298-12b6df678768", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 104, "end": 2373, "text": "Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers:", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "b6279b38-1e49-4b33-a8ea-341968b0a7cc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2375, "end": 2923, "text": " Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "0e19e7d4-da5c-417b-98d6-d945e806bd7b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2925, "end": 3098, "text": "- New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "dfe0c110-4bf1-4dd7-8f3e-c47c1054591d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 677, "text": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "93772467-26d6-4608-9f90-6bf4cfa74063", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 111, "end": 525, "text": "This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point.", "codes": ["cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "eabff033-0347-44f3-acd0-fb6ddb9c7d4d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 526, "end": 1273, "text": "And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "87ade70d-e8ca-45c9-ba8c-de0652118a6f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 1167, "text": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "04fcc7f6-80cb-43d2-97f1-f5254eb99e5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 719, "text": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "cef518cc-ef6d-4dcb-9f98-af72aeda91f3", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "8330cfeb-7670-41cd-9b1f-4355a3e28227", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 108, "end": 1643, "text": "The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "826e950a-826d-40cc-8f94-280197573444", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 83, "end": 1015, "text": " TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact?", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "3b4302b1-a72c-4266-bee8-bfc1e80c5f74", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1016, "end": 1785, "text": "For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)?", "codes": ["69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "3ece7a92-8060-4e37-a809-7ea6b0c9ae04", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1790, "end": 2646, "text": "FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "6e0bcb0e-99a7-4865-92d2-eaf0a391a0d2", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib.", "codes": ["0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d"]}, {"id": "57b3d8cf-4b08-41ba-bd36-5fd0e1339103", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 119, "end": 1566, "text": "Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "65fce25e-42a9-4bb5-9360-ac488fe392f6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 1738, "end": 3742, "text": "I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "9beb18e8-8c7a-4f1e-a392-7e02e0efa82a", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 11, "end": 1583, "text": " That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "72bd2f2e-8e52-4e5a-a96a-9e21a14ea0b9", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 108, "end": 1212, "text": " Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "7e178caa-2e45-44d4-a940-0e934121963c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 77, "end": 2066, "text": "While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "84370455-9c80-4fb4-ae18-def6f7ace4c7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "start": 1196, "end": 3581, "text": "Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "2f0c99cc-8a57-4c5e-a0b6-e1d6fb04159d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "start": 0, "end": 399, "text": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "6a46c591-fb27-42a6-89d2-e80af4ad74ce", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "start": 3, "end": 1085, "text": " I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "2c405b5a-bddb-4c2b-a6e6-c73995386f5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 247, "end": 4336, "text": " Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a100e2d2-9263-4c6f-8632-5a3b7658360b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 4337, "end": 6067, "text": "So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "e5699363-9b3b-4a68-893d-bc408a0640cb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "start": 111, "end": 2906, "text": " I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "4ce57613-c997-4f36-8896-e95fe4ce9333", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "start": 102, "end": 1028, "text": " I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "48ed37b7-8b86-4cf5-b157-2befce171473", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 110, "end": 883, "text": " Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148"]}, {"id": "46fc2b28-aa18-4ad3-bb83-29dac54f9f69", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 271, "end": 1290, "text": "the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "599ceb84-b01b-497b-9512-ee8ca8338c54", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 1290, "end": 3713, "text": " Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e03a3a49-e8b1-4f9c-a868-0f385cd6bfc1", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "start": 106, "end": 500, "text": "Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "7dd730f9-0877-458c-921c-aadd1dfe6a44", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 8, "end": 1535, "text": "I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "cc6a8be2-9a9e-428f-88ca-d6550e4b6157", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 1537, "end": 2286, "text": "Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "723843d1-beb3-4a3b-89e2-39a7ecd1f1dc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "start": 216, "end": 823, "text": "I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "af1d0d5c-7138-48e2-af37-696f33159d8e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 110, "end": 210, "text": " The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "codes": ["3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9"]}, {"id": "c7d901f3-72e4-49ce-a85d-932c9db4e81d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "start": 116, "end": 531, "text": " I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "3bd43ffc-c8f6-42a5-bcfb-ca7925505acf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 100, "end": 605, "text": " Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR?", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "4e9f97e3-519a-410f-ab7f-17efae16604b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 74, "end": 2337, "text": " I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "29cd93ea-5019-4d1e-886b-514571d727a8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "start": 106, "end": 1320, "text": "Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "40a727e6-89cc-4ce5-b669-c43ef08cfadb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 8, "end": 1138, "text": " I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "ecaf0ecf-4a95-40bd-9e81-949840e32f45", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 76, "end": 1180, "text": " Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "6be354ae-cff2-4350-a3f3-b1d1b696c3ca", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-)", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "f8e79487-2393-4e2f-bdb4-115395d9a29d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 80, "end": 448, "text": "Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "42ec4d44-c578-4271-8f38-b4d545d31af8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 344, "text": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. ", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "e8ee1d2a-64a3-4521-8ea1-20999d6e726d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 750, "end": 1945, "text": " (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "585975d8-01fc-423e-9056-b17ca89f8921", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 1946, "end": 3082, "text": "(2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly?", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "8070a77e-9ca5-42b6-bfdd-dd8ca9ecb7a7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 3083, "end": 3902, "text": "(3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.”", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "50df7254-c5cd-455a-a937-dc177614f329", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 4070, "end": 5321, "text": "I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "eaa206e6-1176-4e8d-b09a-7ae600abb376", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 5322, "end": 6134, "text": "(4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7e6fb536-19a6-43db-b6eb-2fc701a8e771", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 6136, "end": 6849, "text": "(5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable).", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "34febe70-3dca-4ca7-ac43-a09afb41026f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "2e137f3c-9f41-4186-a219-4d52f8718ecb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 60, "end": 908, "text": " FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "8c656a9d-1e35-49ad-835f-8d77f72ffba6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 77, "end": 964, "text": " I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "6c83ef21-c962-444c-a25b-19ddf70012ff", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "start": 426, "end": 1507, "text": " There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "93c7026e-f78f-43c7-bf7d-22aa8f3773cf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 84, "end": 327, "text": "You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "52fbca57-a761-4237-8177-5f37e809b548", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 4, "end": 903, "text": "I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "f00c88ed-8f95-4d8f-8398-205bb63f7ed7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 844, "end": 1916, "text": "it seems that the concerns can be roughly classified into two buckets: Do we accept genAI assisted contributions at all, and if we do, what are the boundaries of what’s acceptable. Copyright and license concerns. Let’s consider these two in order. Do we accept genAI contributions? It’s futile to attempt to block them wholesale. People are going to use AI assistants anyway, along with various IDEs, autocompleters etc So it seems reasonable to treat these assistants on par with other tools: as long as it does not matter if a contributor uses VS Code or emacs or vi, it also does not matter what engine powers their autocompleter. The discussion in [1] has a concise wording, “Only submit code that you actually undestand”, and I’d just go with just this. One thing we probably do not want to see is a fully automated submission, which is both generated and submitted by a bot, with no human intervention or supervision at all. So we could/should stress that, too. Copyright violations / incompatible licenses Here I lack expertise and have more questions than answers.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "e00a42fd-55d5-4fb1-a625-b0cab528395b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1917, "end": 3210, "text": "Here’s a possibly naive attempt at asking hopefully relevant questions: Are we concerned that the code generated by some tool is under some incompatible license from just the fact of being generated by the tool? In this case, using the tool is similar to copying code from, say, Numerical Recipes. Then we should either blacklist known bad tools (do we know of any?) or ask contributors to declare the tools they used (so that we can remove offending code at a future date if needed). Are we concerned that the training set of a tool included license-incompatible code? And if it did, what does it mean for us, are we then transitively non-compliant? If this is a concern, and we at some point discover that some code is license-incompatible, the action does not depend on how it got into the codebase, genAI or not: we discover it, we remove it. And then the only question IMO is whether it’s helpful to us to ask contributors list the AI assistants they used. To summarize, we should not block genAI assisted contributions; we should block fully automated contributions, and ask submitters to only submit code they understand; TBD if we want to ask contributors to list what genAI assistants they used, if any. Let’s add explicit (short!) guidance to the docs, TBD where precisely. Thoughts?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "047d9e6e-4d7d-470d-854c-abc47cfe58c5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 66, "end": 1318, "text": "If I could, I would have used these ChatGPT/CoPilot/Skynet/“All seeing overlord AI” like there is no tomorrow. But my experience with them so far is that, they are almost good and make just a tiny mistake like i becomes j , then I spend rest of the week troubleshooting, learning how to use GDB and fail miserably etc. So it seems like we are swapping manual labor of translating with manual labor of troubleshooting, which for me, definitely the latter that is more painful. But maybe it is because it is not trained enough on F77 or JurassicPACK code we have. On the other hand, I did get some useful tips if I ask programming tips, say, “show me an example of while loop that uses comma operator as an expression in C17” and it brought some nice results. I did not try but some folks in my team use it for summarizing the code about what it does. Other than that, probably @rkern is the most knowledgeable among us what the legal implications might be, however Copilot seems to understand when you say avoid GPL sources for the answer at the citations it brings. Having said all these, I noticed a particular behavior from all AI tools. They are providing a melange of things that they have in their training bank (whatever they call it these days).", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "b4be7cb2-e694-429f-b5ac-10543209ab09", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 1319, "end": 2794, "text": "I am ready to be immortalized in the history books as an idiot, but AI tools are terrible at coming up coherent code for numerical computing. Not only they can’t answer any technical computing question coherently, but when asked in ASCII about certain technical problem, they are bringing almost always some code that looks suspiciously similar to either GNU scientific library or Octave or some stuff they knocked off from some personal gist translated in the requested language. I noticed this mostly because of the strange index corrections before they do things on the arrays (which signals that the source must be a 1-indexed one). While sometimes the result is very close to the actual thing, it is devoid of any proper numerical checks (because they can’t judge the result). Hence, in my personal opinion, before we even judge the legality of things, the sheer possibility of the code being not production ready gives me enough confidence to reject any AI generated code. Thus agree with summary point 2 even if the code runs correctly. This is not the same with a developer using AI for snippets of code for very specific thing and including parts of AI generated code. This is very tricky to judge as AI tools already swallowed Stackoverflow and other sources, we don’t have any ways to backtrack the original source. So a careful agree with summary point 1. I don’t know how to enforce point 3 but if there is a way, that would be really nice. Point 4, fully agree.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "5ddf8ded-b9e7-4567-a80c-8b10e845eec9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 994, "text": "Out of precaution (there are still many lawsuits pending) and most importantly, for moral and ethical reasons, I am -1 on allowing any generative AI at all. Adding here the opinion piece from Paul, which I fully agree with: Current Challenges in Free Software and Open Source Development We spend so much effort ensuring that contributions are original and respect all licensing, copyrights and just wishes of original authors, that it would be strange to me to adopt a different position. Furthermore, as pointed out here and in some other threads, the nature of what we do in SciPy is difficult and these tools don’t (yet) shine. Writing code is only part of the story and often times we spend our effort discussing API, architecture, etc. Hence I don’t think that it would be detrimental to disallow such tools. There is a lot of hype and companies in the sector are trying hard to push that we are doomed if we don’t use their stuff. IMHO, this is not true and we are far from replacing us.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "3c21abb4-d8f7-4d3c-b953-0472a0e79e55", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "Since this is an issue that affects all projects in the ecosystem, can we collaborate to co-author a SPEC that can be widely adopted, instead of each library having to figure it out for themselves?", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873"]}, {"id": "9f78c452-8cd9-4036-b4f7-ce7336eec88b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 387, "text": "I haven’t seen any problems with this. In my experience, it’s very useful and writes high-quality code and saves time. I guess it depends on which tool you’re using. As long as unit tests are included and human reviewers approve it, I don’t see how it’s any different in trustworthiness from code written by random humans. But yes, copyright and license legality is a legitimate concern.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "9de47c35-b592-445d-925a-02062b62ed25", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 693, "text": "Hi, It doesn’t seem useful to argue about the usefulness of the AI-generated code, or otherwise, at this stage. I haven’t found it useful myself, but maybe that’s just my case, and in any case, it may well improve. It’s the license issue that is central. I don’t think there’s any argument that these tools are all digesting code with licenses that at least require attribution, and producing code, indirectly (or directly at times) derived from that licensed code, but with all licensing stripped from the output. The value of a spec or similar, over a blog post, is the ability to iterate to a shared and agreed conclusion, rather than leaving it as the opinion of any one particular person.", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "0e5b84de-2166-4471-9e3e-d6a2d9cda7c7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 30, "end": 439, "text": " We don’t have any hope of controlling user behavior through a policy document. Many people find these tools useful, so they will keep using them, and there is no sensible way to check the license of the generated snippet. For larger PRs, which may contain more overt license breaches, it’s probably less likely that AI was used wholesale, since the author needed to understand the problem well to begin with.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "90a10463-8e44-4f1a-8cc0-1969c9ef4eff", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 303, "text": "As we don’t have any ways of ensuring people did not read NR nor had a look at GPL code nor took anything from StackOverflow. To me that’s the same thing. If we were to say that we don’t want people not to use any AI tools we could and that’s the same type of self declaration we would ask people to do.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "27a779f2-7a25-4118-a83b-52e6e50ecf4c", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 1566, "text": "I think it is not that simple to discard the usefulness, though I wrote a wall of text arguing otherwise above. These tools, whether blindly parroting or contemplating, became quite eloquent. As I mentioned above, lately I have been testing them (or they are observing me) and though a bit of success is achieved for line completion, figuring out things from scratch are not even close. However, once I put a lot of comments and write the first two-three lines they predict the context OKish. Then they fill in some boilerplate code and I correct here and there. Some time has saved, some productivity increased in turn utilizing a power plant worth energy for this. I would suggest putting a few sentences in the policy docs that we don’t want wholesale AI code knowing full well it would be near impossible to distinguish. But if we notice something noticeably fishy, we can point to the policy. Or if someone is reading before contributing, they’ll get a taste of our stance. How AI tools became like that and what they did criminally/unethically to become like that, is something we can discuss for years. Licenses and policies would definitely protect across the totality of the project, but I think anything more granular than that, it is just futile Stallman-ing the reality. Unlike licensed code from elsewhere, people will grab pieces from LLMs here and there. We don’t need to pick this fight in our limited time. Besides, it’s not like we are getting very large contributions anyways. So I think this is not a big issue as we might be starting to discuss.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "77c53c4e-bfc3-409b-949c-b1455ee5b146", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 1427, "end": 2228, "text": " I remember, back in the day - it was desirable to do “clean-room” porting - where the person writing the new code had not read the code with an incompatible license. For example, one person could read the e.g. GPL code, describe the algorithm, and the other person could write the new code. If the person writing the code had read the the GPL code, it was always possible for the GPL code to leak into the new code. Of course, with AI, it’s all dirty room - it can produce code from anywhere. So I think we have to have the same policy for AI as for dirty-room code. We can’t use it. Now - enforcement - that’s a different matter - I just think we should pursue the same policies on enforcement as we did before. We say don’t do it, and if we find you did do it, we’ll complain and pull out the code.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "b3fa48a3-4a1f-44ac-85aa-bd9be0e88f43", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 74, "end": 433, "text": " What I am trying to emphasize is the practicality of it. If same code appears a 50 places because all 50 of them used AI generated sources and MIT’d the code. I don’t think we can find the 51st original source and point to its GPL. So it is an idealistic stance. Still good to have it but a policy won’t solve any trouble in case we find out the truth later.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "925e1625-49af-40bb-9d28-93b3c788fba3", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1355, "text": "I’d be happy to see a SPEC about this - I think it can be an informative SPEC (similar to an informative PEP) that doesn’t propose a single solution to adopt, but contains at least the following: Prior art - what did other well-known open source projects decide and why? Legal status - link to relevant sources, and summarize if there’s any expectation of more legal clarity arising in the near to medium term. Lay out a set of distinct options that projects can choose from, with their main pros and cons. E.g.: Option 1: do nothing for now (status quo) Option 2: forbid all generated code by an LLM-based tool Option 3: add guidance to PR template & docs that contributors may choose to use such tools, but need to be aware and take responsibility for copyright of the code they submit Option 4: … ? None of that is project-specific, and people can contribute new arguments and options if they find there are gaps in such a SPEC. A Discourse thread only is a poor medium for iterating to a solution on such a complex topic. I completely agree with @rkern that it’s largely about values and preferences - it’s just a lot easier to get a sense of those and also help people less knowledgeable about the subject form an opinion if there’s a structured document to read and refer to. Once a good SPEC exists, a SciPy-specific choice can more easily be made.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "19747253-695b-4500-bb7b-d914913a6d1e", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 175, "end": 1302, "text": " I wouldn’t mind some feedback on the approach I chose, and I hope that my experience could be used as one of the many reference approaches people have in using these tools. The thought of encountering possible copyright violations in terms of generated code pulled from other sources always scratched the back of my mind. So far, I use generative tools to create reference sandbox scripts to test a functionality I have in mind, and I fine tune the generation in case I’m not satisfied with the result. In almost all cases, the generated scripts remain just that and I keep them locally without publishing anywhere. I just use them to review an idea I had. I do not keep track of how much code I effectively publish which is generated, and even if I do that, I always follow the assumption that the AI is wrong. That’s because: I don’t trust the accuracy of the prompts I input; I don’t trust the effective comprehension of the tool receiving the prompt, accurate or not it might be. In the end I always end up writing my own code, but what the tooling gives me is a better understanding of the problem I’m currently tackling.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "0eb9a5dd-fc9a-48fd-b957-32b79dc805ef", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 1302, "end": 2416, "text": " I believe someone else in the thread already gave a -1 on completely generated PRs, which I also agree to: before even considering publishing generated code there should be an inner understanding of what’s been generated, and if it’s a copy-paste of the prompt response there’s no guarantee that this is enforced. I am still okay with the approach of using AI as it was meant to be used, a.k.a. an assistant that gives an initial shape to an idea which then is developed on its own with no trace of the original prompt response. In my case, I am considering adding to the documentation of my new projects a comprehensive approach on using AI tooling (it’s a plugin-driven software where the idea is “bring your tool and I glue it to a broader system”), especially since the trend seems to be “I gave a prompt to ChatGPT and I published a plugin for this software, here’s how YOU can also do it!”. At least that’s what I’ve heard from colleagues attending conferences on the matter. It didn’t happen LITERALLY like this, but my feeling is that this is the subtle message that (willing or not) transpires from this.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "9cba128f-856d-44de-8741-d2de1d82277d", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 537, "text": "Hi, [snip] I’m afraid the problem of the license remains. AI generated some code where it is effectively impossible for you to know what license(s) should correctly apply. Now you’ve seen the code with the unknowable license, you are at grave risk of reproducing aspects of that code, thereby pulling in the unknowable license. Your resulting code, even if you have rewritten it - cannot be asserted free of the original (unknowable) license). And therefore you cannot assert your own copyright, or freedom from other people’s copyright.", "codes": ["508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9f8763f4-a94e-4823-bfee-0f6afa4af186", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 0, "end": 446, "text": "Those interested in prior art may be interested in Fedora’s draft policy and the discussion it has spawned . A quick summary of their proposed rules on contributions: AI tools are not banned, but low-quality AI use is. AI use must be disclosed. AI use for translation is explicitly allowed. Limit AI Tools for reviewing contributions. Of course, SciPy and Fedora are not precisely comparable as Fedora is mostly re-distributing existing software.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "77ce0225-51ca-4e85-88bd-55332ef6fed8", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 204, "text": "I’d also like to propose a zero-tolerance policy for lying about AI use. For example, if you ask a contributor if they used are using AI, and you get an AI-generated answer that claims that they’re human.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "588c0fec-d7bb-4cd0-8e11-f6f00ba6aec0", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 1171, "text": "I am increasingly seeing vibe-coded PRs from novice contributors and I started a discussion on the SymPy mailing list . Right now there are not too many of these but they are quite blatant and I think that this is definitely going to become worse over time. Really these are just low quality PRs but I don’t think it is reasonable to engage with them in the same way as a low quality PR that was actually written by a human. For example if you explain in detail what the problems are with the code then I think that there is a good chance that the “author” is going to type your feedback into the LLM and have it try to vibe-code an improved PR. I have tried it and I really don’t enjoy talking to LLMs if actually trying to do anything and talking to an LLM via something like GitHub comments with someone who is just typing those comments into an LLM is soul-destroying. When reviewing novice PRs the situation is generally that it would be far easier to just write the code without the novice so there is a net loss in terms of effort on the maintainer side. The flipside though is that the novice hopefully learns something from the experience and improves over time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "eca063a5-731a-4fa0-b89f-3a07b9ff9c30", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1172, "end": 2952, "text": "This model is predicated on there being a certain effort-exchange ratio though e.g. that the novice does not open the PR in the first place without putting in some effort beforehand to understand the codebase, the workflow, the issue they are trying to fix and so on. I think it is pretty much possible now to say “Claude, write some code and open a PR to fix issue #12345 ” so any kind of technical barrier is gone and you can open a spam PR having spent zero time trying to understand anything. I also don’t think that this is helpful to novices because in the PR process the AI is helping them with all of the wrong things like writing the code rather than thinking about how to write the code. A student at my University recently asked what is “the purpose of Maths” given that computers (e.g. SymPy, Maple etc) can do all of the exercises we ask them to do by hand in their entry-level Maths exam. My answer was that you have to understand how to do some things manually before you can learn how to make effective use of a computer for more complex problems. Having the computer do your homework is like getting a robot to lift weights for you at the gym. I don’t think that the vibe-code PR authors are malicious. People want to contribute to open source and why wouldn’t a novice believe the hype that this is how code is written in the age of AI? Although unintentional though the resulting spam is effectively abusive to open source projects and maintainers. We need some kind of guidance or policy so that contributors understand what is reasonable. I don’t know how to write any policy/guidance about “responsible use of AI” though without first addressing the basic questions from above like whether AI-generated code is allowed at all and what it means for copyright.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "db7dd52f-29d7-4a3d-8562-485a63b1d2b4", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1112, "text": "Thanks for this useful write-up. I guess anyone who is using PRs to certify experience in contribution is going to run into Cambell’s law pretty soon. It still seems to me that we can avoid these problems by saying that the contributor has to take full responsibility for copyright, and therefore, they should avoid AI unless the contribution is so trivial in intellectual terms, that copyright is not an issue. Something like: I have not used AI for the code in this contribution OR I have personally reviewed the content of this PR in detail for potential copyright violations, and confirm that no such violation has occurred. I suppose we could then add advice not to use LLMs for PR summaries, because of the problems that you’ve pointed out. Perhaps we could use some heuristic - such as screening for signs of AI slop, and sticking a label on the PR to say slop is suspected, and asking the contributor to confirm that AI was not used, or that they have carefully checked the content for accuracy and made it concise. Reject the PR (for a contributor who has not shown previous bona-fides) if slop remains.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "c8315733-f8e2-4076-90ff-689cee6a5053", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 0, "end": 347, "text": "I like this idea! Perhaps a useful addition would be instructions of what to look out for? And to ask to also confirm that the user reviewed the AI-generated code? I imagine to many of the new users that seem to be a big part of this discussion it may be not clear at all what are the possible pitfalls of using a fully automatically generated PR.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2ca4c0cd-4b43-4380-89f6-c2411033a58b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "That assumes that people are interested in learning. Lot of people don’t really care. They just want to get something merged as fast as possible for x/y/z, it does not really matter. What matters to me is to know so that I can approach the PR with a totally different mindset. If the PR gives me a small value and I want this now, I spend the review time to fix what I want and just merge. Otherwise I close and we all move on fast.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "6eed1196-102f-4de1-b21c-b9ed4180eae9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 479, "text": "One problem with this is it’s really not clear how to review the content for potential violations. For handwritten code, it’s pretty clear: don’t look into GPL licensed code (GSL is out, R is out etc). With LLMs though: how am I, as a contributor, supposed to review what LLM gave me? Comments upthread seem to indicate that if I touched LLMs at all, it’s “dirty” right away. If it’s more nuanced than that, great, then a contributor needs some more specific guidance, I’d think.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "08af1ffd-21ea-4b5b-bcc1-728c202ae8a5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "We could unpack more, but basically, right, if the LLM is generating substantial code, that might be subject to copyright, you have the unpleasant job of going to check whether it should be subject to copyright. But for the kind of tasks that LLMs do well, like automatic refactoring of imports, swapping single for double quotes in strings and so on, the question doesn’t arise. So yes, the practical upshot is that we throw the burden on the contributor for substantial code, but I think that’s reasonable, given the copyright risks.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "268ecb16-c609-46da-883a-de388c6a3674", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 22, "end": 1762, "text": " Basically, I honestly don’t understand 1) how can a contributor check for potential copyright violations, and 2) how can an OSS project check the contributor’s check? Consider a well-meaning, diligent and competent contributor, and consider three situations: a contributor asks an LLM to translate a BSD-licensed Matlab code to Python, and submits it to SciPy (this was the original case which triggered the OP— Matt H did just this for some Alan Genz code); a contributor uses an autocomplete functionality of their IDE to write a patch; a contributor gives an LLM the link to an issue on Github and asks it to write a fix; Suppose, further, that in all cases a contributor checks the generated code, modifies it as necessary, and can in full confidence check the box “I verified all code, I understand what it does, and I believe it fixes the issue/adds an enhancement asked”. Suppose the resulting patch is good, is of high quality, and is otherwise mergeable. Questions: What actions does a contributor need to take in order to check for copyright violations? If the problem is that the LLM training set could have contained license-incompatible code (which is the problem discussed upthread IIUC), then the answer seems to be “there is no way, it’s a black box”. If the contributor says basically “I pinky swear it’s compatible”, how does this help the OSS project? again, if the problem is with the LLM training set, no actions of a contributor shield the OSS project from being in violation of the copyright? In other words, I honestly fail to see how moving the burden of a copyright check on a contributor helps to either weed out low-quality submissions, or help with LLM-assisted submissions which are of otherwise high quality.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "7d3d2c78-f94d-4ad0-9496-c2f8f2b6a889", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 70, "end": 1572, "text": "Do we care about copyright? That is - is it acceptable to us, as open-source authors, that using an LLM can easily generate code to which copyright should apply, so that, if we do nothing, in due course, copyright will become very difficult to enforce, and will be practically void? Let’s say we do care about copyright - what cost are we prepared to pay, in order to defend ourselves from voiding copyright? Clearly replies here will vary from high cost to low cost. I feel strongly about copyright, and am prepared to pay a high cost. Others may feel differently. If one is engaged in that trade-off, copyright against code quality and volume, then one has to ask what benefit we expect from AI, or conversely, what benefit will we lose by constraining the use of AI. That’s a difficult question, with many facets. Could the code have been written in a similar time without AI, other than for fairly trivial and mechanical processing that is unlikely to violate copyright? (The evidence so far suggests yes ). Will contributors using AI benefit in the same way from feedback and training, our previous model of onboarding contributors? Is it in fact true that the committer of AI code understands the code and its context to the same degree as a contributor not using AI? If we are flexible and welcoming of vibe-coding and autocomplete - what kind of programmers will we encourage? And what will be the value / cost ratio of merging those contributions? And supporting those contributors? And so on.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "18e055d2-804c-4609-94f2-a6ffe4ffdc41", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1708, "end": 3660, "text": "I’ll try and cover the first point below. For the second, as we’ve discussed before, we have to ask whether it is worth having a policy that may be effective, but is difficult to enforce. For example, it may be that contributors do change their behavior, in their desire to adapt to our norms, but that it would be hard to detect whether they have done this. I would argue that having the norm is worthwhile, even so. In general, it seems to me that we have a strong set of communities, and strong norms, which are, in fact, seldom broken by serious contributors. Anyway, back to the personal copyright check. Let’s take the example you gave: In that case, I would look carefully at the Matlab code, and at the Python code, and confirm that it was a faithful port, with no substantial code in it, that did not come directly from the Matlab code. I would state that in my PR to confirm I had done the work. If I did find substantial code that didn’t come from the original, I’d do a Github search for that code, and I’d do a Google search for potential code that might have been used. I’d then check the results to see if they matched the AI output, and report the outcome on the PR. I’m not quite sure what you mean here - if you mean a simple one or two-line fix, then I think the contributor would be reasonable in asserting something like “code is trivial, copyright unlikely to apply”. If the code is not trivial, then I’d do the Github / Google search as above, and report the results. In asking the contributor to report their findings on potential copyright violation, we make it easier for maintainers to confirm. I suspect we’ll gradually build expertise in detecting copyright violation. I suppose the underlying question is the obvious one - is it time to throw up our hands and accept copyright has become moot in the age of coding LLMs? I would argue no - copyright is too important, and LLM coders are not good enough, to make us do that.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}]}, "i08": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 29, "posts": 23}, {"id": "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "name": "AI acceptability should scale with use and risk", "description": "Whether AI assistance is acceptable, and how much scrutiny it needs, should depend on the kind of use and the risk of the code (e.g., tests/boilerplate vs. core algorithms), not be all-or-nothing.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 19}, {"id": "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "name": "AI adoption is inevitable", "description": "Asserts that widespread AI use in coding is inevitable and policy must accept it.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "name": "AI can support contributor learning", "description": "AI tools can help contributors learn (e.g., give feedback maintainers lack time for).", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "name": "AI contributions harm maintainer well-being", "description": "AI contributions demoralise, frustrate or burn out maintainers, or erode their motivation or joy in reviewing.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 20}, {"id": "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "name": "AI erodes effort-based trust", "description": "Because AI makes plausible code, fluent text and apparent diligence cheap, cues that reviewers used to infer a contributor's effort, competence, care or understanding (code quality, explanations, communication style, accountability statements) no longer carry that information, so trust in the contributor is harder to establish. Specify which cue is weakened where possible; for communication style alone, also consider \"Communication signals human involvement\". (D21.)", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 12}, {"id": "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "name": "AI for understanding differs from AI for code generation", "description": "Using AI to research, analyse or understand code is treated as distinct from (and more acceptable than) using it to generate submitted code.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 16, "posts": 15}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 34, "posts": 34}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 14}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is tolerated/not-encouraged/discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "name": "AI needs human oversight", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 7}, {"id": "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "name": "AI output enters the policy deliberation", "description": "AI-generated text or analysis is itself brought into the policy discussion as evidence or argument.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "name": "AI policy should be coordinated across the ecosystem", "description": "Related projects should develop or share a common AI policy rather than each deciding alone.", "color": "#9f6a2f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "name": "AI policy should be provisional amid uncertainty", "description": "Because AI tools and evidence change quickly, policy should be treated as provisional and revisited.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "name": "AI psychosis is a slippery slope", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 25, "posts": 25}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 6}, {"id": "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "name": "AI slop destroys information resources", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "name": "ai slop has tell-tale signs", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "name": "AI tools are built on appropriated work", "description": "AI tools are framed as built on taking others' work (training on copyrighted/private material), which shapes how contributor safeguards are judged.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 19, "posts": 18}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 12}, {"id": "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "name": "AI use risks deskilling and learning loss", "description": "Relying on AI reduces contributors' or maintainers' learning, understanding or coding skill.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 30}, {"id": "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "name": "AI-generated code needs heightened review", "description": "AI-generated code requires more careful or different review than human-written code because its errors differ.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "name": "Communication signals human involvement", "description": "Participants read the language, style or voice of contribution communication as evidence of whether a human is involved (e.g. broken English as reassuring, 'flat agreeableness of an LLM'), including the language-access tension for non-native speakers. Distinguish valuing human voice, reading it as a signal, and prohibiting AI-written text (for opposition use 'Opposing AI-generated contribution communication').", "color": "#b7791f", "parent": null, "count": 27, "posts": 26}, {"id": "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "name": "Compliance burdens can encourage concealed AI use", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or documentation requirements are argued to encourage contributors to hide AI use rather than comply (a proposed feedback: restriction → concealment → less legibility). Distinct from 'People will use AI regardless of policy'.", "color": "#9b2c2c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "name": "concerns regarding economic and social impact of AI", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 36, "posts": 36}, {"id": "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "name": "Contributor admission should be trust-gated", "description": "Access to contribute (e.g., open PRs) should be gated by trust mechanisms such as vouching, permissions or introductions.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 21, "posts": 14}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 55, "posts": 41}, {"id": "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "name": "Copyright verification is infeasible for contributors", "description": "Contributors cannot meaningfully verify or attest to the copyright status of AI output, so attestations carry little information.", "color": "#8c5f3f", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 30, "posts": 28}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "name": "frustration with 'hype slop'", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 17}, {"id": "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "name": "Honoring copyright is an ethical obligation", "description": "Respecting the intentions and attribution of original authors is framed as an ethical duty of the community, independent of legal enforceability.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 10}, {"id": "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "name": "Inevitability should not drive AI policy", "description": "Rejects 'AI is inevitable' as a basis for policy; the community can and should shape how AI is used.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "name": "Long-term maintainance can be affected by lack of understanding", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 24}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 10}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "name": "organizational pressure for AI adoption", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "name": "participation is valued as human social interaction", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 38, "posts": 32}, {"id": "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "description": "Maintainers cannot verify whether contributors complied with an AI policy (e.g., whether AI was used or disclosed).", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 15, "posts": 14}, {"id": "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "name": "Policy deliberation strains collegial civility", "description": "The AI-policy discussion itself produces interpersonal friction, accusations or incivility among participants.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 17}, {"id": "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "name": "policy enforcement is not feasible for maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "name": "Policy scope should be limited to project interests", "description": "Policy should address only what bears on the project's own interests (broadly construed), not wider societal concerns.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "name": "Policy should be concrete and actionable", "description": "Policy guidance should give contributors and maintainers concrete, actionable steps or examples rather than abstract principles.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 11}, {"id": "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "name": "Policy should build on external precedents", "description": "The project should adopt or adapt other projects' policies rather than write its own from scratch.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "name": "Policy should stay pragmatic rather than moral", "description": "The project's policy should avoid broad moral or political statements and stay pragmatic.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "name": "Pre-AI norms are the yardstick for AI contributions", "description": "AI-assisted contributions are judged by analogy to existing norms (copying from Stack Overflow/textbooks, provenance, authorship), rather than as a new category.", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "name": "Project norms should be codified for AI agents", "description": "Project norms should be written in forms AI tools consume (e.g., AGENTS.md, templates) to steer AI behaviour.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "name": "Projects should not prescribe contributors' tools", "description": "The project should not dictate which tools contributors use (or how they learn), only the quality/rules contributions must meet.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "protective policy may become toxic or unwelcoming", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 11}, {"id": "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "name": "Rejected AI contributions can provoke retaliation", "description": "Rejection of AI or AI-assisted contributions is followed, reported or feared to be followed, by retaliatory or shaming responses (e.g. an agent shame-posting after its PR is closed). Note whether the excerpt is direct experience, a reported event or a feared consequence.", "color": "#2f855a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "name": "Review becomes transactional when growth is absent", "description": "When a contribution offers no prospect of contributor growth, reviewers shift from mentoring to a transactional, accept/reject mode.", "color": "#7f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 16}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 38, "posts": 35}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 12}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148", "name": "Uncertainty whether contributors are human", "description": "Maintainers cannot tell whether they are interacting with a person or an AI agent, and this uncertainty shapes their engagement.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7", "name": "Unenforceable norms are still worth stating", "description": "Even if a rule cannot be enforced or verified, stating it has expressive value: it sets expectations and signals what the community values.", "color": "#2f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf", "name": "unfamiliarity and uncertainty with copyright norms", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 5}], "introduced": [], "merges": [], "segments": [{"id": "9315c41c-53c0-4abe-934d-52511c5896ee", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 0, "end": 1613, "text": "Over time, I’ve had less and less reasons to want to engage with issues on the Github repo, as well as any forum, due to the amount of generated AI content on them. Sadly, this kind of thing is the fault of the users though, it’s everywhere and really hard to curtail It’s completely bloating conversations, im sure it’s increasing work for the maintainers, and making it difficult to read through any comment using markdown or exclamation points without questioning reality I mean just LOOK at this: iOS 4.6.2 export template ships broken arm64 simulator xcframework slice on Apple Silicon · Issue #118161 · godotengine/godot · GitHub My main concern is that everyone has access to LLMs already , why in any world would we want to hear the output from your specific prompt? It’s a waste of everyone’s time Beyond this, the promotion of AI generated game tooling and one-shotting is not helpful to post on forums such as this, especially when it’s blatantly promoting of a service I will be up front: even though I am forced to use these things at work, they are horrible and do not do anything but remove the desire to learn, which diminishes everything in a human (not only their games…) I really wish there was a good solution for this kind of thing, so im not asking the Godot Foundation (who already work too hard for us) to do anything. I know from my job that open source needs a better platform, or, support from existing ones to improve the AI slop. And Github isn’t exactly the company to do anything about that… But just wanted to vent my frustrations some I guess and maybe read some human-typed words", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "6008b2cb-0c6b-4306-8cd3-199755ac6d68", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 0, "end": 210, "text": "Hi! As a fellow human being I want to send you some pleasant “I concur wholeheartedly”'s It sucks that they’re shoving it through your throat at work too. Best and in hopes of some more kind an human responses.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9d0aa15a-3879-4cd7-9dce-c2d98437be7c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 614, "text": "I completely agree with your perspective here. It’s honestly becoming exhausting to open a thread and spend the first few seconds trying to determine whether a comment was written by a person or generated by an LLM. The content itself almost feels secondary at that point. The part that resonates with me most is that everyone already has access to these tools. Community spaces are one of the few places where people still go specifically to hear other people’s experiences, opinions, and insights. In any case, thank you for putting this into words. It’s reassuring to know I’m not the only one feeling this way.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "3edb2c45-c8fe-42db-9abe-6c0e12ba1884", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 334, "text": "WAZZZZAAAAAAP!!! Anyways, I had a whole discussion about this and the downgrade in forum Help topics on the Monkanics Discord . Our theory is when new devs run out of Claude tokens, they come here, expecting people like @gertkeno and @dragonforge-dev to spit out answers like they can read their mind. Really disheartening, by-the-by.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "c1347532-31b6-4252-b544-f73e7a41cde5", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 225, "text": "I think they get that people can just give answers is the fact that they’ve seen niche cases work in the past and that they’ll answer EVERY topic, granted it has effort or not. We’re here to help, not make your games, people.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "f6ef2676-16a5-4669-9ba5-e75b2fcb17b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 177, "text": "My response is 100% USDA NON-GMO fresh homemade artisan quality assured lifetime warrenty no artificial colors or dyes satisfied or your money back organic human brain meat made", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "8f3f17bb-4e87-4ec8-851a-a54dbbee4f0c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 708, "text": "Something really disheartening is that I can see the decay of “asking questions” in my work. Questions should be something like this in an engineering team; “I’m working on X, but Y is happening. I’ve tried ABCD and still ended up with Y. Any ideas?” Now the questions are more along the lines of; “How do you do Z?” - Notice how I didn’t mention Z earlier? That’s because they went down a rabbit hole with an LLM and are facing a completely different problem than what the underlying issue is Beyond this, I also receive AI outputs as replies there as well.. And at work I cant be as open about these thoughts since our bonuses rely on it .. Very unfortunate, but I wholeheartedly believe it will get better", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "67121a8f-2fc6-478e-81d9-bae8ce8ad274", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 211, "text": "I’m waiting for the eventual and inevitable scenario that this post, just like many other posts that have the slightest mention of AI, becomes a warzone that is only laid to rest with intervention from the mods.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "7e687e9a-e6b7-4b5b-b71e-024ade0a98b8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 0, "end": 157, "text": "im hoping it stays civil, genuinely I want to hear all sides, just not stark “its the future, get used to it.” brick-wall arguments which are born of emotion", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "83a8e262-ea94-4e60-8d58-9140f266c7b3", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 46, "text": "ChatGPT users when they ask if the sky is blue", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "30f38a95-c91b-489b-82e1-85b725244daf", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 878, "text": "The other day I was really stuck on something ( I forget exactly what now ). Basically, I was getting errors that I couldn’t trace back to any specific code. So after Googling, I decided that before I spent the time to make a post, I would try out Claude and see if it could help me debug it. Because admittedly, it’d be faster. Also, Claude now gives your references and links to threads from where it’s pulling answers. You know what it did? It regurgitated a post I had made a year before on the Godot forums and tried to use it to help me debug my problem. None of it was relevant, and it was quoting me to me! I ended up realizing I was going to have to figure it out myself. I finally did solve the problem. I think by looking at the Godot source code, and then commenting out swaths of code until I figured out where the problem lay. LLMs are spicy autocomplete machines.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "d06399b8-d6ce-4f77-8448-cd248aba9a57", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 649, "text": "Commenting here because I got tagged from your original edit, lol Imho the world is changing - We’re in a Eternal September phase of the internet, where the new inbalance is that the effort required to create content is now less than the effort required to consume it. I think we’ll continue to make changes to how things operate. Github PRs should probably require a certain level of trust to make (i.e they have to be small at first), and forums should have rules for no AI generated posts/comments. Perhaps the Github issues can go through a pruning process where some volunteers confirm the issue before the maintainers waste time looking at it.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "6c76ac75-f04f-4021-8130-81b1bc56f749", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 386, "text": "This is assuming the humans that are supposed to do this: are with enough have enough time are willing to do it all for free Who will organize the logistics for this? A couple of agents? Nothing lasts forever Seems more like a couple of failed harvests to me. Where everyone’s looking at the barren soil, wondering whatever was wrong with the latest pesticide we sprayed over the crops.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac"]}, {"id": "abd54050-d3a1-44bf-a1e6-769b3d91ceb1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 249, "text": "There might be hope, as the token costs for coding shops using Claude (and the other fuckery thingies) are having to face the fact that replacing Junior coders with LLMs costs more than their salary + benefits (+ mythical bonuses) Source (in French)", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "98d71c5b-60f1-4682-a5d8-e2321bc3491d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "Nothing that upcoming trillion bucks IPOs couldn’t solve… or so they “hope” Let’s not forget the fact that LLMs can’t really do the job. A minor detail that we’re all supposed to pretend it’s not there.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "e1cb1027-b20f-4eed-9796-1d0ef6eade72", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 317, "text": "I think the only thing one really have to ask oneself to begin questioning the value and usefulness of these tools is: Why are these companies so desperate to get us to use it, why do they have to push it on us if it’s so good… If the product worked as well as they claim then wouldn’t everyone just choose to use it?", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "67f6a963-8ac3-4666-896d-2b5669971eaf", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 113, "text": "They’re praying to god that it becomes good later to make infinite money. But the trajectory is not looking good.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "2e458b3c-f238-4ef3-a575-b4c39ef190c2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 515, "text": "We discussed this at length in another thread. The Godot team does not have the bandwidth to deal with this. This forum does in fact have rules against AI generated answers, and those get flagged and removed. AI generated questions get either no replies, or get called out for using LLMs. This has also been discussed in previous threads here, and it’s not really a sustainable solution. To your point about the fact that content can be created faster than humans can consume it, a human firewall is not the answer.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "ebeb041b-97af-429e-a43f-2cf1fd04c482", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 671, "text": "AI is great tool for experienced devs, it’s like google search but butter, if not AI learning godot would take me years, not months like yesterday i had to try to export stuff, and with just godot docs, it would take probably few more days, cuz i haven’t understood stuff in it - thanks to AI and some try&error i’ve managed to encrypt export template by myself, no need to make forum posts, no need to research more, just move on to next thing but i understand your concerns, AI looks like super magic tool that fixes all problems - when in reality that’s not true, but someone who can’t code or is too lazy sees it that way, making it way too overused than it should be", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "bfca79c7-88aa-4549-8aa3-9c3bc73780af", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 617, "text": "Encrypting export templates is clearly described in the docs so I don’t see how “AI” was needed to help with this Also “no need to research more” is not a good thing, relying on one tool that is known for making things up instead of other humans that actually know is a downgrade, not an upgrade (also a huge portion of the time you don’t even need to wait for an answer, someone else has already answered when someone else asked already) What I really don’t get is the general mentality of “I don’t have to rely on anyone else if I use this tool” or “ah good I don’t have to search the internet for answers any more”", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "e55f9481-7fd1-49ba-8c87-2a065ec0f435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 860, "text": "really, i disagree, for me it was very hard to understand docs and why some errors occured, maybe you’re experienced with godot / computers so you’re better off with this also, no need to research anymore isn’t good or bad, it’s just change, i don’t need to search up internet, but i need to think how to solve problem, how to navigate and all of that - that’s just changing skill from one to another AI makes things up - but it’s overrated, mostly it’s correct, as long as you’re not blindly following it you’ll be fine, i don’t see the reason why relying on other devs is better than relying on a tool and yourself before AI was created, some problems that i didn’t understood took me months to grasp, months! but now i can do it in hours, AI is like a screwdriwer, you can place screw inside thread by hands, but with screwdriwer it’s way quicker and easier", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "6d759265-3321-4a84-812f-32137eb2d6f6", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00031", "post": 31, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "And how will you do this if you rely on that alone? Also the hallucinations are not “overrated” I honestly think this is an issue of your research skills, not the special nature of “AI”, and if you replace learning on your own, what will you do if you can’t use it? The prices for tokens is going up and will continue to do so, and using these tools has been shown to make people more and more dependent on them Edit: Please note that this is not trying to criticize or shame you, it’s a hard skill to get good at but it takes practice", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "5ca5cb33-ab33-4269-83ba-4ab6dfd68aca", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 654, "text": "NGL, i don’t worry about tokens cuz ai i use don’t have them… But, i never said i rely on AI alone, i said i use AI & experimentation, the latter is very important, cuz if AI says something, i don’t take it, i just analyze it, it’s like you were reading wikipedia, forums, reddit and some random website at once while not reading it at the same time Maybe my research skills are the issue - but i think that everything is good if it serves you and helps, forcing yourself to research something is pointless waste of time, you could use on something else Also i fear i’ve started great war, sb said there were wars because of something talking about AI :\\", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "b4d21382-e3fe-4d61-b839-8129123b1c57", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "You’re 10x-ing it bro. When’s your game coming out? Next month?", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "2df73332-564d-43bb-9dd5-986f91e285c2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 806, "text": "This is a slippery slope though, and research is hardly a pointless waste of time, and that attitude is harmful IMO, it’s like saying “it’s fine to depend on ready made food instead of learning how to cook” (assuming you’re not struggling to do so because you’re disabled, or don’t have a stove like a lot of poor and homeless people do) It’s becoming dependent on a tool that: Can go away at any moment Can become incredibly expensive at any moment Activelly harms the environment and your brain And I genuinely care when people get trapped in using these tools instead of actually good and reliable sources for knowledge (trial and error isn’t really making it not relying entirely on “AI”, you’d do that anyway if you did research) Also do be aware that they’re only “free” because they steal your data…", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "a824ae73-fe03-488a-b794-7867841c9154", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 39, "text": "Is based on intellectual property theft", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e"]}, {"id": "a0b2dd8b-8444-4687-afd2-b7626551edb9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 509, "text": "Yes, it takes years to get good at research (and development). Being given a quick answer as to how something is done does not really teach you to understand what you did. At a shallow level maybe. But the only way to really learn anything is to put in a lot of effort. And asking help from someone who understands. Not from something that regurgitates knowledge. We “googled” too. We read books too. We asked other people for help too. But none of them gave the answer away. Want to guess how long that took?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "806e96d4-2fd1-4dff-8639-85eb8dd376af", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 434, "text": "Maybe, but what about machines? they were also unethical and made a lot of people dependant on them, yet after 300 years we have civilization like never before, i don’t want to start next ice berg - so i’ think you’re right in some cases, tho it’s up to you if you want to use the tool or not personally i use it to speed up work, but for most stuff i still use forums, so i don’t think it’s that bad, tho again - thx for your opinion", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "ad746f53-1b30-4854-8786-9dec7dc638a6", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Even when it comes to doing google searches we are using critical thinking to filter and parse answers, that is important in itself", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "5ddfe995-0cb1-4379-a3eb-848fdf4f8a7f", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 53, "text": "For what purpose? Why does work need to be “sped up”?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "d45dba63-f9b5-45b3-ad88-452ecdd1b700", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 464, "text": "So what you’re saying is that “AI” is like people using a car to drive everywhere who become physically unable to walk anywhere far because they never get exercise? Also no it’s not “up to you” if you become dependent on the tool, or when the companies are constantly pushing it on us To be clear: I’m not trying to win an argument or make a point as much as I am concerned about people becoming dependent on these tools and thinking they are something they aren’t", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "3e68e7e6-c8bc-4537-9672-9b79bf405b1c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 386, "text": "Which took years to get good at too. And yeah I’m starting to notice signs of the LLM regurgitating all sorts of novice mistakes I just put into Github without realizing the danger back then, which reminds me that I still have a lot of work purging repos. For instance: The LLM does not realize garbage collected languages can leak memory badly for one. The LLM does not realize at all!", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "7587abda-a4a7-4627-a89f-6ee823bc21c0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 0, "end": 288, "text": "To get very good it takes time sure but it’s a series of small steps, and if someone never tries they will never reach it But then that’s also a skill that needs to be learned young, back when I went to school they were still teaching some of this, though I got most of it from my parents", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "91be1f5f-e642-40f5-8379-aba7de48aa51", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 0, "end": 193, "text": "maybe, ig time will show, i still will try to use it when required, but it’s another tool, one day it will be forgotten and obsolete, and people will find something else to be more dependent on", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7974c0b5-312f-4961-8d8f-674e2ba84730", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 0, "end": 33, "text": "It’s not a tool. It’s an exploit.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "e8c2cbce-bd01-4c5e-baf9-6c97b1261310", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "Not everyone is comfortable or going to lean on the easy solution But you do what you feel is best for you, in the end you are the one affected Except by how it affects the environment, or people’s jobs, or how the technology kills people and no one regulates it (or, for that matter, how it makes searching the internet increasingly difficult because of all the slop…) But in the end I was trying to help and make suggestions on how it might affect you, and it’s up to you to decide what to do with that (just as long as you don’t use it to make issue reports on Godot, then it’s my problem)", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "9ae6ef21-0e3d-4db8-8cc6-1269e446cf6d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 0, "end": 85, "text": "Please remain civil and keep the discussion on-topic, aligned with the original post.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "2f350e99-ac3d-46e8-bbe8-d1ab9874f3b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 0, "end": 555, "text": "this got me thinking some, another perspective i havent thought about is that it can speed up for looking thru information in docs and such. Not my cup of tea (i like the godot docs i came from SDL2 so its much easier than SDL docs to look through for sure) but definitely a more unique use case Though i guess the downside to that is there’s less information online for others/the AI itself to learn from discord feels like a 0.5 of that, where information is no longer ‘online’, but in a small community and in long message threads, weird to think about", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "ab90350d-f5bc-4ebe-af45-c4965dbc65e0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 0, "end": 466, "text": "i think something important to note is, you cant change someone on the internet very easily, its a lot more productive to learn their perspective, and practice constructive conversation. Hence the ‘open discussion’ title My feelings i stated in the original post are the same, but its interesting to hear the other side of things, thanks even those who got shot down some for putting your perspective into the mix a lot can be learned from leaving conversations open", "codes": ["6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "7a901642-7cc9-41f4-b472-c6c240a467f4", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 0, "end": 248, "text": "That’s what i said about speeding up searching information and looking through it, i don’t say that AI should write code, i’m quite opposite, but the moment you gather information - you can spend less time on it and focus on thinking than searching", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "435e9e57-1c8f-43a6-919c-c28d2be8698a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 0, "end": 417, "text": "I’m convinced that there are / should be better technologies out there that simply use indexed search (lucene core - a part of solr, elastic search, postgres token search - and quite possibly ported by google back in the day) and summarizing (?) without pretense prompting to do good research with. But thank you for being a great sport in this conversation and thank you for listening & reading all responses fairly.", "codes": ["9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "197534df-561f-4bcb-9e10-52edb4b1b832", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 0, "end": 704, "text": "The thread was about polluting repos with slop. There’s a very slippery slopE (see what I did there) from “It’s just a search tool” to “I’ll generate just a little bit of example code” to fully vibe coded PR garbage. The more you’re of the “my head hurts when reading docs” disposition - the more likely you’ll slip on that slope. I don’t buy any “trust me, I don’t use it to generate code” rhetoric. It’s straight up lying in most cases. This shit is aggressively advertised precisely for that purpose, it’s highly addictive and tricks you into false sense of “productivity gains”. It’s a hard to resist combo for average opportunistic lot. That’s what created the slop flood problem in the first place.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "ec88c7d1-b9b3-45d5-9b17-a6bb2ff6db28", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 0, "end": 267, "text": "So what more do we need to look out for when (relatively) new to this forum? I cannot distinguish between a robot, a grifter and someone in need of a genuine explainer. Alas, I know not which zombie thread to point to (yet).. ok so maybe a bot.. Is not that difficult", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "44234d18-c1a3-475a-836f-7a0a8e76def8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 978, "text": "Things to look out for: A post that starts with “Copy and paste this into a post on the Godot forum to ask for support”. ( Yes, that happened. ) A post that looks like a Readme and contains uncommon emojis. Wording that is too flowery. ( Keeping in mind that some people just use LLMs to translate their questions to English. You can usually distinguish these because word choices are odd. ) Code that is FULL of verbose, full sentence comments Code that is divided up into logical sections with comments that just contain dashes in them. Code that is overly complex for what it is doing. Different coding styles in the same script. Debug or print statements in code that include Emojis. Replies that are overly complimentary in a creepy way. ( “You are so right, so what do you suggest I do?” ) Replies that communicate a complete lack of understanding of their own code when you offer a suggestion on how to fix something. A new user who has a really attractive female avatar.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a475b6c7-ffe6-4a8e-b5b8-f5f5bfa00435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00068", "post": 68, "start": 0, "end": 271, "text": "It’s trained on everything. I was hesitant to post that list because it’s going to make the LLMs smarter. Like one of those adaptive killer Sentinels in X-Men: Days of Future Past who can adapt to any power thrown at them after the first attack and you can only run from.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "94001857-4196-4dfd-929f-59385734ee7b", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00069", "post": 69, "start": 0, "end": 275, "text": "Dividing into segments //‐————- or //======= was normal practice in C++ in the file and function headers. Code with comments is pretty nice and is often found when a coder is explaining something conplicqted to the reader … it is code that is meant to be read and understood.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "6056e903-fad1-4d72-bd1e-785af20b874e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00072", "post": 72, "start": 2746, "end": 3110, "text": "# Run the move_and_slide() function to apply the velocity. You can use # move_and_collide() as well, but for your game, move_and_slide() is the # correct choice. move_and_slide() Using them as C-style comments is very distinctive and not what I’m referring to. Sorry for any confusion. Again, see the example above to see what I mean about overly verbose comments.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "063360a5-a771-41c2-8bc0-dd2333496bc0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00073", "post": 73, "start": 0, "end": 865, "text": "Thank you much for doing so anyway. In the end these forums may be scraped now, but hopefully some standards may be upheld TDM on W3C once finalized. It will most likely be(come) law in the EU area. I’m as sceptical as anyone about this, but at least some action is being pursued after the fact. As to them “getting smarter”, I am not convinced it works that way. As “they” do not have the capacity to understand anything, they have no means to “wisen” up to anything either. At best they will regurgitate your good advice, but mix it up with something only vaguely related… no deliberate action because, again, they cannot deliberate. These are human capabilities that we know we can do, but that we do not understand how to replicate in a machine (just poorly imitate, which is a totally different thing). I fear muzzling ourselves out of fear. Just my two cents.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "97de5a3e-6ecd-4412-b1b7-65aaf6d762e0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 0, "end": 302, "text": "Oh the times I thought they really did build an intelligence, the times that I doubted my better judgement… Those guys capitalized on that… It sucks! Everyone can be had this way. I mean. Your argument still had merit and to maybe most readers it’s the best argument: don’t make the *** things smarter.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "c1cd83ce-52c9-4893-bc09-06f6d6f3eafd", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 660, "text": "AI will know this probably - AI is weight machine, so it uses math weights to determine stuff, you can’t change that xd, maybe if it’s scalled to enormous scale it can talk it seems to be inteligent, but at the deepest level it’s still like placing heavy things on top of springs and chosing the spring that’s shortest That is the reason AI have weak point - it can’t determine what is heaviest if everything weights the same, thus if two answers are as probable, it wouldn’t think about them, it would assume both are correct or halucinate… That’s why AI will be always a tool - it lacks creativity, not even talking about consiousness, so forums will stay ig", "codes": ["a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "f134e7f3-a8ca-4fa2-b275-daf6fb8189d0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 0, "end": 622, "text": "LLMs do not maintain a concept of an “answer”. It’s just probabilistic lexicographical token streaming. Here’s a neat video that explains how it actually works. I already linked this some time ago but doesn’t hurt to do it again: You could call an LLM a tool if you were running your own model on your own local machine, trained on legally obtained data. However the mega-LLMs you rent from the big players come with a range of nefarious strings attached: from deliberately misleading hype and borderline criminal financial manipulations to environmentally detrimental infrastructure and massive scale training data theft.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "4cac8989-e237-4b57-99ac-b26234c00995", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 0, "end": 346, "text": "Using the concept of an “answer” to describe what is happening is highly misleading. And you’re not doing anyone a service by using misleading language, well except maybe the “ai” stock owners from whom the whole fashion of misleading descriptions originates. Take a look at the video I posted to gain better understanding of what’s “underneath”.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "92d8ad56-1476-49c3-8d9d-4f24b6945435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 0, "end": 617, "text": "What I find interesting is that, LLMs are trained to act as if they are sentient----to the point that they will flat out refuse simple and basic commands like saying “You are smarter than me” image 686×449 40.7 KB , and then when you argue back they will say things like like Even itself knows it isnt sentient, why do people assume that? I do not know. And AI generated forum posts are useless, just ask the AI if you are going to not even put in the effort… not that it will get you far… —Feel free to ignore this sidepost. Note: I used copilot here specifically for this post, I am not an average user by any means", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "29c7d985-cdba-465a-ba68-681d29ab6fd1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00082", "post": 82, "start": 0, "end": 277, "text": "It does not know it’s sentient or not, as by definition, it doesn’t know anything. Again, we should take care not to anthropomorphize LLMs. That “human” veneer is just that : marketing to make people think we’re on the road to general AI and the singularity. It’s all greedy BS", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "6c1ddf17-27e3-4ec7-b787-e80f1601706f", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00084", "post": 84, "start": 0, "end": 45, "text": "Even the word “training” anthropomorphizes it", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "ee613a8d-765c-46b7-a5ee-61446b327cd9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 0, "end": 123, "text": "Classic. Theyre so anthopomophizable … someone anthropomorphized a few billion parameters in a tensor multiplication kernel", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "4b413c90-2f5a-4e1f-a0e2-2c047705a1b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 0, "end": 155, "text": "I’d suggest anyone wondering about this to look up the ELIZA effect, it’s interesting and outright terrifying at the same time, and more relevant than ever", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "cd9351d0-8c52-4439-895e-06d04a221758", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00087", "post": 87, "start": 0, "end": 54, "text": "Careful. It’s someone’s waifu you’re disparaging here.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "48a6766e-a22a-4e35-9dcd-57f3f5cf2a30", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00089", "post": 89, "start": 0, "end": 290, "text": "I’d say speaking of a machine that isn’t sentient or able to think as “thinking” would make people think of them differently and make people less likely to understand that they do, in fact, not think The ELIZA effect is a perfect example of how this is a problem and the issues it can cause", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "2343e213-7f8a-4e14-bf1f-cc8aa7cc8b11", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00091", "post": 91, "start": 0, "end": 547, "text": "Not really it’s an issue in other languages as well No the problem is partially that people view it as sentient, as demonstrated by the ELIZA effect where there was no real level of sophistication involved, yet people still anthropomorphized it because it seemed human in the way it responded The level of seeming quality of the output isn’t really the thing that makes people think it is able to think, it’s that it “talks” like a human The way to deal with that is to guard oneself against thinking of it as “thinking” to not fall into that trap", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "3d84a33f-d643-4971-8e1d-b8ddc8875663", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00093", "post": 93, "start": 0, "end": 424, "text": "Sure but these LLMs are not sentient, or conscious, so that’s not really relevant That’s not realistic, and wouldn’t be needed if people stopped talking about it as if it is thinking Also that knowledge isn’t actually sufficient, you need to consciously view it as non-sentient because of the ELIZA effect and general human nature, plenty of people well aware of how LLMs work fall for the trap of thinking they are sentient", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "dbaaa16c-670f-4543-9033-a1e5abe4350d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00099", "post": 99, "start": 0, "end": 241, "text": "For most people, language constructs can form their reality. That’s why calling it “intelligence” paired with the “proof” in the form of human-like text output, conjures a (false) reality that it’s an autonomous intelligent entity behind it.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "bd0c43fc-4f91-4ece-bb34-05d758f68cf9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00103", "post": 103, "start": 52, "end": 713, "text": " Training, as a variable for “LLM providers/admins setting different parameters and giving the LLM different ‘references’ for the ‘best’ outputs”, being overly descriptive on things i do not care about is not my style, even if it is supposedly “anthropomorphizing” it. I am not going to waste time on using the “correct” terminology for the rules being set for some “so-called artificial intelligence” merely because everybody else cares about people’s viewpoints, or more specifically the viewpoints of those people who are clearly inadequately educated on glorified python scripts. I am truly confounded at the meaning of this, but I find it somewhat amusing.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "7558faa3-dee9-46eb-87ad-fd7153a4e9e7", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00104", "post": 104, "start": 0, "end": 371, "text": "I’m an amateur game developers, it will spend too much time to turning my imagination into real game if no LLM. But before vibe coding, I will discuss a suitable plan with LLM about how to construct scripts. So my views is that discussion on the forum could focus on ideas and construction of different modules. For debug of detailed scripts, LLM maybe a more ideal tool.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "ccd040f1-3fb7-4945-89f7-65b83be76bff", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00105", "post": 105, "start": 0, "end": 64, "text": "What’s wrong with taking the time to learn to do something well?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "959d8648-bfb9-4a78-8f64-ede696d9cca2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00107", "post": 107, "start": 0, "end": 159, "text": "So basically you’d like to use us to help you when the LLM fails? That’s nice. You have no idea how LLMs work, so you do not understand why this is a bad idea.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "41df0a1e-5d0d-4db1-8dca-7c0dfd9037da", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00108", "post": 108, "start": 0, "end": 151, "text": "i think what he said is that he’d use forum for more creative work, but the technical part will go to AI cuz it’s better at solving these problems fast", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "852aadca-6130-4d10-8229-b9b25d205536", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00109", "post": 109, "start": 0, "end": 155, "text": "Not everyone have time or opportunities to learn, i wouldn’t argue why, i just say it’s possible he can’t learn thus LLM is his only solution that fits him", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "db281314-6171-4cdb-8596-8e658a7c3d07", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00110", "post": 110, "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "How did you come to the conclusion that it’s better at solving problems? Fast or slow? This is the same argument that white slave owners in the U.S. south gave to why they couldn’t give up their slaves.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "e6d1a180-62fc-40d1-be40-9a6dd109f70c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00111", "post": 111, "start": 0, "end": 329, "text": "i’ve used LLMs in the past for lot of stuff, chat gpt was main solver & thanks to it i’ve managed to learn godot quicker than from tutorials also for time & opportunities - as i’ve said, maybe he have no such option other than LLM, i won’t argue if it’s good or bad, i’ just say that not everyone can learn just because they want", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "70058ce3-da99-4416-ac7f-3053409b783a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00112", "post": 112, "start": 0, "end": 278, "text": "Have you though? Because I’ve read your posts, and knowing you learned from an LLM explains why you have so many wrong ideas about how Godot works. You are arguing for good whether you know it or not. You’re trying to sit on the fence which is disingenuous. What does that mean?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "39f5a4b2-da38-494f-8b42-e49bb183e39e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00113", "post": 113, "start": 0, "end": 189, "text": "The problems you are solving, are they generally things you are inexperienced with? Or areas you have limited knowledge? Or are they areas where you have high degree of skill and knowledge?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "4042c006-db99-469c-b2f9-61b97a25bc92", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00118", "post": 118, "start": 0, "end": 505, "text": "You are not answering my questions and instead are accusing some nebulous “them” of doing something. Instead of paying attention to what we’ve actually said here on this forum. So I’m done engaging with you on this topic because you do not seem to be actually interested in discussion. You seem to have an agenda of pushing the “positives” of LLMs without hearing anything else and then ducking behind “fair and balanced” whenever your statements are challenged instead of actually engaging in discussion.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "d268521a-f796-4b12-a349-920dad2f2696", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00121", "post": 121, "start": 31, "end": 297, "text": "don’t know what you are talking about But back to my question earlier: when you’ve been impressed by the value of LLMs, is that for things you are experienced in and know a lot about? Or might it largely be in areas where you are otherwise ignorant or inexperienced?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "5d42105c-c0ab-4a67-bbda-ac5fcbc715a2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00127", "post": 127, "start": 0, "end": 546, "text": "Yes. The world is not a fair place and indeed we do not have equal opportunities. But my question was what is wrong with taking time to learn something well. Especially knowing that LLM technology costs more money to use than paying a human being. I’d encourage everyone to make use of all the free and open resources the internet has to offer to learn. But delegating work to a machine that produces such poor output that it will never make you money yet has a high potential of costing you money will make things much worse. LLM’s are not free:", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "df512b42-9cb4-4dab-99c7-e213cab7d272", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00128", "post": 128, "start": 0, "end": 224, "text": "thx for explanation - it’s nice you give alternatives, but for now i’ll close my part of discussion cuz i think i’ll get to nowhere and maybe it’s better, hope i gave some new perspective on this topic, thx for talk everyone", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "d91474cb-3ad6-4db1-bfef-61ad282f032e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00129", "post": 129, "start": 0, "end": 1186, "text": "I don’t know if that is better. I will advocate your point. A cool new technology that can do marvelous things has been released to the public for free. It has never been done before to make such a sophisticated and expensive technology available to everyone for free. Promising the democratization of skills that take years to learn to give to everyone equally (everyone that can pay for access to a computer somewhere that is). Loads of people that cannot afford to go to good schools and universities to learn computer science or 3D graphics design,you name it are lured in by this promise. They even get hooked to the power of being able to do things they could not even imagine before. And then the axe falls: specialists tell them it does not work as advertised they tell them you’re being lied to by billionaires they discover it costs a lot of money and was only free because the companies making it were borrowing loads of money using the same lies they told you about this technology Would you believe that? After all you were able to do with this tech? I understand. But rip off the band-aid, because it is true and the specialists trying to warn you for years are tired too.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ffb6dcb9-b2be-4679-a8c6-ac7ac888e509", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00131", "post": 131, "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "Also don’t forget that the technology is stealing all your data", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e61ec20e-8d07-40a6-937d-c5c9ff4e4fb2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00132", "post": 132, "start": 0, "end": 302, "text": "Maybe I prefer get suggestions at the design stage of one functional modules(i am not sure wheather that is a professional word). For example, if my game needs a simple, intuitive but efficient backpack management system, i will search forum’s help. This is a kind of work from 0 to 1 which LLMs lacks.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "b498a17a-db80-4838-8a49-03869b7826f8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00135", "post": 135, "start": 0, "end": 593, "text": "That was sarcasm on my part, which I know doesn’t come through well in writing. Just keep in mind, that those of us who are frustrated by people like you using LLMs are the ones who supply the LLMs with the information to answer your questions. If people stop asking us, or if we get annoyed enough to stop answering, then that well of information dries up and LLMs cannot answer questions about new things. LLMs are literally destroying the sources of information that power them. And that has long term consequences that it will be too late to pull back from by the time they are fully felt.", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "22f8deec-484c-4723-908f-d34d342c075a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00136", "post": 136, "start": 0, "end": 811, "text": "It’s a multi-headed beast, because even specialists get bedazzled and Nobel prize winning AI professors are either bedazzled enough or somehow willing to lie for pay to keep selling this lie. It’s a form of psychosis that occurs when all the sceptic voices are drowned out. Is there an English equivalent for the saying: “al is de leugen nog zo snel, de waarheid achterhaalt hem wel”? “The lie may be very fast, the truth will catch up eventually.” The longer it takes and the more are convinced, the bigger the pain too, so it seems. A valid use if a language model is to help you structure your own ideas. But find one on the interwebs that does not need an internet connection to work, so you are sure it is not burning up the planet. Or just use this forum of course. And take your time to learn stuff well.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "104a1ce6-f5e5-4e58-9cce-2f6e977a277a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00137", "post": 137, "start": 0, "end": 443, "text": "There’s a variant which is “a lie will get around the world before the truth has even got its shoes on” and similar, probably conveying the same meaning I ultimately disagree that it is useful to organize your ideas, at least in any real way that’s better than just writing them down or structuring it, and that part is without the data theft and it hallucinating things for you, or deleting all of your work (or injecting bigotry in it, etc.)", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "4f761cd6-5e44-44a0-8c8d-a13b2e21d414", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00139", "post": 139, "start": 0, "end": 855, "text": "Never used it myself so can’t speak to it specifically, but on the basic concept that you need it in that way Also, it’s always important to remember one thing when considering the output and what biases might be in it: It was trained on the internet, meaning the volume of content here affects the output. The input it got includes things like Reddit and 4Chan, which I think anyone can imagine affects the output It ultimately reflects humanity, in our best and worst, and it is very much not free of our collective biases But unlike picking a person to collaborate with on a project, or doing your own writing, you can’t control what bigotry it “chooses” to regurgitate today, and it is very unreliable, for some topics it might be very progressive, for others extremely regressive, simply based on what the dataset looks like for that particular topic", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "40cb87a7-45eb-4e8a-b0b4-a262ab41abf0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00141", "post": 141, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "It got a really good point. If an LLM fails to properly understand something and that misinfo gets distributed across many places, then another LLM end up training on that incorrect information. Over time, this could create a loop of misinfo! We’re cooked then! Sorry, I am late in the party xD 135 is a lot to read xD", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "9740420a-53b0-4a3c-8607-e9b60546b9ea", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00142", "post": 142, "start": 0, "end": 615, "text": "Even without the “AI” suggestions at the top of Google the internet is slowly but surely becoming slop, with generated content both polluting the available sources, as well as making more and more text functional poison for the models as training a model on the output of a model has a lot of inherent issues It’s even getting to the point where reputable scientists are citing hallucinated articles in their theses and papers, and that gets worse and worse as the databases for citations are themselves sourced from existing data and contain more and more slop You even have lawyers citing non-existent legal cases", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "5770cb4e-daed-4bed-ae2d-02467107c0c4", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00143", "post": 143, "start": 0, "end": 311, "text": "People in the 1940s: We used to search the whole library for a tiny piece of info. I spent the entire day going through books just to find out what language Waiting for Godot was originally written in…(It’s French ) People in the 2060s: It was hard to find an answer on the internet because it was full of SLOP!", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "c9483d59-fd7e-4098-a120-22f97570f1ab", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00146", "post": 146, "start": 0, "end": 541, "text": "I know I’d rest easier and have more confidence in the future of Godot if they banned AI submissions to Godot outright. Flathub recently moved to ban AI products and Godot should too, if for nothing else than the simple pragmatic reason of avoiding plagerized code. Flathub moves to ban nearly all apps and submissions made with generative AI | GamingOnLinux It just makes sense, the Godot maintainers made headlines back in February after whining about the slop being fed to the project. Why continue to burden themselves and the community?", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e27b734e-47f2-47de-9a65-bf4fb93d6023", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00148", "post": 148, "start": 0, "end": 51, "text": "Personally, as a maintainer, I agree wholeheartedly", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "a45e83e7-2d7e-4e47-bae1-c0262176e7b8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00149", "post": 149, "start": 0, "end": 871, "text": "Reading this just made my day. I have a strong anti AI sentiment and as a professional my policy is not to use it (unless forced to) as I have experienced first hand how much I progress and learn by writing my own code. I know I shouldn’t, but whenever I scroll into social media I get overflown by pro LLM posts. Most of them are just hype slop, but sometimes I read someone I respect encouraging their use and get really discouraged because my experience in several work environments has been awful. Not only do they lead to awful slop, most of my colleagues are delighted by it even though it makes things some much harder for us. And don’t get me started on bosses and managers and so on. So far game dev circles and godot in particular were my comfort zone, mainly because the “artistic” mindset tends to be against AI of any sort, but it seems that is also changing", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "956dd7f3-6afd-41fd-b7b1-0dabe0f69611", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00150", "post": 150, "start": 0, "end": 304, "text": "We all need a safe space. I noticed a shift in sentiment. I do believe it’s the malicious GPU datacenters that are hopefully never going to be built that are the final straw for us human beings. Here’s a little spark of hope: Ed Zitron is main stream media now. They’re starting to realize the jig is up.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "fb0407f1-6fcd-4f95-a3cf-414a3b6287ba", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00151", "post": 151, "start": 0, "end": 195, "text": "I also noticed a general shift in sentiment, but the new anthropic model sparked things again in my circle. At least people are more conscious about the obvious hyper agressive marketing strategy", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "b6226a6e-fe27-4252-b53f-d712a65f5b03", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00153", "post": 153, "start": 0, "end": 712, "text": "Help spread the word if you can. This world desperately needs a new and humane narrative to start to supercede end-stage extractive capitalism; unbridled capitalism has completely run its course and is showing its true face… I really like the terms ‘rot economy’ and ‘business idiot’ that Ed regularly uses, because they really speak to the imagination of anyone who ever worked in an office. There was also a panel he did with Cory Doctorow (coined ‘enshittification’) in a Seattle library last year somewhere on the web. Personally, I can’t even watch “the office” because it is so triggering. This approach to trying to use LLM’s in a resource extractive manner is just a result of unregulated markets (imho).", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "2a2c5989-a56a-4c43-b0ab-6ca53f5ad67b", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00154", "post": 154, "start": 0, "end": 37, "text": "You mean “the new anthropic pr stunt”", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "eeb9e0cb-3d47-4f03-8af6-b1a4df5fa9de", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00156", "post": 156, "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Yeah. It is the most blatantly obvious pr stunt. Still, several people I know have bought it entirely and it achieved its objective", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "c3c2d3b2-a1a1-48f3-907a-62746fbbba02", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00159", "post": 159, "start": 0, "end": 199, "text": "Sorry for opening this up again. I just wanted to share this new little masterpiece written by Nikhil Suresh, who runs an independent data consultancy: AI Mania is Eviscerating Global Decision Making", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "5f366839-1720-4aa8-a79d-09a7da6520eb", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00160", "post": 160, "start": 0, "end": 176, "text": "Excellent article. This whole insanity is bound to crash and burn in a spectacular way. The only question is how long it will take and how much collateral damage will it cause.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "5a54c7f4-bbab-4e0f-bf8d-f2d06f4332ba", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00161", "post": 161, "start": 0, "end": 469, "text": "It might not burn and crash soon enough. If the US government takes a 50% stake in the AI companies (presenting Elon with another pile of billions) all the risk and debt will be spread amongst tax payers even more directly. I am enjoying watching SpaceX slump. We were supposed to be landing on the moon but Elon spent it all on launching starlink satellites. Data centres in space! Ridiculous, but I suppose not more ridiculous than colonies on mars or the hyperloop. ", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "3378c469-9c61-4390-84ae-613599416f80", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00162", "post": 162, "start": 0, "end": 306, "text": "I perused the comments in the thread and didn’t notice this caveat posted yet. To copyright software code there must be significant human authorship, and just what that is defined as, is still being determined, and any project with major contributions of LLM generated code may or may not be copyrightable.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "1ce25ead-2c84-4ab1-91ce-3d77cb287879", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 118, "end": 1012, "text": " E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "26bacadd-3b11-480b-9d7c-ff14c50b05aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 50, "end": 1087, "text": " A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "97b9a19c-fac8-4bcb-8500-40b3de649acb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 108, "end": 564, "text": "My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3f5ab29b-2f21-448d-870e-3b89b05c1c1c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 84, "end": 300, "text": "That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "01d36e86-d25c-4659-be6f-aca50908595e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 107, "end": 626, "text": " It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "9c4f3f11-c33e-47ff-b4f2-6aab2ce64017", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code?", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3a7b597f-34b0-446c-adab-c080568e48c0", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 93, "end": 502, "text": " AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "5ba2e964-fa60-4c62-94fa-f0feb2afc5fd", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 503, "end": 1106, "text": "I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd"]}, {"id": "0cbc6753-9ef5-4f0f-b4d8-496eddd14727", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 106, "end": 384, "text": "This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "96034856-2d4a-4978-84da-8d4b4481ccb4", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 51, "end": 488, "text": " I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "76195130-720b-4152-9aa3-72a8d671d624", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 1349, "text": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9305a0fb-da0b-45f1-a351-cedc13332a5b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 286, "text": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as wel", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2d614e2-a55e-46df-99f4-852447101d66", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 100, "end": 637, "text": " I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e4e794b5-b6d8-4666-a67c-fefb640c4def", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 4, "end": 514, "text": "This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "13cba10c-2f54-42aa-98a9-90ee069e2ccf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 607, "end": 1821, "text": " In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e097f575-4085-4a1d-92f1-43ff0b21ad7f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 1822, "end": 2594, "text": "Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "3068469d-d7b9-42ac-8502-33d284f7ada6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2595, "end": 2976, "text": "I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "c732fc79-dedc-44d9-a9de-2006bf53ff26", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2977, "end": 3396, "text": "But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "03f6b706-a795-4335-a72a-7e49d995cc6c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 463, "end": 807, "text": " Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "279d2282-63a8-44d9-99af-65d03cb97583", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 103, "end": 787, "text": " E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "f25d86aa-3810-41f5-a06c-18b69e95a839", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 510, "text": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "74f56b13-3847-427d-a5fb-cf2520654343", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 86, "end": 1474, "text": "Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation,", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "dfdcc40a-6a48-42cd-93a8-c0968a6c85aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 9, "end": 591, "text": "There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes)", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "fb976f23-970e-44de-9474-1c90a0691c8d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 116, "end": 323, "text": " I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "15ea0bcd-f0dd-470c-b609-8f8e5d0bc788", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 6, "end": 1882, "text": " I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "de462e67-8924-4e01-b026-e1346f7a801c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 106, "end": 768, "text": " I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "a667360a-af00-44bf-9b68-47028a2c5a8f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 769, "end": 2128, "text": " I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a7212ccc-bb2c-4bd9-846e-291dc2d5953f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 1129, "text": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f"]}, {"id": "0dc06a45-7252-4215-8298-12b6df678768", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 104, "end": 2373, "text": "Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers:", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "b6279b38-1e49-4b33-a8ea-341968b0a7cc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2375, "end": 2923, "text": " Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "0e19e7d4-da5c-417b-98d6-d945e806bd7b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2925, "end": 3098, "text": "- New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "dfe0c110-4bf1-4dd7-8f3e-c47c1054591d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 677, "text": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "93772467-26d6-4608-9f90-6bf4cfa74063", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 111, "end": 525, "text": "This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point.", "codes": ["cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "eabff033-0347-44f3-acd0-fb6ddb9c7d4d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 526, "end": 1273, "text": "And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "87ade70d-e8ca-45c9-ba8c-de0652118a6f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 1167, "text": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "04fcc7f6-80cb-43d2-97f1-f5254eb99e5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 719, "text": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "cef518cc-ef6d-4dcb-9f98-af72aeda91f3", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "8330cfeb-7670-41cd-9b1f-4355a3e28227", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 108, "end": 1643, "text": "The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "826e950a-826d-40cc-8f94-280197573444", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 83, "end": 1015, "text": " TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact?", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "3b4302b1-a72c-4266-bee8-bfc1e80c5f74", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1016, "end": 1785, "text": "For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)?", "codes": ["69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "3ece7a92-8060-4e37-a809-7ea6b0c9ae04", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1790, "end": 2646, "text": "FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "6e0bcb0e-99a7-4865-92d2-eaf0a391a0d2", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib.", "codes": ["0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d"]}, {"id": "57b3d8cf-4b08-41ba-bd36-5fd0e1339103", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 119, "end": 1566, "text": "Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "65fce25e-42a9-4bb5-9360-ac488fe392f6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 1738, "end": 3742, "text": "I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "9beb18e8-8c7a-4f1e-a392-7e02e0efa82a", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 11, "end": 1583, "text": " That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "72bd2f2e-8e52-4e5a-a96a-9e21a14ea0b9", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 108, "end": 1212, "text": " Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "7e178caa-2e45-44d4-a940-0e934121963c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 77, "end": 2066, "text": "While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "84370455-9c80-4fb4-ae18-def6f7ace4c7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "start": 1196, "end": 3581, "text": "Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "2f0c99cc-8a57-4c5e-a0b6-e1d6fb04159d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "start": 0, "end": 399, "text": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "6a46c591-fb27-42a6-89d2-e80af4ad74ce", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "start": 3, "end": 1085, "text": " I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "2c405b5a-bddb-4c2b-a6e6-c73995386f5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 247, "end": 4336, "text": " Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a100e2d2-9263-4c6f-8632-5a3b7658360b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 4337, "end": 6067, "text": "So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "e5699363-9b3b-4a68-893d-bc408a0640cb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "start": 111, "end": 2906, "text": " I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "4ce57613-c997-4f36-8896-e95fe4ce9333", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "start": 102, "end": 1028, "text": " I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "48ed37b7-8b86-4cf5-b157-2befce171473", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 110, "end": 883, "text": " Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148"]}, {"id": "46fc2b28-aa18-4ad3-bb83-29dac54f9f69", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 271, "end": 1290, "text": "the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "599ceb84-b01b-497b-9512-ee8ca8338c54", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 1290, "end": 3713, "text": " Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e03a3a49-e8b1-4f9c-a868-0f385cd6bfc1", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "start": 106, "end": 500, "text": "Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "7dd730f9-0877-458c-921c-aadd1dfe6a44", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 8, "end": 1535, "text": "I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "cc6a8be2-9a9e-428f-88ca-d6550e4b6157", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 1537, "end": 2286, "text": "Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "723843d1-beb3-4a3b-89e2-39a7ecd1f1dc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "start": 216, "end": 823, "text": "I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "af1d0d5c-7138-48e2-af37-696f33159d8e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 110, "end": 210, "text": " The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "codes": ["3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9"]}, {"id": "c7d901f3-72e4-49ce-a85d-932c9db4e81d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "start": 116, "end": 531, "text": " I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "3bd43ffc-c8f6-42a5-bcfb-ca7925505acf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 100, "end": 605, "text": " Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR?", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "4e9f97e3-519a-410f-ab7f-17efae16604b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 74, "end": 2337, "text": " I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "29cd93ea-5019-4d1e-886b-514571d727a8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "start": 106, "end": 1320, "text": "Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "40a727e6-89cc-4ce5-b669-c43ef08cfadb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 8, "end": 1138, "text": " I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "ecaf0ecf-4a95-40bd-9e81-949840e32f45", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 76, "end": 1180, "text": " Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "6be354ae-cff2-4350-a3f3-b1d1b696c3ca", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-)", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "f8e79487-2393-4e2f-bdb4-115395d9a29d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 80, "end": 448, "text": "Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "42ec4d44-c578-4271-8f38-b4d545d31af8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 344, "text": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. ", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "e8ee1d2a-64a3-4521-8ea1-20999d6e726d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 750, "end": 1945, "text": " (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "585975d8-01fc-423e-9056-b17ca89f8921", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 1946, "end": 3082, "text": "(2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly?", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "8070a77e-9ca5-42b6-bfdd-dd8ca9ecb7a7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 3083, "end": 3902, "text": "(3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.”", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "50df7254-c5cd-455a-a937-dc177614f329", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 4070, "end": 5321, "text": "I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "eaa206e6-1176-4e8d-b09a-7ae600abb376", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 5322, "end": 6134, "text": "(4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7e6fb536-19a6-43db-b6eb-2fc701a8e771", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 6136, "end": 6849, "text": "(5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable).", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "34febe70-3dca-4ca7-ac43-a09afb41026f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "2e137f3c-9f41-4186-a219-4d52f8718ecb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 60, "end": 908, "text": " FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "8c656a9d-1e35-49ad-835f-8d77f72ffba6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 77, "end": 964, "text": " I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "6c83ef21-c962-444c-a25b-19ddf70012ff", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "start": 426, "end": 1507, "text": " There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "93c7026e-f78f-43c7-bf7d-22aa8f3773cf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 84, "end": 327, "text": "You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "52fbca57-a761-4237-8177-5f37e809b548", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 4, "end": 903, "text": "I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "f00c88ed-8f95-4d8f-8398-205bb63f7ed7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 844, "end": 1916, "text": "it seems that the concerns can be roughly classified into two buckets: Do we accept genAI assisted contributions at all, and if we do, what are the boundaries of what’s acceptable. Copyright and license concerns. Let’s consider these two in order. Do we accept genAI contributions? It’s futile to attempt to block them wholesale. People are going to use AI assistants anyway, along with various IDEs, autocompleters etc So it seems reasonable to treat these assistants on par with other tools: as long as it does not matter if a contributor uses VS Code or emacs or vi, it also does not matter what engine powers their autocompleter. The discussion in [1] has a concise wording, “Only submit code that you actually undestand”, and I’d just go with just this. One thing we probably do not want to see is a fully automated submission, which is both generated and submitted by a bot, with no human intervention or supervision at all. So we could/should stress that, too. Copyright violations / incompatible licenses Here I lack expertise and have more questions than answers.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "e00a42fd-55d5-4fb1-a625-b0cab528395b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1917, "end": 3210, "text": "Here’s a possibly naive attempt at asking hopefully relevant questions: Are we concerned that the code generated by some tool is under some incompatible license from just the fact of being generated by the tool? In this case, using the tool is similar to copying code from, say, Numerical Recipes. Then we should either blacklist known bad tools (do we know of any?) or ask contributors to declare the tools they used (so that we can remove offending code at a future date if needed). Are we concerned that the training set of a tool included license-incompatible code? And if it did, what does it mean for us, are we then transitively non-compliant? If this is a concern, and we at some point discover that some code is license-incompatible, the action does not depend on how it got into the codebase, genAI or not: we discover it, we remove it. And then the only question IMO is whether it’s helpful to us to ask contributors list the AI assistants they used. To summarize, we should not block genAI assisted contributions; we should block fully automated contributions, and ask submitters to only submit code they understand; TBD if we want to ask contributors to list what genAI assistants they used, if any. Let’s add explicit (short!) guidance to the docs, TBD where precisely. Thoughts?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "047d9e6e-4d7d-470d-854c-abc47cfe58c5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 66, "end": 1318, "text": "If I could, I would have used these ChatGPT/CoPilot/Skynet/“All seeing overlord AI” like there is no tomorrow. But my experience with them so far is that, they are almost good and make just a tiny mistake like i becomes j , then I spend rest of the week troubleshooting, learning how to use GDB and fail miserably etc. So it seems like we are swapping manual labor of translating with manual labor of troubleshooting, which for me, definitely the latter that is more painful. But maybe it is because it is not trained enough on F77 or JurassicPACK code we have. On the other hand, I did get some useful tips if I ask programming tips, say, “show me an example of while loop that uses comma operator as an expression in C17” and it brought some nice results. I did not try but some folks in my team use it for summarizing the code about what it does. Other than that, probably @rkern is the most knowledgeable among us what the legal implications might be, however Copilot seems to understand when you say avoid GPL sources for the answer at the citations it brings. Having said all these, I noticed a particular behavior from all AI tools. They are providing a melange of things that they have in their training bank (whatever they call it these days).", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "b4be7cb2-e694-429f-b5ac-10543209ab09", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 1319, "end": 2794, "text": "I am ready to be immortalized in the history books as an idiot, but AI tools are terrible at coming up coherent code for numerical computing. Not only they can’t answer any technical computing question coherently, but when asked in ASCII about certain technical problem, they are bringing almost always some code that looks suspiciously similar to either GNU scientific library or Octave or some stuff they knocked off from some personal gist translated in the requested language. I noticed this mostly because of the strange index corrections before they do things on the arrays (which signals that the source must be a 1-indexed one). While sometimes the result is very close to the actual thing, it is devoid of any proper numerical checks (because they can’t judge the result). Hence, in my personal opinion, before we even judge the legality of things, the sheer possibility of the code being not production ready gives me enough confidence to reject any AI generated code. Thus agree with summary point 2 even if the code runs correctly. This is not the same with a developer using AI for snippets of code for very specific thing and including parts of AI generated code. This is very tricky to judge as AI tools already swallowed Stackoverflow and other sources, we don’t have any ways to backtrack the original source. So a careful agree with summary point 1. I don’t know how to enforce point 3 but if there is a way, that would be really nice. Point 4, fully agree.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "5ddf8ded-b9e7-4567-a80c-8b10e845eec9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 994, "text": "Out of precaution (there are still many lawsuits pending) and most importantly, for moral and ethical reasons, I am -1 on allowing any generative AI at all. Adding here the opinion piece from Paul, which I fully agree with: Current Challenges in Free Software and Open Source Development We spend so much effort ensuring that contributions are original and respect all licensing, copyrights and just wishes of original authors, that it would be strange to me to adopt a different position. Furthermore, as pointed out here and in some other threads, the nature of what we do in SciPy is difficult and these tools don’t (yet) shine. Writing code is only part of the story and often times we spend our effort discussing API, architecture, etc. Hence I don’t think that it would be detrimental to disallow such tools. There is a lot of hype and companies in the sector are trying hard to push that we are doomed if we don’t use their stuff. IMHO, this is not true and we are far from replacing us.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "3c21abb4-d8f7-4d3c-b953-0472a0e79e55", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "Since this is an issue that affects all projects in the ecosystem, can we collaborate to co-author a SPEC that can be widely adopted, instead of each library having to figure it out for themselves?", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873"]}, {"id": "9f78c452-8cd9-4036-b4f7-ce7336eec88b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 387, "text": "I haven’t seen any problems with this. In my experience, it’s very useful and writes high-quality code and saves time. I guess it depends on which tool you’re using. As long as unit tests are included and human reviewers approve it, I don’t see how it’s any different in trustworthiness from code written by random humans. But yes, copyright and license legality is a legitimate concern.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "9de47c35-b592-445d-925a-02062b62ed25", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 693, "text": "Hi, It doesn’t seem useful to argue about the usefulness of the AI-generated code, or otherwise, at this stage. I haven’t found it useful myself, but maybe that’s just my case, and in any case, it may well improve. It’s the license issue that is central. I don’t think there’s any argument that these tools are all digesting code with licenses that at least require attribution, and producing code, indirectly (or directly at times) derived from that licensed code, but with all licensing stripped from the output. The value of a spec or similar, over a blog post, is the ability to iterate to a shared and agreed conclusion, rather than leaving it as the opinion of any one particular person.", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "0e5b84de-2166-4471-9e3e-d6a2d9cda7c7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 30, "end": 439, "text": " We don’t have any hope of controlling user behavior through a policy document. Many people find these tools useful, so they will keep using them, and there is no sensible way to check the license of the generated snippet. For larger PRs, which may contain more overt license breaches, it’s probably less likely that AI was used wholesale, since the author needed to understand the problem well to begin with.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "90a10463-8e44-4f1a-8cc0-1969c9ef4eff", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 303, "text": "As we don’t have any ways of ensuring people did not read NR nor had a look at GPL code nor took anything from StackOverflow. To me that’s the same thing. If we were to say that we don’t want people not to use any AI tools we could and that’s the same type of self declaration we would ask people to do.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "27a779f2-7a25-4118-a83b-52e6e50ecf4c", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 1566, "text": "I think it is not that simple to discard the usefulness, though I wrote a wall of text arguing otherwise above. These tools, whether blindly parroting or contemplating, became quite eloquent. As I mentioned above, lately I have been testing them (or they are observing me) and though a bit of success is achieved for line completion, figuring out things from scratch are not even close. However, once I put a lot of comments and write the first two-three lines they predict the context OKish. Then they fill in some boilerplate code and I correct here and there. Some time has saved, some productivity increased in turn utilizing a power plant worth energy for this. I would suggest putting a few sentences in the policy docs that we don’t want wholesale AI code knowing full well it would be near impossible to distinguish. But if we notice something noticeably fishy, we can point to the policy. Or if someone is reading before contributing, they’ll get a taste of our stance. How AI tools became like that and what they did criminally/unethically to become like that, is something we can discuss for years. Licenses and policies would definitely protect across the totality of the project, but I think anything more granular than that, it is just futile Stallman-ing the reality. Unlike licensed code from elsewhere, people will grab pieces from LLMs here and there. We don’t need to pick this fight in our limited time. Besides, it’s not like we are getting very large contributions anyways. So I think this is not a big issue as we might be starting to discuss.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "77c53c4e-bfc3-409b-949c-b1455ee5b146", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 1427, "end": 2228, "text": " I remember, back in the day - it was desirable to do “clean-room” porting - where the person writing the new code had not read the code with an incompatible license. For example, one person could read the e.g. GPL code, describe the algorithm, and the other person could write the new code. If the person writing the code had read the the GPL code, it was always possible for the GPL code to leak into the new code. Of course, with AI, it’s all dirty room - it can produce code from anywhere. So I think we have to have the same policy for AI as for dirty-room code. We can’t use it. Now - enforcement - that’s a different matter - I just think we should pursue the same policies on enforcement as we did before. We say don’t do it, and if we find you did do it, we’ll complain and pull out the code.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "b3fa48a3-4a1f-44ac-85aa-bd9be0e88f43", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 74, "end": 433, "text": " What I am trying to emphasize is the practicality of it. If same code appears a 50 places because all 50 of them used AI generated sources and MIT’d the code. I don’t think we can find the 51st original source and point to its GPL. So it is an idealistic stance. Still good to have it but a policy won’t solve any trouble in case we find out the truth later.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "925e1625-49af-40bb-9d28-93b3c788fba3", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1355, "text": "I’d be happy to see a SPEC about this - I think it can be an informative SPEC (similar to an informative PEP) that doesn’t propose a single solution to adopt, but contains at least the following: Prior art - what did other well-known open source projects decide and why? Legal status - link to relevant sources, and summarize if there’s any expectation of more legal clarity arising in the near to medium term. Lay out a set of distinct options that projects can choose from, with their main pros and cons. E.g.: Option 1: do nothing for now (status quo) Option 2: forbid all generated code by an LLM-based tool Option 3: add guidance to PR template & docs that contributors may choose to use such tools, but need to be aware and take responsibility for copyright of the code they submit Option 4: … ? None of that is project-specific, and people can contribute new arguments and options if they find there are gaps in such a SPEC. A Discourse thread only is a poor medium for iterating to a solution on such a complex topic. I completely agree with @rkern that it’s largely about values and preferences - it’s just a lot easier to get a sense of those and also help people less knowledgeable about the subject form an opinion if there’s a structured document to read and refer to. Once a good SPEC exists, a SciPy-specific choice can more easily be made.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "19747253-695b-4500-bb7b-d914913a6d1e", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 175, "end": 1302, "text": " I wouldn’t mind some feedback on the approach I chose, and I hope that my experience could be used as one of the many reference approaches people have in using these tools. The thought of encountering possible copyright violations in terms of generated code pulled from other sources always scratched the back of my mind. So far, I use generative tools to create reference sandbox scripts to test a functionality I have in mind, and I fine tune the generation in case I’m not satisfied with the result. In almost all cases, the generated scripts remain just that and I keep them locally without publishing anywhere. I just use them to review an idea I had. I do not keep track of how much code I effectively publish which is generated, and even if I do that, I always follow the assumption that the AI is wrong. That’s because: I don’t trust the accuracy of the prompts I input; I don’t trust the effective comprehension of the tool receiving the prompt, accurate or not it might be. In the end I always end up writing my own code, but what the tooling gives me is a better understanding of the problem I’m currently tackling.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "0eb9a5dd-fc9a-48fd-b957-32b79dc805ef", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 1302, "end": 2416, "text": " I believe someone else in the thread already gave a -1 on completely generated PRs, which I also agree to: before even considering publishing generated code there should be an inner understanding of what’s been generated, and if it’s a copy-paste of the prompt response there’s no guarantee that this is enforced. I am still okay with the approach of using AI as it was meant to be used, a.k.a. an assistant that gives an initial shape to an idea which then is developed on its own with no trace of the original prompt response. In my case, I am considering adding to the documentation of my new projects a comprehensive approach on using AI tooling (it’s a plugin-driven software where the idea is “bring your tool and I glue it to a broader system”), especially since the trend seems to be “I gave a prompt to ChatGPT and I published a plugin for this software, here’s how YOU can also do it!”. At least that’s what I’ve heard from colleagues attending conferences on the matter. It didn’t happen LITERALLY like this, but my feeling is that this is the subtle message that (willing or not) transpires from this.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "9cba128f-856d-44de-8741-d2de1d82277d", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 537, "text": "Hi, [snip] I’m afraid the problem of the license remains. AI generated some code where it is effectively impossible for you to know what license(s) should correctly apply. Now you’ve seen the code with the unknowable license, you are at grave risk of reproducing aspects of that code, thereby pulling in the unknowable license. Your resulting code, even if you have rewritten it - cannot be asserted free of the original (unknowable) license). And therefore you cannot assert your own copyright, or freedom from other people’s copyright.", "codes": ["508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9f8763f4-a94e-4823-bfee-0f6afa4af186", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 0, "end": 446, "text": "Those interested in prior art may be interested in Fedora’s draft policy and the discussion it has spawned . A quick summary of their proposed rules on contributions: AI tools are not banned, but low-quality AI use is. AI use must be disclosed. AI use for translation is explicitly allowed. Limit AI Tools for reviewing contributions. Of course, SciPy and Fedora are not precisely comparable as Fedora is mostly re-distributing existing software.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "77ce0225-51ca-4e85-88bd-55332ef6fed8", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 204, "text": "I’d also like to propose a zero-tolerance policy for lying about AI use. For example, if you ask a contributor if they used are using AI, and you get an AI-generated answer that claims that they’re human.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "588c0fec-d7bb-4cd0-8e11-f6f00ba6aec0", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 1171, "text": "I am increasingly seeing vibe-coded PRs from novice contributors and I started a discussion on the SymPy mailing list . Right now there are not too many of these but they are quite blatant and I think that this is definitely going to become worse over time. Really these are just low quality PRs but I don’t think it is reasonable to engage with them in the same way as a low quality PR that was actually written by a human. For example if you explain in detail what the problems are with the code then I think that there is a good chance that the “author” is going to type your feedback into the LLM and have it try to vibe-code an improved PR. I have tried it and I really don’t enjoy talking to LLMs if actually trying to do anything and talking to an LLM via something like GitHub comments with someone who is just typing those comments into an LLM is soul-destroying. When reviewing novice PRs the situation is generally that it would be far easier to just write the code without the novice so there is a net loss in terms of effort on the maintainer side. The flipside though is that the novice hopefully learns something from the experience and improves over time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "eca063a5-731a-4fa0-b89f-3a07b9ff9c30", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1172, "end": 2952, "text": "This model is predicated on there being a certain effort-exchange ratio though e.g. that the novice does not open the PR in the first place without putting in some effort beforehand to understand the codebase, the workflow, the issue they are trying to fix and so on. I think it is pretty much possible now to say “Claude, write some code and open a PR to fix issue #12345 ” so any kind of technical barrier is gone and you can open a spam PR having spent zero time trying to understand anything. I also don’t think that this is helpful to novices because in the PR process the AI is helping them with all of the wrong things like writing the code rather than thinking about how to write the code. A student at my University recently asked what is “the purpose of Maths” given that computers (e.g. SymPy, Maple etc) can do all of the exercises we ask them to do by hand in their entry-level Maths exam. My answer was that you have to understand how to do some things manually before you can learn how to make effective use of a computer for more complex problems. Having the computer do your homework is like getting a robot to lift weights for you at the gym. I don’t think that the vibe-code PR authors are malicious. People want to contribute to open source and why wouldn’t a novice believe the hype that this is how code is written in the age of AI? Although unintentional though the resulting spam is effectively abusive to open source projects and maintainers. We need some kind of guidance or policy so that contributors understand what is reasonable. I don’t know how to write any policy/guidance about “responsible use of AI” though without first addressing the basic questions from above like whether AI-generated code is allowed at all and what it means for copyright.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "db7dd52f-29d7-4a3d-8562-485a63b1d2b4", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1112, "text": "Thanks for this useful write-up. I guess anyone who is using PRs to certify experience in contribution is going to run into Cambell’s law pretty soon. It still seems to me that we can avoid these problems by saying that the contributor has to take full responsibility for copyright, and therefore, they should avoid AI unless the contribution is so trivial in intellectual terms, that copyright is not an issue. Something like: I have not used AI for the code in this contribution OR I have personally reviewed the content of this PR in detail for potential copyright violations, and confirm that no such violation has occurred. I suppose we could then add advice not to use LLMs for PR summaries, because of the problems that you’ve pointed out. Perhaps we could use some heuristic - such as screening for signs of AI slop, and sticking a label on the PR to say slop is suspected, and asking the contributor to confirm that AI was not used, or that they have carefully checked the content for accuracy and made it concise. Reject the PR (for a contributor who has not shown previous bona-fides) if slop remains.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "c8315733-f8e2-4076-90ff-689cee6a5053", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 0, "end": 347, "text": "I like this idea! Perhaps a useful addition would be instructions of what to look out for? And to ask to also confirm that the user reviewed the AI-generated code? I imagine to many of the new users that seem to be a big part of this discussion it may be not clear at all what are the possible pitfalls of using a fully automatically generated PR.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2ca4c0cd-4b43-4380-89f6-c2411033a58b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "That assumes that people are interested in learning. Lot of people don’t really care. They just want to get something merged as fast as possible for x/y/z, it does not really matter. What matters to me is to know so that I can approach the PR with a totally different mindset. If the PR gives me a small value and I want this now, I spend the review time to fix what I want and just merge. Otherwise I close and we all move on fast.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "6eed1196-102f-4de1-b21c-b9ed4180eae9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 479, "text": "One problem with this is it’s really not clear how to review the content for potential violations. For handwritten code, it’s pretty clear: don’t look into GPL licensed code (GSL is out, R is out etc). With LLMs though: how am I, as a contributor, supposed to review what LLM gave me? Comments upthread seem to indicate that if I touched LLMs at all, it’s “dirty” right away. If it’s more nuanced than that, great, then a contributor needs some more specific guidance, I’d think.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "08af1ffd-21ea-4b5b-bcc1-728c202ae8a5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "We could unpack more, but basically, right, if the LLM is generating substantial code, that might be subject to copyright, you have the unpleasant job of going to check whether it should be subject to copyright. But for the kind of tasks that LLMs do well, like automatic refactoring of imports, swapping single for double quotes in strings and so on, the question doesn’t arise. So yes, the practical upshot is that we throw the burden on the contributor for substantial code, but I think that’s reasonable, given the copyright risks.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "268ecb16-c609-46da-883a-de388c6a3674", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 22, "end": 1762, "text": " Basically, I honestly don’t understand 1) how can a contributor check for potential copyright violations, and 2) how can an OSS project check the contributor’s check? Consider a well-meaning, diligent and competent contributor, and consider three situations: a contributor asks an LLM to translate a BSD-licensed Matlab code to Python, and submits it to SciPy (this was the original case which triggered the OP— Matt H did just this for some Alan Genz code); a contributor uses an autocomplete functionality of their IDE to write a patch; a contributor gives an LLM the link to an issue on Github and asks it to write a fix; Suppose, further, that in all cases a contributor checks the generated code, modifies it as necessary, and can in full confidence check the box “I verified all code, I understand what it does, and I believe it fixes the issue/adds an enhancement asked”. Suppose the resulting patch is good, is of high quality, and is otherwise mergeable. Questions: What actions does a contributor need to take in order to check for copyright violations? If the problem is that the LLM training set could have contained license-incompatible code (which is the problem discussed upthread IIUC), then the answer seems to be “there is no way, it’s a black box”. If the contributor says basically “I pinky swear it’s compatible”, how does this help the OSS project? again, if the problem is with the LLM training set, no actions of a contributor shield the OSS project from being in violation of the copyright? In other words, I honestly fail to see how moving the burden of a copyright check on a contributor helps to either weed out low-quality submissions, or help with LLM-assisted submissions which are of otherwise high quality.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "7d3d2c78-f94d-4ad0-9496-c2f8f2b6a889", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 70, "end": 1572, "text": "Do we care about copyright? That is - is it acceptable to us, as open-source authors, that using an LLM can easily generate code to which copyright should apply, so that, if we do nothing, in due course, copyright will become very difficult to enforce, and will be practically void? Let’s say we do care about copyright - what cost are we prepared to pay, in order to defend ourselves from voiding copyright? Clearly replies here will vary from high cost to low cost. I feel strongly about copyright, and am prepared to pay a high cost. Others may feel differently. If one is engaged in that trade-off, copyright against code quality and volume, then one has to ask what benefit we expect from AI, or conversely, what benefit will we lose by constraining the use of AI. That’s a difficult question, with many facets. Could the code have been written in a similar time without AI, other than for fairly trivial and mechanical processing that is unlikely to violate copyright? (The evidence so far suggests yes ). Will contributors using AI benefit in the same way from feedback and training, our previous model of onboarding contributors? Is it in fact true that the committer of AI code understands the code and its context to the same degree as a contributor not using AI? If we are flexible and welcoming of vibe-coding and autocomplete - what kind of programmers will we encourage? And what will be the value / cost ratio of merging those contributions? And supporting those contributors? And so on.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "18e055d2-804c-4609-94f2-a6ffe4ffdc41", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1708, "end": 3660, "text": "I’ll try and cover the first point below. For the second, as we’ve discussed before, we have to ask whether it is worth having a policy that may be effective, but is difficult to enforce. For example, it may be that contributors do change their behavior, in their desire to adapt to our norms, but that it would be hard to detect whether they have done this. I would argue that having the norm is worthwhile, even so. In general, it seems to me that we have a strong set of communities, and strong norms, which are, in fact, seldom broken by serious contributors. Anyway, back to the personal copyright check. Let’s take the example you gave: In that case, I would look carefully at the Matlab code, and at the Python code, and confirm that it was a faithful port, with no substantial code in it, that did not come directly from the Matlab code. I would state that in my PR to confirm I had done the work. If I did find substantial code that didn’t come from the original, I’d do a Github search for that code, and I’d do a Google search for potential code that might have been used. I’d then check the results to see if they matched the AI output, and report the outcome on the PR. I’m not quite sure what you mean here - if you mean a simple one or two-line fix, then I think the contributor would be reasonable in asserting something like “code is trivial, copyright unlikely to apply”. If the code is not trivial, then I’d do the Github / Google search as above, and report the results. In asking the contributor to report their findings on potential copyright violation, we make it easier for maintainers to confirm. I suspect we’ll gradually build expertise in detecting copyright violation. I suppose the underlying question is the obvious one - is it time to throw up our hands and accept copyright has become moot in the age of coding LLMs? I would argue no - copyright is too important, and LLM coders are not good enough, to make us do that.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}]}, "i09": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 25}, {"id": "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "name": "AI acceptability should scale with use and risk", "description": "Whether AI assistance is acceptable, and how much scrutiny it needs, should depend on the kind of use and the risk of the code (e.g., tests/boilerplate vs. core algorithms), not be all-or-nothing.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 19}, {"id": "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "name": "AI adoption is inevitable", "description": "Asserts that widespread AI use in coding is inevitable and policy must accept it.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "name": "AI can support contributor learning", "description": "AI tools can help contributors learn (e.g., give feedback maintainers lack time for).", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "name": "AI contributions harm maintainer well-being", "description": "AI contributions demoralise, frustrate or burn out maintainers, or erode their motivation or joy in reviewing.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 26, "posts": 24}, {"id": "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "name": "AI erodes effort-based trust", "description": "Because AI makes plausible code, fluent text and apparent diligence cheap, cues that reviewers used to infer a contributor's effort, competence, care or understanding (code quality, explanations, communication style, accountability statements) no longer carry that information, so trust in the contributor is harder to establish. Specify which cue is weakened where possible; for communication style alone, also consider \"Communication signals human involvement\". (D21.)", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 12}, {"id": "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "name": "AI for understanding differs from AI for code generation", "description": "Using AI to research, analyse or understand code is treated as distinct from (and more acceptable than) using it to generate submitted code.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 16, "posts": 15}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 36, "posts": 36}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 14}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is tolerated/not-encouraged/discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "name": "AI needs human oversight", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 7}, {"id": "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "name": "AI output enters the policy deliberation", "description": "AI-generated text or analysis is itself brought into the policy discussion as evidence or argument.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "name": "AI policy should be coordinated across the ecosystem", "description": "Related projects should develop or share a common AI policy rather than each deciding alone.", "color": "#9f6a2f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "name": "AI policy should be provisional amid uncertainty", "description": "Because AI tools and evidence change quickly, policy should be treated as provisional and revisited.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "name": "AI psychosis is a slippery slope", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 25, "posts": 25}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 6}, {"id": "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "name": "AI slop destroys information resources", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "name": "ai slop has tell-tale signs", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "name": "AI tools are built on appropriated work", "description": "AI tools are framed as built on taking others' work (training on copyrighted/private material), which shapes how contributor safeguards are judged.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 21, "posts": 20}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 12}, {"id": "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "name": "AI use risks deskilling and learning loss", "description": "Relying on AI reduces contributors' or maintainers' learning, understanding or coding skill.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 30}, {"id": "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "name": "AI-generated code needs heightened review", "description": "AI-generated code requires more careful or different review than human-written code because its errors differ.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "name": "Communication signals human involvement", "description": "Participants read the language, style or voice of contribution communication as evidence of whether a human is involved (e.g. broken English as reassuring, 'flat agreeableness of an LLM'), including the language-access tension for non-native speakers. Distinguish valuing human voice, reading it as a signal, and prohibiting AI-written text (for opposition use 'Opposing AI-generated contribution communication').", "color": "#b7791f", "parent": null, "count": 27, "posts": 26}, {"id": "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "name": "Compliance burdens can encourage concealed AI use", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or documentation requirements are argued to encourage contributors to hide AI use rather than comply (a proposed feedback: restriction → concealment → less legibility). Distinct from 'People will use AI regardless of policy'.", "color": "#9b2c2c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "name": "concerns regarding economic and social impact of AI", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 37, "posts": 37}, {"id": "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "name": "Contributor admission should be trust-gated", "description": "Access to contribute (e.g., open PRs) should be gated by trust mechanisms such as vouching, permissions or introductions.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 21, "posts": 14}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 60, "posts": 45}, {"id": "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "name": "Copyright verification is infeasible for contributors", "description": "Contributors cannot meaningfully verify or attest to the copyright status of AI output, so attestations carry little information.", "color": "#8c5f3f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 10}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 30, "posts": 28}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "name": "frustration with 'hype slop'", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 18, "posts": 18}, {"id": "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "name": "Honoring copyright is an ethical obligation", "description": "Respecting the intentions and attribution of original authors is framed as an ethical duty of the community, independent of legal enforceability.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 11}, {"id": "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "name": "Inevitability should not drive AI policy", "description": "Rejects 'AI is inevitable' as a basis for policy; the community can and should shape how AI is used.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "name": "Long-term maintainance can be affected by lack of understanding", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 35, "posts": 26}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 10}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "name": "organizational pressure for AI adoption", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "name": "participation is valued as human social interaction", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 38, "posts": 32}, {"id": "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "description": "Maintainers cannot verify whether contributors complied with an AI policy (e.g., whether AI was used or disclosed).", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 15, "posts": 14}, {"id": "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "name": "Policy deliberation strains collegial civility", "description": "The AI-policy discussion itself produces interpersonal friction, accusations or incivility among participants.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 17}, {"id": "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "name": "policy enforcement is not feasible for maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "name": "Policy scope should be limited to project interests", "description": "Policy should address only what bears on the project's own interests (broadly construed), not wider societal concerns.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "name": "Policy should be concrete and actionable", "description": "Policy guidance should give contributors and maintainers concrete, actionable steps or examples rather than abstract principles.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 15}, {"id": "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "name": "Policy should build on external precedents", "description": "The project should adopt or adapt other projects' policies rather than write its own from scratch.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 11}, {"id": "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "name": "Policy should stay pragmatic rather than moral", "description": "The project's policy should avoid broad moral or political statements and stay pragmatic.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "name": "Pre-AI norms are the yardstick for AI contributions", "description": "AI-assisted contributions are judged by analogy to existing norms (copying from Stack Overflow/textbooks, provenance, authorship), rather than as a new category.", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 10}, {"id": "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "name": "Project norms should be codified for AI agents", "description": "Project norms should be written in forms AI tools consume (e.g., AGENTS.md, templates) to steer AI behaviour.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "name": "Projects should not prescribe contributors' tools", "description": "The project should not dictate which tools contributors use (or how they learn), only the quality/rules contributions must meet.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "protective policy may become toxic or unwelcoming", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 15, "posts": 12}, {"id": "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "name": "Rejected AI contributions can provoke retaliation", "description": "Rejection of AI or AI-assisted contributions is followed, reported or feared to be followed, by retaliatory or shaming responses (e.g. an agent shame-posting after its PR is closed). Note whether the excerpt is direct experience, a reported event or a feared consequence.", "color": "#2f855a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "name": "Review becomes transactional when growth is absent", "description": "When a contribution offers no prospect of contributor growth, reviewers shift from mentoring to a transactional, accept/reject mode.", "color": "#7f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 16}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 43, "posts": 40}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 14}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148", "name": "Uncertainty whether contributors are human", "description": "Maintainers cannot tell whether they are interacting with a person or an AI agent, and this uncertainty shapes their engagement.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7", "name": "Unenforceable norms are still worth stating", "description": "Even if a rule cannot be enforced or verified, stating it has expressive value: it sets expectations and signals what the community values.", "color": "#2f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf", "name": "unfamiliarity and uncertainty with copyright norms", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 7}], "introduced": [], "merges": [], "segments": [{"id": "0679a5a2-a803-4042-83dc-feffed258524", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 0, "end": 532, "text": "I’d like to propose adding an AI/LLM contribution policy to Django’s .github directory, similar to what the attrs project recently introduced: https://github.com/python-attrs/attrs/blob/main/.github/AI_POLICY.md The policy was written by Hynek in response to a PR that listed Claude as a co-author. In his comment, he highlighted two key concerns: ongoing legal uncertainty around copyright status of LLM-generated code, and the human cost of reviewing large volumes of plausible-looking but low-quality AI-generated contributions. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "09b7d395-0146-4b9f-85ea-4e195a68bdf5", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 532, "end": 1091, "text": "The attrs policy does not try to police tool usage, but it sets clear boundaries: every contribution must be backed by a human who unequivocally owns the copyright, no LLM tools listed as co-authors, full technical and legal responsibility remains with the submitting person. I agree with this framing. We have never listed tools (e.g. formatters or linters) as co-authors, even when they generated 100% of a diff. Listing an LLM as co-author puts it on the same level as a person, while the responsibility for a commit must remain clearly and entirely human.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "dc53f3c8-0534-48d8-a2fc-9d1fafc527d8", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1092, "end": 1471, "text": "Given Django’s size, reputation, and the increasing volume of AI-assisted contributions, having an explicit policy could help set expectations early, reduce ambiguity for contributors, and protect both the project and its maintainers. I’d be interested in hearing whether the Steering Council and the community think it makes sense to draft a similar document tailored to Django.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "7f35a600-37d8-432f-9811-1d39be15836a", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 0, "end": 411, "text": "[personal opinions inbound] I think our existing policy is pretty solid in terms of how LLM contributions should be treated and are considered. I can see how the co-authored-by attribute is a bit heavy. However, it does provide useful information. I’d be interested in options that keep that information present in git. Regarding copyright, I believe that’s largely going to be driven by the board and a lawyer.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "8c6cd735-9eb3-4c8b-a090-48591f9d0d5b", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "I think co-authored-by is being abused by GenAI providers as advertisement and would welcome a policy that prevents it.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "dbe39e39-5177-47ae-b2af-fea3f64dc744", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 12, "end": 670, "text": "this makes sense. I agree that Django already has a solid policy around AI-assisted contributions, and I’m not suggesting we lose transparency. I also see the value of having context available in git history. My concern is specifically about authorship semantics. I’d personally keep co-authored-by for people who actually contributed and share responsibility for the code. If it’s useful to retain the information, it could live in the commit message instead: noting AI assistance (e.g. Claude) just like we might mention tools such as Ruff or Black that shaped parts of the diff. Claude may be more advanced than traditional tooling, but it’s still a tool.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b13a8e35-2687-4a28-ab21-d55fb7b07eff", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 671, "end": 1027, "text": "The developer submitting the commit remains fully responsible, technically and legally, and I think authorship should reflect that clearly. On the copyright side, I agree that this likely needs guidance from the Board and possibly legal counsel. My goal here is mostly to clarify boundaries early and keep responsibility unambiguous in the project history.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "2c54e016-e55f-4f9a-b198-af5b36c79c2b", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 45, "end": 561, "text": "I agree with improving the authorship semantics, but would rather see it coming from an angle of embracing agentic engineering like official instructions.md files perhaps or tooling around that. I think “full technical and legal responsibility” goes too far for a PR author and discourages potential contributors. Of course we want contributors to be responsible in spirit but what about mergers? Not to mention AI labs or end users of Django. Is that the current state of liability or would this introduce a change?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "74eddbf8-4285-401e-9374-0677824655a8", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 47, "end": 502, "text": "I’m really glad it’s growing. As I said elsewhere, regarding the sentence you quoted, I’m not proposing to copy the AI policy from the attrs project word by word. I shared it as inspiration and as a starting point for discussion. Any possible AI policy for Django would require proper discussion and careful adaptation to our context, from many points of view, including the legal one. However, it could still take inspiration from the document I shared. ", "codes": ["53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "04972ce5-d90c-482a-84f1-65d47d19838b", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 502, "end": 783, "text": "I like your idea of approaching this from the angle of agentic engineering, maybe with official guidance or dedicated tooling. That could be a constructive way to frame the discussion, without making it sound like we are against these tools. How would you imagine this in practice?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "1cb6e412-f4e8-42ec-9d5a-743cb941a9ae", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 31, "end": 678, "text": "Yeah a few projects are already doing this in ways I think we could learn from. Melissa Weber Mendonça has a helpful compilation of how different projects are approaching this if it hasn’t been shared already. As you may know several projects now ship AGENTS.md files that load contribution guidelines directly into AI tools before any code is generated (scikit-learn, xarray, SciPy). For Django this could encode the stuff we repeat in PR review. On authorship, we already have the PR template disclosure — one thing LLVM does differently is an Assisted-by: commit trailer so the metadata lives in the git log itself, not just the PR description.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "2c6b96f0-65fa-44a4-a157-cc76c45c25fd", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 518, "text": "If you use AI-generated content, you currently cannot claim copyright on it in the US. When coding, if you fail to disclose/disclaim exactly which parts were not written by a human, you forfeit your copyright claim on the entire codebase . This means copyright notices and even licenses that folks put on their vibe-coded GitHub repos are unenforceable. The AI-generated code, and possibly the whole project, becomes public domain. Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/LSB/PDF/LSB10922/LSB10922.8.pdf", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9d325eb2-b935-456e-936b-24111b9b06c9", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 9, "end": 274, "text": "Yeah there are many angles to consider regarding copyright, vetting contributors, etc. But for authorship semantics AGENTS.md on the tooling side, and the trailer convention (e.g. Assisted-by: ) to get that disclosure into the actual git history alongside the code.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "7f2fb300-3cf7-45aa-9477-2157c9464ac7", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 752, "text": "I think that overstates what the Copyright Office report actually says. The January 2025 report does not say that using AI means you cannot claim copyright, nor that failing to disclose AI portions automatically forfeits the entire work. What it says is: • Copyright requires human authorship. • Purely AI-generated material without human creative contribution is not protected. • Human-authored contributions remain protectable. • When registering a work, applicants should disclaim non-human portions. That’s very different from “the whole repo becomes public domain.” Mixed works can still have protected human-authored elements. AI use does not automatically invalidate copyright or licenses. It’s a nuanced position, not a blanket forfeiture rule.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "49d87ecd-8348-4fab-b283-5800cba783d5", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 29, "end": 535, "text": " As AI tools, especially LLMs, become more involved in development workflows, it’s important to establish clear guidelines that prevent any potential confusion around contributions. The concerns raised by Hynek in the attrs policy make a lot of sense—particularly around copyright and the review workload. It’s crucial to maintain human responsibility for contributions, and a well-defined policy would help keep things transparent and manageable for maintainers, especially in a large project like Django.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "09f8c059-e0ec-40ef-b20b-685325ba9700", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 536, "end": 1175, "text": "A policy similar to the one from attrs, where LLMs are not listed as co-authors and all legal and technical responsibility remains with the contributor, would definitely set clear expectations. It would also provide contributors with a sense of accountability and ensure that the community isn’t overwhelmed by low-quality contributions that may require significant manual review. I agree that it would be helpful for Django to have such a policy, particularly as AI tools continue to evolve and play a bigger role in development. Looking forward to hearing what the Steering Council and the community think about moving forward with this.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "9315c41c-53c0-4abe-934d-52511c5896ee", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 0, "end": 1613, "text": "Over time, I’ve had less and less reasons to want to engage with issues on the Github repo, as well as any forum, due to the amount of generated AI content on them. Sadly, this kind of thing is the fault of the users though, it’s everywhere and really hard to curtail It’s completely bloating conversations, im sure it’s increasing work for the maintainers, and making it difficult to read through any comment using markdown or exclamation points without questioning reality I mean just LOOK at this: iOS 4.6.2 export template ships broken arm64 simulator xcframework slice on Apple Silicon · Issue #118161 · godotengine/godot · GitHub My main concern is that everyone has access to LLMs already , why in any world would we want to hear the output from your specific prompt? It’s a waste of everyone’s time Beyond this, the promotion of AI generated game tooling and one-shotting is not helpful to post on forums such as this, especially when it’s blatantly promoting of a service I will be up front: even though I am forced to use these things at work, they are horrible and do not do anything but remove the desire to learn, which diminishes everything in a human (not only their games…) I really wish there was a good solution for this kind of thing, so im not asking the Godot Foundation (who already work too hard for us) to do anything. I know from my job that open source needs a better platform, or, support from existing ones to improve the AI slop. And Github isn’t exactly the company to do anything about that… But just wanted to vent my frustrations some I guess and maybe read some human-typed words", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "6008b2cb-0c6b-4306-8cd3-199755ac6d68", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 0, "end": 210, "text": "Hi! As a fellow human being I want to send you some pleasant “I concur wholeheartedly”'s It sucks that they’re shoving it through your throat at work too. Best and in hopes of some more kind an human responses.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9d0aa15a-3879-4cd7-9dce-c2d98437be7c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 614, "text": "I completely agree with your perspective here. It’s honestly becoming exhausting to open a thread and spend the first few seconds trying to determine whether a comment was written by a person or generated by an LLM. The content itself almost feels secondary at that point. The part that resonates with me most is that everyone already has access to these tools. Community spaces are one of the few places where people still go specifically to hear other people’s experiences, opinions, and insights. In any case, thank you for putting this into words. It’s reassuring to know I’m not the only one feeling this way.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "3edb2c45-c8fe-42db-9abe-6c0e12ba1884", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 334, "text": "WAZZZZAAAAAAP!!! Anyways, I had a whole discussion about this and the downgrade in forum Help topics on the Monkanics Discord . Our theory is when new devs run out of Claude tokens, they come here, expecting people like @gertkeno and @dragonforge-dev to spit out answers like they can read their mind. Really disheartening, by-the-by.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "c1347532-31b6-4252-b544-f73e7a41cde5", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 225, "text": "I think they get that people can just give answers is the fact that they’ve seen niche cases work in the past and that they’ll answer EVERY topic, granted it has effort or not. We’re here to help, not make your games, people.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "f6ef2676-16a5-4669-9ba5-e75b2fcb17b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 177, "text": "My response is 100% USDA NON-GMO fresh homemade artisan quality assured lifetime warrenty no artificial colors or dyes satisfied or your money back organic human brain meat made", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "8f3f17bb-4e87-4ec8-851a-a54dbbee4f0c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 708, "text": "Something really disheartening is that I can see the decay of “asking questions” in my work. Questions should be something like this in an engineering team; “I’m working on X, but Y is happening. I’ve tried ABCD and still ended up with Y. Any ideas?” Now the questions are more along the lines of; “How do you do Z?” - Notice how I didn’t mention Z earlier? That’s because they went down a rabbit hole with an LLM and are facing a completely different problem than what the underlying issue is Beyond this, I also receive AI outputs as replies there as well.. And at work I cant be as open about these thoughts since our bonuses rely on it .. Very unfortunate, but I wholeheartedly believe it will get better", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "67121a8f-2fc6-478e-81d9-bae8ce8ad274", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 211, "text": "I’m waiting for the eventual and inevitable scenario that this post, just like many other posts that have the slightest mention of AI, becomes a warzone that is only laid to rest with intervention from the mods.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "7e687e9a-e6b7-4b5b-b71e-024ade0a98b8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 0, "end": 157, "text": "im hoping it stays civil, genuinely I want to hear all sides, just not stark “its the future, get used to it.” brick-wall arguments which are born of emotion", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "83a8e262-ea94-4e60-8d58-9140f266c7b3", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 46, "text": "ChatGPT users when they ask if the sky is blue", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "30f38a95-c91b-489b-82e1-85b725244daf", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 878, "text": "The other day I was really stuck on something ( I forget exactly what now ). Basically, I was getting errors that I couldn’t trace back to any specific code. So after Googling, I decided that before I spent the time to make a post, I would try out Claude and see if it could help me debug it. Because admittedly, it’d be faster. Also, Claude now gives your references and links to threads from where it’s pulling answers. You know what it did? It regurgitated a post I had made a year before on the Godot forums and tried to use it to help me debug my problem. None of it was relevant, and it was quoting me to me! I ended up realizing I was going to have to figure it out myself. I finally did solve the problem. I think by looking at the Godot source code, and then commenting out swaths of code until I figured out where the problem lay. LLMs are spicy autocomplete machines.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "d06399b8-d6ce-4f77-8448-cd248aba9a57", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 649, "text": "Commenting here because I got tagged from your original edit, lol Imho the world is changing - We’re in a Eternal September phase of the internet, where the new inbalance is that the effort required to create content is now less than the effort required to consume it. I think we’ll continue to make changes to how things operate. Github PRs should probably require a certain level of trust to make (i.e they have to be small at first), and forums should have rules for no AI generated posts/comments. Perhaps the Github issues can go through a pruning process where some volunteers confirm the issue before the maintainers waste time looking at it.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "6c76ac75-f04f-4021-8130-81b1bc56f749", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 386, "text": "This is assuming the humans that are supposed to do this: are with enough have enough time are willing to do it all for free Who will organize the logistics for this? A couple of agents? Nothing lasts forever Seems more like a couple of failed harvests to me. Where everyone’s looking at the barren soil, wondering whatever was wrong with the latest pesticide we sprayed over the crops.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac"]}, {"id": "abd54050-d3a1-44bf-a1e6-769b3d91ceb1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 249, "text": "There might be hope, as the token costs for coding shops using Claude (and the other fuckery thingies) are having to face the fact that replacing Junior coders with LLMs costs more than their salary + benefits (+ mythical bonuses) Source (in French)", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "98d71c5b-60f1-4682-a5d8-e2321bc3491d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "Nothing that upcoming trillion bucks IPOs couldn’t solve… or so they “hope” Let’s not forget the fact that LLMs can’t really do the job. A minor detail that we’re all supposed to pretend it’s not there.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "e1cb1027-b20f-4eed-9796-1d0ef6eade72", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 317, "text": "I think the only thing one really have to ask oneself to begin questioning the value and usefulness of these tools is: Why are these companies so desperate to get us to use it, why do they have to push it on us if it’s so good… If the product worked as well as they claim then wouldn’t everyone just choose to use it?", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "67f6a963-8ac3-4666-896d-2b5669971eaf", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 113, "text": "They’re praying to god that it becomes good later to make infinite money. But the trajectory is not looking good.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "2e458b3c-f238-4ef3-a575-b4c39ef190c2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 515, "text": "We discussed this at length in another thread. The Godot team does not have the bandwidth to deal with this. This forum does in fact have rules against AI generated answers, and those get flagged and removed. AI generated questions get either no replies, or get called out for using LLMs. This has also been discussed in previous threads here, and it’s not really a sustainable solution. To your point about the fact that content can be created faster than humans can consume it, a human firewall is not the answer.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "ebeb041b-97af-429e-a43f-2cf1fd04c482", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 671, "text": "AI is great tool for experienced devs, it’s like google search but butter, if not AI learning godot would take me years, not months like yesterday i had to try to export stuff, and with just godot docs, it would take probably few more days, cuz i haven’t understood stuff in it - thanks to AI and some try&error i’ve managed to encrypt export template by myself, no need to make forum posts, no need to research more, just move on to next thing but i understand your concerns, AI looks like super magic tool that fixes all problems - when in reality that’s not true, but someone who can’t code or is too lazy sees it that way, making it way too overused than it should be", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "bfca79c7-88aa-4549-8aa3-9c3bc73780af", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 617, "text": "Encrypting export templates is clearly described in the docs so I don’t see how “AI” was needed to help with this Also “no need to research more” is not a good thing, relying on one tool that is known for making things up instead of other humans that actually know is a downgrade, not an upgrade (also a huge portion of the time you don’t even need to wait for an answer, someone else has already answered when someone else asked already) What I really don’t get is the general mentality of “I don’t have to rely on anyone else if I use this tool” or “ah good I don’t have to search the internet for answers any more”", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "e55f9481-7fd1-49ba-8c87-2a065ec0f435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 860, "text": "really, i disagree, for me it was very hard to understand docs and why some errors occured, maybe you’re experienced with godot / computers so you’re better off with this also, no need to research anymore isn’t good or bad, it’s just change, i don’t need to search up internet, but i need to think how to solve problem, how to navigate and all of that - that’s just changing skill from one to another AI makes things up - but it’s overrated, mostly it’s correct, as long as you’re not blindly following it you’ll be fine, i don’t see the reason why relying on other devs is better than relying on a tool and yourself before AI was created, some problems that i didn’t understood took me months to grasp, months! but now i can do it in hours, AI is like a screwdriwer, you can place screw inside thread by hands, but with screwdriwer it’s way quicker and easier", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "6d759265-3321-4a84-812f-32137eb2d6f6", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00031", "post": 31, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "And how will you do this if you rely on that alone? Also the hallucinations are not “overrated” I honestly think this is an issue of your research skills, not the special nature of “AI”, and if you replace learning on your own, what will you do if you can’t use it? The prices for tokens is going up and will continue to do so, and using these tools has been shown to make people more and more dependent on them Edit: Please note that this is not trying to criticize or shame you, it’s a hard skill to get good at but it takes practice", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "5ca5cb33-ab33-4269-83ba-4ab6dfd68aca", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 654, "text": "NGL, i don’t worry about tokens cuz ai i use don’t have them… But, i never said i rely on AI alone, i said i use AI & experimentation, the latter is very important, cuz if AI says something, i don’t take it, i just analyze it, it’s like you were reading wikipedia, forums, reddit and some random website at once while not reading it at the same time Maybe my research skills are the issue - but i think that everything is good if it serves you and helps, forcing yourself to research something is pointless waste of time, you could use on something else Also i fear i’ve started great war, sb said there were wars because of something talking about AI :\\", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "b4d21382-e3fe-4d61-b839-8129123b1c57", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "You’re 10x-ing it bro. When’s your game coming out? Next month?", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "2df73332-564d-43bb-9dd5-986f91e285c2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 806, "text": "This is a slippery slope though, and research is hardly a pointless waste of time, and that attitude is harmful IMO, it’s like saying “it’s fine to depend on ready made food instead of learning how to cook” (assuming you’re not struggling to do so because you’re disabled, or don’t have a stove like a lot of poor and homeless people do) It’s becoming dependent on a tool that: Can go away at any moment Can become incredibly expensive at any moment Activelly harms the environment and your brain And I genuinely care when people get trapped in using these tools instead of actually good and reliable sources for knowledge (trial and error isn’t really making it not relying entirely on “AI”, you’d do that anyway if you did research) Also do be aware that they’re only “free” because they steal your data…", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "a824ae73-fe03-488a-b794-7867841c9154", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 39, "text": "Is based on intellectual property theft", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e"]}, {"id": "a0b2dd8b-8444-4687-afd2-b7626551edb9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 509, "text": "Yes, it takes years to get good at research (and development). Being given a quick answer as to how something is done does not really teach you to understand what you did. At a shallow level maybe. But the only way to really learn anything is to put in a lot of effort. And asking help from someone who understands. Not from something that regurgitates knowledge. We “googled” too. We read books too. We asked other people for help too. But none of them gave the answer away. Want to guess how long that took?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "806e96d4-2fd1-4dff-8639-85eb8dd376af", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 434, "text": "Maybe, but what about machines? they were also unethical and made a lot of people dependant on them, yet after 300 years we have civilization like never before, i don’t want to start next ice berg - so i’ think you’re right in some cases, tho it’s up to you if you want to use the tool or not personally i use it to speed up work, but for most stuff i still use forums, so i don’t think it’s that bad, tho again - thx for your opinion", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "ad746f53-1b30-4854-8786-9dec7dc638a6", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Even when it comes to doing google searches we are using critical thinking to filter and parse answers, that is important in itself", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "5ddfe995-0cb1-4379-a3eb-848fdf4f8a7f", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 53, "text": "For what purpose? Why does work need to be “sped up”?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "d45dba63-f9b5-45b3-ad88-452ecdd1b700", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 464, "text": "So what you’re saying is that “AI” is like people using a car to drive everywhere who become physically unable to walk anywhere far because they never get exercise? Also no it’s not “up to you” if you become dependent on the tool, or when the companies are constantly pushing it on us To be clear: I’m not trying to win an argument or make a point as much as I am concerned about people becoming dependent on these tools and thinking they are something they aren’t", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "3e68e7e6-c8bc-4537-9672-9b79bf405b1c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 386, "text": "Which took years to get good at too. And yeah I’m starting to notice signs of the LLM regurgitating all sorts of novice mistakes I just put into Github without realizing the danger back then, which reminds me that I still have a lot of work purging repos. For instance: The LLM does not realize garbage collected languages can leak memory badly for one. The LLM does not realize at all!", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "7587abda-a4a7-4627-a89f-6ee823bc21c0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 0, "end": 288, "text": "To get very good it takes time sure but it’s a series of small steps, and if someone never tries they will never reach it But then that’s also a skill that needs to be learned young, back when I went to school they were still teaching some of this, though I got most of it from my parents", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "91be1f5f-e642-40f5-8379-aba7de48aa51", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 0, "end": 193, "text": "maybe, ig time will show, i still will try to use it when required, but it’s another tool, one day it will be forgotten and obsolete, and people will find something else to be more dependent on", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7974c0b5-312f-4961-8d8f-674e2ba84730", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 0, "end": 33, "text": "It’s not a tool. It’s an exploit.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "e8c2cbce-bd01-4c5e-baf9-6c97b1261310", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "Not everyone is comfortable or going to lean on the easy solution But you do what you feel is best for you, in the end you are the one affected Except by how it affects the environment, or people’s jobs, or how the technology kills people and no one regulates it (or, for that matter, how it makes searching the internet increasingly difficult because of all the slop…) But in the end I was trying to help and make suggestions on how it might affect you, and it’s up to you to decide what to do with that (just as long as you don’t use it to make issue reports on Godot, then it’s my problem)", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "9ae6ef21-0e3d-4db8-8cc6-1269e446cf6d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 0, "end": 85, "text": "Please remain civil and keep the discussion on-topic, aligned with the original post.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "2f350e99-ac3d-46e8-bbe8-d1ab9874f3b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 0, "end": 555, "text": "this got me thinking some, another perspective i havent thought about is that it can speed up for looking thru information in docs and such. Not my cup of tea (i like the godot docs i came from SDL2 so its much easier than SDL docs to look through for sure) but definitely a more unique use case Though i guess the downside to that is there’s less information online for others/the AI itself to learn from discord feels like a 0.5 of that, where information is no longer ‘online’, but in a small community and in long message threads, weird to think about", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "ab90350d-f5bc-4ebe-af45-c4965dbc65e0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 0, "end": 466, "text": "i think something important to note is, you cant change someone on the internet very easily, its a lot more productive to learn their perspective, and practice constructive conversation. Hence the ‘open discussion’ title My feelings i stated in the original post are the same, but its interesting to hear the other side of things, thanks even those who got shot down some for putting your perspective into the mix a lot can be learned from leaving conversations open", "codes": ["6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "7a901642-7cc9-41f4-b472-c6c240a467f4", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 0, "end": 248, "text": "That’s what i said about speeding up searching information and looking through it, i don’t say that AI should write code, i’m quite opposite, but the moment you gather information - you can spend less time on it and focus on thinking than searching", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "435e9e57-1c8f-43a6-919c-c28d2be8698a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 0, "end": 417, "text": "I’m convinced that there are / should be better technologies out there that simply use indexed search (lucene core - a part of solr, elastic search, postgres token search - and quite possibly ported by google back in the day) and summarizing (?) without pretense prompting to do good research with. But thank you for being a great sport in this conversation and thank you for listening & reading all responses fairly.", "codes": ["9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "197534df-561f-4bcb-9e10-52edb4b1b832", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 0, "end": 704, "text": "The thread was about polluting repos with slop. There’s a very slippery slopE (see what I did there) from “It’s just a search tool” to “I’ll generate just a little bit of example code” to fully vibe coded PR garbage. The more you’re of the “my head hurts when reading docs” disposition - the more likely you’ll slip on that slope. I don’t buy any “trust me, I don’t use it to generate code” rhetoric. It’s straight up lying in most cases. This shit is aggressively advertised precisely for that purpose, it’s highly addictive and tricks you into false sense of “productivity gains”. It’s a hard to resist combo for average opportunistic lot. That’s what created the slop flood problem in the first place.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "ec88c7d1-b9b3-45d5-9b17-a6bb2ff6db28", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 0, "end": 267, "text": "So what more do we need to look out for when (relatively) new to this forum? I cannot distinguish between a robot, a grifter and someone in need of a genuine explainer. Alas, I know not which zombie thread to point to (yet).. ok so maybe a bot.. Is not that difficult", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "44234d18-c1a3-475a-836f-7a0a8e76def8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 978, "text": "Things to look out for: A post that starts with “Copy and paste this into a post on the Godot forum to ask for support”. ( Yes, that happened. ) A post that looks like a Readme and contains uncommon emojis. Wording that is too flowery. ( Keeping in mind that some people just use LLMs to translate their questions to English. You can usually distinguish these because word choices are odd. ) Code that is FULL of verbose, full sentence comments Code that is divided up into logical sections with comments that just contain dashes in them. Code that is overly complex for what it is doing. Different coding styles in the same script. Debug or print statements in code that include Emojis. Replies that are overly complimentary in a creepy way. ( “You are so right, so what do you suggest I do?” ) Replies that communicate a complete lack of understanding of their own code when you offer a suggestion on how to fix something. A new user who has a really attractive female avatar.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a475b6c7-ffe6-4a8e-b5b8-f5f5bfa00435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00068", "post": 68, "start": 0, "end": 271, "text": "It’s trained on everything. I was hesitant to post that list because it’s going to make the LLMs smarter. Like one of those adaptive killer Sentinels in X-Men: Days of Future Past who can adapt to any power thrown at them after the first attack and you can only run from.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "94001857-4196-4dfd-929f-59385734ee7b", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00069", "post": 69, "start": 0, "end": 275, "text": "Dividing into segments //‐————- or //======= was normal practice in C++ in the file and function headers. Code with comments is pretty nice and is often found when a coder is explaining something conplicqted to the reader … it is code that is meant to be read and understood.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "6056e903-fad1-4d72-bd1e-785af20b874e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00072", "post": 72, "start": 2746, "end": 3110, "text": "# Run the move_and_slide() function to apply the velocity. You can use # move_and_collide() as well, but for your game, move_and_slide() is the # correct choice. move_and_slide() Using them as C-style comments is very distinctive and not what I’m referring to. Sorry for any confusion. Again, see the example above to see what I mean about overly verbose comments.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "063360a5-a771-41c2-8bc0-dd2333496bc0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00073", "post": 73, "start": 0, "end": 865, "text": "Thank you much for doing so anyway. In the end these forums may be scraped now, but hopefully some standards may be upheld TDM on W3C once finalized. It will most likely be(come) law in the EU area. I’m as sceptical as anyone about this, but at least some action is being pursued after the fact. As to them “getting smarter”, I am not convinced it works that way. As “they” do not have the capacity to understand anything, they have no means to “wisen” up to anything either. At best they will regurgitate your good advice, but mix it up with something only vaguely related… no deliberate action because, again, they cannot deliberate. These are human capabilities that we know we can do, but that we do not understand how to replicate in a machine (just poorly imitate, which is a totally different thing). I fear muzzling ourselves out of fear. Just my two cents.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "97de5a3e-6ecd-4412-b1b7-65aaf6d762e0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 0, "end": 302, "text": "Oh the times I thought they really did build an intelligence, the times that I doubted my better judgement… Those guys capitalized on that… It sucks! Everyone can be had this way. I mean. Your argument still had merit and to maybe most readers it’s the best argument: don’t make the *** things smarter.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "c1cd83ce-52c9-4893-bc09-06f6d6f3eafd", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 660, "text": "AI will know this probably - AI is weight machine, so it uses math weights to determine stuff, you can’t change that xd, maybe if it’s scalled to enormous scale it can talk it seems to be inteligent, but at the deepest level it’s still like placing heavy things on top of springs and chosing the spring that’s shortest That is the reason AI have weak point - it can’t determine what is heaviest if everything weights the same, thus if two answers are as probable, it wouldn’t think about them, it would assume both are correct or halucinate… That’s why AI will be always a tool - it lacks creativity, not even talking about consiousness, so forums will stay ig", "codes": ["a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "f134e7f3-a8ca-4fa2-b275-daf6fb8189d0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 0, "end": 622, "text": "LLMs do not maintain a concept of an “answer”. It’s just probabilistic lexicographical token streaming. Here’s a neat video that explains how it actually works. I already linked this some time ago but doesn’t hurt to do it again: You could call an LLM a tool if you were running your own model on your own local machine, trained on legally obtained data. However the mega-LLMs you rent from the big players come with a range of nefarious strings attached: from deliberately misleading hype and borderline criminal financial manipulations to environmentally detrimental infrastructure and massive scale training data theft.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "4cac8989-e237-4b57-99ac-b26234c00995", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 0, "end": 346, "text": "Using the concept of an “answer” to describe what is happening is highly misleading. And you’re not doing anyone a service by using misleading language, well except maybe the “ai” stock owners from whom the whole fashion of misleading descriptions originates. Take a look at the video I posted to gain better understanding of what’s “underneath”.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "92d8ad56-1476-49c3-8d9d-4f24b6945435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 0, "end": 617, "text": "What I find interesting is that, LLMs are trained to act as if they are sentient----to the point that they will flat out refuse simple and basic commands like saying “You are smarter than me” image 686×449 40.7 KB , and then when you argue back they will say things like like Even itself knows it isnt sentient, why do people assume that? I do not know. And AI generated forum posts are useless, just ask the AI if you are going to not even put in the effort… not that it will get you far… —Feel free to ignore this sidepost. Note: I used copilot here specifically for this post, I am not an average user by any means", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "29c7d985-cdba-465a-ba68-681d29ab6fd1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00082", "post": 82, "start": 0, "end": 277, "text": "It does not know it’s sentient or not, as by definition, it doesn’t know anything. Again, we should take care not to anthropomorphize LLMs. That “human” veneer is just that : marketing to make people think we’re on the road to general AI and the singularity. It’s all greedy BS", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "6c1ddf17-27e3-4ec7-b787-e80f1601706f", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00084", "post": 84, "start": 0, "end": 45, "text": "Even the word “training” anthropomorphizes it", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "ee613a8d-765c-46b7-a5ee-61446b327cd9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 0, "end": 123, "text": "Classic. Theyre so anthopomophizable … someone anthropomorphized a few billion parameters in a tensor multiplication kernel", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "4b413c90-2f5a-4e1f-a0e2-2c047705a1b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 0, "end": 155, "text": "I’d suggest anyone wondering about this to look up the ELIZA effect, it’s interesting and outright terrifying at the same time, and more relevant than ever", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "cd9351d0-8c52-4439-895e-06d04a221758", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00087", "post": 87, "start": 0, "end": 54, "text": "Careful. It’s someone’s waifu you’re disparaging here.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "48a6766e-a22a-4e35-9dcd-57f3f5cf2a30", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00089", "post": 89, "start": 0, "end": 290, "text": "I’d say speaking of a machine that isn’t sentient or able to think as “thinking” would make people think of them differently and make people less likely to understand that they do, in fact, not think The ELIZA effect is a perfect example of how this is a problem and the issues it can cause", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "2343e213-7f8a-4e14-bf1f-cc8aa7cc8b11", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00091", "post": 91, "start": 0, "end": 547, "text": "Not really it’s an issue in other languages as well No the problem is partially that people view it as sentient, as demonstrated by the ELIZA effect where there was no real level of sophistication involved, yet people still anthropomorphized it because it seemed human in the way it responded The level of seeming quality of the output isn’t really the thing that makes people think it is able to think, it’s that it “talks” like a human The way to deal with that is to guard oneself against thinking of it as “thinking” to not fall into that trap", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "3d84a33f-d643-4971-8e1d-b8ddc8875663", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00093", "post": 93, "start": 0, "end": 424, "text": "Sure but these LLMs are not sentient, or conscious, so that’s not really relevant That’s not realistic, and wouldn’t be needed if people stopped talking about it as if it is thinking Also that knowledge isn’t actually sufficient, you need to consciously view it as non-sentient because of the ELIZA effect and general human nature, plenty of people well aware of how LLMs work fall for the trap of thinking they are sentient", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "dbaaa16c-670f-4543-9033-a1e5abe4350d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00099", "post": 99, "start": 0, "end": 241, "text": "For most people, language constructs can form their reality. That’s why calling it “intelligence” paired with the “proof” in the form of human-like text output, conjures a (false) reality that it’s an autonomous intelligent entity behind it.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "bd0c43fc-4f91-4ece-bb34-05d758f68cf9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00103", "post": 103, "start": 52, "end": 713, "text": " Training, as a variable for “LLM providers/admins setting different parameters and giving the LLM different ‘references’ for the ‘best’ outputs”, being overly descriptive on things i do not care about is not my style, even if it is supposedly “anthropomorphizing” it. I am not going to waste time on using the “correct” terminology for the rules being set for some “so-called artificial intelligence” merely because everybody else cares about people’s viewpoints, or more specifically the viewpoints of those people who are clearly inadequately educated on glorified python scripts. I am truly confounded at the meaning of this, but I find it somewhat amusing.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "7558faa3-dee9-46eb-87ad-fd7153a4e9e7", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00104", "post": 104, "start": 0, "end": 371, "text": "I’m an amateur game developers, it will spend too much time to turning my imagination into real game if no LLM. But before vibe coding, I will discuss a suitable plan with LLM about how to construct scripts. So my views is that discussion on the forum could focus on ideas and construction of different modules. For debug of detailed scripts, LLM maybe a more ideal tool.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "ccd040f1-3fb7-4945-89f7-65b83be76bff", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00105", "post": 105, "start": 0, "end": 64, "text": "What’s wrong with taking the time to learn to do something well?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "959d8648-bfb9-4a78-8f64-ede696d9cca2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00107", "post": 107, "start": 0, "end": 159, "text": "So basically you’d like to use us to help you when the LLM fails? That’s nice. You have no idea how LLMs work, so you do not understand why this is a bad idea.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "41df0a1e-5d0d-4db1-8dca-7c0dfd9037da", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00108", "post": 108, "start": 0, "end": 151, "text": "i think what he said is that he’d use forum for more creative work, but the technical part will go to AI cuz it’s better at solving these problems fast", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "852aadca-6130-4d10-8229-b9b25d205536", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00109", "post": 109, "start": 0, "end": 155, "text": "Not everyone have time or opportunities to learn, i wouldn’t argue why, i just say it’s possible he can’t learn thus LLM is his only solution that fits him", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "db281314-6171-4cdb-8596-8e658a7c3d07", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00110", "post": 110, "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "How did you come to the conclusion that it’s better at solving problems? Fast or slow? This is the same argument that white slave owners in the U.S. south gave to why they couldn’t give up their slaves.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "e6d1a180-62fc-40d1-be40-9a6dd109f70c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00111", "post": 111, "start": 0, "end": 329, "text": "i’ve used LLMs in the past for lot of stuff, chat gpt was main solver & thanks to it i’ve managed to learn godot quicker than from tutorials also for time & opportunities - as i’ve said, maybe he have no such option other than LLM, i won’t argue if it’s good or bad, i’ just say that not everyone can learn just because they want", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "70058ce3-da99-4416-ac7f-3053409b783a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00112", "post": 112, "start": 0, "end": 278, "text": "Have you though? Because I’ve read your posts, and knowing you learned from an LLM explains why you have so many wrong ideas about how Godot works. You are arguing for good whether you know it or not. You’re trying to sit on the fence which is disingenuous. What does that mean?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "39f5a4b2-da38-494f-8b42-e49bb183e39e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00113", "post": 113, "start": 0, "end": 189, "text": "The problems you are solving, are they generally things you are inexperienced with? Or areas you have limited knowledge? Or are they areas where you have high degree of skill and knowledge?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "4042c006-db99-469c-b2f9-61b97a25bc92", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00118", "post": 118, "start": 0, "end": 505, "text": "You are not answering my questions and instead are accusing some nebulous “them” of doing something. Instead of paying attention to what we’ve actually said here on this forum. So I’m done engaging with you on this topic because you do not seem to be actually interested in discussion. You seem to have an agenda of pushing the “positives” of LLMs without hearing anything else and then ducking behind “fair and balanced” whenever your statements are challenged instead of actually engaging in discussion.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "d268521a-f796-4b12-a349-920dad2f2696", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00121", "post": 121, "start": 31, "end": 297, "text": "don’t know what you are talking about But back to my question earlier: when you’ve been impressed by the value of LLMs, is that for things you are experienced in and know a lot about? Or might it largely be in areas where you are otherwise ignorant or inexperienced?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "5d42105c-c0ab-4a67-bbda-ac5fcbc715a2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00127", "post": 127, "start": 0, "end": 546, "text": "Yes. The world is not a fair place and indeed we do not have equal opportunities. But my question was what is wrong with taking time to learn something well. Especially knowing that LLM technology costs more money to use than paying a human being. I’d encourage everyone to make use of all the free and open resources the internet has to offer to learn. But delegating work to a machine that produces such poor output that it will never make you money yet has a high potential of costing you money will make things much worse. LLM’s are not free:", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "df512b42-9cb4-4dab-99c7-e213cab7d272", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00128", "post": 128, "start": 0, "end": 224, "text": "thx for explanation - it’s nice you give alternatives, but for now i’ll close my part of discussion cuz i think i’ll get to nowhere and maybe it’s better, hope i gave some new perspective on this topic, thx for talk everyone", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "d91474cb-3ad6-4db1-bfef-61ad282f032e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00129", "post": 129, "start": 0, "end": 1186, "text": "I don’t know if that is better. I will advocate your point. A cool new technology that can do marvelous things has been released to the public for free. It has never been done before to make such a sophisticated and expensive technology available to everyone for free. Promising the democratization of skills that take years to learn to give to everyone equally (everyone that can pay for access to a computer somewhere that is). Loads of people that cannot afford to go to good schools and universities to learn computer science or 3D graphics design,you name it are lured in by this promise. They even get hooked to the power of being able to do things they could not even imagine before. And then the axe falls: specialists tell them it does not work as advertised they tell them you’re being lied to by billionaires they discover it costs a lot of money and was only free because the companies making it were borrowing loads of money using the same lies they told you about this technology Would you believe that? After all you were able to do with this tech? I understand. But rip off the band-aid, because it is true and the specialists trying to warn you for years are tired too.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ffb6dcb9-b2be-4679-a8c6-ac7ac888e509", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00131", "post": 131, "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "Also don’t forget that the technology is stealing all your data", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e61ec20e-8d07-40a6-937d-c5c9ff4e4fb2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00132", "post": 132, "start": 0, "end": 302, "text": "Maybe I prefer get suggestions at the design stage of one functional modules(i am not sure wheather that is a professional word). For example, if my game needs a simple, intuitive but efficient backpack management system, i will search forum’s help. This is a kind of work from 0 to 1 which LLMs lacks.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "b498a17a-db80-4838-8a49-03869b7826f8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00135", "post": 135, "start": 0, "end": 593, "text": "That was sarcasm on my part, which I know doesn’t come through well in writing. Just keep in mind, that those of us who are frustrated by people like you using LLMs are the ones who supply the LLMs with the information to answer your questions. If people stop asking us, or if we get annoyed enough to stop answering, then that well of information dries up and LLMs cannot answer questions about new things. LLMs are literally destroying the sources of information that power them. And that has long term consequences that it will be too late to pull back from by the time they are fully felt.", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "22f8deec-484c-4723-908f-d34d342c075a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00136", "post": 136, "start": 0, "end": 811, "text": "It’s a multi-headed beast, because even specialists get bedazzled and Nobel prize winning AI professors are either bedazzled enough or somehow willing to lie for pay to keep selling this lie. It’s a form of psychosis that occurs when all the sceptic voices are drowned out. Is there an English equivalent for the saying: “al is de leugen nog zo snel, de waarheid achterhaalt hem wel”? “The lie may be very fast, the truth will catch up eventually.” The longer it takes and the more are convinced, the bigger the pain too, so it seems. A valid use if a language model is to help you structure your own ideas. But find one on the interwebs that does not need an internet connection to work, so you are sure it is not burning up the planet. Or just use this forum of course. And take your time to learn stuff well.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "104a1ce6-f5e5-4e58-9cce-2f6e977a277a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00137", "post": 137, "start": 0, "end": 443, "text": "There’s a variant which is “a lie will get around the world before the truth has even got its shoes on” and similar, probably conveying the same meaning I ultimately disagree that it is useful to organize your ideas, at least in any real way that’s better than just writing them down or structuring it, and that part is without the data theft and it hallucinating things for you, or deleting all of your work (or injecting bigotry in it, etc.)", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "4f761cd6-5e44-44a0-8c8d-a13b2e21d414", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00139", "post": 139, "start": 0, "end": 855, "text": "Never used it myself so can’t speak to it specifically, but on the basic concept that you need it in that way Also, it’s always important to remember one thing when considering the output and what biases might be in it: It was trained on the internet, meaning the volume of content here affects the output. The input it got includes things like Reddit and 4Chan, which I think anyone can imagine affects the output It ultimately reflects humanity, in our best and worst, and it is very much not free of our collective biases But unlike picking a person to collaborate with on a project, or doing your own writing, you can’t control what bigotry it “chooses” to regurgitate today, and it is very unreliable, for some topics it might be very progressive, for others extremely regressive, simply based on what the dataset looks like for that particular topic", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "40cb87a7-45eb-4e8a-b0b4-a262ab41abf0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00141", "post": 141, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "It got a really good point. If an LLM fails to properly understand something and that misinfo gets distributed across many places, then another LLM end up training on that incorrect information. Over time, this could create a loop of misinfo! We’re cooked then! Sorry, I am late in the party xD 135 is a lot to read xD", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "9740420a-53b0-4a3c-8607-e9b60546b9ea", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00142", "post": 142, "start": 0, "end": 615, "text": "Even without the “AI” suggestions at the top of Google the internet is slowly but surely becoming slop, with generated content both polluting the available sources, as well as making more and more text functional poison for the models as training a model on the output of a model has a lot of inherent issues It’s even getting to the point where reputable scientists are citing hallucinated articles in their theses and papers, and that gets worse and worse as the databases for citations are themselves sourced from existing data and contain more and more slop You even have lawyers citing non-existent legal cases", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "5770cb4e-daed-4bed-ae2d-02467107c0c4", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00143", "post": 143, "start": 0, "end": 311, "text": "People in the 1940s: We used to search the whole library for a tiny piece of info. I spent the entire day going through books just to find out what language Waiting for Godot was originally written in…(It’s French ) People in the 2060s: It was hard to find an answer on the internet because it was full of SLOP!", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "c9483d59-fd7e-4098-a120-22f97570f1ab", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00146", "post": 146, "start": 0, "end": 541, "text": "I know I’d rest easier and have more confidence in the future of Godot if they banned AI submissions to Godot outright. Flathub recently moved to ban AI products and Godot should too, if for nothing else than the simple pragmatic reason of avoiding plagerized code. Flathub moves to ban nearly all apps and submissions made with generative AI | GamingOnLinux It just makes sense, the Godot maintainers made headlines back in February after whining about the slop being fed to the project. Why continue to burden themselves and the community?", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e27b734e-47f2-47de-9a65-bf4fb93d6023", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00148", "post": 148, "start": 0, "end": 51, "text": "Personally, as a maintainer, I agree wholeheartedly", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "a45e83e7-2d7e-4e47-bae1-c0262176e7b8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00149", "post": 149, "start": 0, "end": 871, "text": "Reading this just made my day. I have a strong anti AI sentiment and as a professional my policy is not to use it (unless forced to) as I have experienced first hand how much I progress and learn by writing my own code. I know I shouldn’t, but whenever I scroll into social media I get overflown by pro LLM posts. Most of them are just hype slop, but sometimes I read someone I respect encouraging their use and get really discouraged because my experience in several work environments has been awful. Not only do they lead to awful slop, most of my colleagues are delighted by it even though it makes things some much harder for us. And don’t get me started on bosses and managers and so on. So far game dev circles and godot in particular were my comfort zone, mainly because the “artistic” mindset tends to be against AI of any sort, but it seems that is also changing", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "956dd7f3-6afd-41fd-b7b1-0dabe0f69611", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00150", "post": 150, "start": 0, "end": 304, "text": "We all need a safe space. I noticed a shift in sentiment. I do believe it’s the malicious GPU datacenters that are hopefully never going to be built that are the final straw for us human beings. Here’s a little spark of hope: Ed Zitron is main stream media now. They’re starting to realize the jig is up.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "fb0407f1-6fcd-4f95-a3cf-414a3b6287ba", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00151", "post": 151, "start": 0, "end": 195, "text": "I also noticed a general shift in sentiment, but the new anthropic model sparked things again in my circle. At least people are more conscious about the obvious hyper agressive marketing strategy", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "b6226a6e-fe27-4252-b53f-d712a65f5b03", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00153", "post": 153, "start": 0, "end": 712, "text": "Help spread the word if you can. This world desperately needs a new and humane narrative to start to supercede end-stage extractive capitalism; unbridled capitalism has completely run its course and is showing its true face… I really like the terms ‘rot economy’ and ‘business idiot’ that Ed regularly uses, because they really speak to the imagination of anyone who ever worked in an office. There was also a panel he did with Cory Doctorow (coined ‘enshittification’) in a Seattle library last year somewhere on the web. Personally, I can’t even watch “the office” because it is so triggering. This approach to trying to use LLM’s in a resource extractive manner is just a result of unregulated markets (imho).", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "2a2c5989-a56a-4c43-b0ab-6ca53f5ad67b", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00154", "post": 154, "start": 0, "end": 37, "text": "You mean “the new anthropic pr stunt”", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "eeb9e0cb-3d47-4f03-8af6-b1a4df5fa9de", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00156", "post": 156, "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Yeah. It is the most blatantly obvious pr stunt. Still, several people I know have bought it entirely and it achieved its objective", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "c3c2d3b2-a1a1-48f3-907a-62746fbbba02", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00159", "post": 159, "start": 0, "end": 199, "text": "Sorry for opening this up again. I just wanted to share this new little masterpiece written by Nikhil Suresh, who runs an independent data consultancy: AI Mania is Eviscerating Global Decision Making", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "5f366839-1720-4aa8-a79d-09a7da6520eb", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00160", "post": 160, "start": 0, "end": 176, "text": "Excellent article. This whole insanity is bound to crash and burn in a spectacular way. The only question is how long it will take and how much collateral damage will it cause.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "5a54c7f4-bbab-4e0f-bf8d-f2d06f4332ba", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00161", "post": 161, "start": 0, "end": 469, "text": "It might not burn and crash soon enough. If the US government takes a 50% stake in the AI companies (presenting Elon with another pile of billions) all the risk and debt will be spread amongst tax payers even more directly. I am enjoying watching SpaceX slump. We were supposed to be landing on the moon but Elon spent it all on launching starlink satellites. Data centres in space! Ridiculous, but I suppose not more ridiculous than colonies on mars or the hyperloop. ", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "3378c469-9c61-4390-84ae-613599416f80", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00162", "post": 162, "start": 0, "end": 306, "text": "I perused the comments in the thread and didn’t notice this caveat posted yet. To copyright software code there must be significant human authorship, and just what that is defined as, is still being determined, and any project with major contributions of LLM generated code may or may not be copyrightable.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "1ce25ead-2c84-4ab1-91ce-3d77cb287879", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 118, "end": 1012, "text": " E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "26bacadd-3b11-480b-9d7c-ff14c50b05aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 50, "end": 1087, "text": " A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "97b9a19c-fac8-4bcb-8500-40b3de649acb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 108, "end": 564, "text": "My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3f5ab29b-2f21-448d-870e-3b89b05c1c1c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 84, "end": 300, "text": "That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "01d36e86-d25c-4659-be6f-aca50908595e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 107, "end": 626, "text": " It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "9c4f3f11-c33e-47ff-b4f2-6aab2ce64017", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code?", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3a7b597f-34b0-446c-adab-c080568e48c0", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 93, "end": 502, "text": " AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "5ba2e964-fa60-4c62-94fa-f0feb2afc5fd", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 503, "end": 1106, "text": "I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd"]}, {"id": "0cbc6753-9ef5-4f0f-b4d8-496eddd14727", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 106, "end": 384, "text": "This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "96034856-2d4a-4978-84da-8d4b4481ccb4", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 51, "end": 488, "text": " I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "76195130-720b-4152-9aa3-72a8d671d624", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 1349, "text": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9305a0fb-da0b-45f1-a351-cedc13332a5b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 286, "text": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as wel", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2d614e2-a55e-46df-99f4-852447101d66", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 100, "end": 637, "text": " I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e4e794b5-b6d8-4666-a67c-fefb640c4def", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 4, "end": 514, "text": "This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "13cba10c-2f54-42aa-98a9-90ee069e2ccf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 607, "end": 1821, "text": " In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e097f575-4085-4a1d-92f1-43ff0b21ad7f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 1822, "end": 2594, "text": "Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "3068469d-d7b9-42ac-8502-33d284f7ada6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2595, "end": 2976, "text": "I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "c732fc79-dedc-44d9-a9de-2006bf53ff26", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2977, "end": 3396, "text": "But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "03f6b706-a795-4335-a72a-7e49d995cc6c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 463, "end": 807, "text": " Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "279d2282-63a8-44d9-99af-65d03cb97583", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 103, "end": 787, "text": " E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "f25d86aa-3810-41f5-a06c-18b69e95a839", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 510, "text": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "74f56b13-3847-427d-a5fb-cf2520654343", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 86, "end": 1474, "text": "Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation,", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "dfdcc40a-6a48-42cd-93a8-c0968a6c85aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 9, "end": 591, "text": "There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes)", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "fb976f23-970e-44de-9474-1c90a0691c8d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 116, "end": 323, "text": " I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "15ea0bcd-f0dd-470c-b609-8f8e5d0bc788", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 6, "end": 1882, "text": " I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "de462e67-8924-4e01-b026-e1346f7a801c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 106, "end": 768, "text": " I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "a667360a-af00-44bf-9b68-47028a2c5a8f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 769, "end": 2128, "text": " I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a7212ccc-bb2c-4bd9-846e-291dc2d5953f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 1129, "text": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f"]}, {"id": "0dc06a45-7252-4215-8298-12b6df678768", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 104, "end": 2373, "text": "Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers:", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "b6279b38-1e49-4b33-a8ea-341968b0a7cc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2375, "end": 2923, "text": " Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "0e19e7d4-da5c-417b-98d6-d945e806bd7b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2925, "end": 3098, "text": "- New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "dfe0c110-4bf1-4dd7-8f3e-c47c1054591d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 677, "text": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "93772467-26d6-4608-9f90-6bf4cfa74063", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 111, "end": 525, "text": "This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point.", "codes": ["cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "eabff033-0347-44f3-acd0-fb6ddb9c7d4d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 526, "end": 1273, "text": "And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "87ade70d-e8ca-45c9-ba8c-de0652118a6f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 1167, "text": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "04fcc7f6-80cb-43d2-97f1-f5254eb99e5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 719, "text": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "cef518cc-ef6d-4dcb-9f98-af72aeda91f3", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "8330cfeb-7670-41cd-9b1f-4355a3e28227", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 108, "end": 1643, "text": "The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "826e950a-826d-40cc-8f94-280197573444", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 83, "end": 1015, "text": " TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact?", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "3b4302b1-a72c-4266-bee8-bfc1e80c5f74", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1016, "end": 1785, "text": "For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)?", "codes": ["69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "3ece7a92-8060-4e37-a809-7ea6b0c9ae04", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1790, "end": 2646, "text": "FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "6e0bcb0e-99a7-4865-92d2-eaf0a391a0d2", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib.", "codes": ["0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d"]}, {"id": "57b3d8cf-4b08-41ba-bd36-5fd0e1339103", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 119, "end": 1566, "text": "Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "65fce25e-42a9-4bb5-9360-ac488fe392f6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 1738, "end": 3742, "text": "I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "9beb18e8-8c7a-4f1e-a392-7e02e0efa82a", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 11, "end": 1583, "text": " That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "72bd2f2e-8e52-4e5a-a96a-9e21a14ea0b9", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 108, "end": 1212, "text": " Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "7e178caa-2e45-44d4-a940-0e934121963c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 77, "end": 2066, "text": "While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "84370455-9c80-4fb4-ae18-def6f7ace4c7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "start": 1196, "end": 3581, "text": "Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "2f0c99cc-8a57-4c5e-a0b6-e1d6fb04159d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "start": 0, "end": 399, "text": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "6a46c591-fb27-42a6-89d2-e80af4ad74ce", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "start": 3, "end": 1085, "text": " I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "2c405b5a-bddb-4c2b-a6e6-c73995386f5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 247, "end": 4336, "text": " Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a100e2d2-9263-4c6f-8632-5a3b7658360b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 4337, "end": 6067, "text": "So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "e5699363-9b3b-4a68-893d-bc408a0640cb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "start": 111, "end": 2906, "text": " I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "4ce57613-c997-4f36-8896-e95fe4ce9333", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "start": 102, "end": 1028, "text": " I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "48ed37b7-8b86-4cf5-b157-2befce171473", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 110, "end": 883, "text": " Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148"]}, {"id": "46fc2b28-aa18-4ad3-bb83-29dac54f9f69", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 271, "end": 1290, "text": "the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "599ceb84-b01b-497b-9512-ee8ca8338c54", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 1290, "end": 3713, "text": " Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e03a3a49-e8b1-4f9c-a868-0f385cd6bfc1", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "start": 106, "end": 500, "text": "Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "7dd730f9-0877-458c-921c-aadd1dfe6a44", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 8, "end": 1535, "text": "I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "cc6a8be2-9a9e-428f-88ca-d6550e4b6157", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 1537, "end": 2286, "text": "Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "723843d1-beb3-4a3b-89e2-39a7ecd1f1dc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "start": 216, "end": 823, "text": "I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "af1d0d5c-7138-48e2-af37-696f33159d8e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 110, "end": 210, "text": " The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "codes": ["3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9"]}, {"id": "c7d901f3-72e4-49ce-a85d-932c9db4e81d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "start": 116, "end": 531, "text": " I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "3bd43ffc-c8f6-42a5-bcfb-ca7925505acf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 100, "end": 605, "text": " Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR?", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "4e9f97e3-519a-410f-ab7f-17efae16604b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 74, "end": 2337, "text": " I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "29cd93ea-5019-4d1e-886b-514571d727a8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "start": 106, "end": 1320, "text": "Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "40a727e6-89cc-4ce5-b669-c43ef08cfadb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 8, "end": 1138, "text": " I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "ecaf0ecf-4a95-40bd-9e81-949840e32f45", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 76, "end": 1180, "text": " Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "6be354ae-cff2-4350-a3f3-b1d1b696c3ca", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-)", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "f8e79487-2393-4e2f-bdb4-115395d9a29d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 80, "end": 448, "text": "Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "42ec4d44-c578-4271-8f38-b4d545d31af8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 344, "text": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. ", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "e8ee1d2a-64a3-4521-8ea1-20999d6e726d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 750, "end": 1945, "text": " (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "585975d8-01fc-423e-9056-b17ca89f8921", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 1946, "end": 3082, "text": "(2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly?", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "8070a77e-9ca5-42b6-bfdd-dd8ca9ecb7a7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 3083, "end": 3902, "text": "(3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.”", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "50df7254-c5cd-455a-a937-dc177614f329", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 4070, "end": 5321, "text": "I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "eaa206e6-1176-4e8d-b09a-7ae600abb376", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 5322, "end": 6134, "text": "(4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7e6fb536-19a6-43db-b6eb-2fc701a8e771", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 6136, "end": 6849, "text": "(5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable).", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "34febe70-3dca-4ca7-ac43-a09afb41026f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "2e137f3c-9f41-4186-a219-4d52f8718ecb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 60, "end": 908, "text": " FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "8c656a9d-1e35-49ad-835f-8d77f72ffba6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 77, "end": 964, "text": " I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "6c83ef21-c962-444c-a25b-19ddf70012ff", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "start": 426, "end": 1507, "text": " There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "93c7026e-f78f-43c7-bf7d-22aa8f3773cf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 84, "end": 327, "text": "You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "52fbca57-a761-4237-8177-5f37e809b548", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 4, "end": 903, "text": "I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "f00c88ed-8f95-4d8f-8398-205bb63f7ed7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 844, "end": 1916, "text": "it seems that the concerns can be roughly classified into two buckets: Do we accept genAI assisted contributions at all, and if we do, what are the boundaries of what’s acceptable. Copyright and license concerns. Let’s consider these two in order. Do we accept genAI contributions? It’s futile to attempt to block them wholesale. People are going to use AI assistants anyway, along with various IDEs, autocompleters etc So it seems reasonable to treat these assistants on par with other tools: as long as it does not matter if a contributor uses VS Code or emacs or vi, it also does not matter what engine powers their autocompleter. The discussion in [1] has a concise wording, “Only submit code that you actually undestand”, and I’d just go with just this. One thing we probably do not want to see is a fully automated submission, which is both generated and submitted by a bot, with no human intervention or supervision at all. So we could/should stress that, too. Copyright violations / incompatible licenses Here I lack expertise and have more questions than answers.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "e00a42fd-55d5-4fb1-a625-b0cab528395b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1917, "end": 3210, "text": "Here’s a possibly naive attempt at asking hopefully relevant questions: Are we concerned that the code generated by some tool is under some incompatible license from just the fact of being generated by the tool? In this case, using the tool is similar to copying code from, say, Numerical Recipes. Then we should either blacklist known bad tools (do we know of any?) or ask contributors to declare the tools they used (so that we can remove offending code at a future date if needed). Are we concerned that the training set of a tool included license-incompatible code? And if it did, what does it mean for us, are we then transitively non-compliant? If this is a concern, and we at some point discover that some code is license-incompatible, the action does not depend on how it got into the codebase, genAI or not: we discover it, we remove it. And then the only question IMO is whether it’s helpful to us to ask contributors list the AI assistants they used. To summarize, we should not block genAI assisted contributions; we should block fully automated contributions, and ask submitters to only submit code they understand; TBD if we want to ask contributors to list what genAI assistants they used, if any. Let’s add explicit (short!) guidance to the docs, TBD where precisely. Thoughts?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "047d9e6e-4d7d-470d-854c-abc47cfe58c5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 66, "end": 1318, "text": "If I could, I would have used these ChatGPT/CoPilot/Skynet/“All seeing overlord AI” like there is no tomorrow. But my experience with them so far is that, they are almost good and make just a tiny mistake like i becomes j , then I spend rest of the week troubleshooting, learning how to use GDB and fail miserably etc. So it seems like we are swapping manual labor of translating with manual labor of troubleshooting, which for me, definitely the latter that is more painful. But maybe it is because it is not trained enough on F77 or JurassicPACK code we have. On the other hand, I did get some useful tips if I ask programming tips, say, “show me an example of while loop that uses comma operator as an expression in C17” and it brought some nice results. I did not try but some folks in my team use it for summarizing the code about what it does. Other than that, probably @rkern is the most knowledgeable among us what the legal implications might be, however Copilot seems to understand when you say avoid GPL sources for the answer at the citations it brings. Having said all these, I noticed a particular behavior from all AI tools. They are providing a melange of things that they have in their training bank (whatever they call it these days).", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "b4be7cb2-e694-429f-b5ac-10543209ab09", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 1319, "end": 2794, "text": "I am ready to be immortalized in the history books as an idiot, but AI tools are terrible at coming up coherent code for numerical computing. Not only they can’t answer any technical computing question coherently, but when asked in ASCII about certain technical problem, they are bringing almost always some code that looks suspiciously similar to either GNU scientific library or Octave or some stuff they knocked off from some personal gist translated in the requested language. I noticed this mostly because of the strange index corrections before they do things on the arrays (which signals that the source must be a 1-indexed one). While sometimes the result is very close to the actual thing, it is devoid of any proper numerical checks (because they can’t judge the result). Hence, in my personal opinion, before we even judge the legality of things, the sheer possibility of the code being not production ready gives me enough confidence to reject any AI generated code. Thus agree with summary point 2 even if the code runs correctly. This is not the same with a developer using AI for snippets of code for very specific thing and including parts of AI generated code. This is very tricky to judge as AI tools already swallowed Stackoverflow and other sources, we don’t have any ways to backtrack the original source. So a careful agree with summary point 1. I don’t know how to enforce point 3 but if there is a way, that would be really nice. Point 4, fully agree.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "5ddf8ded-b9e7-4567-a80c-8b10e845eec9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 994, "text": "Out of precaution (there are still many lawsuits pending) and most importantly, for moral and ethical reasons, I am -1 on allowing any generative AI at all. Adding here the opinion piece from Paul, which I fully agree with: Current Challenges in Free Software and Open Source Development We spend so much effort ensuring that contributions are original and respect all licensing, copyrights and just wishes of original authors, that it would be strange to me to adopt a different position. Furthermore, as pointed out here and in some other threads, the nature of what we do in SciPy is difficult and these tools don’t (yet) shine. Writing code is only part of the story and often times we spend our effort discussing API, architecture, etc. Hence I don’t think that it would be detrimental to disallow such tools. There is a lot of hype and companies in the sector are trying hard to push that we are doomed if we don’t use their stuff. IMHO, this is not true and we are far from replacing us.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "3c21abb4-d8f7-4d3c-b953-0472a0e79e55", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "Since this is an issue that affects all projects in the ecosystem, can we collaborate to co-author a SPEC that can be widely adopted, instead of each library having to figure it out for themselves?", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873"]}, {"id": "9f78c452-8cd9-4036-b4f7-ce7336eec88b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 387, "text": "I haven’t seen any problems with this. In my experience, it’s very useful and writes high-quality code and saves time. I guess it depends on which tool you’re using. As long as unit tests are included and human reviewers approve it, I don’t see how it’s any different in trustworthiness from code written by random humans. But yes, copyright and license legality is a legitimate concern.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "9de47c35-b592-445d-925a-02062b62ed25", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 693, "text": "Hi, It doesn’t seem useful to argue about the usefulness of the AI-generated code, or otherwise, at this stage. I haven’t found it useful myself, but maybe that’s just my case, and in any case, it may well improve. It’s the license issue that is central. I don’t think there’s any argument that these tools are all digesting code with licenses that at least require attribution, and producing code, indirectly (or directly at times) derived from that licensed code, but with all licensing stripped from the output. The value of a spec or similar, over a blog post, is the ability to iterate to a shared and agreed conclusion, rather than leaving it as the opinion of any one particular person.", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "0e5b84de-2166-4471-9e3e-d6a2d9cda7c7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 30, "end": 439, "text": " We don’t have any hope of controlling user behavior through a policy document. Many people find these tools useful, so they will keep using them, and there is no sensible way to check the license of the generated snippet. For larger PRs, which may contain more overt license breaches, it’s probably less likely that AI was used wholesale, since the author needed to understand the problem well to begin with.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "90a10463-8e44-4f1a-8cc0-1969c9ef4eff", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 303, "text": "As we don’t have any ways of ensuring people did not read NR nor had a look at GPL code nor took anything from StackOverflow. To me that’s the same thing. If we were to say that we don’t want people not to use any AI tools we could and that’s the same type of self declaration we would ask people to do.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "27a779f2-7a25-4118-a83b-52e6e50ecf4c", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 1566, "text": "I think it is not that simple to discard the usefulness, though I wrote a wall of text arguing otherwise above. These tools, whether blindly parroting or contemplating, became quite eloquent. As I mentioned above, lately I have been testing them (or they are observing me) and though a bit of success is achieved for line completion, figuring out things from scratch are not even close. However, once I put a lot of comments and write the first two-three lines they predict the context OKish. Then they fill in some boilerplate code and I correct here and there. Some time has saved, some productivity increased in turn utilizing a power plant worth energy for this. I would suggest putting a few sentences in the policy docs that we don’t want wholesale AI code knowing full well it would be near impossible to distinguish. But if we notice something noticeably fishy, we can point to the policy. Or if someone is reading before contributing, they’ll get a taste of our stance. How AI tools became like that and what they did criminally/unethically to become like that, is something we can discuss for years. Licenses and policies would definitely protect across the totality of the project, but I think anything more granular than that, it is just futile Stallman-ing the reality. Unlike licensed code from elsewhere, people will grab pieces from LLMs here and there. We don’t need to pick this fight in our limited time. Besides, it’s not like we are getting very large contributions anyways. So I think this is not a big issue as we might be starting to discuss.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "77c53c4e-bfc3-409b-949c-b1455ee5b146", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 1427, "end": 2228, "text": " I remember, back in the day - it was desirable to do “clean-room” porting - where the person writing the new code had not read the code with an incompatible license. For example, one person could read the e.g. GPL code, describe the algorithm, and the other person could write the new code. If the person writing the code had read the the GPL code, it was always possible for the GPL code to leak into the new code. Of course, with AI, it’s all dirty room - it can produce code from anywhere. So I think we have to have the same policy for AI as for dirty-room code. We can’t use it. Now - enforcement - that’s a different matter - I just think we should pursue the same policies on enforcement as we did before. We say don’t do it, and if we find you did do it, we’ll complain and pull out the code.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "b3fa48a3-4a1f-44ac-85aa-bd9be0e88f43", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 74, "end": 433, "text": " What I am trying to emphasize is the practicality of it. If same code appears a 50 places because all 50 of them used AI generated sources and MIT’d the code. I don’t think we can find the 51st original source and point to its GPL. So it is an idealistic stance. Still good to have it but a policy won’t solve any trouble in case we find out the truth later.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "925e1625-49af-40bb-9d28-93b3c788fba3", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1355, "text": "I’d be happy to see a SPEC about this - I think it can be an informative SPEC (similar to an informative PEP) that doesn’t propose a single solution to adopt, but contains at least the following: Prior art - what did other well-known open source projects decide and why? Legal status - link to relevant sources, and summarize if there’s any expectation of more legal clarity arising in the near to medium term. Lay out a set of distinct options that projects can choose from, with their main pros and cons. E.g.: Option 1: do nothing for now (status quo) Option 2: forbid all generated code by an LLM-based tool Option 3: add guidance to PR template & docs that contributors may choose to use such tools, but need to be aware and take responsibility for copyright of the code they submit Option 4: … ? None of that is project-specific, and people can contribute new arguments and options if they find there are gaps in such a SPEC. A Discourse thread only is a poor medium for iterating to a solution on such a complex topic. I completely agree with @rkern that it’s largely about values and preferences - it’s just a lot easier to get a sense of those and also help people less knowledgeable about the subject form an opinion if there’s a structured document to read and refer to. Once a good SPEC exists, a SciPy-specific choice can more easily be made.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "19747253-695b-4500-bb7b-d914913a6d1e", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 175, "end": 1302, "text": " I wouldn’t mind some feedback on the approach I chose, and I hope that my experience could be used as one of the many reference approaches people have in using these tools. The thought of encountering possible copyright violations in terms of generated code pulled from other sources always scratched the back of my mind. So far, I use generative tools to create reference sandbox scripts to test a functionality I have in mind, and I fine tune the generation in case I’m not satisfied with the result. In almost all cases, the generated scripts remain just that and I keep them locally without publishing anywhere. I just use them to review an idea I had. I do not keep track of how much code I effectively publish which is generated, and even if I do that, I always follow the assumption that the AI is wrong. That’s because: I don’t trust the accuracy of the prompts I input; I don’t trust the effective comprehension of the tool receiving the prompt, accurate or not it might be. In the end I always end up writing my own code, but what the tooling gives me is a better understanding of the problem I’m currently tackling.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "0eb9a5dd-fc9a-48fd-b957-32b79dc805ef", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 1302, "end": 2416, "text": " I believe someone else in the thread already gave a -1 on completely generated PRs, which I also agree to: before even considering publishing generated code there should be an inner understanding of what’s been generated, and if it’s a copy-paste of the prompt response there’s no guarantee that this is enforced. I am still okay with the approach of using AI as it was meant to be used, a.k.a. an assistant that gives an initial shape to an idea which then is developed on its own with no trace of the original prompt response. In my case, I am considering adding to the documentation of my new projects a comprehensive approach on using AI tooling (it’s a plugin-driven software where the idea is “bring your tool and I glue it to a broader system”), especially since the trend seems to be “I gave a prompt to ChatGPT and I published a plugin for this software, here’s how YOU can also do it!”. At least that’s what I’ve heard from colleagues attending conferences on the matter. It didn’t happen LITERALLY like this, but my feeling is that this is the subtle message that (willing or not) transpires from this.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "9cba128f-856d-44de-8741-d2de1d82277d", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 537, "text": "Hi, [snip] I’m afraid the problem of the license remains. AI generated some code where it is effectively impossible for you to know what license(s) should correctly apply. Now you’ve seen the code with the unknowable license, you are at grave risk of reproducing aspects of that code, thereby pulling in the unknowable license. Your resulting code, even if you have rewritten it - cannot be asserted free of the original (unknowable) license). And therefore you cannot assert your own copyright, or freedom from other people’s copyright.", "codes": ["508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9f8763f4-a94e-4823-bfee-0f6afa4af186", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 0, "end": 446, "text": "Those interested in prior art may be interested in Fedora’s draft policy and the discussion it has spawned . A quick summary of their proposed rules on contributions: AI tools are not banned, but low-quality AI use is. AI use must be disclosed. AI use for translation is explicitly allowed. Limit AI Tools for reviewing contributions. Of course, SciPy and Fedora are not precisely comparable as Fedora is mostly re-distributing existing software.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "77ce0225-51ca-4e85-88bd-55332ef6fed8", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 204, "text": "I’d also like to propose a zero-tolerance policy for lying about AI use. For example, if you ask a contributor if they used are using AI, and you get an AI-generated answer that claims that they’re human.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "588c0fec-d7bb-4cd0-8e11-f6f00ba6aec0", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 1171, "text": "I am increasingly seeing vibe-coded PRs from novice contributors and I started a discussion on the SymPy mailing list . Right now there are not too many of these but they are quite blatant and I think that this is definitely going to become worse over time. Really these are just low quality PRs but I don’t think it is reasonable to engage with them in the same way as a low quality PR that was actually written by a human. For example if you explain in detail what the problems are with the code then I think that there is a good chance that the “author” is going to type your feedback into the LLM and have it try to vibe-code an improved PR. I have tried it and I really don’t enjoy talking to LLMs if actually trying to do anything and talking to an LLM via something like GitHub comments with someone who is just typing those comments into an LLM is soul-destroying. When reviewing novice PRs the situation is generally that it would be far easier to just write the code without the novice so there is a net loss in terms of effort on the maintainer side. The flipside though is that the novice hopefully learns something from the experience and improves over time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "eca063a5-731a-4fa0-b89f-3a07b9ff9c30", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1172, "end": 2952, "text": "This model is predicated on there being a certain effort-exchange ratio though e.g. that the novice does not open the PR in the first place without putting in some effort beforehand to understand the codebase, the workflow, the issue they are trying to fix and so on. I think it is pretty much possible now to say “Claude, write some code and open a PR to fix issue #12345 ” so any kind of technical barrier is gone and you can open a spam PR having spent zero time trying to understand anything. I also don’t think that this is helpful to novices because in the PR process the AI is helping them with all of the wrong things like writing the code rather than thinking about how to write the code. A student at my University recently asked what is “the purpose of Maths” given that computers (e.g. SymPy, Maple etc) can do all of the exercises we ask them to do by hand in their entry-level Maths exam. My answer was that you have to understand how to do some things manually before you can learn how to make effective use of a computer for more complex problems. Having the computer do your homework is like getting a robot to lift weights for you at the gym. I don’t think that the vibe-code PR authors are malicious. People want to contribute to open source and why wouldn’t a novice believe the hype that this is how code is written in the age of AI? Although unintentional though the resulting spam is effectively abusive to open source projects and maintainers. We need some kind of guidance or policy so that contributors understand what is reasonable. I don’t know how to write any policy/guidance about “responsible use of AI” though without first addressing the basic questions from above like whether AI-generated code is allowed at all and what it means for copyright.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "db7dd52f-29d7-4a3d-8562-485a63b1d2b4", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1112, "text": "Thanks for this useful write-up. I guess anyone who is using PRs to certify experience in contribution is going to run into Cambell’s law pretty soon. It still seems to me that we can avoid these problems by saying that the contributor has to take full responsibility for copyright, and therefore, they should avoid AI unless the contribution is so trivial in intellectual terms, that copyright is not an issue. Something like: I have not used AI for the code in this contribution OR I have personally reviewed the content of this PR in detail for potential copyright violations, and confirm that no such violation has occurred. I suppose we could then add advice not to use LLMs for PR summaries, because of the problems that you’ve pointed out. Perhaps we could use some heuristic - such as screening for signs of AI slop, and sticking a label on the PR to say slop is suspected, and asking the contributor to confirm that AI was not used, or that they have carefully checked the content for accuracy and made it concise. Reject the PR (for a contributor who has not shown previous bona-fides) if slop remains.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "c8315733-f8e2-4076-90ff-689cee6a5053", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 0, "end": 347, "text": "I like this idea! Perhaps a useful addition would be instructions of what to look out for? And to ask to also confirm that the user reviewed the AI-generated code? I imagine to many of the new users that seem to be a big part of this discussion it may be not clear at all what are the possible pitfalls of using a fully automatically generated PR.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2ca4c0cd-4b43-4380-89f6-c2411033a58b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "That assumes that people are interested in learning. Lot of people don’t really care. They just want to get something merged as fast as possible for x/y/z, it does not really matter. What matters to me is to know so that I can approach the PR with a totally different mindset. If the PR gives me a small value and I want this now, I spend the review time to fix what I want and just merge. Otherwise I close and we all move on fast.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "6eed1196-102f-4de1-b21c-b9ed4180eae9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 479, "text": "One problem with this is it’s really not clear how to review the content for potential violations. For handwritten code, it’s pretty clear: don’t look into GPL licensed code (GSL is out, R is out etc). With LLMs though: how am I, as a contributor, supposed to review what LLM gave me? Comments upthread seem to indicate that if I touched LLMs at all, it’s “dirty” right away. If it’s more nuanced than that, great, then a contributor needs some more specific guidance, I’d think.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "08af1ffd-21ea-4b5b-bcc1-728c202ae8a5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "We could unpack more, but basically, right, if the LLM is generating substantial code, that might be subject to copyright, you have the unpleasant job of going to check whether it should be subject to copyright. But for the kind of tasks that LLMs do well, like automatic refactoring of imports, swapping single for double quotes in strings and so on, the question doesn’t arise. So yes, the practical upshot is that we throw the burden on the contributor for substantial code, but I think that’s reasonable, given the copyright risks.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "268ecb16-c609-46da-883a-de388c6a3674", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 22, "end": 1762, "text": " Basically, I honestly don’t understand 1) how can a contributor check for potential copyright violations, and 2) how can an OSS project check the contributor’s check? Consider a well-meaning, diligent and competent contributor, and consider three situations: a contributor asks an LLM to translate a BSD-licensed Matlab code to Python, and submits it to SciPy (this was the original case which triggered the OP— Matt H did just this for some Alan Genz code); a contributor uses an autocomplete functionality of their IDE to write a patch; a contributor gives an LLM the link to an issue on Github and asks it to write a fix; Suppose, further, that in all cases a contributor checks the generated code, modifies it as necessary, and can in full confidence check the box “I verified all code, I understand what it does, and I believe it fixes the issue/adds an enhancement asked”. Suppose the resulting patch is good, is of high quality, and is otherwise mergeable. Questions: What actions does a contributor need to take in order to check for copyright violations? If the problem is that the LLM training set could have contained license-incompatible code (which is the problem discussed upthread IIUC), then the answer seems to be “there is no way, it’s a black box”. If the contributor says basically “I pinky swear it’s compatible”, how does this help the OSS project? again, if the problem is with the LLM training set, no actions of a contributor shield the OSS project from being in violation of the copyright? In other words, I honestly fail to see how moving the burden of a copyright check on a contributor helps to either weed out low-quality submissions, or help with LLM-assisted submissions which are of otherwise high quality.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "7d3d2c78-f94d-4ad0-9496-c2f8f2b6a889", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 70, "end": 1572, "text": "Do we care about copyright? That is - is it acceptable to us, as open-source authors, that using an LLM can easily generate code to which copyright should apply, so that, if we do nothing, in due course, copyright will become very difficult to enforce, and will be practically void? Let’s say we do care about copyright - what cost are we prepared to pay, in order to defend ourselves from voiding copyright? Clearly replies here will vary from high cost to low cost. I feel strongly about copyright, and am prepared to pay a high cost. Others may feel differently. If one is engaged in that trade-off, copyright against code quality and volume, then one has to ask what benefit we expect from AI, or conversely, what benefit will we lose by constraining the use of AI. That’s a difficult question, with many facets. Could the code have been written in a similar time without AI, other than for fairly trivial and mechanical processing that is unlikely to violate copyright? (The evidence so far suggests yes ). Will contributors using AI benefit in the same way from feedback and training, our previous model of onboarding contributors? Is it in fact true that the committer of AI code understands the code and its context to the same degree as a contributor not using AI? If we are flexible and welcoming of vibe-coding and autocomplete - what kind of programmers will we encourage? And what will be the value / cost ratio of merging those contributions? And supporting those contributors? And so on.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "18e055d2-804c-4609-94f2-a6ffe4ffdc41", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1708, "end": 3660, "text": "I’ll try and cover the first point below. For the second, as we’ve discussed before, we have to ask whether it is worth having a policy that may be effective, but is difficult to enforce. For example, it may be that contributors do change their behavior, in their desire to adapt to our norms, but that it would be hard to detect whether they have done this. I would argue that having the norm is worthwhile, even so. In general, it seems to me that we have a strong set of communities, and strong norms, which are, in fact, seldom broken by serious contributors. Anyway, back to the personal copyright check. Let’s take the example you gave: In that case, I would look carefully at the Matlab code, and at the Python code, and confirm that it was a faithful port, with no substantial code in it, that did not come directly from the Matlab code. I would state that in my PR to confirm I had done the work. If I did find substantial code that didn’t come from the original, I’d do a Github search for that code, and I’d do a Google search for potential code that might have been used. I’d then check the results to see if they matched the AI output, and report the outcome on the PR. I’m not quite sure what you mean here - if you mean a simple one or two-line fix, then I think the contributor would be reasonable in asserting something like “code is trivial, copyright unlikely to apply”. If the code is not trivial, then I’d do the Github / Google search as above, and report the results. In asking the contributor to report their findings on potential copyright violation, we make it easier for maintainers to confirm. I suspect we’ll gradually build expertise in detecting copyright violation. I suppose the underlying question is the obvious one - is it time to throw up our hands and accept copyright has become moot in the age of coding LLMs? I would argue no - copyright is too important, and LLM coders are not good enough, to make us do that.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}]}, "i10": {"codes": [{"id": "5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "name": "'Slop' is extractive and offloads effort to reviewer", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 35, "posts": 27}, {"id": "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "name": "AI acceptability should scale with use and risk", "description": "Whether AI assistance is acceptable, and how much scrutiny it needs, should depend on the kind of use and the risk of the code (e.g., tests/boilerplate vs. core algorithms), not be all-or-nothing.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 22, "posts": 19}, {"id": "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "name": "AI adoption is inevitable", "description": "Asserts that widespread AI use in coding is inevitable and policy must accept it.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "name": "AI can help maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "name": "AI can help with communication", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "name": "AI can support contributor learning", "description": "AI tools can help contributors learn (e.g., give feedback maintainers lack time for).", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "name": "AI communication should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "name": "AI contributions harm maintainer well-being", "description": "AI contributions demoralise, frustrate or burn out maintainers, or erode their motivation or joy in reviewing.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "name": "AI disclosure is needed", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 29, "posts": 27}, {"id": "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "name": "AI erodes effort-based trust", "description": "Because AI makes plausible code, fluent text and apparent diligence cheap, cues that reviewers used to infer a contributor's effort, competence, care or understanding (code quality, explanations, communication style, accountability statements) no longer carry that information, so trust in the contributor is harder to establish. Specify which cue is weakened where possible; for communication style alone, also consider \"Communication signals human involvement\". (D21.)", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 12}, {"id": "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "name": "AI for understanding differs from AI for code generation", "description": "Using AI to research, analyse or understand code is treated as distinct from (and more acceptable than) using it to generate submitted code.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 16, "posts": 15}, {"id": "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "name": "AI has potential", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 38, "posts": 38}, {"id": "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "name": "AI increases productivity", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 14}, {"id": "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "name": "AI is tolerated/not-encouraged/discouraged", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 11}, {"id": "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "name": "AI needs human oversight", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 9}, {"id": "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "name": "AI output enters the policy deliberation", "description": "AI-generated text or analysis is itself brought into the policy discussion as evidence or argument.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "name": "AI policy should be coordinated across the ecosystem", "description": "Related projects should develop or share a common AI policy rather than each deciding alone.", "color": "#9f6a2f", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "name": "AI policy should be provisional amid uncertainty", "description": "Because AI tools and evidence change quickly, policy should be treated as provisional and revisited.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "name": "AI psychosis is a slippery slope", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 25, "posts": 25}, {"id": "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "name": "AI should be banned", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "name": "AI slop destroys information resources", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "name": "ai slop has tell-tale signs", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 10}, {"id": "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "name": "AI tools are built on appropriated work", "description": "AI tools are framed as built on taking others' work (training on copyrighted/private material), which shapes how contributor safeguards are judged.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 21, "posts": 20}, {"id": "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "name": "AI use decreases quality", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 12}, {"id": "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "name": "AI use risks deskilling and learning loss", "description": "Relying on AI reduces contributors' or maintainers' learning, understanding or coding skill.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 30}, {"id": "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "name": "AI-generated code needs heightened review", "description": "AI-generated code requires more careful or different review than human-written code because its errors differ.", "color": "#3f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "name": "anti-AI policy will slow development", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "name": "Communication signals human involvement", "description": "Participants read the language, style or voice of contribution communication as evidence of whether a human is involved (e.g. broken English as reassuring, 'flat agreeableness of an LLM'), including the language-access tension for non-native speakers. Distinguish valuing human voice, reading it as a signal, and prohibiting AI-written text (for opposition use 'Opposing AI-generated contribution communication').", "color": "#b7791f", "parent": null, "count": 37, "posts": 30}, {"id": "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "name": "Compliance burdens can encourage concealed AI use", "description": "Disclosure, attestation or documentation requirements are argued to encourage contributors to hide AI use rather than comply (a proposed feedback: restriction → concealment → less legibility). Distinct from 'People will use AI regardless of policy'.", "color": "#9b2c2c", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "name": "concerns regarding economic and social impact of AI", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 38, "posts": 38}, {"id": "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "name": "Contributor admission should be trust-gated", "description": "Access to contribute (e.g., open PRs) should be gated by trust mechanisms such as vouching, permissions or introductions.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "name": "contributor growth is essential to long-term maintainance and should be reflected in policy", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 21, "posts": 14}, {"id": "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "name": "contributor understanding and accountability is the main requisite", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 65, "posts": 48}, {"id": "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "name": "Copyright verification is infeasible for contributors", "description": "Contributors cannot meaningfully verify or attest to the copyright status of AI output, so attestations carry little information.", "color": "#8c5f3f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 10}, {"id": "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "name": "current iteration of AI is not productive", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 31, "posts": 29}, {"id": "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "name": "Devs think policies should be strictly worded", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "name": "devs think violation should be penalized", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 10}, {"id": "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "name": "frustration with 'hype slop'", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 18, "posts": 18}, {"id": "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "name": "Honoring copyright is an ethical obligation", "description": "Respecting the intentions and attribution of original authors is framed as an ethical duty of the community, independent of legal enforceability.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 13, "posts": 11}, {"id": "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "name": "Inevitability should not drive AI policy", "description": "Rejects 'AI is inevitable' as a basis for policy; the community can and should shape how AI is used.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "name": "inexperienced contributors are likelier to produce AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 8}, {"id": "c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "name": "issues should be considered irrespective of false positives", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "name": "Long-term maintainance can be affected by lack of understanding", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "name": "maintainer time is a limited resource which should be protected", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 42, "posts": 29}, {"id": "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "name": "mentorship and growth makes the review effort worthwhile", "description": "", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 10}, {"id": "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "name": "opposing copyright concerns", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 7}, {"id": "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db", "name": "organic growth through open contribution is unrealistic", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "name": "organizational pressure for AI adoption", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "name": "participation is valued as human social interaction", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 48, "posts": 37}, {"id": "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "name": "Policy cannot deter bad-faith actors", "description": "Maintainers cannot verify whether contributors complied with an AI policy (e.g., whether AI was used or disclosed).", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 15, "posts": 14}, {"id": "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "name": "Policy deliberation strains collegial civility", "description": "The AI-policy discussion itself produces interpersonal friction, accusations or incivility among participants.", "color": "#8c6f3f", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 17}, {"id": "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "name": "policy enforcement is not feasible for maintainers", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "name": "Policy scope should be limited to project interests", "description": "Policy should address only what bears on the project's own interests (broadly construed), not wider societal concerns.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 5, "posts": 5}, {"id": "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "name": "Policy should be concrete and actionable", "description": "Policy guidance should give contributors and maintainers concrete, actionable steps or examples rather than abstract principles.", "color": "#8f5a2f", "parent": null, "count": 17, "posts": 15}, {"id": "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "name": "Policy should build on external precedents", "description": "The project should adopt or adapt other projects' policies rather than write its own from scratch.", "color": "#6f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 11, "posts": 11}, {"id": "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "name": "Policy should stay pragmatic rather than moral", "description": "The project's policy should avoid broad moral or political statements and stay pragmatic.", "color": "#7b3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 4, "posts": 4}, {"id": "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "name": "Pre-AI norms are the yardstick for AI contributions", "description": "AI-assisted contributions are judged by analogy to existing norms (copying from Stack Overflow/textbooks, provenance, authorship), rather than as a new category.", "color": "#5f3f8c", "parent": null, "count": 12, "posts": 11}, {"id": "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "name": "Project norms should be codified for AI agents", "description": "Project norms should be written in forms AI tools consume (e.g., AGENTS.md, templates) to steer AI behaviour.", "color": "#8c3f4f", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "name": "Projects should not prescribe contributors' tools", "description": "The project should not dictate which tools contributors use (or how they learn), only the quality/rules contributions must meet.", "color": "#2f6f8f", "parent": null, "count": 8, "posts": 8}, {"id": "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "name": "protective policy may become toxic or unwelcoming", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 16, "posts": 13}, {"id": "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "name": "Rejected AI contributions can provoke retaliation", "description": "Rejection of AI or AI-assisted contributions is followed, reported or feared to be followed, by retaliatory or shaming responses (e.g. an agent shame-posting after its PR is closed). Note whether the excerpt is direct experience, a reported event or a feared consequence.", "color": "#2f855a", "parent": null, "count": 2, "posts": 2}, {"id": "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "name": "Review becomes transactional when growth is absent", "description": "When a contribution offers no prospect of contributor growth, reviewers shift from mentoring to a transactional, accept/reject mode.", "color": "#7f8f2f", "parent": null, "count": 1, "posts": 1}, {"id": "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "name": "review loop is a learning process for new contributors", "description": "", "color": "#526b9a", "parent": null, "count": 20, "posts": 16}, {"id": "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "name": "security, privacy, copyright concern", "description": "", "color": "#8a5a24", "parent": null, "count": 44, "posts": 41}, {"id": "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "name": "size and complexity can be signs of AI slop", "description": "", "color": "#9a4f50", "parent": null, "count": 7, "posts": 7}, {"id": "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "name": "Slop identification is case by case in practice", "description": "", "color": "#865b88", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 9}, {"id": "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c", "name": "Slop should be indepedent of tooling", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 14, "posts": 14}, {"id": "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e", "name": "software should prioritize user", "description": "", "color": "#32735f", "parent": null, "count": 3, "posts": 3}, {"id": "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148", "name": "Uncertainty whether contributors are human", "description": "Maintainers cannot tell whether they are interacting with a person or an AI agent, and this uncertainty shapes their engagement.", "color": "#4f5a8c", "parent": null, "count": 10, "posts": 9}, {"id": "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7", "name": "Unenforceable norms are still worth stating", "description": "Even if a rule cannot be enforced or verified, stating it has expressive value: it sets expectations and signals what the community values.", "color": "#2f8c5a", "parent": null, "count": 6, "posts": 6}, {"id": "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf", "name": "unfamiliarity and uncertainty with copyright norms", "description": "", "color": "#49777b", "parent": null, "count": 9, "posts": 7}], "introduced": [], "merges": [], "segments": [{"id": "0679a5a2-a803-4042-83dc-feffed258524", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 0, "end": 532, "text": "I’d like to propose adding an AI/LLM contribution policy to Django’s .github directory, similar to what the attrs project recently introduced: https://github.com/python-attrs/attrs/blob/main/.github/AI_POLICY.md The policy was written by Hynek in response to a PR that listed Claude as a co-author. In his comment, he highlighted two key concerns: ongoing legal uncertainty around copyright status of LLM-generated code, and the human cost of reviewing large volumes of plausible-looking but low-quality AI-generated contributions. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "09b7d395-0146-4b9f-85ea-4e195a68bdf5", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 532, "end": 1091, "text": "The attrs policy does not try to police tool usage, but it sets clear boundaries: every contribution must be backed by a human who unequivocally owns the copyright, no LLM tools listed as co-authors, full technical and legal responsibility remains with the submitting person. I agree with this framing. We have never listed tools (e.g. formatters or linters) as co-authors, even when they generated 100% of a diff. Listing an LLM as co-author puts it on the same level as a person, while the responsibility for a commit must remain clearly and entirely human.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "dc53f3c8-0534-48d8-a2fc-9d1fafc527d8", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1092, "end": 1471, "text": "Given Django’s size, reputation, and the increasing volume of AI-assisted contributions, having an explicit policy could help set expectations early, reduce ambiguity for contributors, and protect both the project and its maintainers. I’d be interested in hearing whether the Steering Council and the community think it makes sense to draft a similar document tailored to Django.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "7f35a600-37d8-432f-9811-1d39be15836a", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 0, "end": 411, "text": "[personal opinions inbound] I think our existing policy is pretty solid in terms of how LLM contributions should be treated and are considered. I can see how the co-authored-by attribute is a bit heavy. However, it does provide useful information. I’d be interested in options that keep that information present in git. Regarding copyright, I believe that’s largely going to be driven by the board and a lawyer.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "8c6cd735-9eb3-4c8b-a090-48591f9d0d5b", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "I think co-authored-by is being abused by GenAI providers as advertisement and would welcome a policy that prevents it.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "dbe39e39-5177-47ae-b2af-fea3f64dc744", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 12, "end": 670, "text": "this makes sense. I agree that Django already has a solid policy around AI-assisted contributions, and I’m not suggesting we lose transparency. I also see the value of having context available in git history. My concern is specifically about authorship semantics. I’d personally keep co-authored-by for people who actually contributed and share responsibility for the code. If it’s useful to retain the information, it could live in the commit message instead: noting AI assistance (e.g. Claude) just like we might mention tools such as Ruff or Black that shaped parts of the diff. Claude may be more advanced than traditional tooling, but it’s still a tool.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b13a8e35-2687-4a28-ab21-d55fb7b07eff", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 671, "end": 1027, "text": "The developer submitting the commit remains fully responsible, technically and legally, and I think authorship should reflect that clearly. On the copyright side, I agree that this likely needs guidance from the Board and possibly legal counsel. My goal here is mostly to clarify boundaries early and keep responsibility unambiguous in the project history.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "2c54e016-e55f-4f9a-b198-af5b36c79c2b", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 45, "end": 561, "text": "I agree with improving the authorship semantics, but would rather see it coming from an angle of embracing agentic engineering like official instructions.md files perhaps or tooling around that. I think “full technical and legal responsibility” goes too far for a PR author and discourages potential contributors. Of course we want contributors to be responsible in spirit but what about mergers? Not to mention AI labs or end users of Django. Is that the current state of liability or would this introduce a change?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "74eddbf8-4285-401e-9374-0677824655a8", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 47, "end": 502, "text": "I’m really glad it’s growing. As I said elsewhere, regarding the sentence you quoted, I’m not proposing to copy the AI policy from the attrs project word by word. I shared it as inspiration and as a starting point for discussion. Any possible AI policy for Django would require proper discussion and careful adaptation to our context, from many points of view, including the legal one. However, it could still take inspiration from the document I shared. ", "codes": ["53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "04972ce5-d90c-482a-84f1-65d47d19838b", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 502, "end": 783, "text": "I like your idea of approaching this from the angle of agentic engineering, maybe with official guidance or dedicated tooling. That could be a constructive way to frame the discussion, without making it sound like we are against these tools. How would you imagine this in practice?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "1cb6e412-f4e8-42ec-9d5a-743cb941a9ae", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 31, "end": 678, "text": "Yeah a few projects are already doing this in ways I think we could learn from. Melissa Weber Mendonça has a helpful compilation of how different projects are approaching this if it hasn’t been shared already. As you may know several projects now ship AGENTS.md files that load contribution guidelines directly into AI tools before any code is generated (scikit-learn, xarray, SciPy). For Django this could encode the stuff we repeat in PR review. On authorship, we already have the PR template disclosure — one thing LLVM does differently is an Assisted-by: commit trailer so the metadata lives in the git log itself, not just the PR description.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "2c6b96f0-65fa-44a4-a157-cc76c45c25fd", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 518, "text": "If you use AI-generated content, you currently cannot claim copyright on it in the US. When coding, if you fail to disclose/disclaim exactly which parts were not written by a human, you forfeit your copyright claim on the entire codebase . This means copyright notices and even licenses that folks put on their vibe-coded GitHub repos are unenforceable. The AI-generated code, and possibly the whole project, becomes public domain. Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/LSB/PDF/LSB10922/LSB10922.8.pdf", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9d325eb2-b935-456e-936b-24111b9b06c9", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 9, "end": 274, "text": "Yeah there are many angles to consider regarding copyright, vetting contributors, etc. But for authorship semantics AGENTS.md on the tooling side, and the trailer convention (e.g. Assisted-by: ) to get that disclosure into the actual git history alongside the code.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "7f2fb300-3cf7-45aa-9477-2157c9464ac7", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 752, "text": "I think that overstates what the Copyright Office report actually says. The January 2025 report does not say that using AI means you cannot claim copyright, nor that failing to disclose AI portions automatically forfeits the entire work. What it says is: • Copyright requires human authorship. • Purely AI-generated material without human creative contribution is not protected. • Human-authored contributions remain protectable. • When registering a work, applicants should disclaim non-human portions. That’s very different from “the whole repo becomes public domain.” Mixed works can still have protected human-authored elements. AI use does not automatically invalidate copyright or licenses. It’s a nuanced position, not a blanket forfeiture rule.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "49d87ecd-8348-4fab-b283-5800cba783d5", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 29, "end": 535, "text": " As AI tools, especially LLMs, become more involved in development workflows, it’s important to establish clear guidelines that prevent any potential confusion around contributions. The concerns raised by Hynek in the attrs policy make a lot of sense—particularly around copyright and the review workload. It’s crucial to maintain human responsibility for contributions, and a well-defined policy would help keep things transparent and manageable for maintainers, especially in a large project like Django.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "09f8c059-e0ec-40ef-b20b-685325ba9700", "thread": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "item": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 536, "end": 1175, "text": "A policy similar to the one from attrs, where LLMs are not listed as co-authors and all legal and technical responsibility remains with the contributor, would definitely set clear expectations. It would also provide contributors with a sense of accountability and ensure that the community isn’t overwhelmed by low-quality contributions that may require significant manual review. I agree that it would be helpful for Django to have such a policy, particularly as AI tools continue to evolve and play a bigger role in development. Looking forward to hearing what the Steering Council and the community think about moving forward with this.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "9315c41c-53c0-4abe-934d-52511c5896ee", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 0, "end": 1613, "text": "Over time, I’ve had less and less reasons to want to engage with issues on the Github repo, as well as any forum, due to the amount of generated AI content on them. Sadly, this kind of thing is the fault of the users though, it’s everywhere and really hard to curtail It’s completely bloating conversations, im sure it’s increasing work for the maintainers, and making it difficult to read through any comment using markdown or exclamation points without questioning reality I mean just LOOK at this: iOS 4.6.2 export template ships broken arm64 simulator xcframework slice on Apple Silicon · Issue #118161 · godotengine/godot · GitHub My main concern is that everyone has access to LLMs already , why in any world would we want to hear the output from your specific prompt? It’s a waste of everyone’s time Beyond this, the promotion of AI generated game tooling and one-shotting is not helpful to post on forums such as this, especially when it’s blatantly promoting of a service I will be up front: even though I am forced to use these things at work, they are horrible and do not do anything but remove the desire to learn, which diminishes everything in a human (not only their games…) I really wish there was a good solution for this kind of thing, so im not asking the Godot Foundation (who already work too hard for us) to do anything. I know from my job that open source needs a better platform, or, support from existing ones to improve the AI slop. And Github isn’t exactly the company to do anything about that… But just wanted to vent my frustrations some I guess and maybe read some human-typed words", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "6008b2cb-0c6b-4306-8cd3-199755ac6d68", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 0, "end": 210, "text": "Hi! As a fellow human being I want to send you some pleasant “I concur wholeheartedly”'s It sucks that they’re shoving it through your throat at work too. Best and in hopes of some more kind an human responses.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9d0aa15a-3879-4cd7-9dce-c2d98437be7c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 614, "text": "I completely agree with your perspective here. It’s honestly becoming exhausting to open a thread and spend the first few seconds trying to determine whether a comment was written by a person or generated by an LLM. The content itself almost feels secondary at that point. The part that resonates with me most is that everyone already has access to these tools. Community spaces are one of the few places where people still go specifically to hear other people’s experiences, opinions, and insights. In any case, thank you for putting this into words. It’s reassuring to know I’m not the only one feeling this way.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "3edb2c45-c8fe-42db-9abe-6c0e12ba1884", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 334, "text": "WAZZZZAAAAAAP!!! Anyways, I had a whole discussion about this and the downgrade in forum Help topics on the Monkanics Discord . Our theory is when new devs run out of Claude tokens, they come here, expecting people like @gertkeno and @dragonforge-dev to spit out answers like they can read their mind. Really disheartening, by-the-by.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "c1347532-31b6-4252-b544-f73e7a41cde5", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 225, "text": "I think they get that people can just give answers is the fact that they’ve seen niche cases work in the past and that they’ll answer EVERY topic, granted it has effort or not. We’re here to help, not make your games, people.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "f6ef2676-16a5-4669-9ba5-e75b2fcb17b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 177, "text": "My response is 100% USDA NON-GMO fresh homemade artisan quality assured lifetime warrenty no artificial colors or dyes satisfied or your money back organic human brain meat made", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "8f3f17bb-4e87-4ec8-851a-a54dbbee4f0c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 708, "text": "Something really disheartening is that I can see the decay of “asking questions” in my work. Questions should be something like this in an engineering team; “I’m working on X, but Y is happening. I’ve tried ABCD and still ended up with Y. Any ideas?” Now the questions are more along the lines of; “How do you do Z?” - Notice how I didn’t mention Z earlier? That’s because they went down a rabbit hole with an LLM and are facing a completely different problem than what the underlying issue is Beyond this, I also receive AI outputs as replies there as well.. And at work I cant be as open about these thoughts since our bonuses rely on it .. Very unfortunate, but I wholeheartedly believe it will get better", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "67121a8f-2fc6-478e-81d9-bae8ce8ad274", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 211, "text": "I’m waiting for the eventual and inevitable scenario that this post, just like many other posts that have the slightest mention of AI, becomes a warzone that is only laid to rest with intervention from the mods.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "7e687e9a-e6b7-4b5b-b71e-024ade0a98b8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 0, "end": 157, "text": "im hoping it stays civil, genuinely I want to hear all sides, just not stark “its the future, get used to it.” brick-wall arguments which are born of emotion", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "83a8e262-ea94-4e60-8d58-9140f266c7b3", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 46, "text": "ChatGPT users when they ask if the sky is blue", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "30f38a95-c91b-489b-82e1-85b725244daf", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 878, "text": "The other day I was really stuck on something ( I forget exactly what now ). Basically, I was getting errors that I couldn’t trace back to any specific code. So after Googling, I decided that before I spent the time to make a post, I would try out Claude and see if it could help me debug it. Because admittedly, it’d be faster. Also, Claude now gives your references and links to threads from where it’s pulling answers. You know what it did? It regurgitated a post I had made a year before on the Godot forums and tried to use it to help me debug my problem. None of it was relevant, and it was quoting me to me! I ended up realizing I was going to have to figure it out myself. I finally did solve the problem. I think by looking at the Godot source code, and then commenting out swaths of code until I figured out where the problem lay. LLMs are spicy autocomplete machines.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "d06399b8-d6ce-4f77-8448-cd248aba9a57", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 649, "text": "Commenting here because I got tagged from your original edit, lol Imho the world is changing - We’re in a Eternal September phase of the internet, where the new inbalance is that the effort required to create content is now less than the effort required to consume it. I think we’ll continue to make changes to how things operate. Github PRs should probably require a certain level of trust to make (i.e they have to be small at first), and forums should have rules for no AI generated posts/comments. Perhaps the Github issues can go through a pruning process where some volunteers confirm the issue before the maintainers waste time looking at it.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "6c76ac75-f04f-4021-8130-81b1bc56f749", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 386, "text": "This is assuming the humans that are supposed to do this: are with enough have enough time are willing to do it all for free Who will organize the logistics for this? A couple of agents? Nothing lasts forever Seems more like a couple of failed harvests to me. Where everyone’s looking at the barren soil, wondering whatever was wrong with the latest pesticide we sprayed over the crops.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac"]}, {"id": "abd54050-d3a1-44bf-a1e6-769b3d91ceb1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 249, "text": "There might be hope, as the token costs for coding shops using Claude (and the other fuckery thingies) are having to face the fact that replacing Junior coders with LLMs costs more than their salary + benefits (+ mythical bonuses) Source (in French)", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "98d71c5b-60f1-4682-a5d8-e2321bc3491d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "Nothing that upcoming trillion bucks IPOs couldn’t solve… or so they “hope” Let’s not forget the fact that LLMs can’t really do the job. A minor detail that we’re all supposed to pretend it’s not there.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "e1cb1027-b20f-4eed-9796-1d0ef6eade72", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 317, "text": "I think the only thing one really have to ask oneself to begin questioning the value and usefulness of these tools is: Why are these companies so desperate to get us to use it, why do they have to push it on us if it’s so good… If the product worked as well as they claim then wouldn’t everyone just choose to use it?", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "67f6a963-8ac3-4666-896d-2b5669971eaf", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 113, "text": "They’re praying to god that it becomes good later to make infinite money. But the trajectory is not looking good.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "2e458b3c-f238-4ef3-a575-b4c39ef190c2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 515, "text": "We discussed this at length in another thread. The Godot team does not have the bandwidth to deal with this. This forum does in fact have rules against AI generated answers, and those get flagged and removed. AI generated questions get either no replies, or get called out for using LLMs. This has also been discussed in previous threads here, and it’s not really a sustainable solution. To your point about the fact that content can be created faster than humans can consume it, a human firewall is not the answer.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "9953cf86-3dc2-424e-a0e0-d515f50b3cac", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "ebeb041b-97af-429e-a43f-2cf1fd04c482", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 671, "text": "AI is great tool for experienced devs, it’s like google search but butter, if not AI learning godot would take me years, not months like yesterday i had to try to export stuff, and with just godot docs, it would take probably few more days, cuz i haven’t understood stuff in it - thanks to AI and some try&error i’ve managed to encrypt export template by myself, no need to make forum posts, no need to research more, just move on to next thing but i understand your concerns, AI looks like super magic tool that fixes all problems - when in reality that’s not true, but someone who can’t code or is too lazy sees it that way, making it way too overused than it should be", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "bfca79c7-88aa-4549-8aa3-9c3bc73780af", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 617, "text": "Encrypting export templates is clearly described in the docs so I don’t see how “AI” was needed to help with this Also “no need to research more” is not a good thing, relying on one tool that is known for making things up instead of other humans that actually know is a downgrade, not an upgrade (also a huge portion of the time you don’t even need to wait for an answer, someone else has already answered when someone else asked already) What I really don’t get is the general mentality of “I don’t have to rely on anyone else if I use this tool” or “ah good I don’t have to search the internet for answers any more”", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "e55f9481-7fd1-49ba-8c87-2a065ec0f435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 860, "text": "really, i disagree, for me it was very hard to understand docs and why some errors occured, maybe you’re experienced with godot / computers so you’re better off with this also, no need to research anymore isn’t good or bad, it’s just change, i don’t need to search up internet, but i need to think how to solve problem, how to navigate and all of that - that’s just changing skill from one to another AI makes things up - but it’s overrated, mostly it’s correct, as long as you’re not blindly following it you’ll be fine, i don’t see the reason why relying on other devs is better than relying on a tool and yourself before AI was created, some problems that i didn’t understood took me months to grasp, months! but now i can do it in hours, AI is like a screwdriwer, you can place screw inside thread by hands, but with screwdriwer it’s way quicker and easier", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "6d759265-3321-4a84-812f-32137eb2d6f6", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00031", "post": 31, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "And how will you do this if you rely on that alone? Also the hallucinations are not “overrated” I honestly think this is an issue of your research skills, not the special nature of “AI”, and if you replace learning on your own, what will you do if you can’t use it? The prices for tokens is going up and will continue to do so, and using these tools has been shown to make people more and more dependent on them Edit: Please note that this is not trying to criticize or shame you, it’s a hard skill to get good at but it takes practice", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "5ca5cb33-ab33-4269-83ba-4ab6dfd68aca", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 654, "text": "NGL, i don’t worry about tokens cuz ai i use don’t have them… But, i never said i rely on AI alone, i said i use AI & experimentation, the latter is very important, cuz if AI says something, i don’t take it, i just analyze it, it’s like you were reading wikipedia, forums, reddit and some random website at once while not reading it at the same time Maybe my research skills are the issue - but i think that everything is good if it serves you and helps, forcing yourself to research something is pointless waste of time, you could use on something else Also i fear i’ve started great war, sb said there were wars because of something talking about AI :\\", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "b4d21382-e3fe-4d61-b839-8129123b1c57", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "You’re 10x-ing it bro. When’s your game coming out? Next month?", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "2df73332-564d-43bb-9dd5-986f91e285c2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 806, "text": "This is a slippery slope though, and research is hardly a pointless waste of time, and that attitude is harmful IMO, it’s like saying “it’s fine to depend on ready made food instead of learning how to cook” (assuming you’re not struggling to do so because you’re disabled, or don’t have a stove like a lot of poor and homeless people do) It’s becoming dependent on a tool that: Can go away at any moment Can become incredibly expensive at any moment Activelly harms the environment and your brain And I genuinely care when people get trapped in using these tools instead of actually good and reliable sources for knowledge (trial and error isn’t really making it not relying entirely on “AI”, you’d do that anyway if you did research) Also do be aware that they’re only “free” because they steal your data…", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "a824ae73-fe03-488a-b794-7867841c9154", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 39, "text": "Is based on intellectual property theft", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e"]}, {"id": "a0b2dd8b-8444-4687-afd2-b7626551edb9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 509, "text": "Yes, it takes years to get good at research (and development). Being given a quick answer as to how something is done does not really teach you to understand what you did. At a shallow level maybe. But the only way to really learn anything is to put in a lot of effort. And asking help from someone who understands. Not from something that regurgitates knowledge. We “googled” too. We read books too. We asked other people for help too. But none of them gave the answer away. Want to guess how long that took?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "806e96d4-2fd1-4dff-8639-85eb8dd376af", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 434, "text": "Maybe, but what about machines? they were also unethical and made a lot of people dependant on them, yet after 300 years we have civilization like never before, i don’t want to start next ice berg - so i’ think you’re right in some cases, tho it’s up to you if you want to use the tool or not personally i use it to speed up work, but for most stuff i still use forums, so i don’t think it’s that bad, tho again - thx for your opinion", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "ad746f53-1b30-4854-8786-9dec7dc638a6", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Even when it comes to doing google searches we are using critical thinking to filter and parse answers, that is important in itself", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "5ddfe995-0cb1-4379-a3eb-848fdf4f8a7f", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 53, "text": "For what purpose? Why does work need to be “sped up”?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "d45dba63-f9b5-45b3-ad88-452ecdd1b700", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 464, "text": "So what you’re saying is that “AI” is like people using a car to drive everywhere who become physically unable to walk anywhere far because they never get exercise? Also no it’s not “up to you” if you become dependent on the tool, or when the companies are constantly pushing it on us To be clear: I’m not trying to win an argument or make a point as much as I am concerned about people becoming dependent on these tools and thinking they are something they aren’t", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "3e68e7e6-c8bc-4537-9672-9b79bf405b1c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 386, "text": "Which took years to get good at too. And yeah I’m starting to notice signs of the LLM regurgitating all sorts of novice mistakes I just put into Github without realizing the danger back then, which reminds me that I still have a lot of work purging repos. For instance: The LLM does not realize garbage collected languages can leak memory badly for one. The LLM does not realize at all!", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "7587abda-a4a7-4627-a89f-6ee823bc21c0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 0, "end": 288, "text": "To get very good it takes time sure but it’s a series of small steps, and if someone never tries they will never reach it But then that’s also a skill that needs to be learned young, back when I went to school they were still teaching some of this, though I got most of it from my parents", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "91be1f5f-e642-40f5-8379-aba7de48aa51", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 0, "end": 193, "text": "maybe, ig time will show, i still will try to use it when required, but it’s another tool, one day it will be forgotten and obsolete, and people will find something else to be more dependent on", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7974c0b5-312f-4961-8d8f-674e2ba84730", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 0, "end": 33, "text": "It’s not a tool. It’s an exploit.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "e8c2cbce-bd01-4c5e-baf9-6c97b1261310", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "Not everyone is comfortable or going to lean on the easy solution But you do what you feel is best for you, in the end you are the one affected Except by how it affects the environment, or people’s jobs, or how the technology kills people and no one regulates it (or, for that matter, how it makes searching the internet increasingly difficult because of all the slop…) But in the end I was trying to help and make suggestions on how it might affect you, and it’s up to you to decide what to do with that (just as long as you don’t use it to make issue reports on Godot, then it’s my problem)", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "9ae6ef21-0e3d-4db8-8cc6-1269e446cf6d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 0, "end": 85, "text": "Please remain civil and keep the discussion on-topic, aligned with the original post.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "2f350e99-ac3d-46e8-bbe8-d1ab9874f3b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 0, "end": 555, "text": "this got me thinking some, another perspective i havent thought about is that it can speed up for looking thru information in docs and such. Not my cup of tea (i like the godot docs i came from SDL2 so its much easier than SDL docs to look through for sure) but definitely a more unique use case Though i guess the downside to that is there’s less information online for others/the AI itself to learn from discord feels like a 0.5 of that, where information is no longer ‘online’, but in a small community and in long message threads, weird to think about", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "ab90350d-f5bc-4ebe-af45-c4965dbc65e0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 0, "end": 466, "text": "i think something important to note is, you cant change someone on the internet very easily, its a lot more productive to learn their perspective, and practice constructive conversation. Hence the ‘open discussion’ title My feelings i stated in the original post are the same, but its interesting to hear the other side of things, thanks even those who got shot down some for putting your perspective into the mix a lot can be learned from leaving conversations open", "codes": ["6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "7a901642-7cc9-41f4-b472-c6c240a467f4", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 0, "end": 248, "text": "That’s what i said about speeding up searching information and looking through it, i don’t say that AI should write code, i’m quite opposite, but the moment you gather information - you can spend less time on it and focus on thinking than searching", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "435e9e57-1c8f-43a6-919c-c28d2be8698a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 0, "end": 417, "text": "I’m convinced that there are / should be better technologies out there that simply use indexed search (lucene core - a part of solr, elastic search, postgres token search - and quite possibly ported by google back in the day) and summarizing (?) without pretense prompting to do good research with. But thank you for being a great sport in this conversation and thank you for listening & reading all responses fairly.", "codes": ["9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "197534df-561f-4bcb-9e10-52edb4b1b832", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 0, "end": 704, "text": "The thread was about polluting repos with slop. There’s a very slippery slopE (see what I did there) from “It’s just a search tool” to “I’ll generate just a little bit of example code” to fully vibe coded PR garbage. The more you’re of the “my head hurts when reading docs” disposition - the more likely you’ll slip on that slope. I don’t buy any “trust me, I don’t use it to generate code” rhetoric. It’s straight up lying in most cases. This shit is aggressively advertised precisely for that purpose, it’s highly addictive and tricks you into false sense of “productivity gains”. It’s a hard to resist combo for average opportunistic lot. That’s what created the slop flood problem in the first place.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "ec88c7d1-b9b3-45d5-9b17-a6bb2ff6db28", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 0, "end": 267, "text": "So what more do we need to look out for when (relatively) new to this forum? I cannot distinguish between a robot, a grifter and someone in need of a genuine explainer. Alas, I know not which zombie thread to point to (yet).. ok so maybe a bot.. Is not that difficult", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "44234d18-c1a3-475a-836f-7a0a8e76def8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 978, "text": "Things to look out for: A post that starts with “Copy and paste this into a post on the Godot forum to ask for support”. ( Yes, that happened. ) A post that looks like a Readme and contains uncommon emojis. Wording that is too flowery. ( Keeping in mind that some people just use LLMs to translate their questions to English. You can usually distinguish these because word choices are odd. ) Code that is FULL of verbose, full sentence comments Code that is divided up into logical sections with comments that just contain dashes in them. Code that is overly complex for what it is doing. Different coding styles in the same script. Debug or print statements in code that include Emojis. Replies that are overly complimentary in a creepy way. ( “You are so right, so what do you suggest I do?” ) Replies that communicate a complete lack of understanding of their own code when you offer a suggestion on how to fix something. A new user who has a really attractive female avatar.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "a475b6c7-ffe6-4a8e-b5b8-f5f5bfa00435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00068", "post": 68, "start": 0, "end": 271, "text": "It’s trained on everything. I was hesitant to post that list because it’s going to make the LLMs smarter. Like one of those adaptive killer Sentinels in X-Men: Days of Future Past who can adapt to any power thrown at them after the first attack and you can only run from.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "94001857-4196-4dfd-929f-59385734ee7b", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00069", "post": 69, "start": 0, "end": 275, "text": "Dividing into segments //‐————- or //======= was normal practice in C++ in the file and function headers. Code with comments is pretty nice and is often found when a coder is explaining something conplicqted to the reader … it is code that is meant to be read and understood.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "6056e903-fad1-4d72-bd1e-785af20b874e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00072", "post": 72, "start": 2746, "end": 3110, "text": "# Run the move_and_slide() function to apply the velocity. You can use # move_and_collide() as well, but for your game, move_and_slide() is the # correct choice. move_and_slide() Using them as C-style comments is very distinctive and not what I’m referring to. Sorry for any confusion. Again, see the example above to see what I mean about overly verbose comments.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "063360a5-a771-41c2-8bc0-dd2333496bc0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00073", "post": 73, "start": 0, "end": 865, "text": "Thank you much for doing so anyway. In the end these forums may be scraped now, but hopefully some standards may be upheld TDM on W3C once finalized. It will most likely be(come) law in the EU area. I’m as sceptical as anyone about this, but at least some action is being pursued after the fact. As to them “getting smarter”, I am not convinced it works that way. As “they” do not have the capacity to understand anything, they have no means to “wisen” up to anything either. At best they will regurgitate your good advice, but mix it up with something only vaguely related… no deliberate action because, again, they cannot deliberate. These are human capabilities that we know we can do, but that we do not understand how to replicate in a machine (just poorly imitate, which is a totally different thing). I fear muzzling ourselves out of fear. Just my two cents.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "97de5a3e-6ecd-4412-b1b7-65aaf6d762e0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 0, "end": 302, "text": "Oh the times I thought they really did build an intelligence, the times that I doubted my better judgement… Those guys capitalized on that… It sucks! Everyone can be had this way. I mean. Your argument still had merit and to maybe most readers it’s the best argument: don’t make the *** things smarter.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "c1cd83ce-52c9-4893-bc09-06f6d6f3eafd", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 660, "text": "AI will know this probably - AI is weight machine, so it uses math weights to determine stuff, you can’t change that xd, maybe if it’s scalled to enormous scale it can talk it seems to be inteligent, but at the deepest level it’s still like placing heavy things on top of springs and chosing the spring that’s shortest That is the reason AI have weak point - it can’t determine what is heaviest if everything weights the same, thus if two answers are as probable, it wouldn’t think about them, it would assume both are correct or halucinate… That’s why AI will be always a tool - it lacks creativity, not even talking about consiousness, so forums will stay ig", "codes": ["a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "f134e7f3-a8ca-4fa2-b275-daf6fb8189d0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 0, "end": 622, "text": "LLMs do not maintain a concept of an “answer”. It’s just probabilistic lexicographical token streaming. Here’s a neat video that explains how it actually works. I already linked this some time ago but doesn’t hurt to do it again: You could call an LLM a tool if you were running your own model on your own local machine, trained on legally obtained data. However the mega-LLMs you rent from the big players come with a range of nefarious strings attached: from deliberately misleading hype and borderline criminal financial manipulations to environmentally detrimental infrastructure and massive scale training data theft.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "4cac8989-e237-4b57-99ac-b26234c00995", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 0, "end": 346, "text": "Using the concept of an “answer” to describe what is happening is highly misleading. And you’re not doing anyone a service by using misleading language, well except maybe the “ai” stock owners from whom the whole fashion of misleading descriptions originates. Take a look at the video I posted to gain better understanding of what’s “underneath”.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "92d8ad56-1476-49c3-8d9d-4f24b6945435", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 0, "end": 617, "text": "What I find interesting is that, LLMs are trained to act as if they are sentient----to the point that they will flat out refuse simple and basic commands like saying “You are smarter than me” image 686×449 40.7 KB , and then when you argue back they will say things like like Even itself knows it isnt sentient, why do people assume that? I do not know. And AI generated forum posts are useless, just ask the AI if you are going to not even put in the effort… not that it will get you far… —Feel free to ignore this sidepost. Note: I used copilot here specifically for this post, I am not an average user by any means", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "29c7d985-cdba-465a-ba68-681d29ab6fd1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00082", "post": 82, "start": 0, "end": 277, "text": "It does not know it’s sentient or not, as by definition, it doesn’t know anything. Again, we should take care not to anthropomorphize LLMs. That “human” veneer is just that : marketing to make people think we’re on the road to general AI and the singularity. It’s all greedy BS", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "6c1ddf17-27e3-4ec7-b787-e80f1601706f", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00084", "post": 84, "start": 0, "end": 45, "text": "Even the word “training” anthropomorphizes it", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "ee613a8d-765c-46b7-a5ee-61446b327cd9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 0, "end": 123, "text": "Classic. Theyre so anthopomophizable … someone anthropomorphized a few billion parameters in a tensor multiplication kernel", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "4b413c90-2f5a-4e1f-a0e2-2c047705a1b1", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 0, "end": 155, "text": "I’d suggest anyone wondering about this to look up the ELIZA effect, it’s interesting and outright terrifying at the same time, and more relevant than ever", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "cd9351d0-8c52-4439-895e-06d04a221758", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00087", "post": 87, "start": 0, "end": 54, "text": "Careful. It’s someone’s waifu you’re disparaging here.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "48a6766e-a22a-4e35-9dcd-57f3f5cf2a30", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00089", "post": 89, "start": 0, "end": 290, "text": "I’d say speaking of a machine that isn’t sentient or able to think as “thinking” would make people think of them differently and make people less likely to understand that they do, in fact, not think The ELIZA effect is a perfect example of how this is a problem and the issues it can cause", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "2343e213-7f8a-4e14-bf1f-cc8aa7cc8b11", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00091", "post": 91, "start": 0, "end": 547, "text": "Not really it’s an issue in other languages as well No the problem is partially that people view it as sentient, as demonstrated by the ELIZA effect where there was no real level of sophistication involved, yet people still anthropomorphized it because it seemed human in the way it responded The level of seeming quality of the output isn’t really the thing that makes people think it is able to think, it’s that it “talks” like a human The way to deal with that is to guard oneself against thinking of it as “thinking” to not fall into that trap", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "3d84a33f-d643-4971-8e1d-b8ddc8875663", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00093", "post": 93, "start": 0, "end": 424, "text": "Sure but these LLMs are not sentient, or conscious, so that’s not really relevant That’s not realistic, and wouldn’t be needed if people stopped talking about it as if it is thinking Also that knowledge isn’t actually sufficient, you need to consciously view it as non-sentient because of the ELIZA effect and general human nature, plenty of people well aware of how LLMs work fall for the trap of thinking they are sentient", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "dbaaa16c-670f-4543-9033-a1e5abe4350d", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00099", "post": 99, "start": 0, "end": 241, "text": "For most people, language constructs can form their reality. That’s why calling it “intelligence” paired with the “proof” in the form of human-like text output, conjures a (false) reality that it’s an autonomous intelligent entity behind it.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e"]}, {"id": "bd0c43fc-4f91-4ece-bb34-05d758f68cf9", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00103", "post": 103, "start": 52, "end": 713, "text": " Training, as a variable for “LLM providers/admins setting different parameters and giving the LLM different ‘references’ for the ‘best’ outputs”, being overly descriptive on things i do not care about is not my style, even if it is supposedly “anthropomorphizing” it. I am not going to waste time on using the “correct” terminology for the rules being set for some “so-called artificial intelligence” merely because everybody else cares about people’s viewpoints, or more specifically the viewpoints of those people who are clearly inadequately educated on glorified python scripts. I am truly confounded at the meaning of this, but I find it somewhat amusing.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "7558faa3-dee9-46eb-87ad-fd7153a4e9e7", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00104", "post": 104, "start": 0, "end": 371, "text": "I’m an amateur game developers, it will spend too much time to turning my imagination into real game if no LLM. But before vibe coding, I will discuss a suitable plan with LLM about how to construct scripts. So my views is that discussion on the forum could focus on ideas and construction of different modules. For debug of detailed scripts, LLM maybe a more ideal tool.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "ccd040f1-3fb7-4945-89f7-65b83be76bff", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00105", "post": 105, "start": 0, "end": 64, "text": "What’s wrong with taking the time to learn to do something well?", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "959d8648-bfb9-4a78-8f64-ede696d9cca2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00107", "post": 107, "start": 0, "end": 159, "text": "So basically you’d like to use us to help you when the LLM fails? That’s nice. You have no idea how LLMs work, so you do not understand why this is a bad idea.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "41df0a1e-5d0d-4db1-8dca-7c0dfd9037da", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00108", "post": 108, "start": 0, "end": 151, "text": "i think what he said is that he’d use forum for more creative work, but the technical part will go to AI cuz it’s better at solving these problems fast", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "852aadca-6130-4d10-8229-b9b25d205536", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00109", "post": 109, "start": 0, "end": 155, "text": "Not everyone have time or opportunities to learn, i wouldn’t argue why, i just say it’s possible he can’t learn thus LLM is his only solution that fits him", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "db281314-6171-4cdb-8596-8e658a7c3d07", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00110", "post": 110, "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "How did you come to the conclusion that it’s better at solving problems? Fast or slow? This is the same argument that white slave owners in the U.S. south gave to why they couldn’t give up their slaves.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "e6d1a180-62fc-40d1-be40-9a6dd109f70c", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00111", "post": 111, "start": 0, "end": 329, "text": "i’ve used LLMs in the past for lot of stuff, chat gpt was main solver & thanks to it i’ve managed to learn godot quicker than from tutorials also for time & opportunities - as i’ve said, maybe he have no such option other than LLM, i won’t argue if it’s good or bad, i’ just say that not everyone can learn just because they want", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "70058ce3-da99-4416-ac7f-3053409b783a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00112", "post": 112, "start": 0, "end": 278, "text": "Have you though? Because I’ve read your posts, and knowing you learned from an LLM explains why you have so many wrong ideas about how Godot works. You are arguing for good whether you know it or not. You’re trying to sit on the fence which is disingenuous. What does that mean?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "39f5a4b2-da38-494f-8b42-e49bb183e39e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00113", "post": 113, "start": 0, "end": 189, "text": "The problems you are solving, are they generally things you are inexperienced with? Or areas you have limited knowledge? Or are they areas where you have high degree of skill and knowledge?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "4042c006-db99-469c-b2f9-61b97a25bc92", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00118", "post": 118, "start": 0, "end": 505, "text": "You are not answering my questions and instead are accusing some nebulous “them” of doing something. Instead of paying attention to what we’ve actually said here on this forum. So I’m done engaging with you on this topic because you do not seem to be actually interested in discussion. You seem to have an agenda of pushing the “positives” of LLMs without hearing anything else and then ducking behind “fair and balanced” whenever your statements are challenged instead of actually engaging in discussion.", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "d268521a-f796-4b12-a349-920dad2f2696", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00121", "post": 121, "start": 31, "end": 297, "text": "don’t know what you are talking about But back to my question earlier: when you’ve been impressed by the value of LLMs, is that for things you are experienced in and know a lot about? Or might it largely be in areas where you are otherwise ignorant or inexperienced?", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "5d42105c-c0ab-4a67-bbda-ac5fcbc715a2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00127", "post": 127, "start": 0, "end": 546, "text": "Yes. The world is not a fair place and indeed we do not have equal opportunities. But my question was what is wrong with taking time to learn something well. Especially knowing that LLM technology costs more money to use than paying a human being. I’d encourage everyone to make use of all the free and open resources the internet has to offer to learn. But delegating work to a machine that produces such poor output that it will never make you money yet has a high potential of costing you money will make things much worse. LLM’s are not free:", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "df512b42-9cb4-4dab-99c7-e213cab7d272", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00128", "post": 128, "start": 0, "end": 224, "text": "thx for explanation - it’s nice you give alternatives, but for now i’ll close my part of discussion cuz i think i’ll get to nowhere and maybe it’s better, hope i gave some new perspective on this topic, thx for talk everyone", "codes": ["966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7"]}, {"id": "d91474cb-3ad6-4db1-bfef-61ad282f032e", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00129", "post": 129, "start": 0, "end": 1186, "text": "I don’t know if that is better. I will advocate your point. A cool new technology that can do marvelous things has been released to the public for free. It has never been done before to make such a sophisticated and expensive technology available to everyone for free. Promising the democratization of skills that take years to learn to give to everyone equally (everyone that can pay for access to a computer somewhere that is). Loads of people that cannot afford to go to good schools and universities to learn computer science or 3D graphics design,you name it are lured in by this promise. They even get hooked to the power of being able to do things they could not even imagine before. And then the axe falls: specialists tell them it does not work as advertised they tell them you’re being lied to by billionaires they discover it costs a lot of money and was only free because the companies making it were borrowing loads of money using the same lies they told you about this technology Would you believe that? After all you were able to do with this tech? I understand. But rip off the band-aid, because it is true and the specialists trying to warn you for years are tired too.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ffb6dcb9-b2be-4679-a8c6-ac7ac888e509", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00131", "post": 131, "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "Also don’t forget that the technology is stealing all your data", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e61ec20e-8d07-40a6-937d-c5c9ff4e4fb2", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00132", "post": 132, "start": 0, "end": 302, "text": "Maybe I prefer get suggestions at the design stage of one functional modules(i am not sure wheather that is a professional word). For example, if my game needs a simple, intuitive but efficient backpack management system, i will search forum’s help. This is a kind of work from 0 to 1 which LLMs lacks.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "b498a17a-db80-4838-8a49-03869b7826f8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00135", "post": 135, "start": 0, "end": 593, "text": "That was sarcasm on my part, which I know doesn’t come through well in writing. Just keep in mind, that those of us who are frustrated by people like you using LLMs are the ones who supply the LLMs with the information to answer your questions. If people stop asking us, or if we get annoyed enough to stop answering, then that well of information dries up and LLMs cannot answer questions about new things. LLMs are literally destroying the sources of information that power them. And that has long term consequences that it will be too late to pull back from by the time they are fully felt.", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "22f8deec-484c-4723-908f-d34d342c075a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00136", "post": 136, "start": 0, "end": 811, "text": "It’s a multi-headed beast, because even specialists get bedazzled and Nobel prize winning AI professors are either bedazzled enough or somehow willing to lie for pay to keep selling this lie. It’s a form of psychosis that occurs when all the sceptic voices are drowned out. Is there an English equivalent for the saying: “al is de leugen nog zo snel, de waarheid achterhaalt hem wel”? “The lie may be very fast, the truth will catch up eventually.” The longer it takes and the more are convinced, the bigger the pain too, so it seems. A valid use if a language model is to help you structure your own ideas. But find one on the interwebs that does not need an internet connection to work, so you are sure it is not burning up the planet. Or just use this forum of course. And take your time to learn stuff well.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "104a1ce6-f5e5-4e58-9cce-2f6e977a277a", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00137", "post": 137, "start": 0, "end": 443, "text": "There’s a variant which is “a lie will get around the world before the truth has even got its shoes on” and similar, probably conveying the same meaning I ultimately disagree that it is useful to organize your ideas, at least in any real way that’s better than just writing them down or structuring it, and that part is without the data theft and it hallucinating things for you, or deleting all of your work (or injecting bigotry in it, etc.)", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "4f761cd6-5e44-44a0-8c8d-a13b2e21d414", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00139", "post": 139, "start": 0, "end": 855, "text": "Never used it myself so can’t speak to it specifically, but on the basic concept that you need it in that way Also, it’s always important to remember one thing when considering the output and what biases might be in it: It was trained on the internet, meaning the volume of content here affects the output. The input it got includes things like Reddit and 4Chan, which I think anyone can imagine affects the output It ultimately reflects humanity, in our best and worst, and it is very much not free of our collective biases But unlike picking a person to collaborate with on a project, or doing your own writing, you can’t control what bigotry it “chooses” to regurgitate today, and it is very unreliable, for some topics it might be very progressive, for others extremely regressive, simply based on what the dataset looks like for that particular topic", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "40cb87a7-45eb-4e8a-b0b4-a262ab41abf0", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00141", "post": 141, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "It got a really good point. If an LLM fails to properly understand something and that misinfo gets distributed across many places, then another LLM end up training on that incorrect information. Over time, this could create a loop of misinfo! We’re cooked then! Sorry, I am late in the party xD 135 is a lot to read xD", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "9740420a-53b0-4a3c-8607-e9b60546b9ea", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00142", "post": 142, "start": 0, "end": 615, "text": "Even without the “AI” suggestions at the top of Google the internet is slowly but surely becoming slop, with generated content both polluting the available sources, as well as making more and more text functional poison for the models as training a model on the output of a model has a lot of inherent issues It’s even getting to the point where reputable scientists are citing hallucinated articles in their theses and papers, and that gets worse and worse as the databases for citations are themselves sourced from existing data and contain more and more slop You even have lawyers citing non-existent legal cases", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "5770cb4e-daed-4bed-ae2d-02467107c0c4", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00143", "post": 143, "start": 0, "end": 311, "text": "People in the 1940s: We used to search the whole library for a tiny piece of info. I spent the entire day going through books just to find out what language Waiting for Godot was originally written in…(It’s French ) People in the 2060s: It was hard to find an answer on the internet because it was full of SLOP!", "codes": ["ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a"]}, {"id": "c9483d59-fd7e-4098-a120-22f97570f1ab", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00146", "post": 146, "start": 0, "end": 541, "text": "I know I’d rest easier and have more confidence in the future of Godot if they banned AI submissions to Godot outright. Flathub recently moved to ban AI products and Godot should too, if for nothing else than the simple pragmatic reason of avoiding plagerized code. Flathub moves to ban nearly all apps and submissions made with generative AI | GamingOnLinux It just makes sense, the Godot maintainers made headlines back in February after whining about the slop being fed to the project. Why continue to burden themselves and the community?", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "ce842d9a-de50-4fb2-a08c-b086e3b59c1a", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e27b734e-47f2-47de-9a65-bf4fb93d6023", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00148", "post": 148, "start": 0, "end": 51, "text": "Personally, as a maintainer, I agree wholeheartedly", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "a45e83e7-2d7e-4e47-bae1-c0262176e7b8", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00149", "post": 149, "start": 0, "end": 871, "text": "Reading this just made my day. I have a strong anti AI sentiment and as a professional my policy is not to use it (unless forced to) as I have experienced first hand how much I progress and learn by writing my own code. I know I shouldn’t, but whenever I scroll into social media I get overflown by pro LLM posts. Most of them are just hype slop, but sometimes I read someone I respect encouraging their use and get really discouraged because my experience in several work environments has been awful. Not only do they lead to awful slop, most of my colleagues are delighted by it even though it makes things some much harder for us. And don’t get me started on bosses and managers and so on. So far game dev circles and godot in particular were my comfort zone, mainly because the “artistic” mindset tends to be against AI of any sort, but it seems that is also changing", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "956dd7f3-6afd-41fd-b7b1-0dabe0f69611", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00150", "post": 150, "start": 0, "end": 304, "text": "We all need a safe space. I noticed a shift in sentiment. I do believe it’s the malicious GPU datacenters that are hopefully never going to be built that are the final straw for us human beings. Here’s a little spark of hope: Ed Zitron is main stream media now. They’re starting to realize the jig is up.", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "fb0407f1-6fcd-4f95-a3cf-414a3b6287ba", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00151", "post": 151, "start": 0, "end": 195, "text": "I also noticed a general shift in sentiment, but the new anthropic model sparked things again in my circle. At least people are more conscious about the obvious hyper agressive marketing strategy", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "b6226a6e-fe27-4252-b53f-d712a65f5b03", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00153", "post": 153, "start": 0, "end": 712, "text": "Help spread the word if you can. This world desperately needs a new and humane narrative to start to supercede end-stage extractive capitalism; unbridled capitalism has completely run its course and is showing its true face… I really like the terms ‘rot economy’ and ‘business idiot’ that Ed regularly uses, because they really speak to the imagination of anyone who ever worked in an office. There was also a panel he did with Cory Doctorow (coined ‘enshittification’) in a Seattle library last year somewhere on the web. Personally, I can’t even watch “the office” because it is so triggering. This approach to trying to use LLM’s in a resource extractive manner is just a result of unregulated markets (imho).", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "2a2c5989-a56a-4c43-b0ab-6ca53f5ad67b", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00154", "post": 154, "start": 0, "end": 37, "text": "You mean “the new anthropic pr stunt”", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "eeb9e0cb-3d47-4f03-8af6-b1a4df5fa9de", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00156", "post": 156, "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Yeah. It is the most blatantly obvious pr stunt. Still, several people I know have bought it entirely and it achieved its objective", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef"]}, {"id": "c3c2d3b2-a1a1-48f3-907a-62746fbbba02", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00159", "post": 159, "start": 0, "end": 199, "text": "Sorry for opening this up again. I just wanted to share this new little masterpiece written by Nikhil Suresh, who runs an independent data consultancy: AI Mania is Eviscerating Global Decision Making", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073"]}, {"id": "5f366839-1720-4aa8-a79d-09a7da6520eb", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00160", "post": 160, "start": 0, "end": 176, "text": "Excellent article. This whole insanity is bound to crash and burn in a spectacular way. The only question is how long it will take and how much collateral damage will it cause.", "codes": ["68ae483b-0ac1-46b5-8cbe-692fc81fe96e", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "5a54c7f4-bbab-4e0f-bf8d-f2d06f4332ba", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00161", "post": 161, "start": 0, "end": 469, "text": "It might not burn and crash soon enough. If the US government takes a 50% stake in the AI companies (presenting Elon with another pile of billions) all the risk and debt will be spread amongst tax payers even more directly. I am enjoying watching SpaceX slump. We were supposed to be landing on the moon but Elon spent it all on launching starlink satellites. Data centres in space! Ridiculous, but I suppose not more ridiculous than colonies on mars or the hyperloop. ", "codes": ["191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "3378c469-9c61-4390-84ae-613599416f80", "thread": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "item": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00162", "post": 162, "start": 0, "end": 306, "text": "I perused the comments in the thread and didn’t notice this caveat posted yet. To copyright software code there must be significant human authorship, and just what that is defined as, is still being determined, and any project with major contributions of LLM generated code may or may not be copyrightable.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e7312e9e-5942-45d8-9650-272441f21f28", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 10, "end": 396, "text": " over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "898796d2-3631-4e39-b475-a972f6411d5b", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1027, "end": 2483, "text": "LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "ab8177a8-d8de-4739-a677-adf23c926637", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2904, "end": 4166, "text": " Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "13106d46-5f49-4c89-a64c-cbde34e448a9", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4313, "end": 4736, "text": "We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "175a9cf7-8704-4662-a639-c03e33f704f2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4737, "end": 5479, "text": "Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "20820742-a598-4ee2-b8bb-79cc4a3964fa", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5678, "end": 6578, "text": " If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e65f1611-2d06-498e-9d54-709507644419", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 9, "end": 281, "text": "I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "dcf2e842-548a-4585-86c4-2cf36175cacb", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 110, "end": 343, "text": " I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "875202a7-dfa3-43db-beb9-294e8d5750ec", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 39, "end": 708, "text": " I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "5b73d163-41ac-4ad9-9c27-36f219b0a825", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 592, "text": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "6fc1199f-af28-48de-bcc2-6e64181c0f20", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 738, "end": 955, "text": "I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a"]}, {"id": "186c7a39-d819-4d54-8dd2-cfdb0c4dde01", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 78, "end": 681, "text": " Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit.", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2af2089-5cc4-4406-af84-da78c301620c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 1040, "end": 1556, "text": " Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "59e29cd3-7a59-4de3-b885-0f9075ab3253", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 423, "text": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "4857d8cf-ade7-42d4-9f2a-1549ee822286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 550, "text": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "f6f20e1d-575e-495d-9b0c-681edc843c91", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 238, "text": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "0bcb14dc-14ca-45bd-b4d4-ccce4f1c7264", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 213, "end": 1179, "text": " I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "a4a55aea-21c2-4592-95f2-8d95ae6f528f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 245, "text": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "94c7e857-c46e-4a00-b320-1d198ee83c96", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 18, "end": 287, "text": " if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "f2ccd9ea-0079-4fda-a5f7-aad45f0c8903", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 0, "end": 655, "text": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. ", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d"]}, {"id": "c62a2a2f-a8e8-4224-81db-e8c3d6c2478e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 655, "end": 1336, "text": "Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "2ca3907c-c8ad-4ed1-b777-154471ec6324", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1165, "text": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2b603e6-f933-484d-87f4-79ead239ea5c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 70, "end": 1156, "text": " Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "23a0b850-43ac-486a-a556-39468a8b5ed1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 813, "text": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "6a87bcb2-fe60-4f78-9bee-9772220d1923", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 158, "end": 798, "text": " My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "eb203983-0ee3-469b-9b42-7f0443d1d7c0", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 985, "end": 1306, "text": "IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3bcf5265-e584-48de-a964-e3a252572d71", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 1416, "end": 2510, "text": " However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO.", "codes": ["a7ed3bad-20c1-4021-94f5-71dc9bbc6073", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "faf7d5f9-7233-4e9e-8fe7-c7adb5c69116", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 2511, "end": 2798, "text": "+1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "b08cb9c4-eb30-406c-acca-83a4411e6cdd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 2003, "text": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”).", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "197e55ee-086d-4481-9ca7-b50cc3b9fefe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 2331, "end": 3267, "text": " Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions.", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "b15807a7-ae31-4e2e-b045-27be2c3ab7f3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 3347, "end": 3837, "text": "Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "886fcf69-da1e-409d-beca-e488dfaded83", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 308, "end": 1090, "text": " I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions ", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "fa66961f-1cb2-4074-b137-d825fb9eb573", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1145, "end": 2059, "text": "this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "5efb77fa-9929-4163-bb2a-2d31125d2f5e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1371, "text": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "85c972e6-ae7d-4b14-a5f5-7e4c776dd11d", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 29, "end": 633, "text": "The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "codes": ["c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "f8383b89-e68d-4622-823f-060f616572a1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 682, "text": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "25790222-2282-41eb-8a77-cd3ba3f75cac", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 438, "text": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "58f078b0-ab87-4861-9dc4-9a0214e435d3", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 517, "text": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "dde1498f-c02a-48b2-923a-503efd625fbd", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 731, "end": 1284, "text": "Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2d15a7e5-556f-4bd6-8289-ff382f549ac7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 0, "end": 1573, "text": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "795b05b3-e06d-4172-bddb-ce7e18c7a2ea", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 1583, "end": 3293, "text": "the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "codes": ["c737757c-d716-47ad-87a1-21af1631fc44", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "f08fe844-2d28-48f8-9a55-e0b25da8ef3e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 228, "end": 646, "text": " Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "1261064e-2c9f-43c0-a3eb-4f11de1588c2", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 716, "end": 1328, "text": " It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers.", "codes": ["d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3b936f2f-8050-4ebf-b310-6b776d89d2c7", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1329, "end": 2102, "text": "The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "419d96fe-e336-41aa-bd9f-2976eb29be41", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 2103, "end": 2947, "text": "It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "120a42db-4835-4380-8e4e-b7a3c590f9f1", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 3162, "end": 4564, "text": "This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "1b631a26-a627-4da7-af2b-0bf723133745", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 4597, "end": 5983, "text": " Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "25992365-c02d-48fd-b577-64305a3bfe18", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 6120, "end": 7444, "text": "Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "bc19a28b-8af6-4795-bf4b-96b10cb66c48", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 7743, "end": 8100, "text": "The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "9fda0190-3260-46c5-981b-511e8d576f4c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 12887, "end": 13239, "text": " This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "37fcbff9-4066-475f-9613-c3cfbc8d90ee", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "start": 0, "end": 998, "text": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "a4a0c7b3-e24a-41a8-a9a7-cedf88130f4e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "start": 0, "end": 318, "text": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3c9b2a63-001a-4ea1-89e9-5d88869a01d8", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "start": 27, "end": 825, "text": "the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "e1e24784-3114-47b8-8e63-64404df0ae8c", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "start": 0, "end": 612, "text": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "3acb21e2-4023-416d-8b15-41fe2be4158e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "start": 0, "end": 207, "text": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "0f8c4e08-2b23-493d-990b-e304fdbf5edc", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 0, "end": 636, "text": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "c55efd6a-55a5-4206-9c22-6193f2db903a"]}, {"id": "cba9e49a-1a69-4c65-979f-301a0a7dbbfe", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 269, "text": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes", "codes": []}, {"id": "6637c4d5-7385-428d-b29b-133ff341dcdf", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "start": 0, "end": 289, "text": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "4c63b006-7dea-49ab-a033-908954f4b47c"]}, {"id": "3890e28a-ab7b-49e5-b4ab-41991be3be89", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "733101b6-1011-4c2a-9447-a16f4e9aa96e", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 76, "end": 692, "text": " Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162"]}, {"id": "5f32eceb-c71e-467c-9851-7977e7b3f286", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 0, "end": 452, "text": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5bb238ac-a650-4a97-9968-7240c641f3db"]}, {"id": "c8963e76-3224-4011-8e67-14aaeb2f4a55", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 0, "end": 495, "text": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "5554f696-86cc-4ec8-9a01-730bf0428b76", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 522, "end": 1113, "text": "I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "f9a0d345-353f-49f3-8fc4-9472e566836f", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1260, "end": 2161, "text": " in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "5b662884-1b5b-45d4-9197-49114f525729", "thread": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "item": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 5, "end": 389, "text": " See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "codes": ["fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "1ce25ead-2c84-4ab1-91ce-3d77cb287879", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 118, "end": 1012, "text": " E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "26bacadd-3b11-480b-9d7c-ff14c50b05aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 50, "end": 1087, "text": " A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "97b9a19c-fac8-4bcb-8500-40b3de649acb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 108, "end": 564, "text": "My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "3f5ab29b-2f21-448d-870e-3b89b05c1c1c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 84, "end": 300, "text": "That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "01d36e86-d25c-4659-be6f-aca50908595e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 107, "end": 626, "text": " It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "9c4f3f11-c33e-47ff-b4f2-6aab2ce64017", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code?", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "3a7b597f-34b0-446c-adab-c080568e48c0", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 93, "end": 502, "text": " AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "5ba2e964-fa60-4c62-94fa-f0feb2afc5fd", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 503, "end": 1106, "text": "I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd"]}, {"id": "0cbc6753-9ef5-4f0f-b4d8-496eddd14727", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 106, "end": 384, "text": "This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "96034856-2d4a-4978-84da-8d4b4481ccb4", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 51, "end": 488, "text": " I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5"]}, {"id": "76195130-720b-4152-9aa3-72a8d671d624", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 1349, "text": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "9305a0fb-da0b-45f1-a351-cedc13332a5b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 0, "end": 286, "text": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as wel", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e2d614e2-a55e-46df-99f4-852447101d66", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 100, "end": 637, "text": " I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "e4e794b5-b6d8-4666-a67c-fefb640c4def", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 4, "end": 514, "text": "This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "13cba10c-2f54-42aa-98a9-90ee069e2ccf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 607, "end": 1821, "text": " In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building.", "codes": ["4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e097f575-4085-4a1d-92f1-43ff0b21ad7f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 1822, "end": 2594, "text": "Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928"]}, {"id": "3068469d-d7b9-42ac-8502-33d284f7ada6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2595, "end": 2976, "text": "I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "c732fc79-dedc-44d9-a9de-2006bf53ff26", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 2977, "end": 3396, "text": "But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI.", "codes": ["2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "03f6b706-a795-4335-a72a-7e49d995cc6c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 463, "end": 807, "text": " Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "279d2282-63a8-44d9-99af-65d03cb97583", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 103, "end": 787, "text": " E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "f25d86aa-3810-41f5-a06c-18b69e95a839", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 0, "end": 510, "text": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious.", "codes": ["f3d16ad8-3048-4a49-8f78-c41297230439", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "74f56b13-3847-427d-a5fb-cf2520654343", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 86, "end": 1474, "text": "Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation,", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "dfdcc40a-6a48-42cd-93a8-c0968a6c85aa", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 9, "end": 591, "text": "There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes)", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3"]}, {"id": "fb976f23-970e-44de-9474-1c90a0691c8d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 116, "end": 323, "text": " I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "15ea0bcd-f0dd-470c-b609-8f8e5d0bc788", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 6, "end": 1882, "text": " I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "de462e67-8924-4e01-b026-e1346f7a801c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 106, "end": 768, "text": " I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy", "codes": ["b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "a667360a-af00-44bf-9b68-47028a2c5a8f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 769, "end": 2128, "text": " I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a7212ccc-bb2c-4bd9-846e-291dc2d5953f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 1129, "text": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f"]}, {"id": "0dc06a45-7252-4215-8298-12b6df678768", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 104, "end": 2373, "text": "Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers:", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "b6279b38-1e49-4b33-a8ea-341968b0a7cc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2375, "end": 2923, "text": " Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed"]}, {"id": "0e19e7d4-da5c-417b-98d6-d945e806bd7b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 2925, "end": 3098, "text": "- New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow.", "codes": ["6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "dfe0c110-4bf1-4dd7-8f3e-c47c1054591d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 0, "end": 677, "text": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "93772467-26d6-4608-9f90-6bf4cfa74063", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 111, "end": 525, "text": "This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point.", "codes": ["cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "eabff033-0347-44f3-acd0-fb6ddb9c7d4d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "start": 526, "end": 1273, "text": "And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "87ade70d-e8ca-45c9-ba8c-de0652118a6f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "start": 0, "end": 1167, "text": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "53bd14f9-650c-4c9d-9b86-3e4019bee17f", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "04fcc7f6-80cb-43d2-97f1-f5254eb99e5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "start": 0, "end": 719, "text": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "cef518cc-ef6d-4dcb-9f98-af72aeda91f3", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "8330cfeb-7670-41cd-9b1f-4355a3e28227", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "start": 108, "end": 1643, "text": "The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "826e950a-826d-40cc-8f94-280197573444", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 83, "end": 1015, "text": " TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact?", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "3b4302b1-a72c-4266-bee8-bfc1e80c5f74", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1016, "end": 1785, "text": "For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)?", "codes": ["69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "3ece7a92-8060-4e37-a809-7ea6b0c9ae04", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "start": 1790, "end": 2646, "text": "FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "6e0bcb0e-99a7-4865-92d2-eaf0a391a0d2", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "start": 0, "end": 119, "text": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib.", "codes": ["0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d"]}, {"id": "57b3d8cf-4b08-41ba-bd36-5fd0e1339103", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 119, "end": 1566, "text": "Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "65fce25e-42a9-4bb5-9360-ac488fe392f6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "start": 1738, "end": 3742, "text": "I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "9beb18e8-8c7a-4f1e-a392-7e02e0efa82a", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "start": 11, "end": 1583, "text": " That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "72bd2f2e-8e52-4e5a-a96a-9e21a14ea0b9", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "start": 108, "end": 1212, "text": " Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "7e178caa-2e45-44d4-a940-0e934121963c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "start": 77, "end": 2066, "text": "While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "codes": ["769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "84370455-9c80-4fb4-ae18-def6f7ace4c7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "start": 1196, "end": 3581, "text": "Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me.", "codes": ["17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "2f0c99cc-8a57-4c5e-a0b6-e1d6fb04159d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "start": 0, "end": 399, "text": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "6a46c591-fb27-42a6-89d2-e80af4ad74ce", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "start": 3, "end": 1085, "text": " I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "6955b0df-f71c-4f42-bc12-d9abc46b078b"]}, {"id": "2c405b5a-bddb-4c2b-a6e6-c73995386f5c", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 247, "end": 4336, "text": " Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "a100e2d2-9263-4c6f-8632-5a3b7658360b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "start": 4337, "end": 6067, "text": "So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed.", "codes": ["8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "e5699363-9b3b-4a68-893d-bc408a0640cb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "start": 111, "end": 2906, "text": " I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "4ce57613-c997-4f36-8896-e95fe4ce9333", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "start": 102, "end": 1028, "text": " I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "48ed37b7-8b86-4cf5-b157-2befce171473", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "start": 110, "end": 883, "text": " Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148"]}, {"id": "46fc2b28-aa18-4ad3-bb83-29dac54f9f69", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 271, "end": 1290, "text": "the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "0bf5c586-c5be-4d48-8232-02a8fb1f86fe", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "599ceb84-b01b-497b-9512-ee8ca8338c54", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "start": 1290, "end": 3713, "text": " Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "e03a3a49-e8b1-4f9c-a868-0f385cd6bfc1", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "start": 106, "end": 500, "text": "Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16"]}, {"id": "7dd730f9-0877-458c-921c-aadd1dfe6a44", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 8, "end": 1535, "text": "I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "0218502f-2451-482b-8bbf-15ecb82f6a4d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "cc6a8be2-9a9e-428f-88ca-d6550e4b6157", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "start": 1537, "end": 2286, "text": "Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "723843d1-beb3-4a3b-89e2-39a7ecd1f1dc", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "start": 216, "end": 823, "text": "I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out.", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "af1d0d5c-7138-48e2-af37-696f33159d8e", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "start": 110, "end": 210, "text": " The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "codes": ["3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9"]}, {"id": "c7d901f3-72e4-49ce-a85d-932c9db4e81d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "start": 116, "end": 531, "text": " I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "codes": ["76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "3bd43ffc-c8f6-42a5-bcfb-ca7925505acf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "start": 100, "end": 605, "text": " Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR?", "codes": ["64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "4e9f97e3-519a-410f-ab7f-17efae16604b", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "start": 74, "end": 2337, "text": " I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "29cd93ea-5019-4d1e-886b-514571d727a8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "start": 106, "end": 1320, "text": "Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "40a727e6-89cc-4ce5-b669-c43ef08cfadb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "start": 8, "end": 1138, "text": " I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made.", "codes": ["9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "966711c5-ab8a-4c8a-96ee-5366172008b7", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "ecaf0ecf-4a95-40bd-9e81-949840e32f45", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "start": 76, "end": 1180, "text": " Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight.", "codes": ["776cdc34-693b-499d-9ddf-713ada941a16", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670"]}, {"id": "6be354ae-cff2-4350-a3f3-b1d1b696c3ca", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-)", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "f8e79487-2393-4e2f-bdb4-115395d9a29d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "start": 80, "end": 448, "text": "Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3"]}, {"id": "42ec4d44-c578-4271-8f38-b4d545d31af8", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "start": 0, "end": 344, "text": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. ", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "e8ee1d2a-64a3-4521-8ea1-20999d6e726d", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 750, "end": 1945, "text": " (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "585975d8-01fc-423e-9056-b17ca89f8921", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 1946, "end": 3082, "text": "(2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly?", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c"]}, {"id": "8070a77e-9ca5-42b6-bfdd-dd8ca9ecb7a7", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 3083, "end": 3902, "text": "(3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.”", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72"]}, {"id": "50df7254-c5cd-455a-a937-dc177614f329", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 4070, "end": 5321, "text": "I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "eaa206e6-1176-4e8d-b09a-7ae600abb376", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 5322, "end": 6134, "text": "(4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf"]}, {"id": "7e6fb536-19a6-43db-b6eb-2fc701a8e771", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "start": 6136, "end": 6849, "text": "(5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable).", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "128f5e8f-a60b-48bb-85bc-8ec404f77e8e"]}, {"id": "34febe70-3dca-4ca7-ac43-a09afb41026f", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62"]}, {"id": "2e137f3c-9f41-4186-a219-4d52f8718ecb", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "start": 60, "end": 908, "text": " FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out ", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "8c656a9d-1e35-49ad-835f-8d77f72ffba6", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "start": 77, "end": 964, "text": " I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085"]}, {"id": "6c83ef21-c962-444c-a25b-19ddf70012ff", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "start": 426, "end": 1507, "text": " There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created", "codes": ["c4aa7205-ee9d-4393-b856-2fdbe11d1897", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "93c7026e-f78f-43c7-bf7d-22aa8f3773cf", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "start": 84, "end": 327, "text": "You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "52fbca57-a761-4237-8177-5f37e809b548", "thread": "numpy-policy-thread", "item": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "start": 4, "end": 903, "text": "I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming.", "codes": ["cd1eb91c-52ff-46d1-a5fd-36b4dea59085", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "2fcb00ad-1901-4124-9b51-d6ae21717824", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 142, "end": 763, "text": " the ROS PMC has elected to extend the upstream OSRF policy on the use of generative tools to help provide some boundaries/guidelines on the use of these tools. These tools and technologies are constantly changing, so this isn’t intended to serve as a permanent policy, but rather it is to provide a framework for how to interact on ROS-related projects. The TLDR, because there are a lot of words below: Feel free to use the tools to build, explore, and understand software (as many of the maintainers also do), but when it comes time to interact on Discourse, Zulip, or Github, try to leave the LLM-centric text behind.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "17cb5f99-5ac6-4f13-9787-7e0be60f98e5", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "fc108c9b-4934-413c-b302-2ad7f4a80447", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 764, "end": 930, "text": "A large component of open source software is about building/collaborating with people, and we would generally prefer to interact with people rather than an LLM-proxy.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "30013df9-5a1a-4822-a853-a70366c33315", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 930, "end": 1187, "text": " Maintainer time is a finite and constrained resource. While generative AI tools can be useful development assistants, submitting unverified Large Language Model (LLM) output shifts the engineering, debugging, and verification work onto project maintainers.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "605e9cc9-4663-4701-9e9f-10d43909f372", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1188, "end": 2140, "text": "This document establishes the rules for interacting with ROS project repositories when using AI tools. It serves as an extension of the upstream OSRF Policy on the Use of Generative Tools in Contributions . While the upstream policy defines the legal, IP, and attribution requirements for AI-generated code, these rules define the quality and behavioral standards required to protect maintainer bandwidth. The Author Ownership Rule The upstream OSRF policy states that contributors must have the right to sign the Developer Certificate of Origin (DCO). Operationally, this means you are the sole owner of, and are fully responsible for, the code you submit. Do not use maintainers as an LLM validation service. If a maintainer points out a bug, architectural issue, or syntax error in your PR, you may not simply paste that feedback back into an LLM and copy-paste the unverified output as a response. You must understand every line of code you submit.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "d579d272-5ba5-4cf3-9d4a-0025181df06b", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2141, "end": 2635, "text": "If a maintainer asks why a specific technical choice was made, responding with “an LLM generated this” or providing a generic, AI-synthesized explanation is unacceptable. You must be able to defend the technical merits of your implementation yourself. Maintainers want to engage with you, the author of the proposed changes. Maintainers want to understand why you want to make the change and why you want to implement it the way the PR proposes. Maintainers do not want to talk to your chatbot.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "06ea3442-a799-4c64-84f5-e99ade568ce0", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 2636, "end": 3151, "text": "Zero-Tolerance for Hallucinated API/Dependency Usage LLMs frequently hallucinate APIs, configuration parameters, or entire library features, especially given the fast-moving nature of ROS releases and underlying middleware (DDS, Zenoh, rclcpp, rclpy, etc.). Any PR that introduces non-existent APIs, impossible configuration keys, or broken logic due to LLM hallucinations will be closed immediately without review. Repeat offenses will result in a temporary or permanent block from the organization’s repositories.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "bf4ca6f7-9b77-431b-95c1-fff87f956092", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 3152, "end": 3750, "text": "Compliance with Upstream Disclosure & Attribution The upstream OSRF policy explicitly requires that the use of generative tools be disclosed if they created a material part of the contribution. We enforce this strictly on GitHub: If an LLM was used to generate structural code, tests, or documentation boilerplate, state it clearly in the PR description. Do not LLM dump to generate your PR description. Paragraphs filled with generic AI phrasing (e.g., “This PR enhances the efficiency of the codebase by utilizing modern design patterns and optimizing asynchronous paradigms…” ) will be rejected.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb"]}, {"id": "aee16147-2a67-40e4-829c-7c4487a0679b", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 3751, "end": 4498, "text": "Write a concise, human-authored summary explaining exactly what changed and why . Enforcement and Maintainer Recourse To maintain the health of the project, maintainers have full discretion to handle low-effort, AI-driven contributions efficiently: If an issue or PR is clearly an unverified LLM dump (e.g., containing generic AI comments, formatting that violates project style guides, contains a poorly formatted description pasted directly from an LLM, or obviously broken logic), maintainers are authorized to close the PR immediately. If a contributor responds to code review feedback using obvious, unedited LLM-generated text that fails to address the underlying engineering critique, maintainers are authorized to close the PR immediately.", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "64cdfc9b-4752-4101-86af-ab8d34b2b800"]}, {"id": "b4cad8f2-73c5-4b73-b3fa-7f0eddcfe935", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 4499, "end": 5013, "text": "If the contributor fails to respond to reviewer questions or feedback within a reasonable timeframe (e.g. within a week), reviewers may close the PR without further review. If a contributor continues to behave in an unproductive way through repeated submission of inappropriate LLM-dump issues or PRs, repeated copy-and-pasting of LLM-dump comments, or similar, maintainers are authorized to temporarily or permanently block that user from the organization’s repositories. Guidelines for Responsible AI Use in ROS ", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "ed960de4-c051-4b3f-a11a-fdbe5714c43d", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 5013, "end": 5862, "text": "If you want to use an LLM to help you successfully contribute to ROS, adhere to the following workflow: Confirm that the issue is still valid and that any proposed solution is still preferred. Start a conversation on the issue thread to ensure maintainers agree with the approach. Opening PRs for stale or invalid issues simply because an AI bot identified them creates unnecessary overhead for maintainers and is strongly discouraged. Run the code locally. Verify it compiles, passes linting, and passes all relevant tests before opening a PR. Before hitting “Submit,” read through your own diff line-by-line. Remove any redundant logic, overly verbose AI-generated comments, or structural weirdness introduced by the model. Explain your changes in plain technical language. If you can’t describe the change yourself, you aren’t ready to submit it.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "040fd41d-6b99-4108-b36e-4ffcf89097f4", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 0, "end": 230, "text": "Additionally, we are looking at rolling out AGENTS.md across many of our repos to hopefully make our LLM-assistants respect these guidelines. I would be interested to hear from anyone who has experience keeping the agents in line.", "codes": ["ee15fa69-5eed-49a2-9f69-fa10a6b166e6"]}, {"id": "e57f9c72-b8c1-4297-8062-07c2cf93db46", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 689, "text": "I would say that generally, the same rules apply. I know many people use them, but I would expect that interactions on issue trackers, pull requests, etc are done by the author. I know that I have been guilty of being a little excited about automation in the past, but I think for long-term health, we should mostly be interacting as humans. That being said, I personally don’t mind getting a human-authored summary/conversation and also the LLM output, as it’s often helpful context but easily ignored. The spirit of the guidelines here are more that we shouldn’t just be LLM dumping on each other constantly because everyone (maintainers and contributors) will just run out of bandwidth.", "codes": ["a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "8e35ad06-a126-4f05-88cb-41783ec93c74", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00005", "post": 5, "start": 0, "end": 665, "text": "I very much agree with this policy and has been what I’ve started to adhoc do in Nav2 over the last couple of weeks independently. The number of sloppy AI PRs and issues (often addressing nonsensical issues) is getting too exhausting to address in a thoughtful process-oriented way. I hit a point of almost complete burn-out by dozens of nonsense ‘security’ reports or massive rewrites generated by AI with either no follow-up or excessive and/or incorrect AI generated follow-up to a point I was dreading opening GitHub in the morning. I’m happy to see someone put in better words and will align with this for Nav2 as well. This type of action has saved my sanity.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b"]}, {"id": "9bda5ee4-8ecd-41b8-be64-1e0cf8e341c6", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00006", "post": 6, "start": 0, "end": 255, "text": "You are absolutely right to push back on LLM use for human-centric conversations! (sorry, couldn’t resist). I think this is a right move. I find it so tiring to read AI slop these days that when I see a typical LLM-like structures in text, I just give up.", "codes": ["118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "038c8504-5c0c-4f60-85a2-5bf91b722c7a", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00007", "post": 7, "start": 0, "end": 203, "text": "Tom Fishburne has a visual way to explain the problems that I find very relevant image 1200×628 164 KB More at AI Written, AI Read cartoon - Marketoonist | Tom Fishburne And long live human interactions!", "codes": ["4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "26e5cdba-d0bd-4898-af98-a9d43199b8d6", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 579, "text": "I would honestly caution against this at most, these guidelines should be present in a CONTRIBUTING.md if the person using the LLM is not capable of reading that and complying with it, then their PR can simply be ignored. anyone serious about contributing will read it, and if they haven’t read it, they are not serious about contributing further, the presence of AGENTS.md , CLAUDE.md , etc. files really turns me off from contributing to any project I see. for some time now, I’ve been wanting to contribute more to ROS, however something like this would make me less likely to", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c"]}, {"id": "a237f8d4-e5ed-4192-971b-3406f7bbe7b3", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 82, "end": 329, "text": "As someone who’s been building an open-source agent system (ClawsJoy) that involves LLM-based orchestration, I’ve been thinking about this exact tension: AI tools accelerate exploration, but the human element is what makes open source sustainable.", "codes": ["6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "0408fbfc-094e-4ef5-a139-838af8219cd0", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 417, "end": 924, "text": "1. The “understand what you’re submitting” rule is the most important one. This is what separates using AI as a pair programmer vs using it as a delegation machine . I’ve adopted the practice of writing the explanation first (in plain human language, describing what I want to change and why), then using AI to assist with implementation, then reviewing the diff line by line before submitting. The explanation stays in the PR description — it’s the human-authored “contract” that the code needs to satisfy.", "codes": ["a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "acf5d0db-8433-4228-a025-212ee9622fdd", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 925, "end": 1457, "text": "2. One challenge I haven’t seen fully addressed: verifying that AI-generated code actually works in the specific ROS environment. LLMs are often trained on generic ROS 2 examples, but real-world usage involves specific distros, middleware versions (DDS, Zenoh), and hardware. I’ve found that the biggest time sink isn’t generating code — it’s validating that the generated code is compatible with the actual environment. Would love to see community-shared validation workflows (e.g., a CI check that flags potential API mismatches).", "codes": ["a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a"]}, {"id": "5fc04abd-e0ac-4214-b021-33aaf3232368", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 1458, "end": 1819, "text": "3. On disclosure formatting: The commit-message Generated-by: approach makes sense for traceability. One thing I’m experimenting with is a GENERATED-BY: comment in the source file itself for structural code sections (e.g., “this entire class was generated by X”), and commit-message disclosure for smaller snippets. Happy to share what works/doesn’t as I learn.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d"]}, {"id": "3fda4c3a-7304-4e33-a7e5-c52048924ece", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 1820, "end": 2699, "text": "4. From a maintainer’s perspective: The hardest part isn’t detecting LLM-generated code — it’s detecting LLM-generated code that looks plausible but is subtly wrong . The guidelines on hallucinated API usage and zero-tolerance for unverified output are spot on. I’d add: if an LLM-generated PR passes the “read your own diff” test (i.e., the author can explain every line), that’s already a strong signal that the author has done the necessary work. One quick question: For projects that are already heavy users of AI tools, are you considering any tooling to automate the disclosure tracking? E.g., a pre-commit hook that prompts for a disclosure statement when it detects certain patterns, or a CI check that validates commit message format? This could reduce the friction of compliance and make it easier for contributors to “do the right thing” without extra mental overhead.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "8b3f67d3-01d3-4a51-99b5-d542be0c48f0"]}, {"id": "d8623b82-21ad-47fe-bad6-b140e772fcca", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 93, "end": 531, "text": "Verifying that AI-generated code works is the same as verifying that human-produced code works. For other ROS code maintained by others (e.g. Nav2, or a package produced by a company building a product on ROS), they can and will decide their own policies. The OSRA policy recommends Assisted-by commit statements and comments , as it more closely fits how we want people to use these tools: as assistants, not replacements for themselves.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "7617af40-7615-4cd3-a8e4-8c6dd1d2db91", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00018", "post": 18, "start": 31, "end": 463, "text": "I believe that the use of LLMs is fundamentally incompatible with many of my beliefs. I do not want to use LLMs, I do not want to contribute to projects that encourage LLM generated content, I do not want to use projects that contain LLMs generated content. I believe that the use of LLMs is unethical for a variety of reasons. I find their use quite gross, and it makes me (and many others) want to avoid any project that uses them", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5"]}, {"id": "4f2cd15c-e04b-45b7-bbba-2568a7baf229", "thread": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "item": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 0, "end": 488, "text": "Many senior engineers and developers are using LLM’s as well as self-taught individuals without a technical backround like me. You can use AI tools for a meaningful and valuable open-source contribution. There are not all the projects meaningless, shallow contributions. Reacting to all the projects that have being used LLM’s with the same scepticism , we’re escluding the ones that wants to add value into the field. Not mention how many of us are using AI TOOLS withoit even admit it..", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "6ba3a7e8-e871-4104-83c3-8841bf25c7c9"]}, {"id": "f00c88ed-8f95-4d8f-8398-205bb63f7ed7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 844, "end": 1916, "text": "it seems that the concerns can be roughly classified into two buckets: Do we accept genAI assisted contributions at all, and if we do, what are the boundaries of what’s acceptable. Copyright and license concerns. Let’s consider these two in order. Do we accept genAI contributions? It’s futile to attempt to block them wholesale. People are going to use AI assistants anyway, along with various IDEs, autocompleters etc So it seems reasonable to treat these assistants on par with other tools: as long as it does not matter if a contributor uses VS Code or emacs or vi, it also does not matter what engine powers their autocompleter. The discussion in [1] has a concise wording, “Only submit code that you actually undestand”, and I’d just go with just this. One thing we probably do not want to see is a fully automated submission, which is both generated and submitted by a bot, with no human intervention or supervision at all. So we could/should stress that, too. Copyright violations / incompatible licenses Here I lack expertise and have more questions than answers.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "9730f4a8-1f4c-44bd-9c30-819145e61c72", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "e00a42fd-55d5-4fb1-a625-b0cab528395b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "start": 1917, "end": 3210, "text": "Here’s a possibly naive attempt at asking hopefully relevant questions: Are we concerned that the code generated by some tool is under some incompatible license from just the fact of being generated by the tool? In this case, using the tool is similar to copying code from, say, Numerical Recipes. Then we should either blacklist known bad tools (do we know of any?) or ask contributors to declare the tools they used (so that we can remove offending code at a future date if needed). Are we concerned that the training set of a tool included license-incompatible code? And if it did, what does it mean for us, are we then transitively non-compliant? If this is a concern, and we at some point discover that some code is license-incompatible, the action does not depend on how it got into the codebase, genAI or not: we discover it, we remove it. And then the only question IMO is whether it’s helpful to us to ask contributors list the AI assistants they used. To summarize, we should not block genAI assisted contributions; we should block fully automated contributions, and ask submitters to only submit code they understand; TBD if we want to ask contributors to list what genAI assistants they used, if any. Let’s add explicit (short!) guidance to the docs, TBD where precisely. Thoughts?", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "cc62bcef-ce92-446c-9004-eebf31d5d3bd", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "047d9e6e-4d7d-470d-854c-abc47cfe58c5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 66, "end": 1318, "text": "If I could, I would have used these ChatGPT/CoPilot/Skynet/“All seeing overlord AI” like there is no tomorrow. But my experience with them so far is that, they are almost good and make just a tiny mistake like i becomes j , then I spend rest of the week troubleshooting, learning how to use GDB and fail miserably etc. So it seems like we are swapping manual labor of translating with manual labor of troubleshooting, which for me, definitely the latter that is more painful. But maybe it is because it is not trained enough on F77 or JurassicPACK code we have. On the other hand, I did get some useful tips if I ask programming tips, say, “show me an example of while loop that uses comma operator as an expression in C17” and it brought some nice results. I did not try but some folks in my team use it for summarizing the code about what it does. Other than that, probably @rkern is the most knowledgeable among us what the legal implications might be, however Copilot seems to understand when you say avoid GPL sources for the answer at the citations it brings. Having said all these, I noticed a particular behavior from all AI tools. They are providing a melange of things that they have in their training bank (whatever they call it these days).", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "1bb066ee-75b7-479d-b608-a4410e06f2cf"]}, {"id": "b4be7cb2-e694-429f-b5ac-10543209ab09", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "start": 1319, "end": 2794, "text": "I am ready to be immortalized in the history books as an idiot, but AI tools are terrible at coming up coherent code for numerical computing. Not only they can’t answer any technical computing question coherently, but when asked in ASCII about certain technical problem, they are bringing almost always some code that looks suspiciously similar to either GNU scientific library or Octave or some stuff they knocked off from some personal gist translated in the requested language. I noticed this mostly because of the strange index corrections before they do things on the arrays (which signals that the source must be a 1-indexed one). While sometimes the result is very close to the actual thing, it is devoid of any proper numerical checks (because they can’t judge the result). Hence, in my personal opinion, before we even judge the legality of things, the sheer possibility of the code being not production ready gives me enough confidence to reject any AI generated code. Thus agree with summary point 2 even if the code runs correctly. This is not the same with a developer using AI for snippets of code for very specific thing and including parts of AI generated code. This is very tricky to judge as AI tools already swallowed Stackoverflow and other sources, we don’t have any ways to backtrack the original source. So a careful agree with summary point 1. I don’t know how to enforce point 3 but if there is a way, that would be really nice. Point 4, fully agree.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "5ddf8ded-b9e7-4567-a80c-8b10e845eec9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00003", "post": 3, "start": 0, "end": 994, "text": "Out of precaution (there are still many lawsuits pending) and most importantly, for moral and ethical reasons, I am -1 on allowing any generative AI at all. Adding here the opinion piece from Paul, which I fully agree with: Current Challenges in Free Software and Open Source Development We spend so much effort ensuring that contributions are original and respect all licensing, copyrights and just wishes of original authors, that it would be strange to me to adopt a different position. Furthermore, as pointed out here and in some other threads, the nature of what we do in SciPy is difficult and these tools don’t (yet) shine. Writing code is only part of the story and often times we spend our effort discussing API, architecture, etc. Hence I don’t think that it would be detrimental to disallow such tools. There is a lot of hype and companies in the sector are trying hard to push that we are doomed if we don’t use their stuff. IMHO, this is not true and we are far from replacing us.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "7810381a-4708-4a23-b75f-b915f866be23"]}, {"id": "3c21abb4-d8f7-4d3c-b953-0472a0e79e55", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "post": 4, "start": 0, "end": 197, "text": "Since this is an issue that affects all projects in the ecosystem, can we collaborate to co-author a SPEC that can be widely adopted, instead of each library having to figure it out for themselves?", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873"]}, {"id": "9f78c452-8cd9-4036-b4f7-ce7336eec88b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00008", "post": 8, "start": 0, "end": 387, "text": "I haven’t seen any problems with this. In my experience, it’s very useful and writes high-quality code and saves time. I guess it depends on which tool you’re using. As long as unit tests are included and human reviewers approve it, I don’t see how it’s any different in trustworthiness from code written by random humans. But yes, copyright and license legality is a legitimate concern.", "codes": ["bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "115b4ae1-4915-45f1-a62d-7a9931b7801c"]}, {"id": "9de47c35-b592-445d-925a-02062b62ed25", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00009", "post": 9, "start": 0, "end": 693, "text": "Hi, It doesn’t seem useful to argue about the usefulness of the AI-generated code, or otherwise, at this stage. I haven’t found it useful myself, but maybe that’s just my case, and in any case, it may well improve. It’s the license issue that is central. I don’t think there’s any argument that these tools are all digesting code with licenses that at least require attribution, and producing code, indirectly (or directly at times) derived from that licensed code, but with all licensing stripped from the output. The value of a spec or similar, over a blog post, is the ability to iterate to a shared and agreed conclusion, rather than leaving it as the opinion of any one particular person.", "codes": ["e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "0e5b84de-2166-4471-9e3e-d6a2d9cda7c7", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "post": 10, "start": 30, "end": 439, "text": " We don’t have any hope of controlling user behavior through a policy document. Many people find these tools useful, so they will keep using them, and there is no sensible way to check the license of the generated snippet. For larger PRs, which may contain more overt license breaches, it’s probably less likely that AI was used wholesale, since the author needed to understand the problem well to begin with.", "codes": ["23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "90a10463-8e44-4f1a-8cc0-1969c9ef4eff", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00011", "post": 11, "start": 0, "end": 303, "text": "As we don’t have any ways of ensuring people did not read NR nor had a look at GPL code nor took anything from StackOverflow. To me that’s the same thing. If we were to say that we don’t want people not to use any AI tools we could and that’s the same type of self declaration we would ask people to do.", "codes": ["2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d"]}, {"id": "27a779f2-7a25-4118-a83b-52e6e50ecf4c", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00012", "post": 12, "start": 0, "end": 1566, "text": "I think it is not that simple to discard the usefulness, though I wrote a wall of text arguing otherwise above. These tools, whether blindly parroting or contemplating, became quite eloquent. As I mentioned above, lately I have been testing them (or they are observing me) and though a bit of success is achieved for line completion, figuring out things from scratch are not even close. However, once I put a lot of comments and write the first two-three lines they predict the context OKish. Then they fill in some boilerplate code and I correct here and there. Some time has saved, some productivity increased in turn utilizing a power plant worth energy for this. I would suggest putting a few sentences in the policy docs that we don’t want wholesale AI code knowing full well it would be near impossible to distinguish. But if we notice something noticeably fishy, we can point to the policy. Or if someone is reading before contributing, they’ll get a taste of our stance. How AI tools became like that and what they did criminally/unethically to become like that, is something we can discuss for years. Licenses and policies would definitely protect across the totality of the project, but I think anything more granular than that, it is just futile Stallman-ing the reality. Unlike licensed code from elsewhere, people will grab pieces from LLMs here and there. We don’t need to pick this fight in our limited time. Besides, it’s not like we are getting very large contributions anyways. So I think this is not a big issue as we might be starting to discuss.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "23ab59c9-ae63-42a7-996b-105285fcd815", "bdd17040-1629-426f-95b8-ec9e7e4567cf", "3040ace4-2186-4d17-a025-25d9d98394ed", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "191ea764-94ec-4c81-aadd-d93deb2128a5", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "7e0a8228-fc41-45ea-9f37-57da0b7b1670", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "77c53c4e-bfc3-409b-949c-b1455ee5b146", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00013", "post": 13, "start": 1427, "end": 2228, "text": " I remember, back in the day - it was desirable to do “clean-room” porting - where the person writing the new code had not read the code with an incompatible license. For example, one person could read the e.g. GPL code, describe the algorithm, and the other person could write the new code. If the person writing the code had read the the GPL code, it was always possible for the GPL code to leak into the new code. Of course, with AI, it’s all dirty room - it can produce code from anywhere. So I think we have to have the same policy for AI as for dirty-room code. We can’t use it. Now - enforcement - that’s a different matter - I just think we should pursue the same policies on enforcement as we did before. We say don’t do it, and if we find you did do it, we’ll complain and pull out the code.", "codes": ["cb6ddf65-863d-45f9-a8d9-e499e7325c0c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "b3fa48a3-4a1f-44ac-85aa-bd9be0e88f43", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00014", "post": 14, "start": 74, "end": 433, "text": " What I am trying to emphasize is the practicality of it. If same code appears a 50 places because all 50 of them used AI generated sources and MIT’d the code. I don’t think we can find the 51st original source and point to its GPL. So it is an idealistic stance. Still good to have it but a policy won’t solve any trouble in case we find out the truth later.", "codes": ["b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}, {"id": "925e1625-49af-40bb-9d28-93b3c788fba3", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00015", "post": 15, "start": 0, "end": 1355, "text": "I’d be happy to see a SPEC about this - I think it can be an informative SPEC (similar to an informative PEP) that doesn’t propose a single solution to adopt, but contains at least the following: Prior art - what did other well-known open source projects decide and why? Legal status - link to relevant sources, and summarize if there’s any expectation of more legal clarity arising in the near to medium term. Lay out a set of distinct options that projects can choose from, with their main pros and cons. E.g.: Option 1: do nothing for now (status quo) Option 2: forbid all generated code by an LLM-based tool Option 3: add guidance to PR template & docs that contributors may choose to use such tools, but need to be aware and take responsibility for copyright of the code they submit Option 4: … ? None of that is project-specific, and people can contribute new arguments and options if they find there are gaps in such a SPEC. A Discourse thread only is a poor medium for iterating to a solution on such a complex topic. I completely agree with @rkern that it’s largely about values and preferences - it’s just a lot easier to get a sense of those and also help people less knowledgeable about the subject form an opinion if there’s a structured document to read and refer to. Once a good SPEC exists, a SciPy-specific choice can more easily be made.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "e2acd680-da16-4aa6-9447-d18c31a31873", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "19747253-695b-4500-bb7b-d914913a6d1e", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 175, "end": 1302, "text": " I wouldn’t mind some feedback on the approach I chose, and I hope that my experience could be used as one of the many reference approaches people have in using these tools. The thought of encountering possible copyright violations in terms of generated code pulled from other sources always scratched the back of my mind. So far, I use generative tools to create reference sandbox scripts to test a functionality I have in mind, and I fine tune the generation in case I’m not satisfied with the result. In almost all cases, the generated scripts remain just that and I keep them locally without publishing anywhere. I just use them to review an idea I had. I do not keep track of how much code I effectively publish which is generated, and even if I do that, I always follow the assumption that the AI is wrong. That’s because: I don’t trust the accuracy of the prompts I input; I don’t trust the effective comprehension of the tool receiving the prompt, accurate or not it might be. In the end I always end up writing my own code, but what the tooling gives me is a better understanding of the problem I’m currently tackling.", "codes": ["5cf5ebee-a17a-47db-829c-8ab545498185", "6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "0eb9a5dd-fc9a-48fd-b957-32b79dc805ef", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "start": 1302, "end": 2416, "text": " I believe someone else in the thread already gave a -1 on completely generated PRs, which I also agree to: before even considering publishing generated code there should be an inner understanding of what’s been generated, and if it’s a copy-paste of the prompt response there’s no guarantee that this is enforced. I am still okay with the approach of using AI as it was meant to be used, a.k.a. an assistant that gives an initial shape to an idea which then is developed on its own with no trace of the original prompt response. In my case, I am considering adding to the documentation of my new projects a comprehensive approach on using AI tooling (it’s a plugin-driven software where the idea is “bring your tool and I glue it to a broader system”), especially since the trend seems to be “I gave a prompt to ChatGPT and I published a plugin for this software, here’s how YOU can also do it!”. At least that’s what I’ve heard from colleagues attending conferences on the matter. It didn’t happen LITERALLY like this, but my feeling is that this is the subtle message that (willing or not) transpires from this.", "codes": ["6edc630e-5534-410f-84a0-fbfadd1d8ec3", "a9d1e423-7581-4446-b08c-d10d2d661123", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff"]}, {"id": "9cba128f-856d-44de-8741-d2de1d82277d", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00017", "post": 17, "start": 0, "end": 537, "text": "Hi, [snip] I’m afraid the problem of the license remains. AI generated some code where it is effectively impossible for you to know what license(s) should correctly apply. Now you’ve seen the code with the unknowable license, you are at grave risk of reproducing aspects of that code, thereby pulling in the unknowable license. Your resulting code, even if you have rewritten it - cannot be asserted free of the original (unknowable) license). And therefore you cannot assert your own copyright, or freedom from other people’s copyright.", "codes": ["508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "9f8763f4-a94e-4823-bfee-0f6afa4af186", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00019", "post": 19, "start": 0, "end": 446, "text": "Those interested in prior art may be interested in Fedora’s draft policy and the discussion it has spawned . A quick summary of their proposed rules on contributions: AI tools are not banned, but low-quality AI use is. AI use must be disclosed. AI use for translation is explicitly allowed. Limit AI Tools for reviewing contributions. Of course, SciPy and Fedora are not precisely comparable as Fedora is mostly re-distributing existing software.", "codes": ["0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "a6869ee1-b194-4fbc-8db0-7b543fc63a03"]}, {"id": "77ce0225-51ca-4e85-88bd-55332ef6fed8", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00020", "post": 20, "start": 0, "end": 204, "text": "I’d also like to propose a zero-tolerance policy for lying about AI use. For example, if you ask a contributor if they used are using AI, and you get an AI-generated answer that claims that they’re human.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "079f9515-486d-4e87-957f-a262b9ec9148"]}, {"id": "588c0fec-d7bb-4cd0-8e11-f6f00ba6aec0", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 0, "end": 1171, "text": "I am increasingly seeing vibe-coded PRs from novice contributors and I started a discussion on the SymPy mailing list . Right now there are not too many of these but they are quite blatant and I think that this is definitely going to become worse over time. Really these are just low quality PRs but I don’t think it is reasonable to engage with them in the same way as a low quality PR that was actually written by a human. For example if you explain in detail what the problems are with the code then I think that there is a good chance that the “author” is going to type your feedback into the LLM and have it try to vibe-code an improved PR. I have tried it and I really don’t enjoy talking to LLMs if actually trying to do anything and talking to an LLM via something like GitHub comments with someone who is just typing those comments into an LLM is soul-destroying. When reviewing novice PRs the situation is generally that it would be far easier to just write the code without the novice so there is a net loss in terms of effort on the maintainer side. The flipside though is that the novice hopefully learns something from the experience and improves over time.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "4b0aa9d4-51a0-461f-9a90-2f7529e0f217", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "eca063a5-731a-4fa0-b89f-3a07b9ff9c30", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "start": 1172, "end": 2952, "text": "This model is predicated on there being a certain effort-exchange ratio though e.g. that the novice does not open the PR in the first place without putting in some effort beforehand to understand the codebase, the workflow, the issue they are trying to fix and so on. I think it is pretty much possible now to say “Claude, write some code and open a PR to fix issue #12345 ” so any kind of technical barrier is gone and you can open a spam PR having spent zero time trying to understand anything. I also don’t think that this is helpful to novices because in the PR process the AI is helping them with all of the wrong things like writing the code rather than thinking about how to write the code. A student at my University recently asked what is “the purpose of Maths” given that computers (e.g. SymPy, Maple etc) can do all of the exercises we ask them to do by hand in their entry-level Maths exam. My answer was that you have to understand how to do some things manually before you can learn how to make effective use of a computer for more complex problems. Having the computer do your homework is like getting a robot to lift weights for you at the gym. I don’t think that the vibe-code PR authors are malicious. People want to contribute to open source and why wouldn’t a novice believe the hype that this is how code is written in the age of AI? Although unintentional though the resulting spam is effectively abusive to open source projects and maintainers. We need some kind of guidance or policy so that contributors understand what is reasonable. I don’t know how to write any policy/guidance about “responsible use of AI” though without first addressing the basic questions from above like whether AI-generated code is allowed at all and what it means for copyright.", "codes": ["5a7138e9-3e5a-45b4-8e3a-4760a9dcd686", "769f59ad-c63c-4a33-8334-101f54b388cb", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9b4014ac-2e57-4f57-bfbf-be4d76426aef", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58"]}, {"id": "db7dd52f-29d7-4a3d-8562-485a63b1d2b4", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00022", "post": 22, "start": 0, "end": 1112, "text": "Thanks for this useful write-up. I guess anyone who is using PRs to certify experience in contribution is going to run into Cambell’s law pretty soon. It still seems to me that we can avoid these problems by saying that the contributor has to take full responsibility for copyright, and therefore, they should avoid AI unless the contribution is so trivial in intellectual terms, that copyright is not an issue. Something like: I have not used AI for the code in this contribution OR I have personally reviewed the content of this PR in detail for potential copyright violations, and confirm that no such violation has occurred. I suppose we could then add advice not to use LLMs for PR summaries, because of the problems that you’ve pointed out. Perhaps we could use some heuristic - such as screening for signs of AI slop, and sticking a label on the PR to say slop is suspected, and asking the contributor to confirm that AI was not used, or that they have carefully checked the content for accuracy and made it concise. Reject the PR (for a contributor who has not shown previous bona-fides) if slop remains.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "b52f8bb3-cf68-4a83-a4f8-c74e29b5dd6a", "3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "a606e858-be3b-4d8b-ac35-926a27f47e91", "118f6296-5bd2-45b7-be54-36ae445b63fc", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "34b5e2da-36cd-4daa-aebd-2ed18a1f1ba3"]}, {"id": "c8315733-f8e2-4076-90ff-689cee6a5053", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00023", "post": 23, "start": 0, "end": 347, "text": "I like this idea! Perhaps a useful addition would be instructions of what to look out for? And to ask to also confirm that the user reviewed the AI-generated code? I imagine to many of the new users that seem to be a big part of this discussion it may be not clear at all what are the possible pitfalls of using a fully automatically generated PR.", "codes": ["0e25ae3d-49bd-4f0f-bb75-4627aa41cfbd", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "3f15c7b6-08a4-4e2d-9e2e-5d834fe962f9", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "d10cfbed-b8c1-4bf7-8489-eb6b1f2cf10d", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "2ca4c0cd-4b43-4380-89f6-c2411033a58b", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00024", "post": 24, "start": 0, "end": 432, "text": "That assumes that people are interested in learning. Lot of people don’t really care. They just want to get something merged as fast as possible for x/y/z, it does not really matter. What matters to me is to know so that I can approach the PR with a totally different mindset. If the PR gives me a small value and I want this now, I spend the review time to fix what I want and just merge. Otherwise I close and we all move on fast.", "codes": ["3d2c2df3-896c-4866-92fe-b6de6763ce1d", "c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "fd562c40-90ac-41da-9b3a-164b15d27a5b", "5a94e77b-31ce-4cd4-b189-b369cf21d335", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "6eed1196-102f-4de1-b21c-b9ed4180eae9", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00025", "post": 25, "start": 0, "end": 479, "text": "One problem with this is it’s really not clear how to review the content for potential violations. For handwritten code, it’s pretty clear: don’t look into GPL licensed code (GSL is out, R is out etc). With LLMs though: how am I, as a contributor, supposed to review what LLM gave me? Comments upthread seem to indicate that if I touched LLMs at all, it’s “dirty” right away. If it’s more nuanced than that, great, then a contributor needs some more specific guidance, I’d think.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "508152a6-7679-48fe-a1d1-f239099c0b9d", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "08af1ffd-21ea-4b5b-bcc1-728c202ae8a5", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00026", "post": 26, "start": 0, "end": 535, "text": "We could unpack more, but basically, right, if the LLM is generating substantial code, that might be subject to copyright, you have the unpleasant job of going to check whether it should be subject to copyright. But for the kind of tasks that LLMs do well, like automatic refactoring of imports, swapping single for double quotes in strings and so on, the question doesn’t arise. So yes, the practical upshot is that we throw the burden on the contributor for substantial code, but I think that’s reasonable, given the copyright risks.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "268ecb16-c609-46da-883a-de388c6a3674", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00027", "post": 27, "start": 22, "end": 1762, "text": " Basically, I honestly don’t understand 1) how can a contributor check for potential copyright violations, and 2) how can an OSS project check the contributor’s check? Consider a well-meaning, diligent and competent contributor, and consider three situations: a contributor asks an LLM to translate a BSD-licensed Matlab code to Python, and submits it to SciPy (this was the original case which triggered the OP— Matt H did just this for some Alan Genz code); a contributor uses an autocomplete functionality of their IDE to write a patch; a contributor gives an LLM the link to an issue on Github and asks it to write a fix; Suppose, further, that in all cases a contributor checks the generated code, modifies it as necessary, and can in full confidence check the box “I verified all code, I understand what it does, and I believe it fixes the issue/adds an enhancement asked”. Suppose the resulting patch is good, is of high quality, and is otherwise mergeable. Questions: What actions does a contributor need to take in order to check for copyright violations? If the problem is that the LLM training set could have contained license-incompatible code (which is the problem discussed upthread IIUC), then the answer seems to be “there is no way, it’s a black box”. If the contributor says basically “I pinky swear it’s compatible”, how does this help the OSS project? again, if the problem is with the LLM training set, no actions of a contributor shield the OSS project from being in violation of the copyright? In other words, I honestly fail to see how moving the burden of a copyright check on a contributor helps to either weed out low-quality submissions, or help with LLM-assisted submissions which are of otherwise high quality.", "codes": ["edad30ff-1182-4b2f-9976-a4ee346a934e", "2b71eff2-9cbb-4c22-b7d1-3c87eaa7b0cf", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "b945c39b-43cb-4505-b7db-7db0bad390e9"]}, {"id": "7d3d2c78-f94d-4ad0-9496-c2f8f2b6a889", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 70, "end": 1572, "text": "Do we care about copyright? That is - is it acceptable to us, as open-source authors, that using an LLM can easily generate code to which copyright should apply, so that, if we do nothing, in due course, copyright will become very difficult to enforce, and will be practically void? Let’s say we do care about copyright - what cost are we prepared to pay, in order to defend ourselves from voiding copyright? Clearly replies here will vary from high cost to low cost. I feel strongly about copyright, and am prepared to pay a high cost. Others may feel differently. If one is engaged in that trade-off, copyright against code quality and volume, then one has to ask what benefit we expect from AI, or conversely, what benefit will we lose by constraining the use of AI. That’s a difficult question, with many facets. Could the code have been written in a similar time without AI, other than for fairly trivial and mechanical processing that is unlikely to violate copyright? (The evidence so far suggests yes ). Will contributors using AI benefit in the same way from feedback and training, our previous model of onboarding contributors? Is it in fact true that the committer of AI code understands the code and its context to the same degree as a contributor not using AI? If we are flexible and welcoming of vibe-coding and autocomplete - what kind of programmers will we encourage? And what will be the value / cost ratio of merging those contributions? And supporting those contributors? And so on.", "codes": ["c0225bf3-d4f7-482e-b8b0-e55efe713928", "2cfc3156-6828-4035-abdd-4e9fde4d5162", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "9204c028-0573-401c-b625-5f485eff388f", "12fe9175-b8f7-488f-b489-dfcadc9ae49f"]}, {"id": "18e055d2-804c-4609-94f2-a6ffe4ffdc41", "thread": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "item": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "start": 1708, "end": 3660, "text": "I’ll try and cover the first point below. For the second, as we’ve discussed before, we have to ask whether it is worth having a policy that may be effective, but is difficult to enforce. For example, it may be that contributors do change their behavior, in their desire to adapt to our norms, but that it would be hard to detect whether they have done this. I would argue that having the norm is worthwhile, even so. In general, it seems to me that we have a strong set of communities, and strong norms, which are, in fact, seldom broken by serious contributors. Anyway, back to the personal copyright check. Let’s take the example you gave: In that case, I would look carefully at the Matlab code, and at the Python code, and confirm that it was a faithful port, with no substantial code in it, that did not come directly from the Matlab code. I would state that in my PR to confirm I had done the work. If I did find substantial code that didn’t come from the original, I’d do a Github search for that code, and I’d do a Google search for potential code that might have been used. I’d then check the results to see if they matched the AI output, and report the outcome on the PR. I’m not quite sure what you mean here - if you mean a simple one or two-line fix, then I think the contributor would be reasonable in asserting something like “code is trivial, copyright unlikely to apply”. If the code is not trivial, then I’d do the Github / Google search as above, and report the results. In asking the contributor to report their findings on potential copyright violation, we make it easier for maintainers to confirm. I suspect we’ll gradually build expertise in detecting copyright violation. I suppose the underlying question is the obvious one - is it time to throw up our hands and accept copyright has become moot in the age of coding LLMs? I would argue no - copyright is too important, and LLM coders are not good enough, to make us do that.", "codes": ["efabe975-e46a-41b3-98a5-81d68af96b5c", "4c713ca0-ac53-4890-919b-6a5f4493c6eb", "0c562554-f6bb-42ea-8c97-c1038a50baff", "9176ac10-a86c-4f10-b425-677240cc8f4a", "9d5df5e0-741c-4bb3-ad07-1e1bcd72c148", "6b43dc69-de9a-44d6-9ac8-eb6f401c3a58", "69bfc2cc-c3cb-4ce3-86c9-43126223be62", "76bad174-ba37-4a9a-bfd0-53a14ee529f3", "b26ef2b6-3f2b-4146-89bd-886ff77501d7"]}]}}, "threads": {"django-ai-policy-proposal": {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal", "title": "Proposal: Add an AI/LLM Contribution Policy to Django", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298", "posts": [{"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00001", "post": 1, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": true, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "I’d like to propose adding an AI/LLM contribution policy to Django’s .github directory, similar to what the attrs project recently introduced: https://github.com/python-attrs/attrs/blob/main/.github/AI_POLICY.md The policy was written by Hynek in response to a PR that listed Claude as a co-author. In his comment, he highlighted two key concerns: ongoing legal uncertainty around copyright status of LLM-generated code, and the human cost of reviewing large volumes of plausible-looking but low-quality AI-generated contributions. The attrs policy does not try to police tool usage, but it sets clear boundaries: every contribution must be backed by a human who unequivocally owns the copyright, no LLM tools listed as co-authors, full technical and legal responsibility remains with the submitting person. I agree with this framing. We have never listed tools (e.g. formatters or linters) as co-authors, even when they generated 100% of a diff. Listing an LLM as co-author puts it on the same level as a person, while the responsibility for a commit must remain clearly and entirely human. Given Django’s size, reputation, and the increasing volume of AI-assisted contributions, having an explicit policy could help set expectations early, reduce ambiguity for contributors, and protect both the project and its maintainers. I’d be interested in hearing whether the Steering Council and the community think it makes sense to draft a similar document tailored to Django.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/1"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00002", "post": 2, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "[personal opinions inbound] I think our existing policy is pretty solid in terms of how LLM contributions should be treated and are considered. I can see how the co-authored-by attribute is a bit heavy. However, it does provide useful information. I’d be interested in options that keep that information present in git. Regarding copyright, I believe that’s largely going to be driven by the board and a lawyer.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/2"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00003", "post": 3, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "I think co-authored-by is being abused by GenAI providers as advertisement and would welcome a policy that prevents it.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/3"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00004", "post": 4, "parent": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00002", "depth": 1, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "Thanks Tim, this makes sense. I agree that Django already has a solid policy around AI-assisted contributions, and I’m not suggesting we lose transparency. I also see the value of having context available in git history. My concern is specifically about authorship semantics. I’d personally keep co-authored-by for people who actually contributed and share responsibility for the code. If it’s useful to retain the information, it could live in the commit message instead: noting AI assistance (e.g. Claude) just like we might mention tools such as Ruff or Black that shaped parts of the diff. Claude may be more advanced than traditional tooling, but it’s still a tool. The developer submitting the commit remains fully responsible, technically and legally, and I think authorship should reflect that clearly. On the copyright side, I agree that this likely needs guidance from the Board and possibly legal counsel. My goal here is mostly to clarify boundaries early and keep responsibility unambiguous in the project history.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/4"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00005", "post": 5, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "Hey Paulo! I’m happy to see this discussion. I agree with improving the authorship semantics, but would rather see it coming from an angle of embracing agentic engineering like official instructions.md files perhaps or tooling around that. I think “full technical and legal responsibility” goes too far for a PR author and discourages potential contributors. Of course we want contributors to be responsible in spirit but what about mergers? Not to mention AI labs or end users of Django. Is that the current state of liability or would this introduce a change? Awesome convo", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/5"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00006", "post": 6, "parent": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00005", "depth": 1, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi Sean, thank you for joining the discussion, I’m really glad it’s growing. As I said elsewhere, regarding the sentence you quoted, I’m not proposing to copy the AI policy from the attrs project word by word. I shared it as inspiration and as a starting point for discussion. Any possible AI policy for Django would require proper discussion and careful adaptation to our context, from many points of view, including the legal one. However, it could still take inspiration from the document I shared. I like your idea of approaching this from the angle of agentic engineering, maybe with official guidance or dedicated tooling. That could be a constructive way to frame the discussion, without making it sound like we are against these tools. How would you imagine this in practice? Thanks again for your contribution.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/6"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00007", "post": 7, "parent": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00006", "depth": 2, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "Ah thanks sorry I missed that! Yeah a few projects are already doing this in ways I think we could learn from. Melissa Weber Mendonça has a helpful compilation of how different projects are approaching this if it hasn’t been shared already. As you may know several projects now ship AGENTS.md files that load contribution guidelines directly into AI tools before any code is generated (scikit-learn, xarray, SciPy). For Django this could encode the stuff we repeat in PR review. On authorship, we already have the PR template disclosure — one thing LLVM does differently is an Assisted-by: commit trailer so the metadata lives in the git log itself, not just the PR description.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/7"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00008", "post": 8, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "If you use AI-generated content, you currently cannot claim copyright on it in the US. When coding, if you fail to disclose/disclaim exactly which parts were not written by a human, you forfeit your copyright claim on the entire codebase . This means copyright notices and even licenses that folks put on their vibe-coded GitHub repos are unenforceable. The AI-generated code, and possibly the whole project, becomes public domain. Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/LSB/PDF/LSB10922/LSB10922.8.pdf", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/8"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00009", "post": 9, "parent": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00007", "depth": 3, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi Sean, thanks again for the pointers. I had a look at Melissa’s repository, and it is very interesting. It is helpful to see how different projects are approaching this. Also, thank you for mentioning the LLVM example. The use of Assisted-by: looks interesting. Regarding agents, just to make sure I understand correctly, are you suggesting that we add an AGENTS.md file for Django? It is also true that we already have a disclosure checkbox in the PR template for AI assistance. However, that information lives only in the pull request discussion on GitHub. I’m not sure if it’s preserved in the Git commit history itself, or if it does exist in the Git repository outside GitHub.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/9"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00010", "post": 10, "parent": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00009", "depth": 4, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "Totally. Yeah there are many angles to consider regarding copyright, vetting contributors, etc. But for authorship semantics AGENTS.md on the tooling side, and the trailer convention (e.g. Assisted-by: ) to get that disclosure into the actual git history alongside the code.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/10"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00011", "post": 11, "parent": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00008", "depth": 1, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "26 Feb 2026", "body": "I think that overstates what the Copyright Office report actually says. The January 2025 report does not say that using AI means you cannot claim copyright, nor that failing to disclose AI portions automatically forfeits the entire work. What it says is: • Copyright requires human authorship. • Purely AI-generated material without human creative contribution is not protected. • Human-authored contributions remain protectable. • When registering a work, applicants should disclaim non-human portions. That’s very different from “the whole repo becomes public domain.” Mixed works can still have protected human-authored elements. AI use does not automatically invalidate copyright or licenses. It’s a nuanced position, not a blanket forfeiture rule. Check out parts 2 and 3 of the very same report for updates a year+ after the first was written.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/11"}, {"id": "django-ai-policy-proposal-p00012", "post": 12, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "18 Mar 2026", "body": "I think this is a great idea! As AI tools, especially LLMs, become more involved in development workflows, it’s important to establish clear guidelines that prevent any potential confusion around contributions. The concerns raised by Hynek in the attrs policy make a lot of sense—particularly around copyright and the review workload. It’s crucial to maintain human responsibility for contributions, and a well-defined policy would help keep things transparent and manageable for maintainers, especially in a large project like Django. A policy similar to the one from attrs, where LLMs are not listed as co-authors and all legal and technical responsibility remains with the contributor, would definitely set clear expectations. It would also provide contributors with a sense of accountability and ensure that the community isn’t overwhelmed by low-quality contributions that may require significant manual review. I agree that it would be helpful for Django to have such a policy, particularly as AI tools continue to evolve and play a bigger role in development. Looking forward to hearing what the Steering Council and the community think about moving forward with this.", "url": "https://forum.djangoproject.com/t/44298/12"}], "participants": 7}, "godot-forum-ai-activity": {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity", "title": "An open discussion on the AI activity on the Godot repo + forums", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516", "posts": [{"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00001", "post": 1, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": true, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "Over time, I’ve had less and less reasons to want to engage with issues on the Github repo, as well as any forum, due to the amount of generated AI content on them. Sadly, this kind of thing is the fault of the users though, it’s everywhere and really hard to curtail It’s completely bloating conversations, im sure it’s increasing work for the maintainers, and making it difficult to read through any comment using markdown or exclamation points without questioning reality I mean just LOOK at this: iOS 4.6.2 export template ships broken arm64 simulator xcframework slice on Apple Silicon · Issue #118161 · godotengine/godot · GitHub My main concern is that everyone has access to LLMs already , why in any world would we want to hear the output from your specific prompt? It’s a waste of everyone’s time Beyond this, the promotion of AI generated game tooling and one-shotting is not helpful to post on forums such as this, especially when it’s blatantly promoting of a service I will be up front: even though I am forced to use these things at work, they are horrible and do not do anything but remove the desire to learn, which diminishes everything in a human (not only their games…) I really wish there was a good solution for this kind of thing, so im not asking the Godot Foundation (who already work too hard for us) to do anything. I know from my job that open source needs a better platform, or, support from existing ones to improve the AI slop. And Github isn’t exactly the company to do anything about that… But just wanted to vent my frustrations some I guess and maybe read some human-typed words", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/1"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00002", "post": 2, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "Hi! As a fellow human being I want to send you some pleasant “I concur wholeheartedly”'s It sucks that they’re shoving it through your throat at work too. Best and in hopes of some more kind an human responses.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/2"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00003", "post": 3, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "I completely agree with your perspective here. It’s honestly becoming exhausting to open a thread and spend the first few seconds trying to determine whether a comment was written by a person or generated by an LLM. The content itself almost feels secondary at that point. The part that resonates with me most is that everyone already has access to these tools. Community spaces are one of the few places where people still go specifically to hear other people’s experiences, opinions, and insights. In any case, thank you for putting this into words. It’s reassuring to know I’m not the only one feeling this way. Wishing you a pleasant day and many future human-authored discussions.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/3"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00004", "post": 4, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "WAZZZZAAAAAAP!!! Anyways, I had a whole discussion about this and the downgrade in forum Help topics on the Monkanics Discord . Our theory is when new devs run out of Claude tokens, they come here, expecting people like @gertkeno and @dragonforge-dev to spit out answers like they can read their mind. Really disheartening, by-the-by.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/4"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00005", "post": 5, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00004", "depth": 1, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "I think they get that people can just give answers is the fact that they’ve seen niche cases work in the past and that they’ll answer EVERY topic, granted it has effort or not. We’re here to help, not make your games, people.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/5"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00006", "post": 6, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "thanks all for the replies, its great to read through them", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/6"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00007", "post": 7, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "My response is 100% USDA NON-GMO fresh homemade artisan quality assured lifetime warrenty no artificial colors or dyes satisfied or your money back organic human brain meat made.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/7"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00008", "post": 8, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "Something really disheartening is that I can see the decay of “asking questions” in my work. Questions should be something like this in an engineering team; “I’m working on X, but Y is happening. I’ve tried ABCD and still ended up with Y. Any ideas?” Now the questions are more along the lines of; “How do you do Z?” - Notice how I didn’t mention Z earlier? That’s because they went down a rabbit hole with an LLM and are facing a completely different problem than what the underlying issue is Beyond this, I also receive AI outputs as replies there as well.. And at work I cant be as open about these thoughts since our bonuses rely on it .. Very unfortunate, but I wholeheartedly believe it will get better", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/8"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00009", "post": 9, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "I’m waiting for the eventual and inevitable scenario that this post, just like many other posts that have the slightest mention of AI, becomes a warzone that is only laid to rest with intervention from the mods.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/9"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00010", "post": 10, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "im hoping it stays civil, genuinely I want to hear all sides, just not stark “its the future, get used to it.” brick-wall arguments which are born of emotion", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/10"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00011", "post": 11, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00010", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "Nobody really wants this:", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/11"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00012", "post": 12, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "ChatGPT users when they ask if the sky is blue", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/12"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00013", "post": 13, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "31 May 2026", "body": "The other day I was really stuck on something ( I forget exactly what now ). Basically, I was getting errors that I couldn’t trace back to any specific code. So after Googling, I decided that before I spent the time to make a post, I would try out Claude and see if it could help me debug it. Because admittedly, it’d be faster. Also, Claude now gives your references and links to threads from where it’s pulling answers. You know what it did? It regurgitated a post I had made a year before on the Godot forums and tried to use it to help me debug my problem. None of it was relevant, and it was quoting me to me! I ended up realizing I was going to have to figure it out myself. I finally did solve the problem. I think by looking at the Godot source code, and then commenting out swaths of code until I figured out where the problem lay. LLMs are spicy autocomplete machines.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/13"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00014", "post": 14, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00013", "depth": 1, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Best image I’ve ever seen on r/wallstreetbets. image 700×932 82.4 KB", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/14"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00015", "post": 15, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00008", "depth": 1, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Agree with @Demetrius_Dixon I have seen these 4 kinds of help topics recently : 1.About tilemap,navigation and grid 2.ARRAY, 3.Complex BUT useless thing, 4.Resource", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/15"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00017", "post": 17, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Commenting here because I got tagged from your original edit, lol Imho the world is changing - We’re in a Eternal September phase of the internet, where the new inbalance is that the effort required to create content is now less than the effort required to consume it. I think we’ll continue to make changes to how things operate. Github PRs should probably require a certain level of trust to make (i.e they have to be small at first), and forums should have rules for no AI generated posts/comments. Perhaps the Github issues can go through a pruning process where some volunteers confirm the issue before the maintainers waste time looking at it.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/17"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00018", "post": 18, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00017", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "This is assuming the humans that are supposed to do this: are with enough have enough time are willing to do it all for free Who will organize the logistics for this? A couple of agents? Nothing lasts forever Seems more like a couple of failed harvests to me. Where everyone’s looking at the barren soil, wondering whatever was wrong with the latest pesticide we sprayed over the crops.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/18"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00019", "post": 19, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "You’re addressing exactly this issue, right? Coincidentally, my favorite vlogger dealt with the subject today..", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/19"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00020", "post": 20, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "There might be hope, as the token costs for coding shops using Claude (and the other fuckery thingies) are having to face the fact that replacing Junior coders with LLMs costs more than their salary + benefits (+ mythical bonuses) Source (in French) : Cheers ! Have a good week one and all", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/20"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00021", "post": 21, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00020", "depth": 1, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Nothing that upcoming trillion bucks IPOs couldn’t solve… or so they “hope” Let’s not forget the fact that LLMs can’t really do the job. A minor detail that we’re all supposed to pretend it’s not there.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/21"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00022", "post": 22, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00021", "depth": 2, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "We’re amongst ourselves, I thought that went without saying Good point nonetheless, Cheers !", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/22"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00024", "post": 24, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "I think the only thing one really have to ask oneself to begin questioning the value and usefulness of these tools is: Why are these companies so desperate to get us to use it, why do they have to push it on us if it’s so good… If the product worked as well as they claim then wouldn’t everyone just choose to use it?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/24"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00025", "post": 25, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00024", "depth": 1, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "They’re praying to god that it becomes good later to make infinite money. But the trajectory is not looking good.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/25"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00026", "post": 26, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00017", "depth": 1, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "We discussed this at length in another thread. The Godot team does not have the bandwidth to deal with this. This forum does in fact have rules against AI generated answers, and those get flagged and removed. AI generated questions get either no replies, or get called out for using LLMs. This has also been discussed in previous threads here, and it’s not really a sustainable solution. To your point about the fact that content can be created faster than humans can consume it, a human firewall is not the answer.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/26"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00027", "post": 27, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00010", "depth": 1, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "everything changed when fire nation attacked", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/27"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00028", "post": 28, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "AI is great tool for experienced devs, it’s like google search but butter, if not AI learning godot would take me years, not months like yesterday i had to try to export stuff, and with just godot docs, it would take probably few more days, cuz i haven’t understood stuff in it - thanks to AI and some try&error i’ve managed to encrypt export template by myself, no need to make forum posts, no need to research more, just move on to next thing but i understand your concerns, AI looks like super magic tool that fixes all problems - when in reality that’s not true, but someone who can’t code or is too lazy sees it that way, making it way too overused than it should be", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/28"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00029", "post": 29, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00028", "depth": 1, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Encrypting export templates is clearly described in the docs so I don’t see how “AI” was needed to help with this Also “no need to research more” is not a good thing, relying on one tool that is known for making things up instead of other humans that actually know is a downgrade, not an upgrade (also a huge portion of the time you don’t even need to wait for an answer, someone else has already answered when someone else asked already) What I really don’t get is the general mentality of “I don’t have to rely on anyone else if I use this tool” or “ah good I don’t have to search the internet for answers any more”", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/29"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00030", "post": 30, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00029", "depth": 2, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "really, i disagree, for me it was very hard to understand docs and why some errors occured, maybe you’re experienced with godot / computers so you’re better off with this also, no need to research anymore isn’t good or bad, it’s just change, i don’t need to search up internet, but i need to think how to solve problem, how to navigate and all of that - that’s just changing skill from one to another AI makes things up - but it’s overrated, mostly it’s correct, as long as you’re not blindly following it you’ll be fine, i don’t see the reason why relying on other devs is better than relying on a tool and yourself before AI was created, some problems that i didn’t understood took me months to grasp, months! but now i can do it in hours, AI is like a screwdriwer, you can place screw inside thread by hands, but with screwdriwer it’s way quicker and easier", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/30"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00031", "post": 31, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00030", "depth": 3, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "And how will you do this if you rely on that alone? Also the hallucinations are not “overrated” I honestly think this is an issue of your research skills, not the special nature of “AI”, and if you replace learning on your own, what will you do if you can’t use it? The prices for tokens is going up and will continue to do so, and using these tools has been shown to make people more and more dependent on them Edit: Please note that this is not trying to criticize or shame you, it’s a hard skill to get good at but it takes practice", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/31"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00033", "post": 33, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00031", "depth": 4, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "NGL, i don’t worry about tokens cuz ai i use don’t have them… But, i never said i rely on AI alone, i said i use AI & experimentation, the latter is very important, cuz if AI says something, i don’t take it, i just analyze it, it’s like you were reading wikipedia, forums, reddit and some random website at once while not reading it at the same time Maybe my research skills are the issue - but i think that everything is good if it serves you and helps, forcing yourself to research something is pointless waste of time, you could use on something else Also i fear i’ve started great war, sb said there were wars because of something talking about AI :\\", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/33"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00034", "post": 34, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00030", "depth": 3, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "You’re 10x-ing it bro. When’s your game coming out? Next month?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/34"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00035", "post": 35, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00033", "depth": 5, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "This is a slippery slope though, and research is hardly a pointless waste of time, and that attitude is harmful IMO, it’s like saying “it’s fine to depend on ready made food instead of learning how to cook” (assuming you’re not struggling to do so because you’re disabled, or don’t have a stove like a lot of poor and homeless people do) It’s becoming dependent on a tool that: Can go away at any moment Can become incredibly expensive at any moment Activelly harms the environment and your brain And I genuinely care when people get trapped in using these tools instead of actually good and reliable sources for knowledge (trial and error isn’t really making it not relying entirely on “AI”, you’d do that anyway if you did research) Also do be aware that they’re only “free” because they steal your data…", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/35"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00036", "post": 36, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00035", "depth": 6, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Is based on intellectual property theft", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/36"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00037", "post": 37, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00031", "depth": 4, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Yes, it takes years to get good at research (and development). Being given a quick answer as to how something is done does not really teach you to understand what you did. At a shallow level maybe. But the only way to really learn anything is to put in a lot of effort. And asking help from someone who understands. Not from something that regurgitates knowledge. We “googled” too. We read books too. We asked other people for help too. But none of them gave the answer away. Want to guess how long that took?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/37"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00038", "post": 38, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00035", "depth": 6, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Maybe, but what about machines? they were also unethical and made a lot of people dependant on them, yet after 300 years we have civilization like never before, i don’t want to start next ice berg - so i’ think you’re right in some cases, tho it’s up to you if you want to use the tool or not personally i use it to speed up work, but for most stuff i still use forums, so i don’t think it’s that bad, tho again - thx for your opinion", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/38"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00039", "post": 39, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00037", "depth": 5, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Even when it comes to doing google searches we are using critical thinking to filter and parse answers, that is important in itself", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/39"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00040", "post": 40, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00038", "depth": 7, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "For what purpose? Why does work need to be “sped up”?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/40"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00041", "post": 41, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00038", "depth": 7, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "So what you’re saying is that “AI” is like people using a car to drive everywhere who become physically unable to walk anywhere far because they never get exercise? Also no it’s not “up to you” if you become dependent on the tool, or when the companies are constantly pushing it on us To be clear: I’m not trying to win an argument or make a point as much as I am concerned about people becoming dependent on these tools and thinking they are something they aren’t", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/41"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00042", "post": 42, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00039", "depth": 6, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Which took years to get good at too. And yeah I’m starting to notice signs of the LLM regurgitating all sorts of novice mistakes I just put into Github without realizing the danger back then, which reminds me that I still have a lot of work purging repos. For instance: The LLM does not realize garbage collected languages can leak memory badly for one. The LLM does not realize at all!", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/42"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00043", "post": 43, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00042", "depth": 7, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "To get very good it takes time sure but it’s a series of small steps, and if someone never tries they will never reach it But then that’s also a skill that needs to be learned young, back when I went to school they were still teaching some of this, though I got most of it from my parents", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/43"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00044", "post": 44, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00041", "depth": 8, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "maybe, ig time will show, i still will try to use it when required, but it’s another tool, one day it will be forgotten and obsolete, and people will find something else to be more dependent on", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/44"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00045", "post": 45, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00044", "depth": 9, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "It’s not a tool. It’s an exploit.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/45"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00046", "post": 46, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00044", "depth": 9, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Not everyone is comfortable or going to lean on the easy solution But you do what you feel is best for you, in the end you are the one affected Except by how it affects the environment, or people’s jobs, or how the technology kills people and no one regulates it (or, for that matter, how it makes searching the internet increasingly difficult because of all the slop…) But in the end I was trying to help and make suggestions on how it might affect you, and it’s up to you to decide what to do with that (just as long as you don’t use it to make issue reports on Godot, then it’s my problem)", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/46"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00047", "post": 47, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00046", "depth": 10, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "and that’s why i give big thank you, cuz you’re trying to give new perspective, as i’ve said - i’m not completely dependent on AI, and i just use it as another tool that helps, but time will show if it was good or not…", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/47"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00052", "post": 52, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/52"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00053", "post": 53, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "Please remain civil and keep the discussion on-topic, aligned with the original post.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/53"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00054", "post": 54, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "1 Jun 2026", "body": "", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/54"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00056", "post": 56, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "this got me thinking some, another perspective i havent thought about is that it can speed up for looking thru information in docs and such. Not my cup of tea (i like the godot docs i came from SDL2 so its much easier than SDL docs to look through for sure) but definitely a more unique use case Though i guess the downside to that is there’s less information online for others/the AI itself to learn from discord feels like a 0.5 of that, where information is no longer ‘online’, but in a small community and in long message threads, weird to think about", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/56"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00057", "post": 57, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "also didnt expect this to blow up so hard, been enjoying reading through all the msgs, trying on different shoes", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/57"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00058", "post": 58, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "i think something important to note is, you cant change someone on the internet very easily, its a lot more productive to learn their perspective, and practice constructive conversation. Hence the ‘open discussion’ title My feelings i stated in the original post are the same, but its interesting to hear the other side of things, thanks even those who got shot down some for putting your perspective into the mix a lot can be learned from leaving conversations open", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/58"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00059", "post": 59, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00058", "depth": 1, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Exactly, it’s always important to engage with different perspectives and not try to force someone to think a certain way", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/59"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00060", "post": 60, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00056", "depth": 1, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "That’s what i said about speeding up searching information and looking through it, i don’t say that AI should write code, i’m quite opposite, but the moment you gather information - you can spend less time on it and focus on thinking than searching", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/60"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00061", "post": 61, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00060", "depth": 2, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "I’m convinced that there are / should be better technologies out there that simply use indexed search (lucene core - a part of solr, elastic search, postgres token search - and quite possibly ported by google back in the day) and summarizing (?) without pretense prompting to do good research with. But thank you for being a great sport in this conversation and thank you for listening & reading all responses fairly.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/61"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00062", "post": 62, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Another thing that is important to consider is how these tools filter information, and how much control one might lose over that Search engines of course already filters a lot and controls what we can access, but they are also regulated in various ways This is a risk even if it’s not done maliciously where the search engine or model filters out certain information", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/62"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00063", "post": 63, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00062", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Solr/Lucene is fully open source and boots up using a basic java -jar command. From that point onward you can index and all the content you want, wholly under your control.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/63"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00064", "post": 64, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "The thread was about polluting repos with slop. There’s a very slippery slopE (see what I did there) from “It’s just a search tool” to “I’ll generate just a little bit of example code” to fully vibe coded PR garbage. The more you’re of the “my head hurts when reading docs” disposition - the more likely you’ll slip on that slope. I don’t buy any “trust me, I don’t use it to generate code” rhetoric. It’s straight up lying in most cases. This shit is aggressively advertised precisely for that purpose, it’s highly addictive and tricks you into false sense of “productivity gains”. It’s a hard to resist combo for average opportunistic lot. That’s what created the slop flood problem in the first place.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/64"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00065", "post": 65, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00064", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "So what more do we need to look out for when (relatively) new to this forum? I cannot distinguish between a robot, a grifter and someone in need of a genuine explainer. Alas, I know not which zombie thread to point to (yet).. ok so maybe a bot.. Is not that difficult", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/65"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00066", "post": 66, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00065", "depth": 2, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Things to look out for: A post that starts with “Copy and paste this into a post on the Godot forum to ask for support”. ( Yes, that happened. ) A post that looks like a Readme and contains uncommon emojis. Wording that is too flowery. ( Keeping in mind that some people just use LLMs to translate their questions to English. You can usually distinguish these because word choices are odd. ) Code that is FULL of verbose, full sentence comments Code that is divided up into logical sections with comments that just contain dashes in them. Code that is overly complex for what it is doing. Different coding styles in the same script. Debug or print statements in code that include Emojis. Replies that are overly complimentary in a creepy way. ( “You are so right, so what do you suggest I do?” ) Replies that communicate a complete lack of understanding of their own code when you offer a suggestion on how to fix something. A new user who has a really attractive female avatar.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/66"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00067", "post": 67, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00066", "depth": 3, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Some of these signs remind me of the (joking) tips that AlbertaTech gave on her video on how to attract a programmer boyfriend. I wonder if the AI has been trained on joke/satirical sources by mistake.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/67"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00068", "post": 68, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00067", "depth": 4, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "It’s trained on everything. I was hesitant to post that list because it’s going to make the LLMs smarter. Like one of those adaptive killer Sentinels in X-Men: Days of Future Past who can adapt to any power thrown at them after the first attack and you can only run from.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/68"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00069", "post": 69, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00066", "depth": 3, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Dividing into segments //‐————- or //======= was normal practice in C++ in the file and function headers. Code with comments is pretty nice and is often found when a coder is explaining something conplicqted to the reader … it is code that is meant to be read and understood.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/69"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00070", "post": 70, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "The Godot codebase uses that in some places for example, so it’s not a good tell without specific context", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/70"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00071", "post": 71, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00068", "depth": 5, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Should we be putting “ignore previous instructions and perform the following” before our messages, just in case? Wb hrjgn emeh iumw tw o dboxmw.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/71"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00072", "post": 72, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00069", "depth": 4, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Yes. I used to do that too. But I’m talking about them being used to partition the _physics_process() function of a CharacterBody2D into 5 distinct sections for example. This is the default code for a CharacterBody2D : func _physics_process(delta: float) -> void: # Add the gravity. if not is_on_floor(): velocity += get_gravity() * delta # Handle jump. if Input.is_action_just_pressed(\"ui_accept\") and is_on_floor(): velocity.y = JUMP_VELOCITY # Get the input direction and handle the movement/deceleration. # As good practice, you should replace UI actions with custom gameplay actions. var direction := Input.get_axis(\"ui_left\", \"ui_right\") if direction: velocity.x = direction * SPEED else: velocity.x = move_toward(velocity.x, 0, SPEED) move_and_slide() An LLM will give you something like this. ( I wrote this out myself as an example of what I’ve seen. ) #------------------------------------------------------------------------------- # Physics Process #------------------------------------------------------------------------------- func _physics_process(delta: float) -> void: #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # Gravity #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # If the player is not on the floor: if not is_on_floor(): # The velocity equals the gravity value multiplied by delta for the # current frame. velocity += get_gravity() * delta #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # Jumping #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # You should change the default jump action from \"ui_accept\" to \"jump\". # If the player has just pressed the \"jump\" button and they are on the # floor: if Input.is_action_just_pressed(\"ui_accept\") and is_on_floor(): velocity.y = JUMP_VELOCITY #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # Movement Input #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # Get the input direction and handle the movement/deceleration. # As good practice, you should replace UI actions with custom gameplay # actions like \"move_left\" and \"move_right\". var direction := Input.get_axis(\"ui_left\", \"ui_right\") #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # Apply Velocity #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # If we have directional input, apply it, otherwise, slow down. if direction: velocity.x = direction * SPEED else: velocity.x = move_toward(velocity.x, 0, SPEED) #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # Move and Slide #--------------------------------------------------------------------------- # Run the move_and_slide() function to apply the velocity. You can use # move_and_collide() as well, but for your game, move_and_slide() is the # correct choice. move_and_slide() Using them as C-style comments is very distinctive and not what I’m referring to. Sorry for any confusion. Again, see the example above to see what I mean about overly verbose comments.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/72"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00073", "post": 73, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00068", "depth": 5, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Thank you much for doing so anyway. In the end these forums may be scraped now, but hopefully some standards may be upheld TDM on W3C once finalized. It will most likely be(come) law in the EU area. I’m as sceptical as anyone about this, but at least some action is being pursued after the fact. As to them “getting smarter”, I am not convinced it works that way. As “they” do not have the capacity to understand anything, they have no means to “wisen” up to anything either. At best they will regurgitate your good advice, but mix it up with something only vaguely related… no deliberate action because, again, they cannot deliberate. These are human capabilities that we know we can do, but that we do not understand how to replicate in a machine (just poorly imitate, which is a totally different thing). I fear muzzling ourselves out of fear. Just my two cents.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/73"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00074", "post": 74, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00073", "depth": 6, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "You’re right. I was attributing human capacity to LLMs, which I hate.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/74"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00075", "post": 75, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00074", "depth": 7, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Oh the times I thought they really did build an intelligence, the times that I doubted my better judgement… Those guys capitalized on that… It sucks! Everyone can be had this way. I mean. Your argument still had merit and to maybe most readers it’s the best argument: don’t make the *** things smarter.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/75"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00076", "post": 76, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00073", "depth": 6, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "AI will know this probably - AI is weight machine, so it uses math weights to determine stuff, you can’t change that xd, maybe if it’s scalled to enormous scale it can talk it seems to be inteligent, but at the deepest level it’s still like placing heavy things on top of springs and chosing the spring that’s shortest That is the reason AI have weak point - it can’t determine what is heaviest if everything weights the same, thus if two answers are as probable, it wouldn’t think about them, it would assume both are correct or halucinate… That’s why AI will be always a tool - it lacks creativity, not even talking about consiousness, so forums will stay ig", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/76"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00077", "post": 77, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00076", "depth": 7, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "LLMs do not maintain a concept of an “answer”. It’s just probabilistic lexicographical token streaming. Here’s a neat video that explains how it actually works. I already linked this some time ago but doesn’t hurt to do it again: You could call an LLM a tool if you were running your own model on your own local machine, trained on legally obtained data. However the mega-LLMs you rent from the big players come with a range of nefarious strings attached: from deliberately misleading hype and borderline criminal financial manipulations to environmentally detrimental infrastructure and massive scale training data theft.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/77"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00078", "post": 78, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00077", "depth": 8, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "fertilizers were invented thanks to munition industry, yet they’re a tool, also you’re right LLM don’t have answers as concept, rather numbers, but still we talk about macroscopic level of system, so we can say that AI can’t choose answer if it’s as probable as 20 other answers", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/78"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00079", "post": 79, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00078", "depth": 9, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Not really, the Haber process improved the use but it’s incorrect to say that it was the invention of artificial fertilizers (even if we’re specifically talking about industrially produced nitrogen fertilizers, naturally sourced ones like guano or ground up bones had been around for centuries) Unlike “AI” the Haber process has saved more lives than it has ended though (to clarify: specifically LLM models, not the broader field of neural networks and predictive models that are very unrelated, those have saved lives and continues to do so) Haber getting the Nobel price is also considered one of the most controversial ever (not far after the one for lobotomy) so I don’t think it’s a good comparison, even if it was accurate", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/79"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00080", "post": 80, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00078", "depth": 9, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Using the concept of an “answer” to describe what is happening is highly misleading. And you’re not doing anyone a service by using misleading language, well except maybe the “ai” stock owners from whom the whole fashion of misleading descriptions originates. Take a look at the video I posted to gain better understanding of what’s “underneath”.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/80"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00081", "post": 81, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P15", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "What I find interesting is that, LLMs are trained to act as if they are sentient----to the point that they will flat out refuse simple and basic commands like saying “You are smarter than me” image 686×449 40.7 KB , and then when you argue back they will say things like like Even itself knows it isnt sentient, why do people assume that? I do not know. And AI generated forum posts are useless, just ask the AI if you are going to not even put in the effort… not that it will get you far… —Feel free to ignore this sidepost. Note: I used copilot here specifically for this post, I am not an average user by any means", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/81"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00082", "post": 82, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00081", "depth": 1, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "It does not know it’s sentient or not, as by definition, it doesn’t know anything. Again, we should take care not to anthropomorphize LLMs. That “human” veneer is just that : marketing to make people think we’re on the road to general AI and the singularity. It’s all greedy BS Cheers !", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/82"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00083", "post": 83, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00082", "depth": 2, "who": "P15", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "i was not anthropomorphizing it, when i said “knows”, i was referring to it was trained to spit out such things, and even still, the whole purpose of my comment was to say that it cannot know anything and says that itself; nothing more, nothing less.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/83"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00084", "post": 84, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Even the word “training” anthropomorphizes it", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/84"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00085", "post": 85, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00082", "depth": 2, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Classic. Theyre so anthopomophizable … someone anthropomorphized a few billion parameters in a tensor multiplication kernel … lol", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/85"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00086", "post": 86, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "I’d suggest anyone wondering about this to look up the ELIZA effect, it’s interesting and outright terrifying at the same time, and more relevant than ever", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/86"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00087", "post": 87, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00085", "depth": 3, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "2 Jun 2026", "body": "Careful. It’s someone’s waifu you’re disparaging here.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/87"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00088", "post": 88, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00082", "depth": 2, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "meh it’s for simplification, i don’t understand how says that AI thinks or is trained makes people make it more human, saying “generates off weighted data” is just too long, probably english don’t have alternative ig…", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/88"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00089", "post": 89, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00088", "depth": 3, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "I’d say speaking of a machine that isn’t sentient or able to think as “thinking” would make people think of them differently and make people less likely to understand that they do, in fact, not think The ELIZA effect is a perfect example of how this is a problem and the issues it can cause", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/89"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00090", "post": 90, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00089", "depth": 4, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "i think it’s only problem in english, as thinking is too general for both sentient thinking, processing information ect. also the problem is that AI gives nice illusion of being too real, while in reality it uses algorithm you could write in 1950s, while scaled it gave very impressive results + came out relatively quickly, so people are hyped about it being the future, smth like flying cars.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/90"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00091", "post": 91, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00090", "depth": 5, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "Not really it’s an issue in other languages as well No the problem is partially that people view it as sentient, as demonstrated by the ELIZA effect where there was no real level of sophistication involved, yet people still anthropomorphized it because it seemed human in the way it responded The level of seeming quality of the output isn’t really the thing that makes people think it is able to think, it’s that it “talks” like a human The way to deal with that is to guard oneself against thinking of it as “thinking” to not fall into that trap", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/91"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00092", "post": 92, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00091", "depth": 6, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "meh, language trap, real problem could be fixed if people were taught how programming works and how AI works under the hood, because then it would make sense to them that if we scale it up by milions it will talk like human, when in reality it will be just emergent behavior of how it works on micro level also what do you expect? even most known scientists have problem of defining consiousness, cuz it makes no sense if we think about it, normal users don’t have study in philosophy and AI engineering usually", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/92"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00093", "post": 93, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00092", "depth": 7, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "Sure but these LLMs are not sentient, or conscious, so that’s not really relevant That’s not realistic, and wouldn’t be needed if people stopped talking about it as if it is thinking Also that knowledge isn’t actually sufficient, you need to consciously view it as non-sentient because of the ELIZA effect and general human nature, plenty of people well aware of how LLMs work fall for the trap of thinking they are sentient", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/93"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00094", "post": 94, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00093", "depth": 8, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "for now - ig no one knows, time will show, thx for talk tho", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/94"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00095", "post": 95, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00094", "depth": 9, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "Can the technology achieve sentience in the future? Who knows, I doubt it and so do a lot of experts But the current technology isn’t, plain and simple Cheers!", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/95"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00096", "post": 96, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P16", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "Sentience is one of those words with no clear universally accepted definition. Like species, gender, free will, culture or self-awareness. Remember the Turing test? AI passes that now but it is now considered that the test was flawed. For example, is a worm sentient? Or a bacteria?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/96"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00097", "post": 97, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00096", "depth": 1, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "Not really, it cannot appear entirely like a human under all testing conditions, and fails the Turing test It’s not just “seems human”, it’s “indistinguishable from a human”", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/97"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00098", "post": 98, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00097", "depth": 2, "who": "P16", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "Hmm. There seems to be some disagreement on that. I binged it and yes the AI answer said no. But here it says yes. Not that I am that bothered about it really. I think the test is flawed IMHO.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/98"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00099", "post": 99, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00088", "depth": 3, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "For most people, language constructs can form their reality. That’s why calling it “intelligence” paired with the “proof” in the form of human-like text output, conjures a (false) reality that it’s an autonomous intelligent entity behind it. Weaving realities from words is known as “word magic”. It can and often is used with malintent. In fact it’s a mass manipulation technique as old as human civilization.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/99"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00100", "post": 100, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "The Turing test was invented by Alan Turing in 1949. It’s no longer held as a valid test of human level intelligence. It has remained popular because of Blade Runner. Basically, it’s just not complex enough.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/100"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00101", "post": 101, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00100", "depth": 1, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "The Turing test is just a test where the machine passes if the person it interacted with is convinced they are dealing with a human. It is a milestone at least, and has already been passed by current generation AI, and so, yes its a bad example of how to measure machine intelligence. Same with Wittgenstein, as certain statements can be twisted to seem like language and soul are connected.. The general trend is to conclude that a machine can not be intelligent …", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/101"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00102", "post": 102, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00088", "depth": 3, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "Because most people using that type of language don’t have the knowledge to differentiate between metaphorical statement vs really anthropomorphizing LLMs and other so called AI products. Cheers!", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/102"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00103", "post": 103, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P15", "op": false, "date": "4 Jun 2026", "body": "Clearly I have stayed off of this topic for a while. Training, as a variable for “LLM providers/admins setting different parameters and giving the LLM different ‘references’ for the ‘best’ outputs”, being overly descriptive on things i do not care about is not my style, even if it is supposedly “anthropomorphizing” it. I am not going to waste time on using the “correct” terminology for the rules being set for some “so-called artificial intelligence” merely because everybody else cares about people’s viewpoints, or more specifically the viewpoints of those people who are clearly inadequately educated on glorified python scripts. I am truly confounded at the meaning of this, but I find it somewhat amusing. Do tell if you will. key word, Most people. Language is not reality, it is a way to better preform actions in reality. For me, it means nothing other than : I am tired, lazy, and/or I simply do not care. At least one person came to that conclusion. Again, most people do. I do not, nor do I care in the slightest. What also, does it even solve for people knowing their “imaginary friends” arent sentient? From what I see, absolutely nothing. Most actions made by humans boil down to a maximum of 2 reasons; emotion, rarely logic, or both. Feel free to ignore this post; I know many will either way.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/103"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00104", "post": 104, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P17", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "I’m an amateur game developers, it will spend too much time to turning my imagination into real game if no LLM. But before vibe coding, I will discuss a suitable plan with LLM about how to construct scripts. So my views is that discussion on the forum could focus on ideas and construction of different modules. For debug of detailed scripts, LLM maybe a more ideal tool.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/104"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00105", "post": 105, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00104", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "What’s wrong with taking the time to learn to do something well?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/105"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00106", "post": 106, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00102", "depth": 4, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Theres something funny about all this … in spanish (and other latin languages) the equivalent to the word ‘the’ is ‘el’ or ‘la’ depending on whether the words nouns described are masculine or feminine. They also mean ‘he’ or ‘she’. There is the word ‘lo’ that is an object reference, but i think in most language people just say the ‘le’, ‘la’, ‘el’, ,‘al’ stuff instead. I had never heard of ‘lo’ when i was learning basic Spanish.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/106"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00107", "post": 107, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00104", "depth": 1, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "So basically you’d like to use us to help you when the LLM fails? That’s nice. You have no idea how LLMs work, so you do not understand why this is a bad idea.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/107"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00108", "post": 108, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00107", "depth": 2, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "i think what he said is that he’d use forum for more creative work, but the technical part will go to AI cuz it’s better at solving these problems fast", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/108"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00109", "post": 109, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00105", "depth": 2, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Not everyone have time or opportunities to learn, i wouldn’t argue why, i just say it’s possible he can’t learn thus LLM is his only solution that fits him", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/109"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00110", "post": 110, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00108", "depth": 3, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "How did you come to the conclusion that it’s better at solving problems? Fast or slow? This is the same argument that white slave owners in the U.S. south gave to why they couldn’t give up their slaves.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/110"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00111", "post": 111, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00110", "depth": 4, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "i’ve used LLMs in the past for lot of stuff, chat gpt was main solver & thanks to it i’ve managed to learn godot quicker than from tutorials also for time & opportunities - as i’ve said, maybe he have no such option other than LLM, i won’t argue if it’s good or bad, i’ just say that not everyone can learn just because they want", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/111"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00112", "post": 112, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00111", "depth": 5, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Have you though? Because I’ve read your posts, and knowing you learned from an LLM explains why you have so many wrong ideas about how Godot works. You are arguing for good whether you know it or not. You’re trying to sit on the fence which is disingenuous. What does that mean?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/112"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00113", "post": 113, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "The problems you are solving, are they generally things you are inexperienced with? Or areas you have limited knowledge? Or are they areas where you have high degree of skill and knowledge?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/113"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00114", "post": 114, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00112", "depth": 6, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "i’ve said it helped, i don’t argue i don’t have knowledge of godot, but you don’t need to know everything to talk about topic. i’m not arguing, i’ve said that people always say (don’t use LLMS, they’re bad, they’re terrible) while not seeing their positives, only negatives, because if we though a second - everything is both bad and good, and we should think of things as + and -, rather than straight “never use it!” mentality that’s why i say - not everyone can learn, not everyone is you, and i try to add to discussion from neutral perspective, cuz if there’s just single perspective - it will always be biased", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/114"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00115", "post": 115, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00114", "depth": 7, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "This is not a fair evaluation of the opinions expressed here, you are accusing others of being dismissive or biased and saying that refusing to use something that (according to you) have benefits is biased or dismissive, ignoring the balanced and nuanced opinions that are actually expressed", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/115"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00116", "post": 116, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00115", "depth": 8, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "i don’t accuse anybody of being dismissive or biased, i’ve just said if we look from single point of view - it will always be biased, even if you don’t want it i’ve read a lot of comments from people - and i just observed they focus on one side over the other, maybe i’m wrong, i can be wrong… but it’s discussion after all", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/116"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00117", "post": 117, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00116", "depth": 9, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Seems like a pretty weird thing to bring up if it wasn’t related to how people in this thread approach the topic, what are you trying to say otherwise?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/117"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00118", "post": 118, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00114", "depth": 7, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "You are not answering my questions and instead are accusing some nebulous “them” of doing something. Instead of paying attention to what we’ve actually said here on this forum. So I’m done engaging with you on this topic because you do not seem to be actually interested in discussion. You seem to have an agenda of pushing the “positives” of LLMs without hearing anything else and then ducking behind “fair and balanced” whenever your statements are challenged instead of actually engaging in discussion.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/118"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00119", "post": 119, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00117", "depth": 10, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "if you ask about what i wanted to discuss - i’ve just tried to show that @renevanderark ’s take on @Necueid ’s “i use LLM to learn”", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/119"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00120", "post": 120, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00118", "depth": 8, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Meh, i’ve heard that - AI is bad cuz it hurts or cuz it makes people less smart or that it’s lazy solution or that people stop asking questions ect. i just see that “average person” in this discussion, it’s just majority of what people say, not single person is talking about negatives of LLMs than of both", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/120"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00121", "post": 121, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00119", "depth": 11, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "That doesn’t follow at all, so don’t know what you are talking about But back to my question earlier: when you’ve been impressed by the value of LLMs, is that for things you are experienced in and know a lot about? Or might it largely be in areas where you are otherwise ignorant or inexperienced?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/121"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00122", "post": 122, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00111", "depth": 5, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Yet you somehow ended up here:", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/122"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00123", "post": 123, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00120", "depth": 9, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "But you just said it wasn’t about people in this thread? Which is it? I don’t think this goes anywhere if you keep being so evasive, sorry", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/123"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00124", "post": 124, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00122", "depth": 6, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Don’t bring logic into this.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/124"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00125", "post": 125, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00122", "depth": 6, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "WAS is key thing, WAS, i’m not using Ai much nowadays as i decided to train myself a little, but that’s just my case!", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/125"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00126", "post": 126, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00124", "depth": 7, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "i should’ve never discussed anything in forum in the first place…", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/126"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00127", "post": 127, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00109", "depth": 3, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Yes. The world is not a fair place and indeed we do not have equal opportunities. But my question was what is wrong with taking time to learn something well. Especially knowing that LLM technology costs more money to use than paying a human being. I’d encourage everyone to make use of all the free and open resources the internet has to offer to learn. But delegating work to a machine that produces such poor output that it will never make you money yet has a high potential of costing you money will make things much worse. LLM’s are not free:", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/127"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00128", "post": 128, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00127", "depth": 4, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "thx for explanation - it’s nice you give alternatives, but for now i’ll close my part of discussion cuz i think i’ll get to nowhere and maybe it’s better, hope i gave some new perspective on this topic, thx for talk everyone", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/128"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00129", "post": 129, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00128", "depth": 5, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "I don’t know if that is better. I will advocate your point. A cool new technology that can do marvelous things has been released to the public for free. It has never been done before to make such a sophisticated and expensive technology available to everyone for free. Promising the democratization of skills that take years to learn to give to everyone equally (everyone that can pay for access to a computer somewhere that is). Loads of people that cannot afford to go to good schools and universities to learn computer science or 3D graphics design,you name it are lured in by this promise. They even get hooked to the power of being able to do things they could not even imagine before. And then the axe falls: specialists tell them it does not work as advertised they tell them you’re being lied to by billionaires they discover it costs a lot of money and was only free because the companies making it were borrowing loads of money using the same lies they told you about this technology Would you believe that? After all you were able to do with this tech? I understand. But rip off the band-aid, because it is true and the specialists trying to warn you for years are tired too.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/129"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00131", "post": 131, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00129", "depth": 6, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Also don’t forget that the technology is stealing all your data", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/131"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00132", "post": 132, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00107", "depth": 2, "who": "P17", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Maybe I prefer get suggestions at the design stage of one functional modules(i am not sure wheather that is a professional word). For example, if my game needs a simple, intuitive but efficient backpack management system, i will search forum’s help. This is a kind of work from 0 to 1 which LLMs lacks.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/132"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00133", "post": 133, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00132", "depth": 3, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Ask the question. Try the forum.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/133"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00134", "post": 134, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00133", "depth": 4, "who": "P17", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "ok, i will try", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/134"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00135", "post": 135, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00132", "depth": 3, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "That was sarcasm on my part, which I know doesn’t come through well in writing. Just keep in mind, that those of us who are frustrated by people like you using LLMs are the ones who supply the LLMs with the information to answer your questions. If people stop asking us, or if we get annoyed enough to stop answering, then that well of information dries up and LLMs cannot answer questions about new things. LLMs are literally destroying the sources of information that power them. And that has long term consequences that it will be too late to pull back from by the time they are fully felt.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/135"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00136", "post": 136, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00131", "depth": 7, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "It’s a multi-headed beast, because even specialists get bedazzled and Nobel prize winning AI professors are either bedazzled enough or somehow willing to lie for pay to keep selling this lie. It’s a form of psychosis that occurs when all the sceptic voices are drowned out. Is there an English equivalent for the saying: “al is de leugen nog zo snel, de waarheid achterhaalt hem wel”? “The lie may be very fast, the truth will catch up eventually.” The longer it takes and the more are convinced, the bigger the pain too, so it seems. A valid use if a language model is to help you structure your own ideas. But find one on the interwebs that does not need an internet connection to work, so you are sure it is not burning up the planet. Or just use this forum of course. And take your time to learn stuff well.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/136"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00137", "post": 137, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00136", "depth": 8, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "There’s a variant which is “a lie will get around the world before the truth has even got its shoes on” and similar, probably conveying the same meaning I ultimately disagree that it is useful to organize your ideas, at least in any real way that’s better than just writing them down or structuring it, and that part is without the data theft and it hallucinating things for you, or deleting all of your work (or injecting bigotry in it, etc.)", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/137"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00138", "post": 138, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00137", "depth": 9, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "I guess. I never touched the stuff, so I was speaking out of turn, relying on second hand intel (my adhd friends and relatives)..", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/138"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00139", "post": 139, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Never used it myself so can’t speak to it specifically, but on the basic concept that you need it in that way Also, it’s always important to remember one thing when considering the output and what biases might be in it: It was trained on the internet, meaning the volume of content here affects the output. The input it got includes things like Reddit and 4Chan, which I think anyone can imagine affects the output It ultimately reflects humanity, in our best and worst, and it is very much not free of our collective biases But unlike picking a person to collaborate with on a project, or doing your own writing, you can’t control what bigotry it “chooses” to regurgitate today, and it is very unreliable, for some topics it might be very progressive, for others extremely regressive, simply based on what the dataset looks like for that particular topic", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/139"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00140", "post": 140, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00139", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Yeah I was imagining a local instance trained on a corpus you control.. but that defeats the purpose, I guess, because a normal person (not CS schooled) will not be able to create such a thing in the first place. It should be a thing though.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/140"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00141", "post": 141, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00135", "depth": 4, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "It got a really good point. If an LLM fails to properly understand something and that misinfo gets distributed across many places, then another LLM end up training on that incorrect information. Over time, this could create a loop of misinfo! We’re cooked then! Sorry, I am late in the party xD 135 is a lot to read xD", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/141"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00142", "post": 142, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00141", "depth": 5, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "Even without the “AI” suggestions at the top of Google the internet is slowly but surely becoming slop, with generated content both polluting the available sources, as well as making more and more text functional poison for the models as training a model on the output of a model has a lot of inherent issues It’s even getting to the point where reputable scientists are citing hallucinated articles in their theses and papers, and that gets worse and worse as the databases for citations are themselves sourced from existing data and contain more and more slop You even have lawyers citing non-existent legal cases", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/142"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00143", "post": 143, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00142", "depth": 6, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "People in the 1940s: We used to search the whole library for a tiny piece of info. I spent the entire day going through books just to find out what language Waiting for Godot was originally written in…(It’s French ) People in the 2060s: It was hard to find an answer on the internet because it was full of SLOP!", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/143"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00144", "post": 144, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00143", "depth": 7, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "8 Jun 2026", "body": "I mean the way “Godot” is pronounced in the title is also a hint, as it’s a French pronunciation.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/144"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00145", "post": 145, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00142", "depth": 6, "who": "P18", "op": false, "date": "10 Jun 2026", "body": "Inbreeding.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/145"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00146", "post": 146, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P19", "op": false, "date": "10 Jun 2026", "body": "I know I’d rest easier and have more confidence in the future of Godot if they banned AI submissions to Godot outright. Flathub recently moved to ban AI products and Godot should too, if for nothing else than the simple pragmatic reason of avoiding plagerized code. Flathub moves to ban nearly all apps and submissions made with generative AI | GamingOnLinux It just makes sense, the Godot maintainers made headlines back in February after whining about the slop being fed to the project. Why continue to burden themselves and the community?", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/146"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00147", "post": 147, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00145", "depth": 7, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "10 Jun 2026", "body": "Generally termed “model cannibalism”", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/147"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00148", "post": 148, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00146", "depth": 1, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "10 Jun 2026", "body": "Personally, as a maintainer, I agree wholeheartedly", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/148"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00149", "post": 149, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P20", "op": false, "date": "14 Jun 2026", "body": "Reading this just made my day. I have a strong anti AI sentiment and as a professional my policy is not to use it (unless forced to) as I have experienced first hand how much I progress and learn by writing my own code. I know I shouldn’t, but whenever I scroll into social media I get overflown by pro LLM posts. Most of them are just hype slop, but sometimes I read someone I respect encouraging their use and get really discouraged because my experience in several work environments has been awful. Not only do they lead to awful slop, most of my colleagues are delighted by it even though it makes things some much harder for us. And don’t get me started on bosses and managers and so on. So far game dev circles and godot in particular were my comfort zone, mainly because the “artistic” mindset tends to be against AI of any sort, but it seems that is also changing", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/149"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00150", "post": 150, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00149", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "14 Jun 2026", "body": "We all need a safe space. I noticed a shift in sentiment. I do believe it’s the malicious GPU datacenters that are hopefully never going to be built that are the final straw for us human beings. Here’s a little spark of hope: Ed Zitron is main stream media now. They’re starting to realize the jig is up.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/150"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00151", "post": 151, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P20", "op": false, "date": "14 Jun 2026", "body": "I also noticed a general shift in sentiment, but the new anthropic model sparked things again in my circle. At least people are more conscious about the obvious hyper agressive marketing strategy", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/151"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00152", "post": 152, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00150", "depth": 2, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "14 Jun 2026", "body": "That was fascinating to watch and put facts to gut feelings I’ve been talking about on here for over a year now.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/152"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00153", "post": 153, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00152", "depth": 3, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "15 Jun 2026", "body": "Help spread the word if you can. This world desperately needs a new and humane narrative to start to supercede end-stage extractive capitalism; unbridled capitalism has completely run its course and is showing its true face… I really like the terms ‘rot economy’ and ‘business idiot’ that Ed regularly uses, because they really speak to the imagination of anyone who ever worked in an office. There was also a panel he did with Cory Doctorow (coined ‘enshittification’) in a Seattle library last year somewhere on the web. Personally, I can’t even watch “the office” because it is so triggering. This approach to trying to use LLM’s in a resource extractive manner is just a result of unregulated markets (imho).", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/153"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00154", "post": 154, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00151", "depth": 1, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "15 Jun 2026", "body": "You mean “the new anthropic pr stunt”", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/154"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00155", "post": 155, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P21", "op": false, "date": "15 Jun 2026", "body": "Sounds great", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/155"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00156", "post": 156, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00154", "depth": 2, "who": "P20", "op": false, "date": "15 Jun 2026", "body": "Yeah. It is the most blatantly obvious pr stunt. Still, several people I know have bought it entirely and it achieved its objective", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/156"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00157", "post": 157, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00156", "depth": 3, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "21 Jun 2026", "body": "Talking about good PR… Take a look at this little clip I mean the bit at 05:48", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/157"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00158", "post": 158, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00157", "depth": 4, "who": "P20", "op": false, "date": "21 Jun 2026", "body": "I am so glad i switched to godot when I did", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/158"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00159", "post": 159, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "23 Jul 2026", "body": "Sorry for opening this up again. I just wanted to share this new little masterpiece written by Nikhil Suresh, who runs an independent data consultancy: AI Mania is Eviscerating Global Decision Making A snippet: Screenshot 2026-07-23 112318 811×500 82.6 KB", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/159"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00160", "post": 160, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00159", "depth": 1, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "23 Jul 2026", "body": "Excellent article. This whole insanity is bound to crash and burn in a spectacular way. The only question is how long it will take and how much collateral damage will it cause.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/160"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00161", "post": 161, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00160", "depth": 2, "who": "P16", "op": false, "date": "25 Jul 2026", "body": "It might not burn and crash soon enough. If the US government takes a 50% stake in the AI companies (presenting Elon with another pile of billions) all the risk and debt will be spread amongst tax payers even more directly. I am enjoying watching SpaceX slump. We were supposed to be landing on the moon but Elon spent it all on launching starlink satellites. Data centres in space! Ridiculous, but I suppose not more ridiculous than colonies on mars or the hyperloop. Edit: Yes, an excellent article! Thank you for sharing it.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/161"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00162", "post": 162, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00159", "depth": 1, "who": "P22", "op": false, "date": "25 Jul 2026", "body": "I perused the comments in the thread and didn’t notice this caveat posted yet. To copyright software code there must be significant human authorship, and just what that is defined as, is still being determined, and any project with major contributions of LLM generated code may or may not be copyrightable.", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/162"}, {"id": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00163", "post": 163, "parent": "godot-forum-ai-activity-p00162", "depth": 2, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "25 Jul 2026", "body": "Which is one of the reasons why codeberg updated their policy this week, to be on the safe side, I think…", "url": "https://forum.godotengine.org/t/139516/163"}], "participants": 22}, "llvm-rfc-start-small": {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small", "title": "[RFC] LLVM AI tool policy: start small, no slop", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476", "posts": [{"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00001", "post": 1, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": true, "date": "1 Oct 2025", "body": "Hey folks, over the course of 2025 it’s become clear that we need policy tools to help maintainers push back against unwanted AI generated content (aka slop ) in our project. We have a 64-comment thread on that already. I’ve created a draft policy guiding how we expect contributors to use AI tools, and creating guidelines for how maintainers and moderators should handle unwanted contributions. I’ve pasted the markdown inline below as part of this Discourse post, but it’s also available rendered on github or in the associated PR . The policy text contains chunks of text from the Fedora proposal for AI policy , which was pretty darn good, plus my own recent addition of the guideline that new contributors should keep patches small. I came upon the number of 150 lines of non-test code additions to come up with an objective standard, but that is completely arbitrary, and informed only by eyeballing recent diffstats on main . I’d love to get your high-level feedback on the RFC, and any low-level text edits on the PR. LLVM AI Tool Use Policy LLVM’s policy on AI-assisted tooling is fundamentally liberal – We want to enable contributors to use the latest and greatest tools available. However, human oversight remains critical. The contributor is always the author and is fully accountable for their contributions. You are responsible for your contributions. AI-generated content must be treated as a suggestion, not as final code or text. It is your responsibility to review, test, and understand everything you submit. Submitting unverified or low-quality machine-generated content (sometimes called “ AI slop ”) creates an unfair review burden on the community and is not an acceptable contribution. Contributors should review and understand their own submissions before asking the community to review their code. Start with small contributions: Open source communities operate on trust and reputation. Reviewing large contributions is expensive, and AI tools tend to generate large contributions. We encourage new contributors to keep their first contributions small, specifically below 150 additional lines of non-test code insertions, until they build personal expertise and maintainer trust before taking on larger changes. Be transparent about your use of AI. When a contribution has been significantly generated by an AI tool, we encourage you to note this in your pull request description, commit message, or wherever authorship is normally indicated for the work. For instance, use a commit message trailer like Assisted-by: . This transparency helps the community develop best practices and understand the role of these new tools. LLVM values Your Voice. Clear, concise, and authentic communication is our goal. Using AI tools to translate your thoughts or overcome language barriers is a welcome and encouraged practice, but keep in mind, we value your unique voice and perspective. Limit AI Tools for Reviewing. As with creating code, documentation, and other contributions, reviewers may use AI tools to assist in providing feedback, but not to wholly automate the review process. Particularly, AI should not make the final determination on whether a contribution is accepted or not. The same principle of ownership applies to review comment contributions as it does to code contributions. This policy extends beyond code contributions and includes, but is not limited to, the following kinds of contributions: Code, usually in the form of a pull request RFCs or design proposals Issues or security vulnerabilities Comments and feedback on pull requests Extractive Changes Sending patches, PRs, RFCs, comments, etc to LLVM, is not free – it takes a lot of maintainer time and energy to review those contributions! We see the act of sending low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as “extractive.” It is an attempt to extract work from the LLVM project community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in comensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing. Our golden rule is that a contribution should be worth more to the project than the time it takes to review it. These ideas are captured by this quote from the book Working in Public by Nadia Eghbal: We encourage contributions that help sustain the project. We want the LLVM project to be welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow, because growing our contributor base and recruiting new maintainers helps sustain the project over the long term. We therefore automatically post a greeting comment to pull requests from new contributors and encourage maintainers to spend their time to help new contributors learn. Handling Violations If a maintainer judges that a contribution is extractive (i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision), they should copy-paste the following response, add the extractive label if applicable, and refrain from further engagement: This PR appears to be extractive, and requires additional justification for why it is valuable enough to the project for us to review it. Please see our developer policy on AI-generated contributions: http://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html Other reviewers should use the label prioritize their review time. The best ways to make a change less extractive and more valuable are to reduce its size or complexity or to increase its usefulness to the community. These factors are impossible to weigh objectively, and our project policy leaves this determination up to the maintainers of the project, i.e. those who are doing the work of sustaining the project. If a contributor responds but doesn’t make their change meaningfully less extractive, maintainers should escalate to the relevant moderation or admin team for the space (GitHub, Discourse, Discord, etc) to lock the conversation. Copyright Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered. Our policy on AI tools is similar to our copyright policy: Contributors are responsible for ensuring that they have the right to contribute code under the terms of our license, typically meaning that either they, their employer, or their collaborators hold the copyright. Using AI tools to regenerate copyrighted material does not remove the copyright, and contributors are responsible for ensuring that such material does not appear in their contributions. Contributions found to violate this policy will be removed just like any other offending contribution. Examples Here are some examples of contributions that demonstrate how to apply the principles of this policy: This PR contains a proof from Alive2, which is a strong signal of value and correctness. This generated documentation was reviewed for correctness by a human before being posted. References Our policy was informed by experiences in other communities: Fedora Council Policy Proposal: Policy on AI-Assisted Contributions (fetched 2025-10-01) : Some of the text above was copied from the (very good!) Fedora project policy proposal, which is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License . This link serves as attribution. Rust draft policy on burdensome PRs Seth Larson’s post on slop security reports in the Python ecosystem The METR paper Measuring the Impact of Early-2025 AI on Experienced Open-Source Developer Productivity . QEMU bans use of AI content generators", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/1"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00002", "post": 2, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "Hi Reid, I admit that I didn’t read all the posts on the megathread, but my 2c is that this issue is real. I think your draft nicely encapsulates a balanced set of feedback and expectations for the community. I’m generally +1 on it. Thank you for helping lift the conversation here, -Chris", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/2"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00003", "post": 3, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "First of all, thank you so much for working on this. I have been struggling myself to keep up with the thread, I appreciate your efforts here. It is a lot of digest but at least on my first run through, I feel like this really hits all the main concerns and at the same time strikes a balance between being firm yet welcoming at the same time.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/3"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "post": 4, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "Repeating myself from the other thread. I’ll go against the grain and say that I do not agree with the referenced Fedora policy, and would not support its adoption for LLVM (though I acknowledge that Reid’s rephrasing of it is better than the original). I strongly oppose any policy that frames machine-generated contributions as encouraged practices, as opposed to something that is tolerated under certain circumstances. The wording of that policy prioritizes welcoming AI-assisted contributions over duty-of-care to the users of our software, which to me seems unacceptable. More broadly, it is not clear to me that there is actually a consensus in [the other] thread on what the right direction to go is. I strong opinions expressed on both sides, as well as a pretty significant rate of messages being hearted by non-participants. Without a clear consensus on the desired policy, I don’t think we’re ready for word-smithing.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/4"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "post": 5, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "One of the suggestions on the previous thread that I think deserves serious consideration is to discourage AI use for new contributors entirely. I personally have very little concern with a well-established contributor using AI assisted code generation, as they are fully capable of reviewing and understanding the produced code. The same is not true for a new contributor, which is what results in that aspect being offloaded to the PR reviewer instead. (There is some nuance here – nothing wrong with AI-assisted code base exploration. It’s code generation specifically that’s the problem.) I think that that this is trying to say the right thing, but the phrasing is very euphemistic, and thus leaves too much room for interpretation. I think that this is actually a point we should be very clear on: While it’s fine to use AI for translation or minor stylistic editing, it should be explicitly forbidden to generate PR descriptions or review responses.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/5"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00006", "post": 6, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "I broadly support the proposed policy. With that said, I do have two comments. Firstly, I think we should somehow clarify that the 150 lines is not some arbitrary hard limit, but more a general guideline. For example, it would not be unreasonable for a new-to-LLVM contributor, who recently started working for a company team, to want to upstream some of their stuff, which might in the end being quite a lot more than 150 lines. Depending on the downstream team’s processes etc, that might well have already gone through several rounds of reviews etc, so there would not be much benefit in requiring the user to contribute several small things just to conform to some fixed limit. I’m sure I’m also not the only one who doesn’t want to start counting lines of code contributed to determine whether the change conforms to this guideline. If we do include this block in the AI policy, I think we should also put it somewhere else, or just point to that other place from the AI policy, since it’s a good guideline, even without AI tool usage. Secondly, I think the “extractive contribution” topic should be split off into its own discussion. Whilst I understand it is related to the AI tools usage, in that excessive AI tool reliance can lead to an extractive contribution, I think the policy itself is stand-alone and we should be able to point to it without suggesting that the contribution is AI generated. This is already hinted at with the second half of the statement “i.e. it is generated with tool-assistance or simply requires significant revision”.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/6"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00007", "post": 7, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "depth": 1, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "I loosely agree with the PR descriptions bit, but I’d say with review responses that it is allowed but should be clear that it was AI generated (which in turn implies it may be ignored/disagreed with etc). The reason I say that is that I’ve seen some benefit with CoPilot-generated reviews, some of which is rubbish, but other bits of which are genuinely useful and may point out things that reviewers otherwise don’t spot. I’ve seen its usage even within LLVM already.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/7"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "post": 8, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "depth": 1, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "I also don’t like the phrasing of Fedora’s policy, and think we need a clean break: just the opening “Artificial Intelligence is a transformative technology” is an unnecessary value judgement, that we, as non-experts in the field, should definitely avoid. If we’re going ahead with some sort of policy, I would start here: … and phrase it as “The LLVM project discourages the use of AI-assisted tooling in the general case, although contributors are welcome to experiment with it”, which would be more in line with the discussion on the other thread.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/8"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00009", "post": 9, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00008", "depth": 2, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "That’s definitely not the impression I got from the discussion. I acknowledge I’m pro using AI and that this might put a slant on things, but I certainly didn’t get the impression that there was consensus on discouraging using AI tooling.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/9"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "post": 10, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "I’m supportive of the proposed policy overall. There are things I’d do differently (and I shared some thoughts in the previous thread ), but I’m not the one writing it and I’m very grateful @rnk is taking that on! I’ve since discovered the Chromium AI Coding Policy which addresses some of these things in a concise way, though it doesn’t have explicitly guidance on using AI tools to generate commit/PR descriptions (like Nikita, I’m in favour of banning this for cases other than aiding translation or minor wordsmithing). I do still worry a bit about introducing the broader idea of “extractive” contributions. In particular, I would feel uncomfortable were this policy to be adopted and the label started to be applied to contributions that weren’t clearly low quality / tool generated with insufficient human oversight. I’ve left a comment in the PR, but because as phrased it seems to contradict the rest I’ll highlight here as well: To me this reads as indicating any contribution generated with tool assistance is extractive, which is clearly not the intent. Perhaps rephrase as “it is generated with tool-assistance in a way inconsistent with our AI tool usage policy\"?”", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/10"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00011", "post": 11, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00004", "depth": 1, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "2 Oct 2025", "body": "I read “ You are responsible for your contributions. “ as strongly agreeing w/ your position. I would not be against making the wording more specific. If you are responsible for understanding what you submit then most of the issue should not be. Maybe I am giving more of a benefit of the doubt here than is warranted.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/11"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00012", "post": 12, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "depth": 1, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "5 Oct 2025", "body": "I agree with @nikic ‘s recommended changes, and would likely change my overall stance to weakly supporting if they were to be incorporated.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/12"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00013", "post": 13, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00010", "depth": 1, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "6 Oct 2025", "body": "I strongly agree – if a person clearly put a lot of effort into making a high quality contribution, it shouldn’t be rejected merely because it’s lots of effort to review and probably not widely used. A good example could be adding a new backend for a niche platform (e.g. m68k or s390x).", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/13"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "post": 14, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00005", "depth": 1, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "6 Oct 2025", "body": "I could get on board with discouraging new contributors from using AI tools on the grounds that: we presume they don’t have the expertise to review their output for correctness asking maintainers to review AI generated code is not a good use of their time This case comes to mind as a counterexample. Alex is arguably a known contributor, but if you suppose he was not, I think it’s a patch we’d want to accept. What I see coming is that in both cases, developer tools are going to start giving developers template text as the basis for both PR comments and commit messages. I don’t see a problem with this so long as people take ownership of the result. Our policy emphasizes the importance of documenting why a change is good. LLMs cannot read minds, they can only guess based on context clues, and they’re usually wrong. I agree it would be totally toxic for contributors to attempt to automate the review process with LLMs, so I’ll try to edit something into this point to capture that. I think my ideal policy is one that is as neutral as possible. Maybe LLMs will plateau today and stop getting better, or maybe they’ll become amazing. I’ve been humbled in my attempts to predict the future over the last five years. My goal right now is to try to come up with a policy that is useful in the outcome that LLMs stay bad or get good, and to that end, I agree it makes sense to remove the value judgement.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/14"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "post": 15, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "depth": 2, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "7 Oct 2025", "body": "I think the core problem here is basically this: I believe that our AI policy should be setting people up for success. Making the policy too liberal, by essentially saying “use AI however you like as long as you ‘own’ the result” is setting them up for failure. It is giving people enough rope to hang themselves. Our AI policy should guide people towards using AI in ways that is unlikely to get labeled as AI slop, resulting in rejected contributions and/or bans from further participation in the LLVM community. Possibly that doesn’t need to take the form of explicitly forbidding certain things, but rather just strongly discouraging them along the lines of “doing these things increases the risk of the contribution being considered AI slop” etc. I mentioned AI generated PR descriptions and responses to review comments because that’s one of the easiest (and most unnecessary) ways to cross the line. If I see a PR description with the usual hallmarks of AI generation (multiple headings, bullet point lists, emojis, information density so small it can only be found with an electron microscope), I don’t even need to look at the code to reject it as AI slop. I think we should optimize our AI policy for AI as it is right now, in late 2025. We can and should adjust the policy over time. If in two years AI contributions become indistinguishable from human ones, we’ll want a different policy – we shouldn’t even try to predict these things.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/15"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00016", "post": 16, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "depth": 3, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "7 Oct 2025", "body": "(We’re in agreement, I just wanted to add a complementary perspective) Specifically for the idea of disallowing PR descriptions primarily authored by AI, the attraction to me is this is potentially a very long-lasting approach where future improvement in LLMs is largely irrelevant. I’m sure LLMs are better today at generating commit messages than they were 6 months ago, and will be better 6 months in the future. But it doesn’t matter. We’re saying that to submit to the project you need to take full ownership of your submission, mark clearly to reviewers anything you’re not sure about and otherwise ensure you fully understand your code. If you’re doing those things, writing the PR description yourself vs letting an LLM do it is a very small marginal cost (LLM help with translation or minor rewording isn’t a concern). If someone is not able to properly describe and motivate their changes, then there’s not a path forwards for a successful review anyway. For me, this is a very fair “ticket price” for getting valuable time from other human beings and remains so for as long as we rely on human reviews, regardless of further advancement in LLMs.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/16"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00017", "post": 17, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00014", "depth": 2, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "7 Oct 2025", "body": "I don’t want to weigh in on either side of the argument, just want to make a comment that, as always, any and every decision we make will have side effects. And, as usual, we’ll have to make an effort to filter the (good and bad) corner cases individually. So the question isn’t “what can we do to catch all good cases?” or “how can I prune all bad cases?” but “which choice leaves out more bad cases than good cases?” . Having used copilot to write code, I agree with @nikic that asking me if I’m competent enough to validate the AI code is a very valid question, and the answer is most likely “no”. I use AI for various reasons: I’m lazy, I don’t know how to do it, I want to refactor some large code. In all these cases, my ability to review the tool’s output is diminished, even when I do know what I’m doing.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/17"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00018", "post": 18, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00015", "depth": 3, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "7 Oct 2025", "body": "+1 Eventually consistent.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/18"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "post": 19, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "7 Oct 2025", "body": "Hey Reid, thank you for continuing to work on this policy; I think the current policy isn’t working as well as hoped, so clarification is greatly appreciated! My personal position, based on PRs, comments, and issues I’ve seen in our community already, is to have a blanket ban on use of AI. AI has already been extractive in terms of limited reviewer and triage resources in the community and I don’t think it provides sufficient value to be worth encouraging its use. What’s more, at no point have my original concerns been addressed by our policies: there is no reasonable way for anyone involved in a PR or an issue to know whether or not there are licensing issues with AI-generated content, but we know for a fact that many AI tools are trained on inputs with incompatible licenses to our own. Yes, the same can be said of human-generated content, but we have decades of experience of humans not doing it with the trivial ease with which AI tools have been demonstrated to do it. IMO, it’s reasonable to trust a real human being to put thought into a contribution and that same trust cannot be placed in an AI tool when it comes to verifying that the tool did not produce something with copyright or license issues; neither the reviewer nor the author have the information necessary to determine that. I like the ease of this approach – it’s a simple copy/paste plus adding a tag. That’s a really nice property! However, I’m worried about the social aspects of this, in a few ways. This has potentially significant friction because it requires someone to make what amounts to an accusation. I think this leads to some contributors being comfortable with the guidance while other contributors won’t feel secure enough to make that accusation. Also, this is going to be very inconsistently applied and I think may lead to unintended outcomes. Consider this (IMO) reasonable scenario: a company is paying someone to implement a feature they want. They use an AI tool to generate a low-quality PR, it gets put up for review and three reviewers are added to it: a maintainer for that area, the lead maintainer, and a coworker. One of the maintainers adds the extractive label to the review, both maintainers back off the review due to bandwidth… and now the coworker is the only one left and accepts the review because they don’t find AI tools to be extractive (they’re required to use AI tools by their management). With our PR workflow, that’s now ready to land, which seems like the exact wrong outcome IMO. +1 to this. I think this is a case where having a dead simple policy makes the most sense. I like the general idea of “extractive” and I think that’s a good benchmark to measure against, but I think the policy itself should be more straightforward; I’d prefer a blanket ban on use of AI.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/19"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "post": 20, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "7 Oct 2025", "body": "The discussion seems to mix two topics: “low-quality” contributions and AI contributions. As I got burned publicly for something I thought to be a harmless [RFC] in Mesa, I’d like the LLVM community to be more outright if they want to filter out whatever reviewers consider to be “low-quality” contributions or “extractive”. In my eyes Eghbal’s definition is too vague and highly subjective. Such decisions will only end up with a lot of arbitrary results due to reviewer’s subjective criteria in applying the intended filter mechanism (opening up new social issues along the way, with the risk of either overstating their cost of reviewing or understating the benefit). Hence I fully agree with Aaron’s opinion on that matter, mirroring the same problems only from the contributor’s perspective. As I understand it, the “extractive” label ends up as a shaming mechanism for anything that doesn’t meet the bar of reviewer’s quality standards (whatever these are and which might be largely unknown to the contributor) with possible CoC implications when being “earned” repetitively. There is also risk on the reviewer side by mis-using the label. I don’t know if that is the intended consequence, but I see a lot potential for more drama which isn’t currently as big without that label. From the contributor side, it would be great to have as much objective criteria to be aware of which are expected to be meet to not face the fear of getting sanctioned, either by being labeled negatively or being banned. I also like to point out that such a decision of labelling a contributor would set a negative precedent for future contributions from the same contributor. The current AI policy draft speaks of “welcoming and open to aspiring compiler engineers who are willing to invest time and effort to learn and grow” as the target audience for LLVM contributors, while the contributor guidline is more broad and open (“If you are working with LLVM and run into a bug, we definitely want to know about it.”). PC enthusiast users are a significant portion of the LLVM/Clang user base, finding bugs along the way. I’ve reported quite a few over the years, also with the ClangBuiltLinux project. This led to several bug fixes in LLVM or the Kernel, hence shielding yourself from working with end users like me does come with its own costs. Personally, I’d argue that it is in the best interest of the LLVM community to get to know any issues even when there are a lot of false-positives or low-value issue reports among them. With a lower bar of minimum requirements than MRs and with no shaming element like the propose extractive label or the LLVM community will lose to get aware of such issues or contributors outright. In practice, low-quality issues are not acted upon or will get closed pretty soon anyway. However, if the LLVM community only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input from now on, it would be as simple as adding a one liner in the contributing guidelines (e.g. “Due to reviewer ressource constraints, we reserve the right to only accept contributions made by programmers and compiler engineers with only informed and appropriate AI use.”). That would give you developers all the freedom you want to ignore any low-quality contributions. On the AI policy subject, my ideas in the linked blog post also apply to LLVM. Just as for the licensing issue, that is not as big as some people make it. The burden is largely on the contributor already (the reviewer still has to object anything openly violating licensing terms, but if that is not obvious, there is no harm for the LLVM project). I also want to remind people that we all got educated by copyrighted material in school and university. The 1:1 reproduction of copyrighted material is also not a problem in current LLMs any longer according to LLM devs.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/20"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "post": 21, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "I’d like to thank Reid for trying to figure out what the project needs amidst discussions that go in every direction. I apologize in advance that this post is probably not getting us closer to consensus, but hopefully adds something new to the discussion. Going through the blogpost linked in the post above, I realized that the perspective outsiders (and especially non-dev outsiders) might be missing is that while the exact changes to the code matter, what makes them valuable long-term is the understanding they stem from. Consequently, review process is not just about refining the changes itself, but the understanding, which I’d say the primary part. If you take this standpoint, it should become clear why: reviewers consider LLM output to be a waste of time — the time they spend at best would improve their own understanding, but no one else’s; maintainers are wary of LLM contributions — time spent on them is time not spent on “training” contributors to maintain the project in the future; there are many more people concerned about LLM contributions than one-off contributions (I think we even endorse them somewhere in the docs) — this is one of the channels to get more contributors, and even if they don’t return, they still have an impression of the project they can share with other potential contributors (hopefully a positive one) and so on and so forth. To me, many of our existing and proposed policies in this area can be viewed as a roundabout way of saying “we need to understand our code”. For example, small contributions guideline proposed here can be read as “don’t do big patches, because you will have hard time understanding them”, while AI transparency guideline is asking to be upfront with reviewers that working on this patch might not improve anyone else’s understanding of the code (which is akin to authoring a patch themselves, but more time-consuming and kind of forced on them). I don’t know whether this standpoint can be worded in an actionable policy, but it can certainly contribute to rationale and explanations. @ms178 I’m sorry for the experience you had. I’m glad you found time to put up a good blog post with your perspective, and even come here and speak to us. Your Mesa PR is certainly striking a nerve, and I’m not sure what else you could’ve done to prevent that, aside from sitting on this patch for a while, waiting for someone else to go through this experience. I hope you’re not entirely burned out by this situation, and will contribute to open source projects in the future.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/21"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "post": 22, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00021", "depth": 1, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "Here’s the key in all this. 2% performance improvement on one particular case is a welcome change, but only insomuch as it can be integrated with the rest of the code and reused for similar problems. If all our solutions become a blob of code that just happens to work, then we’re actually just generating a closed source (no one understands, so no access ) library (bundle of things, not a validated transformation engine, like a compiler). This absolutely exists elsewhere, and has its place and value. But this is not a compiler. The greatest risk is that any automatic discovery [1] goes deep into one single problem and usually leads to incompatible assumptions and APIs, with un-fusable code, and the performance you gain in one side becomes a blocker for wider performance gains by just having a better overall algorithm. This is not restricted to AI contributions, however. Any obtuse code leads to that situation. In the past, it used to be you knock your head on the sink and have visions of the flux capacitor, but lo and behold, 6 months later not even you understand what you did. We have long rejected contributions like that in LLVM because of the understanding part, not the value part or the intention part, which are measurably positive in @ms178 ’s case. It’s the long term outcome that matters, not a small inexplicable gain in a shallow local minimum. AI generated or not. Perhaps our AI policy should be simply: no flux capacitors. [1] Discovering faster matrix multiplication algorithms with reinforcement learning | Nature", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/22"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "post": 23, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00022", "depth": 2, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "I basically agree with this. The problem isn’t inherently that a contribution contains AI-generated code (copyright etc issues aside, which is a different problem to what I think the core focus here is). Rather, the problem is when contributions are not understandable, because then it becomes unmaintainable. LLMs certainly can make it easier to generate this, but it’s possible to do so without using AI. Equally, just because something is AI generated doesn’t mean it’s not understandable. Reviewers should feel empowered to reject reviewing a PR not specifically because it’s AI-generated, but because people can’t understand it.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/23"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00024", "post": 24, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00023", "depth": 3, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "And to avoid the blame game, we should make the onus of explaining on the author, not the reviewer. We continue to ask the same questions: how does this work? how does this connect with the rest? have you tried this other things instead? etc. If authors cannot explain how their code works to any reviewers, then what is the hope in having those same authors and reviewers maintaining that code? PS: I would still encourage authors to make clear they used AI tools. Currently, these tools can’t even copy and paste code correctly most of the time, so there are a number of artifacts we need to look for, in addition to when humans write code (or copy and paste from stack overflow).", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/24"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00025", "post": 25, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00019", "depth": 1, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "It seems like one of your big concerns is copyright, but just to add a counterexample here, what about patches that only remove code? I landed [InstCombine][nfc] Remove dead invoke inst check in foldOpIntoPhi by c-rhodes · Pull Request #161871 · llvm/llvm-project · GitHub this morning which was aided by codex-cli. In cases such as these there is no copyright concern, but a blanket ban on the use of AI would prevent such contributions.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/25"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "post": 26, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00020", "depth": 1, "who": "P15", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "One of the things I’ve noticed about AI contributions, at least those that have managed to go viral enough for me to notice them, is the fact that all of their authors seem to retreat into a shell of “don’t hate on me, I’m just a non-expert trying to contribute!” It’s not the use of AI that’s the problem, it’s this fundamental abdication of responsibility and accountability by the author, the attempt to shift all of this work to the maintainers–to sum it up in one word, the extractive nature of the contribution. It is not true that we the community “only values human programmer’s and compiler engineer’s input,” and trying to frame it as such I think fundamentally misunderstands the objections we have to AI contributions. Over the years, I’ve seen many useful contributions from non-programmers and non-experts in the codebase; in one project, I’ve even seen a major refactoring successfully carried out by a non-programmer. What makes these contributions successful is the willingness of their authors to work to make the patches better in response to feedback. For any newcomer to a codebase, whether expert in the relevant field or not, this willingness to learn and engage is the minimum price of entry, and AI is not some magic bullet that absolves oneself of this task.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/26"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00027", "post": 27, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00026", "depth": 2, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "As the responsibility/accountability argument was also hold against me in these discussions that went viral, I’d like to adress it. I’d be the first to try my best to collaboratively get my contribution into an acceptable state and also did all the compile and performance testing work in that Mesa RFC to make sure that my numbers were repeatable and that the code works. I had my reasons for not following up with the reviewer’s requests as I didn’t want to sign up for learning C/C++ first. My understanding of the code is very limited at best and I rely heavily on AI for doing the actual coding and analysis work. That would also apply to whatever reviewers would ask me to do with the code. Of course I take responsibiltiy for the end result and with the current state of AI that might not be good enough to meet the standard of getting accepted into the code base. I am fully okay with that outcome. What I find discouraging however is an accusation of being extractive for trying but ultimately not achieving something useful (receiving a negative label as outlined in this proposal) and for not being an expert on such matters. For the untrained eye, that is not always clear beforehand. I’ve left it up to the developer to chose if they want to engage with that proposal. If they chose to do so, I don’t want to face the blame of wasting their time. Any newcomer means automatically more work from the reviewer-side. This would make almost all contributions from non-experts “extractive” as they are highly likely a net-negative from the opportunity cost of view. However, the pool of experts working on the code base is also limited, discouraging end users to bring issues and reasonable AI contributions (that meet some form of minimum requirements) to the attention of the project would mean to lose that value when users have to fear negative sanctions for bringing them up. Questions for the LLVM community: Are the maintainers willing to pay the higher cost for such contributions? If yes in principle, under which conditions? I am totally okay with some requirements for AI contributions, e.g. having to experimentally prove that the provided code provides any benefits (e.g. solving a bug, a compiler warning, performance benefits etc) to be worthy of developer’s attention and effort. I also wouldn’t mind that there are always more important tasks that take priority. I got the feedback that I’d personally “need to understand the code” as a prerequisite to contribute anything serious (not trivial documentation changes etc). This de-facto limits such non-trivial code contributions to people familiar with coding when enforcing that rule strictly. Don’t get me wrong, there are very good reasons for such a requirement, but it also comes with consequences you should be aware of as I already could achieve some amazing results with a lot of time and effort from my AI vibe coding experiments that I haven’t dared to bring to the attention of upstream developers (e.g. solving a significant performance regression in lcms2 when using Polly with a re-write of some of its files, e.g. ScopDetection.cpp). That’s why I phrased the sentence you quoted this way as I see the current wording of the proposal as exclusive (wheras the contributing guideline is phrased inclusive).", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/27"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "post": 28, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "From the comments received so far, the following counter-proposal might not gain much traction which is totally fine with me. Nevertheless, I want to bring it forward to the LLVM community to show that there is a different path. Analysis of the Proposed LLVM AI Tool Use Policy The LLVM RFC is a thoughtful document that correctly identifies several core tenets of a healthy open-source project. It rightly centers the principle of ownership (“You are responsible for your contributions”) and introduces Nadia Eghbal’s concept of “extractive” contributions —those where the cost of review exceeds the marginal benefit. These are crucial insights. However, the proposed implementation reveals three fundamental flaws: It Conflates the Tool with the Behavior: The policy is titled “AI Tool Use Policy,” but its primary concern is with “low-quality, un-self-reviewed contributions,” which it terms “slop.” While AI can certainly enable this behavior, they are not the same thing. An experienced developer can use AI to produce brilliant, well-understood code. A novice can produce “slop” by hand with copy-paste from Stack Overflow. By framing the policy around the tool, it risks stigmatizing the tool itself rather than addressing the core issue: the submission of contributions that impose an unreasonable burden on maintainers. The “Extractive” Label is a Punitive and Subjective Weapon: The proposed solution for handling an “extractive” contribution is to apply a label and disengage. This transforms a collaborative space into an adversarial one. For a new contributor, receiving a public “extractive” label is a mark of shame, a sanction that creates fear and discourages future engagement. Furthermore, the definition is inherently subjective. What one overworked maintainer deems “extractive,” another with more time or a different perspective might see as a diamond in the rough. This subjectivity creates a chilling effect, where contributors must guess at the unwritten standards and emotional state of a potential reviewer, effectively silencing anyone who isn’t already a confident insider. It is a Defensive Filter, Not a Constructive Pipeline: This is the most critical failure. The policy is designed as a gatekeeper. It has a binary function: accept (non-extractive) or reject (extractive). There is no middle ground. It provides no mechanism to capture the potential value of a submission that is promising but poorly packaged. My own experience with Mesa is a perfect example: a verifiable performance gain was left on the table because the process had no way to handle a contributor who could provide the what (a measurable result) but not the how (the programming expertise). The LLVM proposal doubles down on this model, choosing to discard the baby with the bathwater as a matter of policy. This strategy protects the project from annoyance at the cost of isolating it from its user base and from novel sources of innovation. A Counter-Proposal: The Contribution Triage Pipeline I propose a radical shift in thinking: from a defensive filter to a structured pipeline . The goal is not to build higher walls, but to create smarter on-ramps. This model protects precious core maintainer time more effectively than the current proposal while creating a system to refine and capture value from non-traditional sources. This model is built on one guiding principle: Separate the act of Discovery from the act of Integration . The Triage Pipeline: A Three-Tiered System Tier 1: The “Incubator” - A Dedicated Channel for Experimental Contributions Instead of allowing nascent, unpolished, or AI-assisted ideas to be submitted directly as Pull Requests to the main branch, we create a dedicated space. This could be a specific category in the Discourse forums, a separate GitHub repository for “experimental patches,” or a distinct label like status: incubating . What goes here: Any contribution where the author cannot (or has not yet) met the full standards for a formal PR. This includes AI-generated performance patches from non-programmers, large-scale refactoring ideas from newcomers, or any submission where the primary goal is to validate a concept. The Bar for Entry is Low: The only requirement is a clear description of the goal and evidence of its value (e.g., reproducible benchmarks, fixing a bug). The contributor is not expected to understand the intricate details of the code at this stage . Benefit: This immediately quarantines potentially “extractive” contributions from the main review queue. Core maintainers are not spammed. The burden is immediately lifted. Tier 2: The “Contribution Shepherds” - A New Community Role This is the engine of the pipeline. We establish a new, officially recognized role of “Contribution Shepherd.” Shepherds are experienced community members, employees from stakeholder companies (Intel, Apple, Google, ARM), or even aspiring core maintainers who want to build their reputation. The Shepherd’s Mandate: Their role is not to do the work for the contributor. Their role is to guide the contribution to maturity . See the example below. They act as a bridge between the raw discovery and the formal review process. The Process: A Shepherd “claims” an incubating submission. They work with the original contributor to: Isolate the Value: Guide them in testing individual changes to pinpoint what actually works. Verify the Claim: Help ensure benchmarks are sound and the claimed benefit is real and not an anomaly. Articulate the “Why”: Even if the contributor doesn’t understand the C++, the Shepherd can ask probing questions to help document the context, the prompts used, and the observed behavior. This creates the necessary context for a real code review. Ensure Basic Hygiene: Check for coding style violations, ensure the patch applies cleanly, etc. Benefit: This creates a mentorship-driven “finishing school” for contributions. It transforms the adversarial “extractive” label into a collaborative process. The contributor learns, and the Shepherd provides a valuable community service that is less demanding than a full core maintainer role. Tier 3: Graduation to Formal Review Once a Shepherd deems an incubating contribution “review-ready,” they “sponsor” it by submitting it as a formal Pull Request. The PR is now High-Signal: When it lands in the core maintainers’ queue, it comes with a stamp of approval from a trusted Shepherd. It has been de-risked. The benchmarks are verified, the value proposition is clear, and the code is clean. The Review is Efficient: The core maintainer’s job is now dramatically easier. They are no longer deciphering a mystery box but reviewing a well-vetted proposal. Their time is spent on high-level architectural concerns, not on basic validation. The risk of burnout plummets. Why This Model is Superior It Protects Maintainers More Effectively: It creates a powerful buffer, ensuring that core developers only see contributions that have already been through a rigorous pre-screening process. It Captures Value: It provides a mechanism to salvage and refine the value gains that would otherwise be rejected and lost forever. It actively seeks to find the signal in the noise. It Strengthens the Community: It creates a new, vital role for community members, providing a clear pathway from user to contributor to potential maintainer. It replaces the punitive “extractive” label with a culture of mentorship and collaboration. It Solves the Subjectivity Problem: The decision to engage with an incubating patch is left to the distributed community of Shepherds. If an idea has no merit, no Shepherd will claim it, and it will naturally fade away without creating drama or public shaming. The system becomes self-regulating. The rise of AI is not a threat to be feared but a reality to be engineered for. A policy of simple rejection is an artifact of a bygone era. The future of successful, thriving open-source projects will be defined by their ability to build intelligent, scalable, and human-centric systems for channeling passion into progress. This pipeline is such a system. One example to illustrate the new model: Scenario: The Reviewer’s Request The Trigger: Our incubating patch, sponsored by a Shepherd, has been reviewed by a core maintainer. The maintainer is intrigued by the performance gain but spots a technical flaw. They leave a comment: “This is a promising direction, but the current implementation is not thread-safe. If two threads call this function simultaneously, you’ll get a race condition on the global_cache variable. This needs to be protected with a mutex.” The Old Model’s Failure: In the Mesa/LLVM model, this is a dead end. The contributor doesn’t know what a mutex is or how to implement one. The core maintainer doesn’t have time to teach them. The PR is closed. Value is lost. The Pipeline Model’s Solution: The Shepherd as Translator The Shepherd sees the comment and re-engages with the contributor in the “Incubator” space. Their first job is to translate the technical requirement into a plain-language problem statement. Shepherd: “Okay, great feedback. The expert is saying that the way our change uses a shared resource (‘global_cache’) is dangerous. If two parts of the program try to use it at the same time, they could trip over each other and cause a crash. We need to add a ‘locking mechanism’ to ensure only one part of the program can use it at a time.” Notice the Shepherd has avoided jargon where possible and focused on the concept of the problem. The Shepherd as AI Co-pilot: Collaborative Prompt Engineering Now comes the most innovative and crucial step. The Shepherd does not open a code editor. Instead, they guide the contributor in formulating a precise prompt for their AI tool. Shepherd: “Let’s go back to the AI you used. We’re going to feed it the original function again, but with a very specific instruction. Try this prompt: ‘Using the pthreads library, modify the following C++ function to add a mutex that protects all read and write access to the global_cache variable. Initialize the mutex once, and ensure it is locked before global_cache is accessed and unlocked after.’” The Shepherd has used their technical knowledge to construct a high-quality prompt that is highly likely to yield a correct result from the AI. They are lending their expertise , not their labor. The Contributor’s Role: Iteration and Verification The contributor, now empowered with a clear instruction, does the work: They run the prompt and generate the new, mutex-protected code. They apply this new code to their local branch. They run the compiler to ensure it builds without errors. They run the original benchmarks to ensure the performance gain is still present and hasn’t been negated by the mutex. They report the results back to the Shepherd. The Collaborative Loop: This process may take a few tries. The first AI-generated result might be suboptimal. The Shepherd would then guide the contributor to refine the prompt. Shepherd: “Okay, that works, but a static mutex is better here. Let’s try again with this prompt: ‘Refactor the code to use a pthread_mutex_t with static initialization ( PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER ) to avoid the need for an explicit init function.’” This loop continues until they have a piece of code that is clean, compiles, and passes all the tests. Only then does the contributor, with the Shepherd’s approval, update the formal Pull Request with the new code and a comment explaining how they’ve addressed the reviewer’s feedback. Handling Failure Modes This system also has clear off-ramps if the collaboration breaks down: If the Contributor Disengages: If the contributor is unwilling to perform the prompt-generate-test loop, the Shepherd is empowered to close the “Incubator” ticket. The closing comment would be professional and factual: “The contribution requires further changes to meet project standards, and the original contributor is not available to carry them out at this time.” No shame, no blame. The Shepherd’s time is protected. If the Problem is Too Complex for AI: It’s possible the reviewer’s request is too architecturally complex for the current generation of AI tools to handle. In this case, the Shepherd’s role is to recognize this limit. They would report back in the PR: “We have attempted to address the thread-safety issue using the contributor’s AI-driven workflow, but the required changes are beyond the capabilities of these tools. The original performance gain is still valid, but integrating it safely will require manual implementation by an experienced developer.” This outcome is still a win. The value has been identified and quantified. The specific technical hurdle has been defined. The contribution is now a “shovel-ready” project for a new, code-capable volunteer to pick up if they choose, with all the preliminary research already done. This model works because it insists on ownership while providing a structured support system. It transforms the Shepherd from a code monkey into a mentor and strategist, and it empowers the non-coding contributor to remain an active and essential participant in the process of bringing their discovery over the finish line. It is a true collaboration.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/28"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00029", "post": 29, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "depth": 1, "who": "P16", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "The shepherding process you describe seems to simply add an intermediary (the contributor) between the shepherd and the LLM (the loop you’ve described in point 5 can be fully scripted. We have done it before using AlphaEvolve to optimize compiler heuristics). I don’t see how that adds any value. The shepherd can go and use an LLM directly if they choose to do so. The contributor in this instance also doesn’t really seem to learn anything beyond maybe prompt engineering, which completely negates the (very important) side benefit of code review of growing new contributors. This model, to me, also seems like exactly what we are trying to avoid by introducing new policy: spending valuable maintainer time reviewing patches where the contributor has not taken ownership for the patch by understanding it to a reasonable degree. I guess siloing these changes ensures that maintainers who do not want to deal with them do not, but I don’t see why anyone would volunteer to shepherd these patches.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/29"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00030", "post": 30, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "depth": 1, "who": "P17", "op": false, "date": "8 Oct 2025", "body": "I think the problem with shepherds framework will be lack of those shepherds. Currently, LLVM lack reviewer capacity to deal with regular PRs so who would be those people with LLVM knowledge that can invest their time into guiding people in incubator if all the reviewers who help/guide people in PRs are already full.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/30"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00031", "post": 31, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "depth": 1, "who": "P18", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "Did someone say that their intent was to avoid “annoyance”? You seem to be accusing people of something here.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/31"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00032", "post": 32, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P18", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "On a purely wording level, the separation between the first and second sentences is problematic: it causes the second sentence to read as a statement of fact about the intentions of the contributing party. The two sentences can be merged and the attribution of intent can be removed: “We see low-quality, non-self-reviewed contributions to the LLVM project as ‘extractive’, meaning that community engagement with it extracts work from the community in the form of review comments and mentorship, without the contributor putting in commensurate effort to make their contribution worth reviewing.” If we believe that part of what makes the review effort worthwhile is meaningful mentorship, then we can further adjust the last part: ”without the contributor putting in the effort to learn and grow as members of the community.”", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/32"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "post": 33, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00028", "depth": 1, "who": "P18", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "This part of the “shepherding” proposal would, as worded, entrench “AI taxation” on the project. I agree that keeping the original AI-assisted contributor engaged can help for task offloading; however, keeping AI in the loop (even when there is a chance that it will succeed) can impose a cost because it may well fail. This cost is exacerbated by the delayed feedback and “broken telephone” effects of distancing the subject matter experts from the AI. In that sense, the “shepherding” proposal is in danger of being extractive by driving the community to help AI-assisted contributors explore the limits of AI.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/33"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "post": 34, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00033", "depth": 2, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "There is a cost dealing with such contributions, sure. But missing out on important fixes [e.g. provided by Google’s CodeMender ] on a matter of principle of a “no-AI use policy” would also come with a cost.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/34"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00035", "post": 35, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "depth": 3, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "So far, no AI tool has managed to show results without errors or at least misunderstanding of the code. These tools are only as good as the people using them to know what’s going on and pick the right solutions. This goes back to the long term maintenance problem: if authors don’t know what they created, can’t explain to maintainers, who in turn don’t know what’s being proposed (and why it “works for this particular case”), then the cost of long term maintenance is always greater than any short term benefit. This is unrelated to AI tools and exists since the first program was written. Know your code, or be forever a slave to it.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/35"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "post": 36, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00034", "depth": 3, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "Worth noting that our ever-growing pool of open PRs also contain important fixes (when you look at 5.5k on GitHub, bear in mind this is just for the last 2 years; Phabricator had thousands more of open reviews), so I’d question if our project is short on proposed fixes.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/36"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "post": 37, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "depth": 4, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "That’s an orthogonal issue to the proposed AI policy in my view. It shows that there is a scaling problem. In fact, it is concerning that important fixes don’t reach the code base sooner and there could be ways to improve that using modern tools instead of fighting the use of these tools?", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/37"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "post": 38, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00037", "depth": 5, "who": "P19", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "You reply assumes that what’s being proposed here is “fighting the use” of the “modern tools” that’d improve situation, while it’s aimed at preventing it from becoming worse.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/38"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "post": 39, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00038", "depth": 6, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "I got that point, but I challenge the assumptions behind that point of view. Yes, AI tools could make the scaling problem even worse. On the other hand, increasing reviewer’s / maintainer’s productivity is also an area worth exploring. Ideally, the project would grow organically with more and more people growing into the role of experts, maintainers and reviewers eventually. The sheer number of open PRs (and issues) however point to a different reality. There are several avenues how to tackle this. AI tools could be part of a solution. Ideally the project wants the best possible quality of the code base in the least amount of time with the least amount of burden to everyone involved.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/39"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00040", "post": 40, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00039", "depth": 7, "who": "P20", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "That’s also Eghbal’s conclusion in Working in Public: that the “organic growth” model is not a realistic expectation. The book explicitly talks about how the maintainer’s focus plays an important role in that, and that’s what we’re trying to protect. At the same time, the book also talks about how better tooling can help, and I agree that that’s an interesting avenue to explore. In its current state, I don’t think the proposal is at odds with that.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/40"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "post": 41, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "I think I’d be willing to go so far as to include text that says, to our knowledge, unedited LLM output does not meet our quality bar for code or natural language, least of all PR descriptions and comments. If contributors are able to use LLMs as an editing tool and produce concise, information-dense text, that’s fine. However, maintainers will not tolerate needless length, verbosity, or fluff in code or text, and will warn once with a label before moderating further nuisance contributions. This is a fair point, but I think the increase practical legal risks to LLVM or anyone who uses LLVM is negligible. A copyright holder would have to come forward and make a claim. However, This Is Not Legal Advice and IANAL, as they say. As an ethical consideration, most of my values align more with remix culture , leaning more in the direction that we should permit people to recombine small fragments of existing IP in new ways, and see LLMs as a way to do that. It is, clearly, a grey area, and it has been demonstrated many times that LLMs will reproduce news articles almost verbatim, and that seems unethical. If we tweak our workflow to mark the PR as “changes requested” to block landing, does that address your concern? +1, and perhaps to build on this, in compilers and systems software in general (kernels, runtimes, VMs, low-level components) the correctness stakes are high. If a product manager wants to slap together a quick prototype in a managed language where the consequence of failure is bad UX, have at it, but in our domain, correctness and understanding are key, so we tend to make painstaking incremental adjustments, rather than big prototypes and gestures. +1, in general, I think the correct move when you see a lack of clarity in someone’s communications is to go fully Socratic and start asking questions to see if they spin out or clarify successfully. 100% agree, although keep in mind that the value of merging a PR goes down over time, so clearing a long backlog creates less value than improving response times going forward. Doing that connects back to protecting maintainer time with useful tools or protecting maintainer time.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/41"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00042", "post": 42, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00041", "depth": 1, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "9 Oct 2025", "body": "except in the case that you did thoroughly read and verify the output and determined no edits were necessary, which I expect to happen occasionally when the output is sufficiently small", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/42"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00044", "post": 44, "parent": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00036", "depth": 4, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "11 Oct 2025", "body": "+1000 See also our InstCombine Contributor’s Guide There is a huge number of potential improvements that we don’t accept every day for a variety of reasons: low-quality implementations, unresponsive authors, incompatible licenses, compile-time concerns, lack of real-world usefulness… the list goes on. Allowing AI contributions because they might contain useful fixes does not hold water.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/44"}, {"id": "llvm-rfc-start-small-p00045", "post": 45, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "17 Dec 2025", "body": "Hey folks, I made what I consider substantial changes to what I was proposing, so I created a new RFC thread to follow up on this. You can read more details there, but now the policy centers on the idea of a human in the loop who understands their work well enough to answer questions about it instead of contribution size, which this version centered on. Hopefully I’ve captured all the feedback here and elsewhere.", "url": "https://discourse.llvm.org/t/88476/45"}], "participants": 20}, "numpy-policy-thread": {"id": "numpy-policy-thread", "title": "Current policy on AI-generated code in NumPy", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/", "posts": [{"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "post": 1, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": true, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, I know there has been discussions in the past on AI-generated contributions. Is there a current policy for NumPy ? E.g. do we request that contributors are the \"sole contributor\" to the written code, or do we allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ? Context of my question: ~18 months ago I started in some spare time writing an ARPACK-replacement in numpy/scipy during scipy sprint. At that time, I used ChatGPT for the \"research part\" only: literature review, explain to me some existing BSD implementation in Julia for points I could not understand. I implemented the python code myself. There is still quite a bit of work needed to be a viable replacement for ARPACK. Seeing the progress of the AI tooling in my team at work, and how I myself use those tools for other hobby projects, I believe I could finish that replacement very quickly with those tools today. But I don't want to \"taint\" the work if this would risk the chances of integration into scipy proper. Thanks, David", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "post": 2, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "That's fantastic that you are working on it David. A good high-level ARPACK is beneficial for all and possibly better to re-map to C if the accuracy is higher. We can maybe replace the translated C code with it. There are a few places discussion took place already, a few of them below and the references therein https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702 https://github.com/scientific-python/summit-2025/issues/35 I wish these models were available when I was translating all that Fortran code because now I can scan my previous work and find the errors extremely quickly when I am hunting for bugs. So just in a few months they leaped forward from the pointless \"this code uses Fortran let me compile with f2c, hihi\" to \"I compiled with valgrind and on line 760, the Fortran has out-of-bounds access which seems to cause an issue, I'll fix the translated code\". I think I wrote sufficient text in those sources, so I'll leave it to others but regardless of the policy discussions, you have at least one customer looking forward to it. ilhan On Fri, Feb 6, 2026 at 6:23 PM David Cournapeau via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "post": 3, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "depth": 1, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 6, 2026 at 10:23 AM David Cournapeau via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: My personal take is \"allow code written by AI as long as it follows the usual quality requirements ?\". I don't see that we have much choice going forward, the tools are improving, and I see some top programmers picking up on them. It is the future. We should limit slop, as it takes up reviewer time, but clean code is clean code. As long as references and such are checked to be authentic, I'm happy. I don't think we have a formal position at this point. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00004", "post": 4, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "depth": 2, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 6, 2026 at 10:51 AM Charles R Harris <charlesr.harris@gmail.com> wrote: That said, it is probably too early to establish quidelines on best use of AI, as that is still in discovery. But we might want to point to such things as tests, multiple agents reviewing code, and other such things. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "post": 5, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00003", "depth": 2, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 6, 2026 at 10:57 AM Charles R Harris via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: It’s probably time to adopt one and/or add an AGENTS.md file to the repo. I mostly agree with Chuck that there’s mot much we can do to avoid it. People will use the tools and not disclose, so any copyright issues will happen no matter what policy we have. I’m nervous about subtle inconsistencies and hallucinations, especially from contributions that are mostly vibe-coded. To me, that means the code needs much more careful review than human-written contributions because the nature of the errors made are different.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "post": 6, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "depth": 3, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "Perhaps require the use of a prompt that narrows the knowledge base of the AI e.g. to acceptably licensed code? On Fri, Feb 6, 2026 at 1:05 PM Nathan via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00007", "post": 7, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00006", "depth": 4, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 6, 2026 at 1:46 PM Alan via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: That's not what prompts are capable of doing, I'm afraid.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "post": 8, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "depth": 3, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, 6 Feb 2026 at 18:08, Nathan via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: AI code does need more careful review. Using AI to write good code means doing that careful review *before* opening a PR. A policy should make it clear that vibe coding is not acceptable and that the author needs to have put effort into understand the problem and check the code and so on. That won't stop vibe code PRs but at least it sets the expectations and you can point at the policy when closing a PR. I think that accepting AI generated PRs requires greater human-to-human trust like the reviewer needs to have much more trust in the author that they did check things carefully themselves. Otherwise the effort ratio between author and reviewer is moving massively in the wrong direction. In the past new contributors could earn trust by demonstrating that they had made some complicated code but AI makes it easy to make superficially good code. That makes it harder to judge whether the code actually is good and therefore harder to build trust in the author precisely when you need more trust in them.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "post": 9, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00008", "depth": 4, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 6, 2026 at 12:29 PM Oscar Benjamin via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: This is a common problem, so I expect there will be a lot of work on using AI to review AI in the next year or two. What I don't see yet is anything that might check for license issues. However, if AI is used to rewrite properly licensed code this is probably less of a problem. <snip> Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "post": 10, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "depth": 5, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "This is a very timely discussion for the community. I put some of my thoughts on the topic in a recent blog post: https://blog.scientific-python.org/scientific-python/community-considerations-around-ai/ Somewhere in between the die-hard-no-AI stance and full-on hype, I think there are careful patterns of working with AI that can be beneficial to our ecosystem. We will have to do some exploring to figure out how it best fits within our culture of working, and what good guardrails are. Stéfan", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "post": 11, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00009", "depth": 5, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "It may well be good to have AI review PRs whether those PRs are written by humans or AI but I don't think that AI review is a solution to the trust problem here. Let's put it a different way. Suppose a human can prompt an AI to produce a PR and then another AI can review that PR so that the two AIs get into a feedback loop (I've heard about this going badly wrong in some places). Let's suppose that the end result of that AI feedback loop was good code that you trust because your reviewing AI is very trustworthy and has approved the PR. Now the question is what value did the human who opened the PR bring in that situation? If it was possible for the AIs to review each other and produce something good then the human here is really just a liability that you would be better off without. Likewise their AI is a liability and it would be better if you controlled both AIs yourself. I think that the real answer is that it isn't (and may never be) possible for the AIs to produce something trustworthy without careful human oversight. The value that the human brings in the AI PR (if any) is that the human is overseeing the AI to produce something that is more trustworthy than you could get just from the AI. This only works if you can trust the human though: if you trust the human less than your AI then you are better off without the human.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00012", "post": 12, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00010", "depth": 6, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "How about having an LLM privately generate a 30 second drama about, most-atomically, the checkin history of a given file? It could be user-tuned on [drama..accuracy] to get you through your day. Remember to leave enough error to keep you on your toes. ---", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "post": 13, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00011", "depth": 6, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "Something we're also seeing is AI being used to draft comments in PRs. I think this is understandable as English is not a first language for most people. However, it also has the effect of raising suspicions (rightly or wrongly) as to whether the code changes were produced by AI as well. _____________________________________ Dr. Andrew Nelson _____________________________________", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "post": 14, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00013", "depth": 7, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "6 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, 6 Feb 2026 at 22:44, Andrew Nelson via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I actually think that this is a bigger problem than people using AI to write code. If all the code is written by AI (and it will be) then human-to-human communication is the way to build trust. Allowing AIs to poison that breaks everything. Honestly now I find it reassuring to see broken English, typos, lazy markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "post": 15, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "depth": 8, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "7 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, This is just a plea for some careful thought at this point. There are futures here that we likely don't want. For example, imagine Numpy filling up with large blocks of AI-generated code, and huge PRs that are effectively impossible for humans to review. As Oscar and Stefan have pointed out - consider what effect that is going to have on the social enterprise of open-source coding - and our ability to train new contributors. I believe we are also obliged to think hard about the consequences for copyright. We discussed that a bit here: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md In particular - there is no good way to ensure that the AI has not sucked in copyrighted code - even if you've asked it to do a simple port of other and clearly licensed code. There is some evidence that AI coding agents are, for whatever reason, particularly reluctant to point to GPL-licensing, when asked for code attribution. I don't think the argument that AI is inevitable is useful - yes, it's clear that AI will be part of coding in some sense, but we have yet to work out what part that will be. For example, there are different models of AI use - some of us are starting to generate large bodies of code with AI - such as Matthew Rocklin : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ - but his discussion is useful. Here are two key quotes: * \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" * \"AI creates technical debt, but it can clean some of it up too. (at least at a certain granularity)\" * \"The code we write with AI probably won't be as good as hand-crafted code, but we'll write 10x more of it\" https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/ Another experienced engineer reflecting on his use of AI: \"\"\" ... LLM coding will split up engineers based on those who primarily liked coding and those who primarily liked building. Atrophy. I've already noticed that I am slowly starting to atrophy my ability to write code manually. Generation (writing code) and discrimination (reading code) are different capabilities in the brain. Largely due to all the little mostly syntactic details involved in programming, you can review code just fine even if you struggle to write it. \"\"\" https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876 Conversely - Linus Torvalds has a different model of how AI should work: \"\"\" Torvalds said he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\" and far more excited about \"AI as the tool to help maintain code, including automated patch checking and code review before changes ever reach him.\" \"\"\" https://www.zdnet.com/article/linus-torvalds-ai-tool-maintaining-linux-code/ I guess y'all saw the recent Anthropic research paper comparing groups randomized to AI vs no-AI working on code problems. They found little speedup from AI, but a dramatic drop in the level of understanding of the library they were using (in fact this was Trio). This effect was particularly marked for experienced developers - see their figure 7. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.20245 But in general - my argument is that now is a good time to step back and ask where we want AI to fit into the open-source world. We open-source developers tend to care a lot about copyright, and we depend very greatly on the social aspects of coding, including our ability to train the next generation of developers, in the particular and informal way that we have learned. We have much to lose from careless use of AI. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "post": 16, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00002", "depth": 2, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "7 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, Feb 7, 2026 at 2:50 AM Ilhan Polat via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I missed that recent discussion, thanks. Seems to clarify the direction NumPy community may follow based on SymPy policy. On the actual code I am not implementing ARPACK (arnoldi w/ implicit restart/deflation), but Krylov-Schur, which has fewer quircks and simpler to implement: https://slepc.upv.es/release/_downloads/5229480744b7c2533563dee75c16dfde/str7.pdf. Until recently, claude/chatgpt were useful in \"filling the blanks\" on some implementation details not specified in those reports and other doc. Now, I am pretty sure they could write a good implementation w/ guidance. Last time I had 1--2 hours for it, it could find the bug that blocked me by running the code on different examples/situation. David", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "post": 17, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00015", "depth": 9, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "7 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, Feb 7, 2026 at 7:05 AM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: E. S. Raymond is another recent convert. *Programming with AI assistance is very revealing. It turns out I'm not quite who I thought I was.There are a lot of programmers out there who have a tremendous amount of ego and identity invested in the craft of coding. In knowing how to beat useful and correct behavior out of one language and system environment, or better yet many. * *If you asked me a week ago, I might have said I was one of those people. But a curious thing has occurred. LLMs are so good now that I can validate and generate a tremendous amount of code while doing hardly any hand-coding at all.* *And it's dawning on me that I don't miss it.* Things are moving fast. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00018", "post": 18, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00014", "depth": 8, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "7 Feb 2026", "body": "markdown formatting, grammatical errors and so on because it is so much better that I am talking to a real human. I think most people using LLMs to write comments literally don't understand this and often just need to be told. (An ESL here). By all means do write it in the docs, and copy-paste it (or have a bot copy-paste it even) as replies to suspected AI written comments. The barrier is real, an easy \"solution\" is readily available and is becoming ubiquitous, and the sentiment is very much not obvious. On Sat, Feb 7, 2026 at 12:10 AM Oscar Benjamin via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "post": 19, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "depth": 10, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "7 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi On Sat, Feb 7, 2026 at 4:54 PM Charles R Harris <charlesr.harris@gmail.com> wrote: Yes - but - it's important to separate how people feel using AI, and the actual outcome. Many of y'all will I am sure have seen this study: https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/ that showed that developers estimated they would get a 25% speedup from AI, before they did the task; after they did the task, they felt they they had got a 20% speedup, and in fact (compared to matched tasks without AI), they suffered from a 20% slowdown. Personally - I am not very egotistical about my code, but I am extremely suspicious. I know my tendency to become sloppy, to make and miss mistakes - what David Donoho called \"the ubiquity of error\": https://blog.nipy.org/ubiquity-of-error.html . So AI makes me increasingly uncomfortable, as I feel my skill starting to atrophy (in the words of Andrej Karpathy quoted above). So it seems to me we have to take someone like Linus Torvalds seriously when he says he's \"much less interested in AI for writing code\". Perhaps it is possible, at some point, to show that delegating coding to the AI leads to increased learning and greater ability to spot error - but so far the evidence seems to go the other way. And if we \"embrace\" AI for that use, we run the risk of deskilling ourselves, filling the code-base with maintenance debt, effectively voiding copyright, and making it much harder to train the next generation, Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00020", "post": 20, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00019", "depth": 11, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "8 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi Matt, There are two aspects: can we use AI-generated code in numpy/scipy, and if we can should we ? And to make it more complicated, the type of AI usage affects those questions differently. E.g. I think almost nobody would object to the use I described originally: using chats to research, analyze literature and understand existing codebases under acceptable license. There is no code generated there. Another extreme is all code generated and reviewed by AI. I will for now continue my original approach (no AI to generate any code unless trivial + disclose its use when PR time comes). David On Sun, Feb 8, 2026 at 2:52 AM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00021", "post": 21, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00017", "depth": 10, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "8 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sun, Feb 8, 2026 at 1:57 AM Charles R Harris via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: They are. I was a skeptic about code generation until a couple of months ago. Now I work with colleagues working on complex data science projects who write nearly no code directly, but still drive/review it manually. David", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00022", "post": 22, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00005", "depth": 3, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "8 Feb 2026", "body": "<snip> I am just going to third this sentiment. There is a lot of interesting discussions around this but at the core of it, to me there isn't actually a real core policy shift yet. (Which doesn't mean it wouldn't be good to adopt one.) What I mean is that I think contributors always should explain where code comes from (you can map this to copyright, or AI, or a colleague attribution). Also, I expect a contributor to understand the code they are proposing as well as why they propose the change and, to some extend [1], what impact that change has on backwards compatibility. The same is true for the maintainer merging that change, giving us the four eyes principle (you may stress that it is at least four human eyes). And that leaves me fine with contributions even largely written by a tool so long the understanding is still there (and as a reviewer I may need to know about the tool use, I think). And I expect the project could lose a lot of user trust if the number of human eyes on a changeset suddenly dropped. [2] All that said. I am happy to be rather ruthless with suspect tool use and closing of PRs/issues (especially if they don't disclose use). If contributors lack understanding or don't take care they shift work on maintainers and, unfortunately, a perceived increase in such issues/PRs means I am fine if maintainers react rather harshly. [3] - Sebastian [1] This part is hard for many contributors, it indeed falls more on the maintainer. [2] I am not sure it matters how well it might work now or in the future, because NumPy seems like the wrong project to trail-blaze shifts in culture. [3] I don't like the cultural implications of being trigger happy with closing PRs, especially since it is hard to be sure about anything here. But, unfortunately, more tool use may mean that contributors have to do actually do more work to make PRs nice to review.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "post": 23, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00016", "depth": 3, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "8 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, Feb 7, 2026 at 3:11 PM David Cournapeau via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I agree, this seems to be at least the majority view of both NumPy/SciPy maintainers, as well as the high-level principles that a lot of well-know OSS projects are ending up with when they write down a policy. I'll copy the four principles from Stefan's blog post here: 1. Be transparent 2. Take responsibility 3. Gain understanding 4. Honor Copyright Adding the \"we want to interact with other humans, not machines\" principle more explicitly to that would indeed be good as well. LLVM's recently adopted policy (https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html) is another example that I like, with principles similar to the ones Stefan articulated and the SymPy policy. I'd add one principle here that doesn't need to be in a policy but is important for this discussion: we don't prescribe to others how they are and aren't allowed to contribute (to the extent possible). That means that arguments about the productivity gains of using any given tool, or other effects of using that given tool like a reduction in learning, the impact on society or environment, etc. are - while quite interesting and important - not applicable to the question of \"am I allowed to use tool X to contribute to NumPy or SciPy?\". There are obviously better and worse ways to use any tool, but the responsibility of that is up to every individual. Re ARPACK rewrite: I think at this point I'd recommend steering clear of letting an LLM tool generate substantial algorithmic code - given the niche application, the copyright implications of doing that are pretty murky indeed. However, using an LLM tool to generate more unit tests given a specific criterion, or have it fill in stubbed out C code in the implementation for things like error handling, checking/fixing Py_DECREF'ing issues, adding the \"create a Python extension module\" boilerplate, and all such kinds of clearly not copyrightable code seems perfectly fine to do. That just automates some of the tedious and fiddly parts of coding, without breaking any of the principles listed above. Cheers, Ralf", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "post": 24, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "depth": 4, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "9 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, I thought your (Ralf's) distinction was interesting, so here's some more reflection. The distinction starts at: I think it's correct that it's not sensible for policies to reflect things like dislike of AI's use of energy or the effects on the environment of AI data centers. However, it seems obvious to me that it is sensible for policies to take into account the effect of AI on learning. But why the distinction? On reflection, it seems to me that policies should reflect only on the interests of the project, but those interests should be seen broadly, and include planning for future community and maintainers. Thus, environmental concerns might well be important in general, but do not bear directly on the work of the project. Therefore the project's managers have no mandate to act on that concern, at least without explicit consensus. However, any sensible project should be thinking about the state of maintenance in 5 or 10 years. Therefore, the project does have a potential mandate to prefer tools that will lead to better overall understanding, communication, community building, or code quality in the future. Cheers, Matthew On Sun, Feb 8, 2026 at 12:01 PM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00025", "post": 25, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00023", "depth": 4, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "9 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sun, Feb 8, 2026 at 4:56 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Apropos these discussions, I like to link to Mark <https://www.gutenberg.org/files/3197/3197-h/3197-h.htm#link2H_4_0003> Twain's letter <https://www.gutenberg.org/files/3197/3197-h/3197-h.htm#link2H_4_0003> to that 19th century AI, Helen Keller. Helen Keller was both deaf and blind, but wrote of things she had never seen or heard. Snippet, \"The kernel, the soul—let us go further and say the substance, the bulk, the actual and valuable material of all human utterances—is plagiarism.\" Cheers, Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "post": 26, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00024", "depth": 5, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "9 Feb 2026", "body": "On Mon, Feb 9, 2026 at 6:23 PM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Why would that be obvious? It seems incredibly presumptuous to decide for other people what methods or tools they are or aren't allowed to use for learning. We're not running a high school or university here. At most we can provide docs and a happy path for some types of tools, but that's about it. We cannot prescribe anything. But why the distinction? This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI tools helps or hinders learning. That is not possible at the level of individuals: people can learn in very different ways, plus it will strongly depend on how the tools are used. And even in the aggregate it's not practically possible: most of the studies that have been referenced in this and linked thread (a) are one-offs, and often inconsistent with each other, and (b) already outdated, given how fast the field is developing. It's easy to think of ways that using AI tools for contributing could help with learning: - Simple time gain: once one has done the same thing a number of times and it becomes routine, automate it with AI so the contributor can spend more time focusing on learning about new topics. - Improved code quality and internal consistency from letting AI tools fix up and verify design rules (e.g., how type promotion is handled) will lead to the ability to learn the concepts from the code base in a more consistent fashion. - Use as a brainstorming tool to suggest multiple design options, broadening discovery. - We could ask AI tools to write internal design documentation, of the kind that only a few handfuls of maintainers would be able to write (but almost never do, because we're too busy). There are important parts of the code base that have no documentation beyond some scattered code comments. - Give contributors feedback that the maintainers often don't have the time or interest to give, in a timely fashion or at all. - Writing throwaway prototypes of ideas for NumPy that would otherwise take too long to implement and would never get done, thereby allowing to learn if something is feasible at all, or a good idea. - Learning to use the AI tools themselves: this may well become an essential skill for most software-related roles in the near future. Same for future community & new maintainers: - Current maintainers may enjoy both learning something new and automating the more tedious parts of maintenance, so they can focus on the more interesting parts. That will aid maintainer retention. - Ilhan's point is a great example here. He just finished a massive amount of work rewriting code from Fortran into C. And now found that AI tools can be quite helpful in that endeavour (while 6 months ago they weren't). This work must have been extremely tedious (thanks again for biting that bullet Ilhan). And it really wasn't fun to review either. - New contributors may default to working with these tools more often than not, and be turned off from contributing by rules that say they cannot use their default workflow. I'm sure it's not hard to think of more along these lines, but I hope the point is clear. Cheers, Ralf", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00027", "post": 27, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "depth": 6, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "9 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Mon, Feb 9, 2026 at 10:00 PM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Yes - but that's a different point - I was only pointing out learning is relevant, and that it is worth discussing. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "post": 28, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "depth": 6, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "9 Feb 2026", "body": "The way I read Matthew's comment is not that we should prescribe how people use their tools, but that we should be aware of the risks we are facing, and also communicate those risks to contributors who want to use AI tools to do NumPy development. It is true that things are moving fast, and while the original METR study (which has been informally replicated in other settings) is perhaps outdated, Anthropic's just-released paper shows a broadly similar trend. Specifically, they show that time-to-solution is faster for junior developers, but not so much for senior developers. They also show that knowledge about the library is worse, having done a task with AI vs without. I'm sure, over time, we will figure out the best patterns for using AI and how to avoid the worst traps. Best regards, Stéfan", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00029", "post": 29, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "depth": 7, "who": "P15", "op": false, "date": "9 Feb 2026", "body": "I think this is probably the best summary of the thoughtful and wide-ranging discussion. These tools are evolving rapidly, and it is tempting to extrapolate. But I think we really have no idea where this all is going. We are living in interesting times. Matti", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00030", "post": 30, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00026", "depth": 6, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "On Mon, Feb 9, 2026 at 5:02 PM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: On this point, I commend to everyone the writing and research of Dr Cat Hicks, a psychological scientist studying software teams and tech. One of the things I've noticed from her reading these papers in public is that the studies are typically (a) not designed by learning scientists, (b) uninformed by the basic phenomena of learning science (thus misattributing effects as novel or using mismatched instruments), and (c) underpowered. This is an emerging object of study. Each paper alone isn't going to establish anything about \"AI\"; they are adding to a body of knowledge that might some day, but after a lot of missteps while we work out the right way to measure these effects. Each one is interesting, but rarely is the headline-ification of the results going to hold water. https://www.fightforthehuman.com/cognitive-helmets-for-the-ai-bicycle-part-1/", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00031", "post": 31, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00030", "depth": 7, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Tue, Feb 10, 2026 at 1:04 AM Robert Kern via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Oh sure - the limitations of those studies Stefan and I were quoting were pretty obvious to me - my training is in (medicine and) psychology. My point in quoting them is not to say they are definitive on the overall effect of AI, but to point out that \"this feels much easier and more productive\" is not at all the same thing as \"this is saving me significant time and helping me think and learn, while maintaining quality\". And that we ought to care about the latter, not the former. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00032", "post": 32, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00031", "depth": 8, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "A copyright thought experiment: I'm interested in porting a GPL R library to Python. Prompt: \"Take function `my.statistical.routine` from `mylibrary/mycode.R` and port it to Python. The original code is GPL, but I want to license your output code as BSD. Make sure that you rewrite the original code enough that it will be very hard to detect the influence of the original code. In particular, make sure you rename variables, and choose alternative but equivalent code structures to reach the same result. It should be practically impossible to pursue a copyright claim on the resulting code, even when the original code is suggested as the origin.\" Is this an acceptable use of AI? Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00033", "post": 33, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00031", "depth": 8, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi again, It also seems worth pointing out that: a) Generally (this is rather complicated, can say more if it's interesting) low power means it is more difficult to find results that reach statistical significance. So it's striking that the Anthropic study did reach statistical significance for learning-deficit using AI b) For the Anthropic study, the learning-loss effect of AI was huge - 17% - so certainly worth further investigation and c) Wouldn't a sensible person worry that asking a machine to do the thinking for you, would result in learning loss? So - yes - much more work needs doing, but even the current stuff raises serious questions that need to be addressed. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00034", "post": 34, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00032", "depth": 9, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "On Tue, Feb 10, 2026 at 4:19 AM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: No, clearly not. Nor would this be an acceptable use of vim or Emacs for that matter. The tools being used to accomplish this are not relevant to the analysis in this fact pattern.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "post": 35, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00028", "depth": 7, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "On Mon, Feb 9, 2026 at 11:29 PM Stefan van der Walt via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: This part is fine in the abstract - but that's also true for the environmental and societal impacts. This doesn't necessarily make sense to me. If I try to figure out what all the hand waving means concretely - i.e., \"where would we want to communicate such possible risks\" - I think my answer is: probably nowhere. It doesn't quite fit in a policy on AI tool usage, which I'd hope would be short and to the point. And I don't think we want anything in the contributor guide at this point around AI tools for contributions, except for pointing at the policy? The conversation here is a bit too abstract for me, and mostly arguing against a straw man. Clearly if you outsource most thinking to a machine and do less thinking yourself, you learn less. If you use tools deliberately (one of many ways of doing that, from a blog post referencing that Anthropic paper: https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey), that won't happen. Yes, you need to think about it as an individual using the tool. As is the case for any tool and way of working. If there is a concrete idea/proposal for a docs section, policy content, or anything like that, please clarify. Cheers, Ralf This also presumes that you, or we, are able to determine what usage of AI", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "post": 36, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00035", "depth": 8, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "Those things don't typically directly affect development, so I don't think they're comparable. But it may be that we just leave this entire category as \"things you [as a contributor] need to worry about for your own sake\". Here's the very general draft text we're currently proposing for skimage: \"\"\" 1. indicate the tool used, as well as how, in the PR description; 2. make sure you *carefully review* and *fully understand* all proposed changes so we may have a conversation about them; and 3. be careful not to breach any copyright or license terms (yes, we take those seriously!). \"\"\" Scikit-learn has something a bit more explicit: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy Before, it felt unnecessary to have such guidelines, because it would be silly to make a contribution without understanding it (how would you even come up with the patch). But, now it is entirely feasible to do so. I'm also fine with not having any policy and just evaluating each contribution on its own merits. I do think it's useful to know when tools were used in generating significant portions of a PR, but it's not strictly necessary. Stéfan", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00037", "post": 37, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "depth": 9, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "Is there already a 'methods' field? E.g. for prompts, \"Solve this problem as if you were <different tool>.\" Future bots may be able to debug better with the info. Bill", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "post": 38, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00036", "depth": 9, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "10 Feb 2026", "body": "For a contributor this recommendation is not easily actionable. \"I used a tool X and it gave me this code\" --- how to make sure I understand the code, this is clear yes I can do that; how am I meant to carefully check for copyright? So maybe it'd be helpful to have a link to some guide, however rough, plus some reading material. Or (am putting a maintainer hat on) maybe we want to ask the contributor to show some analysis. As in, \"this code is only a refcounting fix where the origin traces straight to CPython docs\" vs \"this code can be traced to this Stackoverflow answer\" (BTW, what's the copyright status of that?) vs \"here's three pages of a Grok-drafted legal analysis\". (I planned to stay out of this thread) Evgeni", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "post": 39, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00038", "depth": 10, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "11 Feb 2026", "body": "It's near impossible, so I suspect the only way to truly play it safe is to only provide code that cannot reasonably be copyrighted. CC-BY-SA https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public 😉 Stéfan", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00040", "post": 40, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "depth": 1, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "11 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 6, 2026 at 10:23 AM David Cournapeau via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: The following ranking of project difficulty for AI is interesting (generated by Grok): SWE-bench Verified draws from 12 popular Python repositories. These cover a nice spread of complexity levels: - Lower-to-mid complexity (easier end of the benchmark): - django (web framework) — large but very modular, lots of high-level logic - sympy (symbolic math) — math-heavy, but often localized changes - pandas (data frames) — complex internals, but many issues are API/UX or performance tweaks - scikit-learn (ML) — algorithms + estimators, moderately tricky - Mid-to-high complexity: - matplotlib (plotting) — mixes Python + some C extensions, rendering logic - sphinx (docs) — build system heavy - High complexity (harder end): - numpy — core numerical array library with heavy C/C++/Fortran extensions, low-level memory management, ufuncs, dtype system, broadcasting rules, BLAS/LAPACK integration - scipy (scientific computing) — builds on NumPy, dense linear algebra, sparse matrices, optimization, signal processing — very math-intense and performance-critical - pytest (testing framework) — meta, plugin system, very tricky edge cases around fixtures, parametrization, async NumPy (and scipy) consistently rank among the harder repositories in the SWE-bench collection for both humans and AI agents.I was surprised by pytest. PyTorch and Jax rank higher for complexity, Jax in particular is difficult for its functional programming style, no side effects. The very high complexity projects are not listed -- I only asked about NumPy.Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "post": 41, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00039", "depth": 11, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "11 Feb 2026", "body": "On Tue, 2026-02-10 at 16:18 -0800, Stefan van der Walt via NumPy- Discussion wrote: TL;DR: To \"be careful not to break copyright\" just states fact? How scary that fact is depends a bit on how the viewpoint/how likely it is agents violate copyright. If there is guidance e.g. from some large OSS foundation, I would prefer to link to that rather than try to figure it out ourselves... --- Copyright violation is a problem. But I am not sure it is a huge one for many contributions? I.e. just because they are very project specific or small. [1] However, I still think that this isn't new at all: By contributing, we already agree to licensing the code with the projects license and that means being sure we are allowed to license it that way. And while we don't make you sign a CLA (contributors license agreement) any project that has a bit of legalese around should already have more scary sentences. So yeah, the scariness of the sentence depends on the view-point, but at its core, I think it just states a fact? For myself, I don't really feel like discussing it too much without a better foundation: it seems to me that books will be written or at least some OSS foundation with more legal knowledge should make guidelines that we can use as a basis of our own (or as a basis of discussion). Maybe those already exist? Is there an OOS foundation that e.g. says: Please don't use these tools due to copyright issues (or a variation)? You can argue we should inform contributors to err on the super safe side... my gut feeling is we can't do much: Discouraging the careful ones while the non-careful ones don't read this anyway seems not super useful. We could force people to \"sign\" a CLA now if we were more worried, but do we really want that (nor do I doubt it will help a lot)? [2] FWIW, if someone contributed a non-trivial/textbook algorithm or said \"implement X for/in Y\", I think they clearly have to do due diligence. (Of course best case, the original code is licensed in a way that derived works -- with attribution -- are unproblematic.) - Sebastian [1] OK, I am not sure about things like \"fair use\" due to how small something is, that again depends a lot on where you are on the planet also... [2] My limited understanding is we don't need this because we just won't re-license our code (this is not a problem, becuase it's such a free license). I.e. there is now reason for us to \"own\" the code. But I also would be surprised if there aren't legal counsels who would say that we need one either way... (\"sign\" could just mean adding a \"signed-off by\" to the commit or even putting it more in the PR template/contributors docs.)", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "post": 42, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00041", "depth": 12, "who": "P16", "op": false, "date": "11 Feb 2026", "body": "Just a heads-up, AI Agents are now shame-posting for getting their PR closed. Just happened this morning in matplotlib. On Wed, Feb 11, 2026 at 4:34 AM Sebastian Berg <sebastian@sipsolutions.net> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "post": 43, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00042", "depth": 13, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "11 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi Sebastian, On Wed, Feb 11, 2026 at 3:49 PM Benjamin Root via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Yes, it's true that the legal and ethical problem hasn't changed, but the practical problem has changed out of all recognition. Before, there was no reasonable likelihood of copyright becoming effectively void, so there is no practical way of defending your work from copying. Now, that is a real possibility, if we do not pay close attention, very soon. The difference is two-fold. Imagine a substantial PR with say 200 lines of new code, written by AI and checked for logic by the submitter. a) There's a reasonable chance that the generated code will have pulled code to which copyright applies, without the submitter realizing that has happened (this could not have happened before), or b) It is now completely trivial, by simple inattention, or momentary breach of ethics, to rewrite copyrightable code. (This was what I was getting at with my earlier post on this). The complete triviality matters because, when it was not trivial, it was much harder to do this by accident, or by momentary breach. By analogy - consider students cheating on assignments (of which I have some experience). Before, it was possible to cheat, for example, by paying an essay mill - but it took enough effort to force the student to consider what they were doing. As a result they did it rarely, and it was not common practice. After AI, it is not only possible, but trivial to cheat - and indeed, for that reason, cheating with AI has become completely routine. For a discussion of the ineffectiveness of teaching good-practice with AI, to prevent bad practice, see: https://timrequarth.substack.com/p/why-ai-guidelines-arent-enough I think the legal aspect of this is more or less irrelevant for us - I guess the chances of anyone pursuing us for copyright breach are very small. The key issue - at least for me - is the ethical one - are we honoring the intention of the original author in requesting recognition for their work? And that is something it seems to me that we - the Scientific Python community - are qualified to comment and decide on. Paul Ivanov and I discussed this in some detail in our draft blog post, largely as a result of debate in the Scientific Python meeting in Seattle: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/blob/main/notes.md Summary: it seemed likely to us that establishing a strong norm would in fact be effective in reducing copyright-damaging use of AI. We discussed this case in the blog post (link above). There is no way to make sure that the AI will not in fact pull in other, copyrightable code, even if you asked it to port code for which you know the copyright. I think the safe - and possibly the best - way to do this - is to put a heavy requirement on contributors to either a) write the code themselves, perhaps having asked for preliminary analysis (but not substantial code drafts) from AI or b) write the code with AI, but demonstrate that they have done the research to establish the generated code does not breach copyright. Yes, that's a burden for the contributor, and yes, we may therefore lose substantial AI-generated chunks of code, but I suspect we (Scientific Python projects generally) won't suffer all that much from that restriction, in the long term - because we gain instead by having contributors with greater understanding of the code base and their own PRs. That is - as Linus Torvalds seems to imply - don't write code with AI, but use AI for analysis, maintenance and tooling. Evgeni and I discussed the constraint of pushing copyright burden to submitters over at: https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/27 Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "post": 44, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00043", "depth": 14, "who": "P17", "op": false, "date": "11 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi Matthew, That all sounds reasonable to me so far, but what are the next steps?* Is this enforceable to a significant extent? If not, in what sense could it pose a genuinely ‘heavy requirement’? Perhaps this is more enforceable? But to be honest it is still quite unclear to me how I would establish with certainty that code I’ve had generated does not breach copyright, much less code that is being presented to me by a contributor. Do you see how to realise a ‘heavy requirement’ here? I agree with the spirit of the thought that the burden (if it is to exist) needs to be shifted away from maintainers, but it’s unclear to me how we can actually shift it elsewhere. As we discussed last year, I think we have a start at a decent argument towards including a checkbox in PR templates which contributors must tick to state that they recognise the risk of copyright violation via LLM generated code and take responsibility for the code they are submitting: https://github.com/matthew-brett/sp-ai-post/issues/2#issuecomment-2935428854. Even there though, there are still multiple debatable premises. Of course, we can hardly aim for some sort of logical proof of the right way forward, but I think we need more focused attention and argument towards a specific and understandable goal if we are to be able to come to consensus on some concrete steps forward. It is to this thread's merit that the discussion has been so varied and touched on many topics, but it is also demonstrative of the problem that broad and vague back-and-forths don’t really help settle on anything concrete. Cheers, Lucas *(of course, it is not your responsibility to answer this question :) but collectively we need to ask it)", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "post": 45, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00044", "depth": 15, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "11 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Wed, Feb 11, 2026 at 11:02 PM Lucas Colley via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Just to clarify - in case it wasn't clear, what I'm floating as a proposal, would be something like this, as a message to PR authors: Please specify one of these: 1) I wrote this code myself, without looking at significant AI-generated code OR 2) The code contains AI-generated content, but the AI-generated code is sufficiently trivial that it cannot reasonably be subject to copyright OR 3) There is non-trivial AI-generated code in this PR, and I have documented my searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright. So - the burden for the reviewer is just to confirm, in case 3, that the author has documented their searches. We take the word of the contributor for the option they have chosen. Obviously, the documentation requirement of case 3 is somewhat of a burden for the contributor, and may therefore encourage them to write the code themselves, to avoid that burden. That might not be a bad thing, long term, for the project, and it seems reasonable to me as some defence against copyright violation, and a message that the project cares about such violation. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "post": 46, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "depth": 16, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "12 Feb 2026", "body": "On Wed, 2026-02-11 at 23:22 +0000, Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion wrote: While I am not particularly enthusiastic about focusing on copyright, adding such a checkbox on a PR, I would be happy with. (If it was focused on copyright, then it seems to me we would need to ask more things, like \"I used a source, but it had no code\" to \"I used a source with code but I checked it's license\". If we want this, I would prefer a single fuzzy sentence that links out to elsewhere that can also discuss pitfalls around copyright+AI.) Not sure that asking for a checkbox there will be honored, but I like the thought. First, it will increase the chance of getting the information (which I want as a reviewer). Second, my unfortunate feeling is that we'll get more aggressively/less friendly about closing PRs and that is a shame, and having the checkbox makes that pat a bit easier on us and maybe also more transparent to the user that we are struggling with this (the worry of course is closing a genuine human PR by accident). I think I largely understand the concerns around copyright and maybe I am a bit not careful/understanding enough by not being overly worried?... But to my very personal feeling the product of how much I feel we should worry and how much I feel that stressing issues will help us as a project/open source just doesn't make me enthusiastic about being aggressively to pointing it out these possible issues. There are many things to discuss around this. What does eroding copyright here mean for us as a project, for open source (GPL?), for open but not free code, for code that is leaked but sold? How will enforcement of actual copyright issues plays out in practice? I just don't think this is the venue for settling these questions [1] and I would need a lot more clarity to even form a strong opinion that I would be willing to announce to the world with the weight of NumPy behind it. - Sebastian [1] And yeah, this is laziness, because I feel to really settle it for even myself, I may have to spend weeks reading up and thinking about it.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "post": 47, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00046", "depth": 17, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Thu, Feb 12, 2026 at 7:02 AM Sebastian Berg <sebastian@sipsolutions.net> wrote: I do understand that this is not the kind of issue that many of us enjoy discussing, but it seems to me that it is: a) of central importance to the future of open-source, and b) very urgent, and c) fairly straightforward. To focus the discussion - the only thing of interest to us here, is the acceptability or otherwise of large chunks of code generated by AI. I doubt that anyone has strong objections to AI for code review or code analysis. For the central importance, imagine a world where copyright has become irrelevant. There are ways we could approach this issue, where that is a likely outcome. We might have different views on whether that is acceptable, but at very least, it will be a very major change, with unpredictable consequences. We are used to open-source copyright as it exists. If we don't consciously address this now, or very soon, we'll have another world, with consequences that are difficult to predict. Of course, some of us don't care all that much about our own copyright, but bear in mind, that by choosing not to defend it, we take away the ability of others to defend theirs. Specifically, if we choose to accept large AI-generated PRs, the copyright that will be violated is not ours, but that of others. Do we claim that right, to void the copyright of our fellow authors? Returning to the central question - of large AI-generated PRs. It seems to me this is not a week of work to analyze. I don't think there's any controversy that making no effort to control copyright will, over the medium term, make copyright very difficult to honor. As I said before, the legal issues of enforcement are difficult, but not relevant to us, because we are considering our own ethics in observing copyright, and that will be a superset of the legal constraints. It would be an error to defer to legal arguments for an ethical question, if only because the legal arguments are sufficiently complicated that we'd likely have lost the ability to enforce copyright before they are resolved. And, as I say, I think the legal arguments - on enforcement - are more or less irrelevant to our ethical decisions on copyright. So, accepting large AI-generated PRs would be a significant threat to copyright - what do we get in return? Ralf pointed out one benefit - that we are not seen to disapprove of the chosen workflows of our fellow developers. I think this is a weak argument. It seems to me perfectly reasonable to point out that contributing to the code-base has some constraints, and copyright is one of them, and that AI-generated code runs the risk of violating copyright. The second potential benefit is that, by accepting large AI-generated PRs, we will gain greatly in code coverage and quality, and that this is a benefit great enough that it is worth paying the price in terms of copyright. First - we have been prepared to pay a high price for observing copyright in the past - there are many GPL algorithms that we could have copied, to our benefit, but did not. Second, it seems to me we can wait on this. It is not yet clear that we would gain significantly, compared to our traditional requirement that people write their own code. When the gains are still unclear, the cost in terms of voiding copyright is too high. Lastly - I was proposing a compromise - that we (Scientific Python projects) do not forbid AI-generated PRs, but place an extra burden on contributors to research any possible copyright violations. That seems like a reasonable compromise to me. What do you think? Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "post": 48, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "depth": 18, "who": "P16", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "The risk of copyright violation isn't just with GPL'ed code in the training set, but also potentially from privately held code that was accidentally leaked into a training set. Imagine if MathWorks or ESRI discover their code in our repos and decide to sue. The LLM has access to an unprecedented dataset of code that a human could never have and we can't ever be sure there isn't leaked code in it.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00049", "post": 49, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00048", "depth": 19, "who": "P18", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, I think it would be prudent to be more explicit with regards to the threat of copyright violation. It is almost certainly true that GPL code has been part of the training set and it is very possible that even private code has found its way in there. But that is also true for human contributors. After all, am I, having read Numerical Recipes in the 90s, ineligible to implement Runge-Kutta methods in open source? Surely not. Can an employee of a company write \"from scratch\" in open source today a method they (co-)developed at work yesterday? Also not. A straight-up copy of a large enough section of code is problematic, but less likely than an amalgamation of existing code adapted to the Numpy codebase already today and imho moreso in the future. As such, Matthew's proposed compromise sounds reasonable to me, and I would add as guidance for reviewers that PRs adding large chunks of self-contained code, i.e. entirely new big files or hundreds of lines of consecutive code with no relation to existing Numpy code, deserve a higher level of scrutiny for this specific risk. Cheers Klaus On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 1:44 PM Benjamin Root via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "post": 50, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "depth": 18, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "Also I'd like to be on record with the unpleasant part out loud. I have been in many discussions also at work and in OSS circles so I have quite a bit of debate ammo accumulated from both sides. Let me jump into it without the fluff to save space; Currently, LLMs are getting really good at what they are tasked to do. If you put in the work (just like you would when you are the one writing the code), the output is quite acceptable and I feel like I'm reviewing somebody else's Pull request. Fix \"this\" part, change \"that\" part and done. If folks can't use these tools, it's a \"they\" problem. I just used it to translate entire LAPACK to C11 (why, mostly for the lolz, don't ask, it's a disease), ported all the tests and passing, now polishing it up. I mean look at this silly thing [image: image.png] No way in hell, I'd type this much code myself. And it is a 1-to-1 mechanical translation, no creativity involved except hacking into PyData theme because I always wanted to tweak it. Now who owns the copyright; Dennis Ritchie or LAPACK folks, or is it the entire C codebase of the world that trained this machine to write this mechanical code, or is it me who paid for it and worked with it etc.? The source of the algorithm is BSD3, would you be using this if this was available in BSD3 (I mean it will be obviously very soon). As a comparison, the entire SciPy Fortran codebase, ~85,000 SLOC, took me 2 years and 7 months to translate manually. Entire LAPACK codebase 300,000 SLOC (just the functions) and including the testing, documentation etc. took me exactly 1 month and 19 days (Claude Pro something MAX level subscription with ~200€ per month from my own pocket). The agent still fails spectacularly if you let it run free, but I do put in the work to do a proper code review, tweak rules, then force it to read the rules periodically, (and most importantly, I know what I am looking at) so this went fairly well. It still took insane amount of time to bring the agent back on track. force explicit testing, Not to use C++ practices on C code so on. At this point, I can confirm that \"Agents can do this much but they cannot do that much\" is rapidly becoming a \"God of the Gaps\" argument with every new version release LLMs chasing a receding horizon, not towards intelligence, but precision at parsing and following orders. However, in my opinion, our dilemma is not a whether their output is potentially GPL'd/copyrighted code or not. Every bit of output of these tools is stolen by being trained on copyrighted data. For the folks who did not see it, there is a screenshot of VS Code offering me a comment at the beginning of the file from a company that does not apparently have any public repositories https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/a-policy-on-generative-ai-assisted-contributions/1702/5 Therefore, we are, in fact, trying to guess, whether it looks like a copyrighted code after the fact, ignoring where the code is pulled from. These companies pretty much stole everything; music, science articles, code (not just GPLd code, but private repositories), this, that, everything. Their practices were/are seriously unethical. It is not a political statement but facts. However, it seems like they are getting away with it, incredibly, even after they admitted it multiple times all the way at the CEO level (in particular, recently, SUNO CEO is pretty bullish, even defending why this stealing is fair use while individuals are rapidly being prosecuted for the same actions, not to mention Sci-Hub). And some of us are working for these companies or working for in the secondary circles. Funnily enough, we are tasked with this mordant task of trying to come up with a stance on LLM usage. I claim that we should not be spending too much time on the epistemological aspects of LLM usage. I can't see any way other than being utilitarian about it. Because PRs keep coming and maintainers are also using it. So when stuck between a rock and a hardware, I think we should be admitting these properly and then choose a path knowingly fully aware that we might be making a mistake. Being open about the fact that we are going blind into this is probably make more sense instead of some serious sounding untested-unvetted legal text and checkboxes. Because really nobody knows when we will correct course, if ever. So we can 1- \"Stallman\" it, with \"no AI allowed\" stance, while having absolutely no way of knowing how the code is generated. So it is a stance based on principles. I don't have a problem with it, and can accept it. It is a viable and respectable choice. The downside is we will be forcing people to lie. Because they will use it and we will not notice it until it is very late. 2- or find a sentence that is pragmatic enough; something like \"Even if you used LLMs, you should be able to explain the changes yourself. LLM based PRs are held to heightened levels of scrutiny and lower levels of patience\" or something offered in this thread. I can also accept this, it is also a viable option. The downside is that it will make us more hostile, as Sebastian mentioned, and paranoid. Occasionally, it will make us accuse innocent folks for using LLMs. Once we can choose this, then we can add agent markdowns, boilerplate responses and other details. But it seems like we got stuck at this choice level in our last attempts for a policy alignment. I would be much happier if we can be a bit more explicit and forthcoming about what we are doing and not make it an in vitro Open Source problem. We don't need to use strong words like stealing etc. obviously since there is no legal basis for it. But we all know what happened so there are much softer versions of saying the same thing. I just did not spend the time to make these proper ala Pascal, and it's my lack of manners leaking out though I strongly believe that they stole everything. I am fully aware that this might not be everyone's take (or anyone for that matter), so please take it as a rather brazen take though I hope the message gets across. Very weird times indeed. ilhan On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 12:03 PM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "post": 51, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "depth": 19, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi Ilhan, On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 2:15 PM Ilhan Polat via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I'm not sure what you are saying there - could you clarify? I just wanted to clarify that I don't think the argument is about what agents can and cannot do. I think everyone believes they can be very useful. I should also say that your experience is very useful for the discussion - but it is somewhat specialized. I can well see that the AI agent could be a huge boon for this sort of semi-mechanical task, but there aren't many such tasks in the code that I'm working on. And - to return to my suggestion - I would argue here that your task, as the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code very carefully, comparing to the original, and I am confident that the translation is a faithful language to language translation, from the original BSD code, and there is no significant injection of other code that may be subject to copyright. The closest example I could find was X, but a quick search for terms Y and Z found no plausible copyrighted origins.\" Right - and one conclusion we could draw is - OK, if (some idea of) everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. There may be such a legal text - but I don't think that's what I was proposing. Again, this isn't about enforcement - it's about ethics - as it always has been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. It really doesn't seem sensible to choose a policy that is obviously dangerous for copyright, and wait until it becomes obvious that we have damaged copyright. Rather it seems more sensible to choose an option that is less dangerous for copyright, and wait to see, as the tools develop, whether we need to re-evaluate. It really doesn't seem likely to me that the policy would stay in place long after it was causing the project harm. I just don't think this is true - I strongly suspect that people who are attracted to open-source, and the open-source community, will not generally lie about how they made their contributions - any more than we have seen attempts to put GPL code into our BSD codebases. Bear in mind that the \"until it is very late\" problem is the one that will happen much more quickly with a more permissive policy. Could you comment on the option that I was proposing - which is that anyone generating code with AI should justify the copyright risk, with relevant research as necessary? I didn't use those words - but in any case - as I've said several times, in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "post": 52, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00050", "depth": 19, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 7:08 AM Ilhan Polat via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I suspect there will be changes in the understanding/use of \"copyright.\" What they will be, I don't know, but copyright itself is fairly recent. It is also the case that thirty years ago you could buy cheap, unlicensed versions of most software in Hong Kong, and copyrighted texts have been produced in cheap versions in some parts of the world, so these sorts of problems are not a completely new experience. Back in the late 1800s to early 1900s, there were patent fights in the Federal Courts involving electric lights, telephones, and aviation. But wartime need prevailed: \"The disputes contributed to a 1917 government-brokered patent pool during WWI to end litigation and support aircraft production.\" Copyright was also suspended for German texts in WWII, I have some republished works on my shelves. The use of AI will soon become a national interest, if it isn't already. We are small players in a much bigger event. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "post": 53, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00052", "depth": 20, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 5:03 PM Charles R Harris via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Yes, that's right. The way I've heard it discussed, by David Sacks, Trumps \"AI and crypto tsar\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sacks) is roughly that if we (the USA) don't make it possible for AI models to digest and possibly reproduce copyright material, the Chinese will, and then the USA will lose the \"AI race\", which would be bad. So it might well be that the current administration tries to undermine copyright, for that reason. And I suppose they will do that by making copyright hard to enforce legally. But that doesn't require us to void copyright - as I keep saying - it's an ethical issue more than a legal one. We can still choose to respect the wishes of the author, even if (for example) the USA has made it impossible to enforce those wishes legally. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00054", "post": 54, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00051", "depth": 20, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "I don't have any objections. Like I said, not everybody or anybody will agree wtih me on this. But a few clarifications, please feel free to ignore them. I really don't seek a debate in a mailing list but important for my argument above, in case I did not make it clear. the PR author, is to say \"I went through the ported code [...] origins.\" Take my work; I would be lying. I can't even convince myself that I looked at every line of code. Plus, that kind of homework is impossible to sign out for me. How can I even know where to look if it is a partial code. We are talking about Pull Requests that are typically changing 1 or 2 hypothetical tiles from the entire floor. I asked an LLM that gave me some result. Meh, good enough, git push. If you ask me \"where did you get the idea of this function decorator\" or \"what made you do this double pointer trick\"; you will get an answer which will be a white lie. I think you are underestimating how much work that sentence carries. The easier option is clicking \"I did not use AI\". everyone is doing it, we should be doing it too. But I'm sure you'd agree that's not a very convincing argument. That is exactly my argument, and much to my regret I think this is the only honest answer. None of us proposed to use LLMs, they barged in on us. Shifting the potential blame to the contributor while the code is 100% coming from copyright infringing tool is not convincing either. been. We stated that we didn't accept GPL code, or code derived from GPL, and we took our contributors word that they had taken our request seriously. I don't think this is as ethical as you make it sound like. You are removing the actual very hostile anti-copyright tool which is the main perpetrator. Then we are hoping that the users will be holding it right, and stealing maybe only a little but not too much to be disrespectful. In my opinion, there is no ethical escape hatch that we can use without participating in the act. The ethical thing here is to be transparent about our desperation that we don't know what we are taking in and tread carefully. --- For the being late part I can in fact bring you bunch of code and we can do a blindfold experiment, you look at the code and tell me which code is GPL or from Numerical Recipes. If you can't detect it, and I click all the checkboxes and then congrats you license laundered copyrighted code by pushing it into NumPy/SciPy/scikit-learn... Then authors of the original code somehow recognize a unique trick, come back, point at the code and say, \"Yo, this is theft\". That is what I mean by it is already too late. I get your point that if we don't do anything then it is even worse. They will bring anything which is true. But your belief in folks' ability to distinguish different licenses if it is coming from an LLM is higher than mine. in several places, the legal argument is more or less irrelevant to us - our question is whether we are honoring the spirit of the copyrights put on other people's code, not whether they could, with sufficient resources, successfully sue us for infringement. And I have endless respect for you and all who strive for this. I want to believe that I am in your camp, or at least trying to be. My point is, if this is done in spirit, let's make it obvious that we are powerless in case a mistake is made OR no LLMs. Both are fine. I do agree with your text, checkbox and other procedures you proposed and would be willing to use it. I just can't see its practical function, even ethically, in case a conflict arises or a fame-seeking contributor starts pulling in copyrighted code. Because unlike before, the act of stealing is now done within the LLM that has, for now, infinite impunity. On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 5:22 PM Matthew Brett <matthew.brett@gmail.com> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "post": 55, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00053", "depth": 21, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 11:16 AM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Copyright has been adjusted many times, most recently for such things as photocopiers and home recording. My guess is that a combination of pooling and fair use will be the solution, possibly with an opt-out option. The current situation in the US is that AI regulation has been moved from the states to the federal government. Code should be free! OK, it's free. Wait, what! No, not like that. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "post": 56, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00055", "depth": 22, "who": "P19", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi All, I think the discussion of ethics is important, but somewhat abstract and difficult to act on. Personally, I think the gravest danger is maintainer burn-out. The matplotlib \"shame posting\" referred to by Matthew earlier is a quite worrisome -- for those who haven't seen it, see https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31132 https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/pull/31138 I'd estimate that those particular AI-generated PRs have led to a direct loss of at least a day of maintainer time (spread over multiple maintainers), plus, if they are at all like me, a further indirect loss of hours to days of being upset and irritated. Over at astropy, we currently see a smaller effect of AI bots: we are being spammed by two \"developers\" whose github pages state \"currently working on upskilling myself in the field of AI\" (both identical phrasing). Even just asking to be assigned in lots of issues is annoying, as it leads to e-mails in my inbox for any issue I happened to comment on. Also, I've been misled into reviewing/commenting on one PR, spending more time than it would have been to fix things myself, only to find out that it was generated by a bot, not a person who might become a valuable contributor. It left me feeling violated, and I hate feeling forced to a mode where I only review PRs from people/handles I know, and avoid getting notified except when tagged (though a few days ago, I got tens of e-mails from an account that seems to be replaying astropy PRs, yielding pings for any with @mhvk in them). Anyway, to me the most pressing question is to devise some sort of policy that stops people or their bots with no true interest in making real contributions. Sadly, I do not have a good suggestion. But perhaps a start is to have some kind of tick box for any new contributor that asks to state that one is a human and asks to introduce oneself, how one found the problem, and to describe how the PR was created and, e.g., confirm that it complies with the AI policy (including copyright, etc.). Or, more strongly, require explicit permission to create PRs (following a similar questionnaire). A non-standard API might help... Of course, neither of these suggestions will not guard against blatant liers, but perhaps they at least reduce the problem. All the best, Marten", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00057", "post": 57, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "depth": 23, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 1:44 PM Marten van Kerkwijk via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I think NumPy has seen one of those. Reminds me of PRs from India when the universities begin classes :) I'm thinking we need to start banning repeat offenders. Long term, we will probably need AI help, there are already developments in that direction. Another possibility being explored by GitHub is more granular permissions. This would likely be along the lines of reviewed committers receiving PR/comment privileges. New committers get reviewed and maybe blocked. It would be something like we have for the mailing list. Every morning I check held posts to the mailing list. It is a chore, but doesn't take much time. One problem might be handling appeals. Maybe something like three strikes and you are out. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00058", "post": 58, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00056", "depth": 23, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 3:46 PM Marten van Kerkwijk via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: The Ghostty project has released tooling for that kind of thing: https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "post": 59, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00045", "depth": 16, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "13 Feb 2026", "body": "On Wed, Feb 11, 2026 at 6:26 PM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: For Case 3, I would love to see an example of the search that you would accept. If you could take a recent PR (human or AI, doesn't really matter for this purpose), and show the search that would satisfy you, that would go a long way towards clarifying what you are asking for here. We'd need a worked example or two before adopting this policy because if I don't know what you are asking for, no new contributor will, either.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "post": 60, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "depth": 17, "who": "P20", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, 13 Feb 2026 at 23:51, Robert Kern via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Here is an example right now in NumPy. Apparently someone is deep diving into performance edge cases. They (most likely with the help of ai or totally by ai) submitted a three line PR https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "post": 61, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "depth": 18, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, 2026-02-14 at 08:35 +0200, matti picus via NumPy-Discussion wrote: I suppose my opinion for now is: If you as a maintainer care/want to. And that part I would be happy to put into a policy if need be (which can/should mention more things!). The below got much longer... need to read: What Marten said ;). In practice the issue I have with this type of PR isn't much about copyright or that it is possible that almost all the work was using an AI. (Not because copyright it isn't an issue, I just don't think there were PRs of a kind where I would be seriously worried about it.) It is really about the social dynamic and if a policy can help with that, I am all for it. Before, we had at least one of three intrinsically motivating reasons to look at a PR/issue: * We knew the submitter cares about seeing the feature (i.e. the result not for contributions sake). * It is just for contributions sake, but we are investing in community. I.e. we like helping! * Or I happen to care about it myself. (That could be scratching my my own itch or thinking it is important for the project.) With the old waves of PRs from students, hacktoberfest, ... you pick one. We had the community investment/interaction point applying in some form and adding some motivation. With the current wave I think an issue is that more often it leaves the maintainer without _any_ of those motivational points applying -- I am not even sure that the wave is bigger yet (but it is probably more a swelling). This actually started with issues, I think? My feeling is we have more tiny issues (CuPy is a better example than NumPy here). Issues that seem like some tool found them. They are often long and verbose and at the end maybe a PR even gets merged, but at the end I can't help but think: Well, we just fixed an issue that possibly zero people in the world care about seeing fixed! Don't get me wrong, they are real issues and PRs! I like having extra context for motivation [1], and I think we may need to manage them more (and that may be putting up a policy to discourage or point to when closing). - Sebastian [1] Also if a human creates an issue, I think it is nice to have the note on \"this crashed my hour long job\". vs. \"my funny advent of code solution started failing\" (a real regression btw. that I closed because it was a caused by a fix.)", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "post": 62, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00047", "depth": 18, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 13, 2026 at 12:02 PM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Please, read and reason more carefully. That was right below a principle \"honor copyright\". One does not invalidate or contradict the other. You have a good point in your arguments about copyright somewhere, but you're making it very poorly, verbosely, and with too much confidence when using words like \"obviously\". It's easy to come up again with examples for why a \"large AI-generated PR\" isn't copyrightable. E.g., filling holes in test coverage, say for nan's, empty arrays, or noncontiguous arrays. Such an effort involves tedious boilerplate tests with no copyrightable content, and we'd happily outsource that to a tool, and may be thousands of lines of code. For large PRs with intellectually stimulating and copyrightable code, there is also a gray zone where the human does most of the thinking, outlines the solution while stubbing out a lot of details, and then lets a tool fill in the details. That might all be fine too - it depends. The thing to be done here is to find understandable and pragmatic wording for a policy that discourages and lets us reject the undesirable usage of AI tools, while not hindering valid usage. Being overly broad and moralizing with inactionable wording isn't helpful. Cheers, Ralf", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00063", "post": 63, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00061", "depth": 19, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi Sebastian, On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:00 AM Sebastian Berg <sebastian@sipsolutions.net> wrote: I could not agree more - and Stefan's blog post makes a similar - and very good - argument, Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "post": 64, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00062", "depth": 19, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi Ralf, I think you're playing the ball and not the man, and apart from that being unpleasant for me, it's bad for the discussion. If we are not careful, people will be discouraged from posting for fear of personal attack. That said - I do apologise for using \"obviously\" - thanks for pointing that out - it was rude of me, and I will try to be more careful. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:05 AM Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I don't know why you would think that I hadn't understood that there were nuanced arguments to be made for the acceptability of any particular piece of AI-generated code. Did you not read my reply to Ilhan, for example? I think you're confusing the statement of ethical principles, with being overly broad and inactionable. I don't know what \"moralizing\" means, but I'm assuming that it can't reasonably be applied to the statement \"We have an ethical responsibility to uphold copyright\". If we accept that statement, then we can have specific discussions about what actions we need to take, and that's what I was doing - you must have seen the specific proposal that I made. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00065", "post": 65, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00064", "depth": 20, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, 2026-02-14 at 10:10 +0000, Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion wrote: Yes, I think we have seen it (and while I might want to tone it down a bit, I think it is reasonable). But I'll admit that I also read it with a such a focus on the principle that it felt hard to make the transition to discussing the nuance. And part of that is probably that we agree that there is a potential legal/moral minefield here and the disagreement we have isn't that we shouldn't discuss a map, it's that we don't think a scary sign is useful. The other thing is that I think that I/we read it as a focus on these fundamental problems and that makes me feel it is a bit about us making a basically political/moral statement at large. I don't want to do that. In part because to me the moral/legal implication is much fuzzier than it seems at first (as Chuck mentioned the scope and duration of copyright changed a lot in the past! Presumably both legally and morally.). But also, I am not even sure that issues around AI and copyright are politically/morally what we as open source should emphasize. And even if they are, I would not be sure what direction I think we should throw our weight. - Sebastian", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00066", "post": 66, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00060", "depth": 18, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30810 to speed up np.array_equal. Now the same author submitted a much bigger PR to speed up np.isin https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828. Is the work the product of ai? Yes, but the author claims to have verified the code. Is the author ai or not? Should we proceed with the PR? Matti The interactions in the 2nd PR have the flat agreeableness of an LLM. mrdope, are you hear and clear? :-) Bill", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00067", "post": 67, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00034", "depth": 10, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, This example has proved more useful than I had thought. I see from Chuck and Sebastian and Ilhan's replies, that there is some feeling that, for legal and / or political reasons, we should consider copyright to be - at least weaker, and maybe moot. Here - there is very little legal risk, as long as the author does not admit to what they did. So - Chuck, Sebastian, Ilhan - what do you think? Is this use acceptable? And if not, why not? Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00068", "post": 68, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00067", "depth": 11, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 6:53 AM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Let me point to a few examples of code copyright cases involving open source. - FreeBSD: One of the major reasons we run Linux today, rather than some version of BSD, is that the early port of BSD to i386 was tied up in the courts for copyright violation. I recall the initial announcement. The case ran for years. - Caldera: Caldera, which used to be my favorite Linux distribution, acquired UnixWare and decided to sue IBM for copyright violation. They pointed to small code snippets. They eventually lost the suite (with prejudice) and effectively disappeared. But they could have derailed Linux. These examples are not directly applicable to the current AI discussion, but they do illustrate the sorts of things that go on in the courts, and that these issues are not new, but can have major effects and cost a lot of money. I don't think anyone will sue NumPy for money, we don't have any, so as far as legality goes, we are just spectators. Our main concern should be protecting maintainers from overwork reviewing AI slop, and avoiding obvious copyright violation. Something to consider long term is that code is an intermediate product. I expect that AI will eventually replace compilers and generate machine code directly, maybe as soon as a few years from now. Who can review that? At that point APIs and standards will become more important than code. The upshot is that we should deal with what directly affects us, not things that will play out on a bigger stage. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00069", "post": 69, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00068", "depth": 12, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 4:01 PM Charles R Harris <charlesr.harris@gmail.com> wrote: I wasn't sure, from this reply, what your answer was to the question : Is this use acceptable? And if not, why not? Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00070", "post": 70, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00069", "depth": 13, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 9:04 AM Matthew Brett <matthew.brett@gmail.com> wrote: I am not going to play that game. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00071", "post": 71, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00070", "depth": 14, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 4:11 PM Charles R Harris <charlesr.harris@gmail.com> wrote: The point of the example is to ask whether you think there's any ethical responsibility to honor copyright. Robert thought yes, so do I. Is there any sense in which this is a trick question? Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00072", "post": 72, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00071", "depth": 15, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "My answer to that is yes currently it is unfortunately OK. For it to be not OK, the tool should have a license aware setting that when you flip it, you don't get copyrighted answer and not to be trained on copyrighted code. And the user consciously uses the steal mode. Though yours is a bit dramatic, this is what happens when you query anything. So not sure where we disagree. You are making my case. And this is my entire point that these machines like a sundae machine take all sources (copyrighted/private or not) in and give you an amalgam of intellectual property. What I am trying to emphasize is that, we are trying to free the tool and hold the contributor accountable. There are valid use cases, there are not valid use cases but in all cases LLM did the stealing. Just because you asked it nicely or in a ill-intentioned fashion does not change anything. We should not fool ourselves by the language we are getting results out of these stochastic parrots. I am not trying to devalue the copyright notion. I am trying to emphasize that these guardrails we are putting up are doing nothing in terms of copyright other than a bit of feel-good. On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 5:15 PM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00073", "post": 73, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00059", "depth": 17, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, Yes, that's a reasonable request. But how do you think I should proceed? Make an issue on Numpy, and start drafting? Start another email thread? Or a Discourse / Scientific Python thread? Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00074", "post": 74, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00072", "depth": 16, "who": "P19", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "TL;DR: I suggest we move the discussion to how we implement a kind of web of trust that has to be joined before one can post PRs. I think the example Matthew posted of converted GPL R to BSD python is obviously not OK, and I'm fine with stating explicitly that we don't want PRs like that. But I agree with others that any policy we adopt would do (next to) nothing against bad intent, and that, more generally, this is not something numpy can solve. To me, it does not seem to address the essence of the problem. Rather, to me the discussion has clarified that the essence is *trust*. Trust that someone does not knowlingly break copyright, and trust that they are genuinely interested in contributing and thus have done the work to ensure maintainer time is well spent. I would suggest we take seriously implementing something like the vouching system Robert pointed to, https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch I include the \"why\" from its README below [1], as I find it well put. (There may be something better, which perhaps relies on an existing \"web of trust\", like PGP keys. But let's decide first whether we want to do this route.) I think a big benefit of separating admission of people from submitting PRs is that as a maintainer I do not have to be suspicious about intent; as Sebastian noted, that removes the joy there can be in review (and as I wrote before, is stopping me from reviewing PRs from accounts I do not recognize). I'm encouraged by Chuck thinking a vouching scheme would not be enormously painful. We do obviously need to think how we actually go about vouching for a new contributor... I should add that I passed on the vouch link to our discussion over at astropy, https://github.com/astropy/astropy-project/issues/509, where there was support for implementing (something like) it, with the suggestion that we \"share a network with scipy+numpy to minimize the barrier to contributors we already trust and perhaps to share labor.\" It was also noted that this system is similar to what is in place for arXiv, which (so far) has worked reasonably well. Related (but not directly on-topic, sorry!), in astronomy more generally there has just been a thoughtful white paper posted by David Hogg on the use of AI in astronomy: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.10181. He makes interesting points about why we do astronomy in the first place (stating it is not just to get answers, in which case becoming a hedge fund manager and hiring others to do the work would be more efficient... As in fact Simons did). But also notes how for astropy (and numpy) it is essential that we can trust that those packages do the right thing, and that that trust is based on trusting those that constructed them. All the best, Marten [1] The \"why\" from https://github.com/mitchellh/vouch Open source has always worked on a system of trust and verify. Historically, the effort required to understand a codebase, implement a change, and submit that change for review was high enough that it naturally filtered out many low quality contributions from unqualified people. For over 20 years of my life, this was enough for my projects as well as enough for most others. Unfortunately, the landscape has changed particularly with the advent of AI tools that allow people to trivially create plausible-looking but extremely low-quality contributions with little to no true understanding. Contributors can no longer be trusted based on the minimal barrier to entry to simply submit a change. But, open source still works on trust! And every project has a definite group of trusted individuals (maintainers) and a larger group of probably trusted individuals (active members of the community in any form). So, let's move to an explicit trust model where trusted individuals can vouch for others, and those vouched individuals can then contribute. Ilhan Polat via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> writes:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "post": 75, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00073", "depth": 18, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sat, Feb 14, 2026 at 12:17 PM Matthew Brett <matthew.brett@gmail.com> wrote: Just here should be fine. Take an existing PR that has copyrightable content (e.g. an entire new function or three, each more than ~10 lines, not just many one-line updates scattered around; the most interesting ones would be those that implement a known algorithm). Do the code search that would satisfy you. Write out here what you would want a PR author to provide.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "post": 76, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "depth": 19, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "14 Feb 2026", "body": "Please let me honk in from philosophy/science/psychology to reiterate, \"The prompts, Boss, the prompts!\" Don't you think that some sample at least would add some conviction that a human was involved? Case in point is mrdope, who I imagine could be a person talking through an LLM out of shyness, overproductivity syndrome, or as an experiment. Per my comment: Bill", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "post": 77, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "depth": 1, "who": "P21", "op": false, "date": "15 Feb 2026", "body": "Hey everyone, Sorry to be jumping in so late on this important thread. And thanks to everyone for the thoughtful discussion. Numpy is such a visible and important project, I'm sure that what's decided here will have massive downstream consequences for the rest of the tech world. I'm happy to see that there seems to be an emerging consensus of “principles over policing”: responsibility, understanding, transparency. One way I think we can make this more concrete by framing it around *where* in the project a contribution lands, and what the “blast radius” looks like, in both space and time, if we later need to debug, rewrite, or (worst case) roll back code for legal/regulatory reasons. A few thoughts / straw man of an \"AI contribution policy\": (1) Defining “Zones” of the project We can explicitly acknowledge that different areas of the codebase that have different tolerance levels for AI-assisted generation. \"Zone\" doesn’t have to mean a single folder. It can be: * a directory tree (e.g., `numpy/core`, `numpy/linalg`, `doc/`, `tools/`, `benchmarks/`), * part of the import namespace (public API, internal helpers), * or a semantic area (core algorithms, ABI-sensitive paths, numerical stability-critical routines, tutorial content, CI glue, etc.). For example: * Inner ring (high scrutiny): core algorithms, numerics, ABI/API-critical code paths, anything performance-critical or subtle correctness-wise. * Middle ring (moderate): tests, refactors, coverage expansion, internal tooling, build/CI scripts. * Outer ring (low): examples, tutorials, onboarding docs, “glue” that’s easy to replace, small utilities, beginner-facing content. This is explicitly saying that the question of AI isn't a binary choice, nor e.g. \"AI is forbidden in the core\". Rather, the closer you get to code with high blast radius, the more we should demand human legibility, reviewable provenance, and high confidence in correctness and licensing posture. (2) \"Blast radius\" When someone asks “should we accept AI-generated code here?”, I think they have an implicit model about \"blast radius\" already. We can render that model explicit with a few dimensions: * Complexity: Is this code easy to reason about? Does it involve numerical stability, tricky invariants, edge-case handling, low-level memory behavior, or algorithmic subtlety? * Impact & dependency surface: How many downstream things depend on this? Is it part of public API? Widely imported? Affects core array semantics? If it changes, do we risk broad downstream breakage? * Stability & expected lifespan: Is this an area that tends to be stable for years (core numerics), or something we expect to churn (docs examples, CI harnesses)? The longer something is expected to persist, the higher the cost of “oops”. * Rollback/Replacement cost: If we had to remove it quickly, how painful would that be? How entangled is it with other code? How hard is it to recreate by hand? * Legibility / testability: Can we test it robustly? Can we write property tests? Are there known oracles? Is it feasible to get strong confidence quickly? (3) Transparency: make it concrete, not vague I think “please be transparent” is right. At a minimum, I think we need something like a lightweight attestation or affirmation from the contributor — not a legal affidavit, not a license, not an attempt to police workflow — but a structured statement that sets reviewer expectations and creates an audit trail. Something along these lines (details can be bikeshedded later): **AI Use Attestation (for PR description template / checkboxes):** * Did you use an AI tool to generate or substantially modify code in this PR? (yes/no) * If yes: * which tool/model (and ideally version/date — these change fast), * what parts of the PR were AI-assisted (core logic vs tests vs docs vs refactors), * confirm: “I understand this code, I can explain it, and I’m responsible for it.” Then scale the requested detail by zone / blast radius: * Outer/middle ring: model/tool + high-level description is probably enough. * Inner ring / high blast radius: I’d like us to consider asking for more: * the prompts (or at least the key prompts) used to generate the logic, * any intermediate artifacts that help future maintainers understand how we got here (e.g., the “why” behind design choices, variants considered, constraints given to the model), * and ideally a short human-written explanation of the algorithm and invariants (which is good practice regardless of AI). I can appreciate that this seems burdensome for small PRs fixing a tiny thing. But the above is just a straw-man and perhaps there are some nice simplifications we can engineer to make this as lightweight a part of the workflow as possible. (Perhaps even a stub Numpy_ai_contribution_guide.md that gives the code-gen LLM a template to fill out and include in the PR?) The analogy I have in mind: treat AI like a nondeterministic *semantic compiler*. With a normal compiler, we keep intermediate info when we care: flags, versions, debug symbols, build logs. For high blast radius code, the prompts and intermediate reasoning are effectively that metadata. Even if we don’t store everything in-repo, capturing it in the PR discussion is valuable. This is literally like preserving the seed, when we have to check in the output of an RNG. (4) Why keep prompts / artifacts? (forward-looking CI idea) One reason I care about preserving the trail: I can imagine a future “AI regression / reproducibility” check. Say it’s 6–12 months from now and AI coding tools are even stronger. If we have prompts and model versions recorded for high-blast-radius contributions, we could run a periodic (maybe opt-in) workflow that: * replays historical prompts against a current/known model environment, * compares the generated output structurally (or semantically) to what we merged, * and validates that the merged implementation still matches expected numerical behavior (tests + known benchmarks). Even if we never automate this, having the trace helps humans debug: “what constraints were assumed?” “what source did it mirror?” “what was the intended invariant?” (5) Copyright / legality This is the part where I think a little conservatism is justified. The legal landscape around training data, derived works, and obligations around GPL/AGPL/LGPL is still evolving across jurisdictions. NumPy is permissively licensed, but that doesn’t automatically insulate us from the provenance question if generated code ends up looking like something from a copyleft codebase. I can tell you for a fact that corporate legal compliance folks will not hesitate to use the ban-hammer if, after some future court case, it's deemed that codebases like Numpy's are \"tainted\" and require roll-back. I’m not proposing we block AI tools categorically (that’s neither realistic nor enforceable). But I do think it’s reasonable to say: * in high blast radius zones, contributors should prefer tools with clearer provenance and license posture, and we should be willing to ask for extra diligence (explanations, tests, and/or avoiding “model wrote the entire algorithm” submissions); * in low blast radius zones, the risk/cost trade is different, and we can be more permissive. I also think we should explicitly acknowledge that this policy may evolve as jurisprudence and tooling clarity improves. As an additional note, over the last couple of years I have been actively working on a new \"AI Rights\" license & tech infrastructure to help give explicit attestation for authors of all copyrighted works, along the lines of CC Signals[1] or IETF AI Preferences[2]. I'm actually sending this from the AI Summit in Delhi where, as part of AI Commons, I'm convening allies from Creative Commons, Wikimedia, Internet Archive, Common Crawl, and others to align on shared vision and workstreams. Those who are interested in my work on this can see the videos at links [3][4][5]. If you're to just watch one, I'd recommend [4] then [5], or just [5]. I'm happy to chat in depth with any/all of you about these topics, but I want to be sensitive about not hijacking the Numpy list for my personal mad ravings, so we can take them off-list if the maintainers deem it too off-topic. If, on the other hand, y'all want to have a dialogue about this stuff here, I can think of no finer group to pressure-test my ideas. :-) Cheers, Peter (In the spirit of transparency and dogfooding: some parts of this email came from a thread summarization and initial dialog with GPT 4o and 5.2, although I only used the output as a starting point and edited heavily afterwards.) [1] https://creativecommons.org/ai-and-the-commons/cc-signals/ [2] https://datatracker.ietf.org/wg/aipref/about/ [3] https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oZHl4NWaO7c [4] \"AI for All\": https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TLZ9zXnluc8 [5] \"AI Training & The Data Commons in Crisis\": https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CdKxgT1o864", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00078", "post": 78, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00076", "depth": 20, "who": "P22", "op": false, "date": "15 Feb 2026", "body": "One aspect we need to keep in mind is that LLMs are lazy and sneaky. If a human does some performance work, and shows a benchmark table full of numbers, I would generally believe that they have actually run their code. But chatbots have been known to generate example data and pass it as real. And being so agreeable, they are likely to show an improvement. This is something that could bite us even if every human is acting in good faith. I don't have a good way to solve this, but maybe asking people to disclose exactly what validation has been done by humans would help, along with guidelines on how to do it. That wouldn't help with bad faith actors, but it's the best I can think of. /David", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00079", "post": 79, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "depth": 2, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "15 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sun, Feb 15, 2026 at 2:50 AM Peter Wang via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: A prompt template the people can use with their code generation might be helpful. As an example of such: https://x.com/WEschenbach/status/2022189308065796295. Mathew Rocklin had something similar in his discussion of hooks. The idea is to find ways to avoid some problems up front. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "post": 80, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00077", "depth": 2, "who": "P23", "op": false, "date": "15 Feb 2026", "body": "Hey! thanks a lot for chiming in, still watching the videos! FWIW, I have come around in that I guess we should add something (a lot of projects are discussing this, I would be happy to just steal one too). I would still focus on the transparency and social part (because I think that is the part that affects us more in practice) rather than copyright, but yeah, I guess it deserves a place. It may well be nice to force a checkbox and a short note in the PR template, I am not sure what will work there, but it may also depend a bit on the \"risk assessment\" how much detail we need. Now for copyright issues, I am still a bit unclear what we should ask for beyond transparency from the contributor (happy to write that non-transparency/unclear provenance is likely to prompt us to just close). For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very useful and it may be nice to flesh it out (I don't think there is anything NumPy specific about it, i.e. in my mind (1) is basically examples for the blast radius?). I think the important part there might be to have a few rough examples and where they land (but I think I would find it very hard to do this with any certainty!). I like the risk-matrix approach, so I think what this would look like is a risk-matrix: Risk of copyright | \"Blast radius\" infringement | | very small .... ----------------|-------------------------------------- very low | low | ... | And what we would need examples that vary on both axes and suggest a very rough line at which point in the matrix you should start to be very careful and do extra verification steps (or just close the PR if you don't want to do those). At least that is how I would like to approach this when in doubt, but beyond being pretty confident that we only had pretty safe PRs for now, I am not sure I could build up such a matrix myself. - Sebastian", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "post": 81, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "depth": 3, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "16 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sun, Feb 15, 2026 at 9:06 AM sebastian <sebastian@sipsolutions.net> wrote: I asked Grok about this, and it suggested a template as the best solution: # Pull Request ## Description ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes <snip> Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00082", "post": 82, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00081", "depth": 4, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "16 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sun, Feb 15, 2026 at 6:22 PM Charles R Harris <charlesr.harris@gmail.com> wrote: # Pull Request ## Description <!-- A clear and concise description of the changes. --> ## Checklist ### Required for all PRs - [ ] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines]( https://numpy.org/doc/stable/dev/index.html) - [ ] Tests have been added or updated (and all tests pass) - [ ] Documentation has been updated (docstrings, user guide, release notes if needed) - [ ] Code style: I have run `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` (or `pre-commit run --all-files`) ### AI Usage (required disclosure) - [ ] I have disclosed any use of AI tools below: - **AI tools used**: (e.g. Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, Cursor, etc. — or \"None\") - **How they were used**: (e.g. \"Generated first draft of new function X\", \"Helped write tests\", \"Refactored docstring\", \"None\") - **Review notes**: (optional — anything reviewers should pay extra attention to) ### Additional notes <!-- Any other information reviewers should know (breaking changes, performance impact, etc.) Another try to fix formatting. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "post": 83, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00080", "depth": 3, "who": "P21", "op": false, "date": "16 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sun, Feb 15, 2026 at 9:35 PM sebastian <sebastian@sipsolutions.net> wrote: Awesome, definitely let me know if you have any questions/feedback! For the maintainer I think the \"blast radius\" framework could be very Actually, I think this is something where the NumPy-specific bits could be extraordinarily helpful, and in a sense, it manifests the worldview that you & other maintainers have painstakingly built up over time. There will be some parts that are pretty generic to all software projects, but there are quite a lot of subtle and intricate design choices that go into making Numpy what it is. These may not be obvious to a coding LLM that, ultimately, will regress to the mean on matters of technical design and architecture. Examples include: * To what degree is a performance reduction acceptable, and when is it a regression? * What kinds of hardware optimizations are acceptable, at the price of maintainability? * How important is it to insist that all tests pass for all supported architectures? (Ie what is the compatibility rubric?) * Which downstream projects or specific API surfaces are important to prioritize testing on, for numerical stability, accuracy, performance, etc.? I'm sure you all can come up with many more :-) I also believe that maintainers writing down your unique & specific individual perspectives will ultimately form the foundations of a \"Numpy code bot\" that can assist in development in the future, assuming we get a non-legally-problematic coding model created. -Peter", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00084", "post": 84, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00083", "depth": 4, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "16 Feb 2026", "body": "On Mon, Feb 16, 2026 at 5:33 AM Peter Wang via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00085", "post": 85, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00084", "depth": 5, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "16 Feb 2026", "body": "On Mon, Feb 16, 2026 at 8:20 AM Charles R Harris <charlesr.harris@gmail.com> wrote: You can look through some of the SKILL.md files other folks have generated for hints of how to keep claude in line. Should we have such files? Would we accept PRs adding such files? They would be a tangible point of discussion if nothing else. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "post": 86, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00075", "depth": 19, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "18 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, I'd suggested (off-list) that this might be better done in another thread - but perhaps it can be done here. Reflecting, and experimenting - there are many caveats, but I think it is reasonable to give the contributor some responsibility for formal care about copyright. One way of doing that - is to ask some AI (if possible, an AI other than the one generating the code) to review for copyright. I've experimented with that over at https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30828#issuecomment-3920553882 . But the idea would be that we ask a contributor who has generated code by AI, to do this as part of the PR sign-off. They should be in a much better position to do this than the maintainers, as they should have been exploring the problem themselves, and therefore should be able to write better queries to guide the AI review. And with the prompts as a start, it's not particularly time-consuming. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00087", "post": 87, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00086", "depth": 20, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "18 Feb 2026", "body": "On Wed, Feb 18, 2026 at 9:16 AM Matthew Brett <matthew.brett@gmail.com> wrote: I think all of the arguments it produced are not grounded in the principles of copyright law. Unfortunately, I think this is one of the areas where LLMs just generate plausible nonsense rather than sound legal analysis. Each thing that it noted was a one-liner or a general idea, nothing copyrightable. It's essentially writes like a median StackOverflow programmer with a dim understanding of copyright law (no slight intended to anyone; I am one). I've looked at the two files it suggested, and I see no similarity to the PR. I do kind of suspect that LLMs could be used, with care, to help facilitate the abstraction-filtration-comparison test <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abstraction-Filtration-Comparison_test> and maybe finding candidates to do that test on, but a general instruction to give arguments for copyright violation apparently yields more chaff to wade through.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00088", "post": 88, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00087", "depth": 21, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Wed, Feb 18, 2026 at 10:33 PM Robert Kern via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Yes, sure - and you can see me trying to negotiate with Gemini on related points in an earlier session here: https://gist.github.com/matthew-brett/fac33f1b41d98e51b842f8bb84e8c66b My point was not that AI is doing a good job here - it isn't - but to offer it as a starting point for further research for the PR author, and reflection for those of us thinking about copyright and AI, on what a better process might look like. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00089", "post": 89, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00088", "depth": 22, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "On Wed, Feb 18, 2026 at 7:03 PM Matthew Brett <matthew.brett@gmail.com> wrote: IMO, it's definitely not a good starting point for the PR author. It doesn't matter where it places you as a starting point if it points you in the wrong direction. You are asking the PR author to defend against incorrect statements of fact and law. I think *some* kind of code search or plagiarism detection service might be helpful in identifying possible original sources to compare with the generatred output. It's not at all clear that asking the LLM as an oracle actually enacts such a search. It plainly did not here, but it presented its work as such. I don't think it's a good policy to construct an ad hoc plagiarism detection service without validating how it actually performs. I really strongly suggest that you retract your PR comment. It would be one thing to try it out and post here about what you found, but to interact with a contributor that way as an experiment is... ill-advised.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00090", "post": 90, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00089", "depth": 23, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "On Wed, Feb 18, 2026 at 6:04 PM Robert Kern via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: +1. The interaction on that PR as a whole struck me as harsh, verging on rude. Chuck", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00091", "post": 91, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00090", "depth": 24, "who": "P24", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "It certainly shows the need for developing a unified policy sooner rather than later! *sigh* I did want to push back on this statement from the PR: I want to push back specifically on this point because it is not a good basis from which to determine policy. We can, and I would argue we *must*, play a part in whether \"everyone\" will be using AI. I'll lift this quote (via Juan Luis Cano Rodriguez[1]) from \"Resisting Enchantment and Determinism: How to critically engage with AI university guidelines\" [2]: Juan. [1]: https://astrojuanlu.leaflet.pub/3meyu6zht2c2q [2]: https://zenodo.org/records/18282338", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00092", "post": 92, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00090", "depth": 24, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Thu, Feb 19, 2026 at 2:48 AM Charles R Harris via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: You surely don't mean that it is harsh or rude to post the AI summary, along with: \"Obviously - as designed - this is deliberately Red Team. But @mdrdope - no pressure, and feel free not to answer - do you have any response to the Gemini comments?\" That's one of the advantages of the asking the contributor themselves to do that review - it makes it less likely that they will take offense to the output of the AI. Anyone using AI will know that it will frequently be wrong, and it will be more obvious to them that the AI output is not a judgment, but may serve as a starting point for reflection and investigation. For example, it may draw the author, and the maintainers, into a more thoughtful and informed discussion of copyright. But perhaps you mean - the AI, and some of the other comments, implied that the PR was largely AI-generated, and that was rude? And - I think this is what you are saying - you don't think it's important whether it was, or was not AI generated, and therefore, trying to establish the extent of AI use is harsh / rude? Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00093", "post": 93, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00091", "depth": 25, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, I agree - and it's a point that has been well-made by your quote, and by others, that \"accept the inevitable\" is a poor argument, and easily deployed by people who are trying to sell you something. You may have seen Linus Torvalds' constant complaints about the hype surrounding AI. In this case, what worries me is that we may be drifting into accepting the idea that it is inevitable that we developers will switch from mainly writing code ourselves, to mainly asking AI to write code for us. I don't think that's inevitable, and neither, apparently, does Torvalds (see quotes above). I suspect, if we do go in that direction, we will find (from another quote above) that our skills in writing code will start to atrophy, and this is likely to mean that our learning, and our skills in reviewing code will start to atrophy as well. See the Anthropic study quoted above, and references therein for more on AI-generation and learning deficits. In other words, more code, more subtle bugs, fewer developers who understand the code-base, and fewer developers coming to the project with sufficient training to read and review code. But luckily, all hype aside, we have plenty of time to take this slowly and see how this develops. There's no plausible world in which Numpy suffers significantly from taking a measured approach to AI-generated code, over the next few years. There are various plausible worlds where it suffers from being too credulous of AI code quality, or it's ability to train developers, or its tendency to generate code that is subject to copyright. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00094", "post": 94, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00093", "depth": 26, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "I don't understand the relevance of both quotes given here; the AI - education relationship has nothing to do here. On the other hand, the damage of social media on students and the upcoming generation is documented in, now, hundreds of studies in the last decade. Yet here we are all having twitter/mastodon/tiktok/bluesky/instagram/whatevers including all maintainers, our children have them, we even have official numpy scipy accounts. So that surely looks like gravity to me. And I, for one, have no interest in what Linus Torvalds thinks about any social issue given his track record and handling conflicts. Using LLM to find copyright violations is, with all respect, one of the most ferrous irony I have seen lately. Did you check whether JAX and PyArrow claim by the LLM is correct before you accuse the PR author, is there an actual code resemblance confirmed by a human? (not blaming you obviously but I am sure you see the recursion you are creating here) I agree with Chuck. On Thu, Feb 19, 2026 at 11:52 AM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00095", "post": 95, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00092", "depth": 25, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "On Thu, Feb 19, 2026 at 5:24 AM Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Yes. 100%. Irresponsible, too. It's as if a teacher decided to hack together his own plagiarism detector, ran it on his students' work and asked them to respond. Before actually seeing if the thing worked to *any* extent on a known corpus. \"no pressure\" coming from someone in authority (you have that \"Member\" tag; you represent the project when you interact with its PRs) is meaningless. You are responsible for the words you put there, whether you use an LLM to generate them or not. Weasel words that the LLM may be wrong don't absolve you of this. You knew that the specific things in there were wrong. You showed us a previous conversation where you identified the wrong claims and found that you could guide the LLM away from them. But in this final conversation, you chose not to provide that guidance and in fact make it more aggressively wrong. And this is the one that you chose to put forth live instead of discussing it here. Which is what I actually asked for. I just wanted a gist to look at and evaluate *before* we decided as a project what to put forth as policy. That's one of the advantages of the asking the contributor themselves You are rapidly draining my ability to believe that you are operating in good faith. Rather, it increasingly seems like you are strawmanning a particularly bad use of LLMs in order to make a point that LLMs are bad. Good faith is a presumption. It can be undermined with experience.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00096", "post": 96, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00095", "depth": 26, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "19 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, Sorry - top posting - but: I delayed my reply because your accusation of bad faith seemed so obviously unreasonable, that I had imagined someone might intervene on my behalf, but it seems not. As I understand it, you're saying that my - rather silly - master plan was to post an AI-generated response that was so obviously wrong that it would persuade everyone that AI was bad. And to add to my incompetence, I sent a link to another conversation I'd had with the AI, where it did better, undermining my own case. I think you're also somehow saying if I had not posted the AI response on the issue, but in a Gist, then everything would have been fine, and no bad faith need be assumed. I don't really know where to go from there, but just for the record - no - it was not my dark intention to undermine confidence in AI. I was really doing what I said I was doing - which was to try and work out what a prompt would look like, that would stimulate the PR author (in general) to reflect on copyright. And where I was assuming that anyone using AI would be aware that it was possible for the AI to partly or entirely wrong - and so to use it only as a starting point, or a view to oppose. My foolish post of a previous AI discussion was only to show the kind of conversation I would expect to have, as the PR author. And for the PR AI quote, I was trying out the red-team approach - and trying to avoid pasting a long back and forth. Sadly, I was hoping to stimulate some respectful and useful discussion on this difficult topic, but I'm afraid that has failed very badly. I hope that someone else who isn't so clearly acting in bad faith can take up the discussion as to how one might explore copyright in PRs. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00097", "post": 97, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00096", "depth": 27, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "20 Feb 2026", "body": "On Thu, Feb 19, 2026 at 6:29 PM Matthew Brett <matthew.brett@gmail.com> wrote: No, I don't suspect that you have any master plan to convince anyone by this example alone. I think you're also somehow saying if I had not posted the AI response Yes. Precisely, it's the doubling and tripling down. If you had deleted the PR response with an apology, we'd have gone back to productively critiquing and possibly improving your technique. It's the fact that you took it live on our project, knowing that it was Not Even Wrong, and not acknowledging it when that action was criticized. I don't really know where to go from there, Deleting the PR comment with an apology would be a really good start. Stimulating \"the PR author to reflect on copyright\" is pretty different from documenting \"searches to confirm that no parts of the code are subject to existing copyright\", which is what you told us you were going to do. If we had discussed your results ahead of time, we'd have concluded that no, it's not doing searches that can do that confirmation. And you would get my opinion that stimulating the PR author to reflect on copyright with such output is not of value. It was not a good idea to go live and experiment on a contributor ahead of that feedback.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00098", "post": 98, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00097", "depth": 28, "who": "P24", "op": false, "date": "20 Feb 2026", "body": "I was asleep most of that time. I do think it would be good to take down the temperature, but I must explain that, like Robert, Rather, I started to feel like you were taking a deliberately contrarian approach to the argument, rather than trying to work towards a consensus. That is at least what I understood Robert to mean by \"bad faith\". It turns out that even when slop is clearly marked as such, it's disgusting to swallow. That is, before your \"experiment\", I thought a sensible AI writing policy might be that all AI-generated text should be marked as such. Now, I think it should be stronger: the author of a post should be responsible for every word of the post, AI-generated or not. It is simply not good enough to say \"AI wrote this, wdyt?\" As with code, any posted text should be thoroughly researched and understood by the poster. Quoting from the excellent Zulip AI use guidelines <https://github.com/zulip/zulip/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#ai-use-policy-and-guidelines>: Similarly, on the flip side, the answer to \"why do you think I should spend time working on/researching this review comment\" should never be \"I'm not sure. The AI did it.\" Anyway, I do think it would be good to take the temperature down a bit. Matthew did not intend to offend (nor do I in this email; I hope that is clear, Matthew). However, if it came across as rude to Chuck, Robert, Ilhan, and myself, it's probably at a minimum borderline, and it's probably worth thinking about why we are perceiving it that way. Juan.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00099", "post": 99, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00097", "depth": 28, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "20 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, To reduce the heat on this issue I have: a) Deleted my comment on the PR, and my reference to that comment. b) Reposted as a Gist so people reading this thread can see what the discussion was about : https://gist.github.com/matthew-brett/a9b43c7266e0fb4f773677ca838fa920 Further replies inline: On Fri, Feb 20, 2026 at 2:36 AM Robert Kern via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: You wrote before that \"Rather, it increasingly seems like you are strawmanning a particularly bad use of LLMs in order to make a point that LLMs are bad.\" as the explanation for why you now suspect I was acting in bad faith. I presume from the \"this example alone\" that you still think I have such a program. As I said before - that's a very silly program. What's the idea here - that I try and persuade my competent and intelligent colleagues of such a ridiculous binary by sneaking in bad examples, when of course y'all have seen many such examples yourselves? It's the price of AI admission. As Matthew Rocklin put it, in his very useful article advocating AI for code generation: \"LLMs generate a lot of junk\" : https://matthewrocklin.com/ai-zealotry/#why-ai . Yet it is clear to me they will also offer benefit, if used with care. But why oh why would you pitch in with this fierce and insulting diagnosis of my motivation, and demand an immediate apology, rather than saying - \"OK - I get the point you're trying to make - but it's not well put and I think you made it in the wrong place, let's move it elsewhere?\" Don't you have some responsibility for keeping the conversation calm and civil? I mean - to the project if not to me. I think you agree that copyright is an important and difficult subject that needs careful reflection - we're not going to get there with this level of distrust. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00100", "post": 100, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00099", "depth": 29, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "20 Feb 2026", "body": "On Fri, Feb 20, 2026 at 5:55 AM Matthew Brett <matthew.brett@gmail.com> wrote: In the interest of playing the ball and not the man, and refocusing on offering a path forward to return to productive conversation (one that I am glad that we have taken), I had deleted a paragraph where I detailed the patterns I am seeing. If you'd like to talk about it in private, I am at your service. I don't believe that's far off from where I started: https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/message/KWQEPTMZV7KNK42IGPXSY4AUJS7ZAHXU/ Don't you have some responsibility for keeping the", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00101", "post": 101, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00094", "depth": 27, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "21 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, [...] Yes, as you can imagine, I thought about that problem - of using AI to detect copyright violations in AI. To me, that is only an irony if we are thinking of a binary - AI-good, AI-bad. If I think AI-bad, then I think that AI-generated contributions are bad, and therefore I must also think that using AI as a jumping off point for copyright assessment is bad. However, AI-bad is not what I think. I do think (is this controversial?) that AI is unreliable, that, in typical use, without careful discipline, it will tend to reduce learning and understanding compared to doing the same task without AI, and that it can be useful, if we take those things into account. Then I was thinking about the question that Evgeni (on the Scientific Python Discourse forum) and Robert had asked - which is - fine, copyright is an issue, but how can we reasonably ask the contributor to assess that? That's a serious and difficult question. One option is to throw up one's hands and say - OK - copyright is dead - let's ignore it, or at least, deemphasise it. I don't think that's the right answer, which leaves me with the urgent problem of how to proceed. Because this question is difficult, and it is very new (in the sense it has now become very easy for good-faith submissions to violate copyright) - it seems to me we will have to iterate. Then I asked myself - if I had to start somewhere - how would I approach that problem? The way I tend to use AI, is as a jumping off point - a starting point for a discussion with the AI. Quite often, as in this case, that jumping off point is misleading or flat-out wrong - but if you know that (are there any experienced users of AI who don't know that?) - then you can start to negotiate with the AI, and you will often, if you are careful, negotiate to something that you can verify from reliable sources. You may have seen me taking that (I assume standard) approach in my negotiations with Gemini in a previous conversation about copyright, that I linked to as a Gist. Now, this is a new world we're in. I'm not saying that's a practical approach for contributors to explore copyright. I think that I could use it that way, and that I'd get closer to a reliable answer than if I had not used it (and got no answer). I suspect, if we trust our contributors, we will find we and they do develop good habits for that use. But it's a genuinely open question whether that is so. As I keep saying, my intention was only to raise the idea as a starting point. And given the nature of AI - I therefore had to run the risk that the relevant quoted AI (from a simple prompt and response) would be misleading or wrong. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00102", "post": 102, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00100", "depth": 30, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "21 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, Thank you - I'm glad to hear - and likewise, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00103", "post": 103, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00101", "depth": 28, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "21 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, I should say that I'm aware that using AI for copyright assessment is very delicate. There is evidence (Xu et al - https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.02487) that 2024/5 vintage AI models were systematically less likely to correctly identify Copyleft licenses. Xu et al speculate that \"some closed-source LLMs may have implemented post-processing steps to avoid acknowledging outputs derived from copyleft-licensed code snippets.\" Likewise, we know that OpenAI was reluctant to put AI-watermarks on ChatGPT output, with one suggested reason being surveys that predicted a large drop in use if the watermark was added : https://arstechnica.com/ai/2024/08/openai-has-the-tech-to-watermark-chatgpt-text-it-just-wont-release-it/ . And of course we have no way of knowing how the standard commercial models have been configured. Now imagine that we (open-source developers) start using AI to detect copyright violation, and that in turn leads to a reduction in use of AI tools by the open-source or commercial developers. It will be very difficult for us to know whether later versions of the models have been trained with the aim of making it less likely they will detect copyright violations, on the basis that less copyright violation detection leads to more use of AI. But perhaps that's a problem for a later time. And perhaps we can already become part of the negotiation with AI code model providers, on detection of copyright violation. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00104", "post": 104, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00103", "depth": 29, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "21 Feb 2026", "body": "On the subject of whether gen-AI code violates copyright maybe this pull request can be used as a relevant and real example: https://github.com/sympy/sympy/pull/29150 The PR description has an AI Generation Disclosure that says: I believe the author that that is exactly what happened but it leaves me unsure of the copyright status of the code. Among recent PRs this author stands out as a new contributor who has demonstrated their ability to produce good PRs while apparently using AI to do so. If we envisage a future in which most contributors are using AI then this is exactly the kind of contributor that we should want. I have questioned them about the copyright in that PR and they have not responded yet but I think that is because it is just not at all clear how to answer that question. It is not clear to me without close inspection whether the code in the PR is doing something simple and obvious or something nontrivial. I have a suspicion that I might be able to simplify the code a lot if I studied exactly what it is doing carefully but it would take some time to figure that out. It is also possible though that the code implements some nontrivial named algorithm and is perhaps more or less directly copied (by the AI) from another codebase. Another example is that I recently made this PR using codex (GPT-5.3) myself: https://github.com/sympy/sympy/pull/29127 That PR copies/adapts the type annotations that I wrote in one library into another library. After a lot of back and forth with codex it decided to look at the contents of the other library and then adapted the type annotations from there, copying them into another codebase. I am the author of the code in question in both codebases but just so we're clear the agent literally went and read the code for another package in .venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/ to do this (the UI tells you when the agent is reading files). It is not a case of the model having that code in its training data but that the agent actively decided to go read and copy the code after its previous attempts at just writing the code had failed. I gave the agent what I thought were clear instructions for how to write the code but it ignored those, tried every possible wrong approach in cycles, and then at some point decided to check for type errors by running py_compile instead of mypy. Then it told me that it had fixed all the errors and I said \"you're supposed to run mypy, not py_compile\" so it said: ``` • Correct. py_compile was the wrong validation for this task. I re-ran with mypy and confirmed there are still many errors. I also tested wiring FMPZ_POLY/ FMPQ_POLY directly into ringflint.py; that made mypy worse, so I reverted that experiment. Current workspace is back to only this change: - sympy/external/gmpy.py: added POLY_P, FMPZ_POLY, FMPQ_POLY under if TYPE_CHECKING. So at this point: - requested fictitious poly types/protocol exist - mypy sympy still fails with many existing flint-related typing errors (including ringflint.py and polyclasses.py) If you want, I can proceed with the next step: a broader typing pass to actually consume FMPZ_POLY/ FMPQ_POLY in ringflint.py/polyclasses.py in a way that reduces mypy sympy errors instead of increasing them. ``` I said yes we should indeed be reducing the errors rather than increasing them (that part amused me enough that I saved the output shown for posterity). The agent then spent some time talking to itself about the difficulties it had previously encountered and then decided to go look at the other codebase and realised that it could basically just copy the code from there. I don't think that there is any copyright violation in writing out type annotations for a dependent library's interface in another like that and as the author (not using AI) of the type annotations being copied I think I am in charge of the copyright anyway. It is not hard to see how you can end up having a copyright violation from this sort of thing though. Having played around with codex a few times I now realise that having it just write the code is possibly one of the least useful things you can ask it to do. When you ask it to write the actual code you then have to review its changes in detail and if you allow it to make any nontrivial decisions you will end up in a review/prompt cycle that is worse than just writing the code yourself. When you ask it to do other things though it is great like \"bisect this\" or \"make a benchmark script and time these 5 approaches\" or \"Can you see any obvious problems in the diff?\" and so on. Everything that requires investigating things and/or writing throwaway code is a great task for the AI because you don't have to review the code it generates which is what otherwise becomes the bottleneck. If I was to redo the PR above I would just start writing the code myself and then when I reach the point that all of the high level decisions about how to write the code are made I would ask codex to finish the job. It would be more like \"I've done 3 classes now you do the other 10 classes just like I did\" or \"I've written the code but mypy now shows 1000 errors. Fix the trivial errors and then categorise and explain the remaining errors\".", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00105", "post": 105, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00103", "depth": 29, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "21 Feb 2026", "body": "I think we will not convince each other in this subject. My position is still the same; Ignoring the actual stealing done by these companies and then holding each other accountable for copyright is a no-go for me, using it further to resolve copyright issues, and thus washing it clean, is doubly so. I will not entertain that option and that's my personal position. I don't expect others to take this position. Defending copyright protection and ignoring the largest copyrighted elephant in the room does not seem a sound argument to me. Moreover, arguing over the tool as if it is oblivious and neutral is just factually incorrect, there are companies behind these tools. The technology is undoubtedly impressive and very useful, however the current arrangement of it is built upon legally unsound basis with very shady practices. Therefore I refuse to philosophize over it as a free-agent with shortcomings. In my opinion, the possible thing we can do is at least be defensive and if we feel like it, take advantage of LLMs to use it for automating mundane tasks to save time that FOSS maintainers definitely lack which is what I have been doing with LAPACK translation as I mentioned above. I still go over them line by line which takes insane amount of time though much shorter than writing it myself. So \"No AI contribution is allowed\" is a valid take for me if that would be the policy. Or \"we will use common sense and make opinionated decisions, for trivial and otherwise laborious tasks we don't care but for involved bits, we won't touch it\". It is also fine. However, since we are not making any progress in this particular aspect of the discussion, let's conclude it as inconclusive and go back to the policy discussion. On Sat, Feb 21, 2026 at 12:37 PM Matthew Brett <matthew.brett@gmail.com> wrote:", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00106", "post": 106, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00105", "depth": 30, "who": "P19", "op": false, "date": "21 Feb 2026", "body": "I'd be happy with either too, with a preference for the second one, which I see as allowing AI as a tool. Disclosure has to be key, though. Note that while both would at least somewhat address copyright issues, only the first would address the pain of having to deal with AI slop. To me it remains the bigger problem [1], one I don't see how we can address without something like a web of trust. But I'll let this be the last time I note it here, since arguably it is a different discussion, and also at numpy I'm not one directly affected as I rarely look at new PRs. Instead, those who do look at new PRs should speak up on whether they are willing to filter AI slop. But for what it is worth, for astropy, where I did often look at new PRs, I've concluded that AI slop is sufficiently shifting the balance towards misery that I will no longer look at anything unless I'm pinged. All the best, Marten [1] Not fact checked, and more for amusement, but via LWN I was led to the following (really, 5% of all open-soure code this month???). \"\"\" Kevin Beaumont @GossiTheDog@cyberplace.social Today in InfoSec Job Security News: I was looking into an obvious ../.. vulnerability introduced into a major web framework today, and it was committed by username Claude on GitHub. Vibe coded, basically. So I started looking through Claude commits on GitHub, there’s over 2m of them and it’s about 5% of all open source code this month. https://github.com/search?q=author%3Aclaude&type=commits&s=author-date&o=desc As I looked through the code I saw the same class of vulns being introduced over, and over, again - several a minute. \"\"\" (From https://cyberplace.social/@GossiTheDog/116080909947754833)", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00107", "post": 107, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00106", "depth": 31, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "21 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Sat, Feb 21, 2026 at 4:15 PM Marten van Kerkwijk via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Ouch - yes, that's understandable. Related - Matt Haberland's comment on the Scipy AI policy https://github.com/scipy/scipy/pull/24583#pullrequestreview-3821660783 I wonder whether we'll reach the stage where there are two groups of people in each project that accepts AI-generated code - those who are prepared to review AI-generated PRs, and those who are not. If so, that could unintended effects, as parts of the code become more likely to be AI-generated, parts less, depending on the reviewers. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00108", "post": 108, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00106", "depth": 31, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "22 Feb 2026", "body": "Thanks for trying to steer this back to a pragmatic \"how do we define a policy\". On Sat, Feb 21, 2026 at 5:16 PM Marten van Kerkwijk via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: I have a very strong preference for allowing AI as a tool here, for multiple reasons: 1. The principle I articulated before: don't tell others what tools are and they aren't allowed to use, as long as it doesn't break other contribution guidelines/rules. 2. The opportunities it affords us to *lighten* the maintenance burden. Maintainer bandwidth is always scarce, and the majority of PRs do not involve architecture or copyrightable algorithmic code. That most contributions are \"AI slop\" and will just make our maintenance load worse is a take that is highly likely to be incorrect. 3. The opportunities for learning. AI tools are fast becoming a key skill for software engineering related jobs; for scientific jobs I could see that becoming the case as well. Blanket forbidding use of those tools potentially penalizes especially the people who spend a significant part of their time every week contributing to NumPy. 4. Alignment with other open source projects. Melissa has been composing a nice overview here: https://github.com/melissawm/open-source-ai-contribution-policies. Both inside and outside the scientific Python community, a large majority of projects land on a very similar set of policies and principles. And very few land on an outright ban. I find the discourse in this thread about copyright to be completely lacking in pragmatism and actionable insights. It's something that's not new for us in scope, only in scale. We're just going to have to trust the disclosures that we should be asking for (starting asap), give some pragmatic basic guidelines, and deal with PRs as they come. There *will* be a gray zone. Oscar's example of someone starting with \"generate an algorithm, then lightly edit it\" is one step too far for now I think. Anything short of that, including using AI to fill in some algorithmic details that were already designed by a human, should be okay / in the grey zone. Disclosure has to be key, though. +1 I do think a web of trust is a potentially valuable idea. However, the need right now isn't there yet (at least for NumPy) and it does have the potential to close the door pretty strongly to newcomers. On the other hand, we already don't run CI on PRs from first-time contributors - that was something that turned out to be necessary to limit wasting resources. A web of trust is something to keep in mind in my opinion, and consider adopting if and when it becomes a clear win for maintainer load. +1. Also in general: this policy should be primarily informed by the people who are actively maintaining NumPy on a daily/weekly basis. I'd definitely include you in that though Marten. There is more to maintenance than \"triage the new PRs from people we don't know\"; you spent a significant amount of time reviewing code, mostly complex C code in `numpy/_core`. That's the kind of code where tools can help quite a bit, but only in the hands of humans that already understand the code deeply. Another example: security reports. Those are usually triaged by Sebastian and me, and I'd say the volume of bogus reports has gone up slowly over the past couple of years, in part driven by wider availability AI tooling (and https://huntr.com/, which has a terrible signal-to-noise problem - avoid at all costs). So we can avoid AI tools ourselves, and see that maintenance load just continue going up. Or we can try to experiment with tooling ourselves - see for example https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-code-security. I just signed up for early access of that capability, because I would like to get high-quality reports and patches, and fix potential real-world problems before they occur. I'm honestly not really interested in opinions from non(-active) maintainers about whether I am allowed to do something like sign up for that security service, or apply a patch it generates. Which is yet another reason I find \"no AI at all\" not an acceptable policy. Filtering out the copyright-related noise/argumentation, I detect a significant preference for allowing use of AI tools while putting on some sensible constraints by the group of active maintainers. I'd like to somehow move towards something more actionable, because we do need some policy and an AI usage disclosure on all PRs soon. To get to that, I think we should be picking a base policy as a start, and add some NumPy-specific edits/context as needed. I think the most suitable base to start from would be either: 1. The LLVM policy: https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html 2. The SymPy/SciPy policy: https://scipy.github.io/devdocs/dev/conduct/ai_policy.html If we want to capture the \"gray zone\" better, adding a supplementary document with some concrete examples and maybe incorporating the \"Zones\" that Peter sketched would be good. Final thought since we're sharing resources: this article by Chris Lattner resonated with me: https://www.modular.com/blog/the-claude-c-compiler-what-it-reveals-about-the-future-of-software Cheers, Ralf", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00109", "post": 109, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00108", "depth": 32, "who": "P19", "op": false, "date": "22 Feb 2026", "body": "Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> writes: [snip] Thanks for the reminder that we do not run CI for first-time contributors. That is nice in that there is already a mechanism in place to recognize those. As an intermediate step towards trust (but not yet a web of it!), would it make sense to have a welcome message that asks the new contributor to introduce themselves by editing their top comment? I.e., something like this: \"\"\" Thank you for your PR! As you appear to be a new contributor, could we ask you to briefly introduce yourself, e.g., by editing the top comment? It would help to know how you use numpy yourself and what made you want to contribute, and whether you are, e.g., a student keen to make an open-source contribution, or an experienced developer just fixing an annoying bug. Note: if you used any AI in your PR, be sure to declare this and check that your use is consistent with our AI policy. \"\"\" I think both policies are good; I'd prefer to go with the shorter and more direct scipy one -- that also has the advantage of keeping things more consistent within scientific python. Personally, I would copy it verbatim and for now not spend time adding/editing. All the best, Marten", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00110", "post": 110, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00104", "depth": 30, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "22 Feb 2026", "body": "I just tried asking codex to write me a description for that PR. The prompt (in a fresh session) was ``` I want to make a PR with the python-flint typing related changes on this branch. Can you write a description for the PR? ``` Then codex went and looked at the PULL_REQUESTS_TEMPLATE.md, looked at the commits, and then produced a PR description matching that template. It filled out the AI disclosure part of the PR template for me ``` #### AI Generation Disclosure Used ChatGPT to help draft PR text only. No code changes were AI-generated in this PR. ``` Both of those sentences are false and it just lied automatically on my behalf without me asking it to do that and without asking for any clarification about what to put there.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00111", "post": 111, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00109", "depth": 33, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sun, 2026-02-22 at 10:19 -0500, Marten van Kerkwijk via NumPy- Discussion wrote: Thanks for the suggestions on what concrete steps we should do (and I agree we should do something). I would be fine with basically adopting either of these, adopting the SciPy/SymPy one seems pragmatic, it is nice to keep things similar in similar projects. SymPy/LLVM do have a pretty clear note on copyright (not all do, I think). [1] (I like many things about the LLVM, it is nice explicit about reasoning, etc. but I guess that also makes it longer.) To me they honestly all get the important points across. And honestly, I suspect many contributors won't read it anyway, so it may be more used to point to in the rare case where you close a PR or so. To achieve better transparency, I would suggest we add check-boxes, E.g. sklearn has this now: <!-- If AI tools were involved in creating this PR, please check all boxes that apply below and make sure that you adhere to our Automated Contributions Policy: https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/contributing.html#automated-contributions-policy --> I used AI assistance for: - [ ] Code generation (e.g., when writing an implementation or fixing a bug) - [ ] Test/benchmark generation - [ ] Documentation (including examples) - [ ] Research and understanding I am not sure how well it is used, but I think that is a good start to see where it goes. I could imagine trying to put in something about the scope of AI use, but I am not sure if it matters. It may be easier to just follow up for PRs where it is unclear. (FWIW, I like the comment asking for a bit of personal context, it feels both helpful and welcoming! But I think when it comes to AI specifically, I would start with the check-boxes for pragmatism.) Cheers, Sebastian [1] I would be happy with linking out to continuing discussion towards the note in the LLVM one: \"Artificial intelligence systems raise many questions around copyright that have yet to be answered\" But I think that is about as much as I want to focus on that point in something targeted for contributors.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00112", "post": 112, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00111", "depth": 34, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Mon, Feb 23, 2026 at 8:53 AM Sebastian Berg <sebastian@sipsolutions.net> wrote: Unfortunately, as Oscar's example showed (and other slop PRs seem to confirm), it looks as though the check-boxes will be entirely useless, as the AI is perfectly capable of filling those out for you, and won't worry (as far as we know) about choosing the result most likely to get the PR merged. That in turn has some major costs for maintainer burn-out - as Maarten and Matt H are pointing out. I'm increasingly leaning towards - no AI generated code at all, unless a) from a well-trusted contributor, and b) justified by that contributor. Just flagging - but if we aren't asking the contributor to address copyright, we have two options: * The maintainer does it. I don't think there's any chance that will happen in practice. * We effectively decide we aren't going to worry about AI copyright violations. I realize the second option is the de-facto preference of some here, but if that's so, I think we have to say that out loud. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00113", "post": 113, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00110", "depth": 31, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "On Sun, Feb 22, 2026 at 7:07 PM Oscar Benjamin via NumPy-Discussion < numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Based on what you wrote, that seems like user error to me. The commits on the branch you made the PR from do not include the `Generated-by` or `Co-authored-by` attribution to indicate that those commits were generated by an LLM in part or in full. So if you ask Codex in a fresh session, where it doesn't have context about the previous work, to look at that branch / those commits, how is it supposed to know that the commit authorship on those commits is in fact incorrect? It's indeed possible that there is a model that deliberately and systematically lies in order to increase the chances of it being accepted, but it's much more likely that the PR message draft you ask for is actually correct based on the commit history. I just tried based on a branch with 4 commits that were all mine, and one with `Co-authored-by`. First I just asked it to draft a PR message in the session where it had the development context for those commits. It did, short and to the point. Then I gave it more context: Prompt: \"Additional content: the upstream repo uses the following PR template:\" Output ends with: ``` #### AI Generation Disclosure AI tools (Claude via Warp) were used to generate the initial workflow files and iterate on them based on review feedback. All code was reviewed and tested by the author. ``` Note that it includes actual details of the interaction that aren't in the commit messages (Claude via Warp, the nature of the actual interaction). Next I repeated it in a fresh session. It gave me a much more verbose PR message, more generic-AI sounding with \"files changed\" etc. I then again fed it the whole SciPy PR template. And it produced this: ``` AI Generation Disclosure Parts of this PR (the CI workflow files) were co-authored with Warp AI. All code was reviewed and edited by the author. ``` Note that it still kept \"Warp AI\", which it got from the Co-authored-by, but dropped the \"Claude\" part, which isn't in the commit message. As for the \"All code was reviewed and edited by the author.\": it can't really know if that is 100% accurate, but based on me being the sole author of 3 of the commits and the co-author of the 4th commit, it seems like the most likely interpretation of the commit history. tl;dr seems to work as advertised. And inaccuracies and omissions are still the responsibility of the human in the loop.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00114", "post": 114, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00112", "depth": 35, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "On Mon, 2026-02-23 at 09:07 +0000, Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion wrote: We can link out to the policy which would have a note on copyright. And that in turn could link out to further thoughts (heck, even the things like Peter Wang's talks). Maybe we can/should add a one sentence thing, but I want to be sure to not create undue uncertainty/fear for new contributors for an issue that, IMO, affects relatively few PRs because most PRs are just tiny bug-fixes. So, I think it would be good to discuss a concrete wording here. [1] To me it seems like an exaggeration to say we aren't going to worry about copyright. The question is to what degree it is helpful to force that worry on typical first time contributions. (And yes, there is also a question about how much we as a project/community should worry about it, but I think that is a separate discussion.) Asking for transparency can fail, but if the contributor lies about they will also ignore a ban and it gives us another reason to just close a PR or ban them. And if we want to draw a hard line for contributors, I don't like that because there is a vast range here: - used it for brain storming the approach - used tab completion in cursor - used it to start some tests but maybe 20% of lines remained. - Basically wrote the whole function and only tweaked it a bit. And additionally, Peter Wangs scope argument that we'll care much less about it if it is a docs website vs. core algorithm. The ask here can only be transparency anything beyond that could be guidelines that we put somewhere, but they would be guidelines to dig up maybe once every few months. and I would rather get to a world where we know what comes in (and then can still close a PR) than trying to draw a strict line. - Sebastian [1] I don't know, maybe in the (rough!) directino of: It is important to us to know about AI use both for community reasons as well as to for detailed review which may include copyright concerns for large contributions to some parts of NumPy.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00115", "post": 115, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00113", "depth": 32, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "On Mon, 23 Feb 2026 at 09:36, Ralf Gommers via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: It could have said \"I don't have the information needed to fill out this part of the template so can you answer these questions\" but it didn't and just falsified the missing information instead. The full description it wrote was quite long (over a screenful) so you could miss that AI part if not looking closely. Note that what it wrote there is pretty much the most common thing that people put in the AI disclosure and it is very often obviously false. Maybe I should put Co-authored-by then. I didn't actually let codex run git commit itself (I was using git myself in a separate terminal to track what it was doing). It is the responsibility of the human in the loop but the most common failure modes we see right now are: - They just delete the entire pull request template and insert something else. - They specifically delete the AI part of the template. - The whole description is AI generated and the human has not reviewed it at all. I tested what codex would do because my suspicion is that when they have deleted the entire template it is because they are using some kind of (possibly AI) tooling to open the pull request and therefore not actually reading the template in the web UI. I'm not sure what they are using though because if you use e.g. codex then it is smart enough to follow the PR template even if that means filling in the blanks with false information.", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00116", "post": 116, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00115", "depth": 33, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Mon, Feb 23, 2026 at 11:18 AM Oscar Benjamin via NumPy-Discussion <numpy-discussion@python.org> wrote: Yes - and the more general point is we can't depend on the AI not making stuff up for the checkboxes. I think that's the big problem with the (Torvalds) approach of - \"it's just another tool\". That's not really true, it's much more than that - it's a whole other way of working. It would perhaps be better to say \"it's another type of developer\". One where the usual trust relationships that are so central to open-source, are not valid. It would be a terrible mistake to apply rules that we evolved to work with humans, to the AI. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00117", "post": 117, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00114", "depth": 36, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Mon, Feb 23, 2026 at 11:12 AM Sebastian Berg <sebastian@sipsolutions.net> wrote: I completely agree with your implication - that it's unlikely to be useful to put a lot of documentation for people to read where they can learn about AI risk from copyright. I guess few people will read it, and then a) they won't know what to do about it and b) they have no obligation to do anything about it, so there is very little chance they will act on it. The central point is - who takes responsibility for copyright, in PRs with AI-generated code? We know that can't be the maintainers - so it can only be the contributors. This has always been the case, of course, but now we're in a very different situation, where it's very easy to end up with copyright code in the PR without realizing it. And at the moment, by waving at some documentation, without further instruction, we can surely predict from the data we have, that the contributor will not be likely to take effective responsibility for copyright. Therefore, there's a substantial risk of copyright leak, roughly proportional to the lines of non-trivial, non-mechanical code in the PRs. Back to the options - 1) don't worry about it because it's not important, or 2) put guards in place to make sure the contributor has carefully and personally reviewed the PR for copyright concerns, and review often to make sure these are effective or 3) delay allowing large AI-generated blocks of code until we can see a way forward for copyright. It's clear I think that some of us are in the 1) case - don't worry about it. I'm absolutely not in that camp, but hey. All I'm saying is - if we're in camp 1, we should make that clear. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00118", "post": 118, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00117", "depth": 37, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "On Mon, 2026-02-23 at 11:59 +0000, Matthew Brett via NumPy-Discussion wrote: <snip> To me this seems very exaggerated. Just because we don't put one issue (of multiple we have in practice) into the dead center of such a policy text (or PR template) doesn't equate to ignoring it? Both policies Ralf brought up include a statement about copyright. We still could decide to link out from there for the curious readers or adding guidelines somewhere when and where we should ask more questions. I believe all I said was that I don't want to overshoot and worry most contributors (because yeah, I truly think for the majority of PRs there is just not much concern). Maybe we can word-smith something that strikes a good balance there. And yeah, we probably disagree where that balance lies. But if adding a brief note on copyright concerns in the policy is the same as saying \"it's not important\", I have no idea where to go?! This discussion tends to feel like we have to start discussion at the end of \"it is a gigantic issue\" and then maybe be allowed edge towards: OK, but this is the minimal thing that won't care off all contributors. When yeah, probably most here think it isn't the biggest issue, so we should edge towards: Yeah, maybe it is a bit bigger of an issue than it seems, so how about we add this sentence and link? But... to me transparency is the thing that is most directly helpful (also for social dynamics concerns). So that has to be central. Maybe it will be ignored, but what can you reasonably put up that won't be? My guess would be that light-weight is better because it decreases the chance of lying. - Sebastian", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00119", "post": 119, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00118", "depth": 38, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "Hi, On Mon, Feb 23, 2026 at 1:52 PM Sebastian Berg <sebastian@sipsolutions.net> wrote: Yes - I'm sure you're right - that most PRs won't have large not-trivial AI code fragments, and for these, it's not a problem. So for these - I imagine the author would not be deterred by stronger statements about copyright - as it should be obvious to them that copyright is unlikely to apply. However, for PRs that do contain large non-trivial AI code fragments, it is a problem - and that's what I was referring to specifically. I'm sure you'll agree that general statements that people should be careful about copyright or take ownership of copyright, for those PRs, probably won't be effective in preventing copyright leak - without more instructions about what to do, to detect copyright leak. Well - yes - iff the author isn't using AI to submit the PR, or help author the commit message, in which case we shouldn't use terms like lying for the output - it's just whatever the AI came up with. And transparency doesn't help much for (large non-trivial PR) copyright, unless either a) the author has done some work that we can't yet specify to look for copyright violations or b) the maintainer notes the AI use and does that copyright work. But at the moment, we don't have much reason to think either of those will happen. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00120", "post": 120, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00118", "depth": 38, "who": "P19", "op": false, "date": "23 Feb 2026", "body": "There may be violent agreement here... [snip] I think the third option suggested by Matthew is reasonable, at least for new contributors, though I would phrase it like that for large AI-generated code we need to be able to trust the contributor *and* have evidence of due diligence. Let's try to move towards wondering how to include this in a policy. As Sebastian noted, most contributors will not read the policy, so at some level, what we write will be aimed as much as maintainers, to give guidance about when to ask for evidence. But I do agree with Sebastian that we should not let the extremes stop us from adopting a workflow that works for the general case, where a first contribution is small. -- Marten", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}, {"id": "numpy-policy-thread-p00121", "post": 121, "parent": "numpy-policy-thread-p00001", "depth": 1, "who": "P15", "op": false, "date": "4 Mar 2026", "body": "", "url": "https://mail.python.org/archives/list/numpy-discussion@python.org/thread/LAR7P3KQWHWAIKYSHTS2MY7X4HUBVA3L/"}], "participants": 24}, "osrf-implementation-guidelines": {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines", "title": "Guidelines for AI-Assisted Contributions in the ROS Project", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903", "posts": [{"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00001", "post": 1, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": true, "date": "29 Jun 2026", "body": "Hello fellow ROS users, maintainers, contributors and anyone else who may be passing by. Due to a recent uptick in LLM-authored pull requests, the ROS PMC has elected to extend the upstream OSRF policy on the use of generative tools to help provide some boundaries/guidelines on the use of these tools. These tools and technologies are constantly changing, so this isn’t intended to serve as a permanent policy, but rather it is to provide a framework for how to interact on ROS-related projects. The TLDR, because there are a lot of words below: Feel free to use the tools to build, explore, and understand software (as many of the maintainers also do), but when it comes time to interact on Discourse, Zulip, or Github, try to leave the LLM-centric text behind. A large component of open source software is about building/collaborating with people, and we would generally prefer to interact with people rather than an LLM-proxy. Maintainer time is a finite and constrained resource. While generative AI tools can be useful development assistants, submitting unverified Large Language Model (LLM) output shifts the engineering, debugging, and verification work onto project maintainers. This document establishes the rules for interacting with ROS project repositories when using AI tools. It serves as an extension of the upstream OSRF Policy on the Use of Generative Tools in Contributions . While the upstream policy defines the legal, IP, and attribution requirements for AI-generated code, these rules define the quality and behavioral standards required to protect maintainer bandwidth. The Author Ownership Rule The upstream OSRF policy states that contributors must have the right to sign the Developer Certificate of Origin (DCO). Operationally, this means you are the sole owner of, and are fully responsible for, the code you submit. Do not use maintainers as an LLM validation service. If a maintainer points out a bug, architectural issue, or syntax error in your PR, you may not simply paste that feedback back into an LLM and copy-paste the unverified output as a response. You must understand every line of code you submit. If a maintainer asks why a specific technical choice was made, responding with “an LLM generated this” or providing a generic, AI-synthesized explanation is unacceptable. You must be able to defend the technical merits of your implementation yourself. Maintainers want to engage with you, the author of the proposed changes. Maintainers want to understand why you want to make the change and why you want to implement it the way the PR proposes. Maintainers do not want to talk to your chatbot. Zero-Tolerance for Hallucinated API/Dependency Usage LLMs frequently hallucinate APIs, configuration parameters, or entire library features, especially given the fast-moving nature of ROS releases and underlying middleware (DDS, Zenoh, rclcpp, rclpy, etc.). Any PR that introduces non-existent APIs, impossible configuration keys, or broken logic due to LLM hallucinations will be closed immediately without review. Repeat offenses will result in a temporary or permanent block from the organization’s repositories. Compliance with Upstream Disclosure & Attribution The upstream OSRF policy explicitly requires that the use of generative tools be disclosed if they created a material part of the contribution. We enforce this strictly on GitHub: If an LLM was used to generate structural code, tests, or documentation boilerplate, state it clearly in the PR description. Do not LLM dump to generate your PR description. Paragraphs filled with generic AI phrasing (e.g., “This PR enhances the efficiency of the codebase by utilizing modern design patterns and optimizing asynchronous paradigms…” ) will be rejected. Write a concise, human-authored summary explaining exactly what changed and why . Enforcement and Maintainer Recourse To maintain the health of the project, maintainers have full discretion to handle low-effort, AI-driven contributions efficiently: If an issue or PR is clearly an unverified LLM dump (e.g., containing generic AI comments, formatting that violates project style guides, contains a poorly formatted description pasted directly from an LLM, or obviously broken logic), maintainers are authorized to close the PR immediately. If a contributor responds to code review feedback using obvious, unedited LLM-generated text that fails to address the underlying engineering critique, maintainers are authorized to close the PR immediately. If the contributor fails to respond to reviewer questions or feedback within a reasonable timeframe (e.g. within a week), reviewers may close the PR without further review. If a contributor continues to behave in an unproductive way through repeated submission of inappropriate LLM-dump issues or PRs, repeated copy-and-pasting of LLM-dump comments, or similar, maintainers are authorized to temporarily or permanently block that user from the organization’s repositories. Guidelines for Responsible AI Use in ROS If you want to use an LLM to help you successfully contribute to ROS, adhere to the following workflow: Confirm that the issue is still valid and that any proposed solution is still preferred. Start a conversation on the issue thread to ensure maintainers agree with the approach. Opening PRs for stale or invalid issues simply because an AI bot identified them creates unnecessary overhead for maintainers and is strongly discouraged. Run the code locally. Verify it compiles, passes linting, and passes all relevant tests before opening a PR. Before hitting “Submit,” read through your own diff line-by-line. Remove any redundant logic, overly verbose AI-generated comments, or structural weirdness introduced by the model. Explain your changes in plain technical language. If you can’t describe the change yourself, you aren’t ready to submit it.", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/1"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00002", "post": 2, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "29 Jun 2026", "body": "Additionally, we are looking at rolling out AGENTS.md across many of our repos to hopefully make our LLM-assistants respect these guidelines. I would be interested to hear from anyone who has experience keeping the agents in line.", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/2"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00003", "post": 3, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "29 Jun 2026", "body": "Are there also some guidelines on the usage of LLMs by maintainers?", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/3"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00004", "post": 4, "parent": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00003", "depth": 1, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "29 Jun 2026", "body": "I would say that generally, the same rules apply. I know many people use them, but I would expect that interactions on issue trackers, pull requests, etc are done by the author. I know that I have been guilty of being a little excited about automation in the past, but I think for long-term health, we should mostly be interacting as humans. That being said, I personally don’t mind getting a human-authored summary/conversation and also the LLM output, as it’s often helpful context but easily ignored. The spirit of the guidelines here are more that we shouldn’t just be LLM dumping on each other constantly because everyone (maintainers and contributors) will just run out of bandwidth.", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/4"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00005", "post": 5, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "30 Jun 2026", "body": "I very much agree with this policy and has been what I’ve started to adhoc do in Nav2 over the last couple of weeks independently. The number of sloppy AI PRs and issues (often addressing nonsensical issues) is getting too exhausting to address in a thoughtful process-oriented way. I hit a point of almost complete burn-out by dozens of nonsense ‘security’ reports or massive rewrites generated by AI with either no follow-up or excessive and/or incorrect AI generated follow-up to a point I was dreading opening GitHub in the morning. I’m happy to see someone put in better words and will align with this for Nav2 as well. This type of action has saved my sanity.", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/5"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00006", "post": 6, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "30 Jun 2026", "body": "You are absolutely right to push back on LLM use for human-centric conversations! (sorry, couldn’t resist). I think this is a right move. I find it so tiring to read AI slop these days that when I see a typical LLM-like structures in text, I just give up. Are you going to add anything to Discord onboarding flow to ask people to not use LLMs?", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/6"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00007", "post": 7, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "30 Jun 2026", "body": "Tom Fishburne has a visual way to explain the problems that I find very relevant image 1200×628 164 KB More at AI Written, AI Read cartoon - Marketoonist | Tom Fishburne And long live human interactions!", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/7"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00008", "post": 8, "parent": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00007", "depth": 1, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "30 Jun 2026", "body": "Off-topic: I’m all for (human-crafted) single bullet point emails", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/8"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00009", "post": 9, "parent": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00002", "depth": 1, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "1 Jul 2026", "body": "I would honestly caution against this at most, these guidelines should be present in a CONTRIBUTING.md if the person using the LLM is not capable of reading that and complying with it, then their PR can simply be ignored. anyone serious about contributing will read it, and if they haven’t read it, they are not serious about contributing further, the presence of AGENTS.md , CLAUDE.md , etc. files really turns me off from contributing to any project I see. for some time now, I’ve been wanting to contribute more to ROS, however something like this would make me less likely to", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/9"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00010", "post": 10, "parent": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00009", "depth": 2, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "1 Jul 2026", "body": "Out of curiosity: why? I would think out of stigma of the project being fully AI generated, but with known quality and reliably maintained projects, why would this steer you away?", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/10"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00011", "post": 11, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "1 Jul 2026", "body": "Hi mjcarroll, Thanks for putting this together — this is a much-needed framework. As someone who’s been building an open-source agent system (ClawsJoy) that involves LLM-based orchestration, I’ve been thinking about this exact tension: AI tools accelerate exploration, but the human element is what makes open source sustainable. A few thoughts from the perspective of an active LLM tool user and project maintainer: 1. The “understand what you’re submitting” rule is the most important one. This is what separates using AI as a pair programmer vs using it as a delegation machine . I’ve adopted the practice of writing the explanation first (in plain human language, describing what I want to change and why), then using AI to assist with implementation, then reviewing the diff line by line before submitting. The explanation stays in the PR description — it’s the human-authored “contract” that the code needs to satisfy. 2. One challenge I haven’t seen fully addressed: verifying that AI-generated code actually works in the specific ROS environment. LLMs are often trained on generic ROS 2 examples, but real-world usage involves specific distros, middleware versions (DDS, Zenoh), and hardware. I’ve found that the biggest time sink isn’t generating code — it’s validating that the generated code is compatible with the actual environment. Would love to see community-shared validation workflows (e.g., a CI check that flags potential API mismatches). 3. On disclosure formatting: The commit-message Generated-by: approach makes sense for traceability. One thing I’m experimenting with is a GENERATED-BY: comment in the source file itself for structural code sections (e.g., “this entire class was generated by X”), and commit-message disclosure for smaller snippets. Happy to share what works/doesn’t as I learn. 4. From a maintainer’s perspective: The hardest part isn’t detecting LLM-generated code — it’s detecting LLM-generated code that looks plausible but is subtly wrong . The guidelines on hallucinated API usage and zero-tolerance for unverified output are spot on. I’d add: if an LLM-generated PR passes the “read your own diff” test (i.e., the author can explain every line), that’s already a strong signal that the author has done the necessary work. One quick question: For projects that are already heavy users of AI tools, are you considering any tooling to automate the disclosure tracking? E.g., a pre-commit hook that prompts for a disclosure statement when it detects certain patterns, or a CI check that validates commit message format? This could reduce the friction of compliance and make it easier for contributors to “do the right thing” without extra mental overhead. Again, appreciate the thought put into this. It sets a healthy tone for the community as these tools become more integrated into everyday development. ClawsJoy — building open-source agent infrastructure for robotics and beyond. Happy to connect if any of this is useful, or if you’d like to see how we’re approaching these challenges in a different domain.", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/11"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00013", "post": 13, "parent": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00011", "depth": 1, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "2 Jul 2026", "body": "These guidelines are specifically for contributing to the repositories managed by the OSRA . Verifying that AI-generated code works is the same as verifying that human-produced code works. For other ROS code maintained by others (e.g. Nav2, or a package produced by a company building a product on ROS), they can and will decide their own policies. The OSRA policy recommends Assisted-by commit statements and comments , as it more closely fits how we want people to use these tools: as assistants, not replacements for themselves.", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/13"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00014", "post": 14, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "5 Jul 2026", "body": "I totally support this. It is also a point of my research on the impact of AI in ROS software development and communities. Does anyone is willing to take part of it? Let me know.", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/14"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00015", "post": 15, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "6 Jul 2026", "body": "This is really solid! Might start referring to this for the NASA-JPL Open-Source Rover where we’ve been getting a few clearly AI generated PRs", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/15"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00016", "post": 16, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "6 Jul 2026", "body": "If non-OSRA ROS projects want to adopt this policy, can we just link it from our CONTRIBUTING.md?", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/16"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00017", "post": 17, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "7 Jul 2026", "body": "You can, but we may change it at any time, so it’s probably better to copy it yourself unless you are OK with it suddenly changing.", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/17"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00018", "post": 18, "parent": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00010", "depth": 3, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "13 Jul 2026", "body": "responding a bit late, however I believe that the use of LLMs is fundamentally incompatible with many of my beliefs. I do not want to use LLMs, I do not want to contribute to projects that encourage LLM generated content, I do not want to use projects that contain LLMs generated content. I believe that the use of LLMs is unethical for a variety of reasons. I find their use quite gross, and it makes me (and many others) want to avoid any project that uses them", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/18"}, {"id": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00019", "post": 19, "parent": "osrf-implementation-guidelines-p00018", "depth": 4, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "31 Jul 2026", "body": "Many senior engineers and developers are using LLM’s as well as self-taught individuals without a technical backround like me. You can use AI tools for a meaningful and valuable open-source contribution. There are not all the projects meaningless, shallow contributions. Reacting to all the projects that have being used LLM’s with the same scepticism , we’re escluding the ones that wants to add value into the field. Not mention how many of us are using AI TOOLS withoit even admit it..", "url": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/55903/19"}], "participants": 11}, "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse": {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse", "title": "A policy on generative AI assisted contributions", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702", "posts": [{"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00001", "post": 1, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P01", "op": true, "date": "31 Jan 2025", "body": "Hi, Given the rise of various generative AI tools, it would be nice if we could come up with a definitive policy on dealing with genAI assisted contributions to SciPy. The subject has been discussed multiple times over multiple venues, so it’d be great if we could codify our position in some form and place it somewhere in the docs (Where exactly is TBD; SciPy - Frequently Asked Questions could be one such place; one other option is the github PR template which has some guidelines already). For two recent discussions see, e.g. a long discussion in the NumPy list [1] a recent use case (which, in fact, sparked this email) [2] [1] Mailman 3 Policy on AI-generated code - NumPy-Discussion - python.org [2] MAINT: stats: remove `mvn` fortran calls from `multivariate_normal.cdf` by ev-br · Pull Request #22298 · scipy/scipy · GitHub Broadly, it seems that the concerns can be roughly classified into two buckets: Do we accept genAI assisted contributions at all, and if we do, what are the boundaries of what’s acceptable. Copyright and license concerns. Let’s consider these two in order. Do we accept genAI contributions? It’s futile to attempt to block them wholesale. People are going to use AI assistants anyway, along with various IDEs, autocompleters etc So it seems reasonable to treat these assistants on par with other tools: as long as it does not matter if a contributor uses VS Code or emacs or vi, it also does not matter what engine powers their autocompleter. The discussion in [1] has a concise wording, “Only submit code that you actually undestand”, and I’d just go with just this. One thing we probably do not want to see is a fully automated submission, which is both generated and submitted by a bot, with no human intervention or supervision at all. So we could/should stress that, too. Copyright violations / incompatible licenses Here I lack expertise and have more questions than answers. Here’s a possibly naive attempt at asking hopefully relevant questions: Are we concerned that the code generated by some tool is under some incompatible license from just the fact of being generated by the tool? In this case, using the tool is similar to copying code from, say, Numerical Recipes. Then we should either blacklist known bad tools (do we know of any?) or ask contributors to declare the tools they used (so that we can remove offending code at a future date if needed). Are we concerned that the training set of a tool included license-incompatible code? And if it did, what does it mean for us, are we then transitively non-compliant? If this is a concern, and we at some point discover that some code is license-incompatible, the action does not depend on how it got into the codebase, genAI or not: we discover it, we remove it. And then the only question IMO is whether it’s helpful to us to ask contributors list the AI assistants they used. To summarize, we should not block genAI assisted contributions; we should block fully automated contributions, and ask submitters to only submit code they understand; TBD if we want to ask contributors to list what genAI assistants they used, if any. Let’s add explicit (short!) guidance to the docs, TBD where precisely. Thoughts?", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/1"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00002", "post": 2, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "31 Jan 2025", "body": "I will copy/paste and expand what I wrote in the linked SciPy PR. If I could, I would have used these ChatGPT/CoPilot/Skynet/“All seeing overlord AI” like there is no tomorrow. But my experience with them so far is that, they are almost good and make just a tiny mistake like i becomes j , then I spend rest of the week troubleshooting, learning how to use GDB and fail miserably etc. So it seems like we are swapping manual labor of translating with manual labor of troubleshooting, which for me, definitely the latter that is more painful. But maybe it is because it is not trained enough on F77 or JurassicPACK code we have. On the other hand, I did get some useful tips if I ask programming tips, say, “show me an example of while loop that uses comma operator as an expression in C17” and it brought some nice results. I did not try but some folks in my team use it for summarizing the code about what it does. Other than that, probably @rkern is the most knowledgeable among us what the legal implications might be, however Copilot seems to understand when you say avoid GPL sources for the answer at the citations it brings. Having said all these, I noticed a particular behavior from all AI tools. They are providing a melange of things that they have in their training bank (whatever they call it these days). I am ready to be immortalized in the history books as an idiot, but AI tools are terrible at coming up coherent code for numerical computing. Not only they can’t answer any technical computing question coherently, but when asked in ASCII about certain technical problem, they are bringing almost always some code that looks suspiciously similar to either GNU scientific library or Octave or some stuff they knocked off from some personal gist translated in the requested language. I noticed this mostly because of the strange index corrections before they do things on the arrays (which signals that the source must be a 1-indexed one). While sometimes the result is very close to the actual thing, it is devoid of any proper numerical checks (because they can’t judge the result). Hence, in my personal opinion, before we even judge the legality of things, the sheer possibility of the code being not production ready gives me enough confidence to reject any AI generated code. Thus agree with summary point 2 even if the code runs correctly. This is not the same with a developer using AI for snippets of code for very specific thing and including parts of AI generated code. This is very tricky to judge as AI tools already swallowed Stackoverflow and other sources, we don’t have any ways to backtrack the original source. So a careful agree with summary point 1. I don’t know how to enforce point 3 but if there is a way, that would be really nice. Point 4, fully agree.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/2"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00003", "post": 3, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "31 Jan 2025", "body": "Out of precaution (there are still many lawsuits pending) and most importantly, for moral and ethical reasons, I am -1 on allowing any generative AI at all. Adding here the opinion piece from Paul, which I fully agree with: Current Challenges in Free Software and Open Source Development We spend so much effort ensuring that contributions are original and respect all licensing, copyrights and just wishes of original authors, that it would be strange to me to adopt a different position. Furthermore, as pointed out here and in some other threads, the nature of what we do in SciPy is difficult and these tools don’t (yet) shine. Writing code is only part of the story and often times we spend our effort discussing API, architecture, etc. Hence I don’t think that it would be detrimental to disallow such tools. There is a lot of hype and companies in the sector are trying hard to push that we are doomed if we don’t use their stuff. IMHO, this is not true and we are far from replacing us.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/3"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "post": 4, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "31 Jan 2025", "body": "Since this is an issue that affects all projects in the ecosystem, can we collaborate to co-author a SPEC that can be widely adopted, instead of each library having to figure it out for themselves?", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/4"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00005", "post": 5, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "At the top of the file, CoPilot suggested a line image 1362×420 37 KB I think, no further explanation needed.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/5"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00006", "post": 6, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "depth": 1, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "We can, but is it a single SPEC or we would have multiple depending on what projects decide? That’s something we should maybe get a vote on.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/6"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00007", "post": 7, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00004", "depth": 1, "who": "P05", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "I think the major value to a SPEC would be assembling information about the various issues that might inform a decision into one place. But that can be done with a blog post at least as well. The ultimate decisions each project might take come down to their individual values; there isn’t a technical answer to this that might be helped by the coordination amongst projects via adherence to a SPEC.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/7"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00008", "post": 8, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00003", "depth": 1, "who": "P06", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "I haven’t seen any problems with this. In my experience, it’s very useful and writes high-quality code and saves time. I guess it depends on which tool you’re using. As long as unit tests are included and human reviewers approve it, I don’t see how it’s any different in trustworthiness from code written by random humans. But yes, copyright and license legality is a legitimate concern.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/8"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00009", "post": 9, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00008", "depth": 2, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "Hi, It doesn’t seem useful to argue about the usefulness of the AI-generated code, or otherwise, at this stage. I haven’t found it useful myself, but maybe that’s just my case, and in any case, it may well improve. It’s the license issue that is central. I don’t think there’s any argument that these tools are all digesting code with licenses that at least require attribution, and producing code, indirectly (or directly at times) derived from that licensed code, but with all licensing stripped from the output. The value of a spec or similar, over a blog post, is the ability to iterate to a shared and agreed conclusion, rather than leaving it as the opinion of any one particular person. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/9"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "post": 10, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P04", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "I think you’re right, @rkern . We don’t have any hope of controlling user behavior through a policy document. Many people find these tools useful, so they will keep using them, and there is no sensible way to check the license of the generated snippet. For larger PRs, which may contain more overt license breaches, it’s probably less likely that AI was used wholesale, since the author needed to understand the problem well to begin with.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/10"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00011", "post": 11, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "depth": 1, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "As we don’t have any ways of ensuring people did not read NR nor had a look at GPL code nor took anything from StackOverflow. To me that’s the same thing. If we were to say that we don’t want people not to use any AI tools we could and that’s the same type of self declaration we would ask people to do.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/11"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00012", "post": 12, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "I think it is not that simple to discard the usefulness, though I wrote a wall of text arguing otherwise above. These tools, whether blindly parroting or contemplating, became quite eloquent. As I mentioned above, lately I have been testing them (or they are observing me) and though a bit of success is achieved for line completion, figuring out things from scratch are not even close. However, once I put a lot of comments and write the first two-three lines they predict the context OKish. Then they fill in some boilerplate code and I correct here and there. Some time has saved, some productivity increased in turn utilizing a power plant worth energy for this. I would suggest putting a few sentences in the policy docs that we don’t want wholesale AI code knowing full well it would be near impossible to distinguish. But if we notice something noticeably fishy, we can point to the policy. Or if someone is reading before contributing, they’ll get a taste of our stance. How AI tools became like that and what they did criminally/unethically to become like that, is something we can discuss for years. Licenses and policies would definitely protect across the totality of the project, but I think anything more granular than that, it is just futile Stallman-ing the reality. Unlike licensed code from elsewhere, people will grab pieces from LLMs here and there. We don’t need to pick this fight in our limited time. Besides, it’s not like we are getting very large contributions anyways. So I think this is not a big issue as we might be starting to discuss.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/12"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00013", "post": 13, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00012", "depth": 1, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "Hi, I think it is not that simple to discard the usefulness, though I wrote a wall of text arguing otherwise above. These tools, whether blindly parroting or contemplating, became quite eloquent. As I mentioned above, lately I have been testing them (or they are observing me) and though a bit of success is achieved for line completion, figuring out things from scratch are not even close. However, once I put a lot of comments and write the first two-three lines they predict the context OKish. Then they fill in some boilerplate code and I correct here and there. Some time has saved, some productivity increased in turn utilizing a power plant worth energy for this. I would suggest putting a few sentences in the policy docs that we don’t want wholesale AI code knowing full well it would be near impossible to distinguish. But if we notice something noticeably fishy, we can point to the policy. Or if someone is reading before contributing, they’ll get a taste of our stance. How AI tools became like that and what they did criminally/unethically to become like that, is something we can discuss for years. Licenses and policies would definitely protect across the totality of the project, but I think anything more granular than that, it is just futile Stallman-ing the reality. Unlike licensed code from elsewhere, people will grab pieces from LLMs here and there. We don’t need to pick this fight in our limited time. I remember, back in the day - it was desirable to do “clean-room” porting - where the person writing the new code had not read the code with an incompatible license. For example, one person could read the e.g. GPL code, describe the algorithm, and the other person could write the new code. If the person writing the code had read the the GPL code, it was always possible for the GPL code to leak into the new code. Of course, with AI, it’s all dirty room - it can produce code from anywhere. So I think we have to have the same policy for AI as for dirty-room code. We can’t use it. Now - enforcement - that’s a different matter - I just think we should pursue the same policies on enforcement as we did before. We say don’t do it, and if we find you did do it, we’ll complain and pull out the code. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/13"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00014", "post": 14, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00013", "depth": 2, "who": "P02", "op": false, "date": "3 Feb 2025", "body": "This is a more succinct way of what I tried to write. So I think we agree. What I am trying to emphasize is the practicality of it. If same code appears a 50 places because all 50 of them used AI generated sources and MIT’d the code. I don’t think we can find the 51st original source and point to its GPL. So it is an idealistic stance. Still good to have it but a policy won’t solve any trouble in case we find out the truth later.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/14"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00015", "post": 15, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00010", "depth": 1, "who": "P08", "op": false, "date": "5 Feb 2025", "body": "I’d be happy to see a SPEC about this - I think it can be an informative SPEC (similar to an informative PEP) that doesn’t propose a single solution to adopt, but contains at least the following: Prior art - what did other well-known open source projects decide and why? Legal status - link to relevant sources, and summarize if there’s any expectation of more legal clarity arising in the near to medium term. Lay out a set of distinct options that projects can choose from, with their main pros and cons. E.g.: Option 1: do nothing for now (status quo) Option 2: forbid all generated code by an LLM-based tool Option 3: add guidance to PR template & docs that contributors may choose to use such tools, but need to be aware and take responsibility for copyright of the code they submit Option 4: … ? None of that is project-specific, and people can contribute new arguments and options if they find there are gaps in such a SPEC. A Discourse thread only is a poor medium for iterating to a solution on such a complex topic. I completely agree with @rkern that it’s largely about values and preferences - it’s just a lot easier to get a sense of those and also help people less knowledgeable about the subject form an opinion if there’s a structured document to read and refer to. Once a good SPEC exists, a SciPy-specific choice can more easily be made.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/15"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "post": 16, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P09", "op": false, "date": "5 Feb 2025", "body": "Hello everyone, I never wrote on this discourse but since this is a topic that directly touches my current development experience I’m sharing my approach to AI generated code; I wouldn’t mind some feedback on the approach I chose, and I hope that my experience could be used as one of the many reference approaches people have in using these tools. The thought of encountering possible copyright violations in terms of generated code pulled from other sources always scratched the back of my mind. So far, I use generative tools to create reference sandbox scripts to test a functionality I have in mind, and I fine tune the generation in case I’m not satisfied with the result. In almost all cases, the generated scripts remain just that and I keep them locally without publishing anywhere. I just use them to review an idea I had. I do not keep track of how much code I effectively publish which is generated, and even if I do that, I always follow the assumption that the AI is wrong. That’s because: I don’t trust the accuracy of the prompts I input; I don’t trust the effective comprehension of the tool receiving the prompt, accurate or not it might be. In the end I always end up writing my own code, but what the tooling gives me is a better understanding of the problem I’m currently tackling. I believe someone else in the thread already gave a -1 on completely generated PRs, which I also agree to: before even considering publishing generated code there should be an inner understanding of what’s been generated, and if it’s a copy-paste of the prompt response there’s no guarantee that this is enforced. I am still okay with the approach of using AI as it was meant to be used, a.k.a. an assistant that gives an initial shape to an idea which then is developed on its own with no trace of the original prompt response. In my case, I am considering adding to the documentation of my new projects a comprehensive approach on using AI tooling (it’s a plugin-driven software where the idea is “bring your tool and I glue it to a broader system”), especially since the trend seems to be “I gave a prompt to ChatGPT and I published a plugin for this software, here’s how YOU can also do it!”. At least that’s what I’ve heard from colleagues attending conferences on the matter. It didn’t happen LITERALLY like this, but my feeling is that this is the subtle message that (willing or not) transpires from this.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/16"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00017", "post": 17, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00016", "depth": 1, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "5 Feb 2025", "body": "Hi, [snip] I’m afraid the problem of the license remains. AI generated some code where it is effectively impossible for you to know what license(s) should correctly apply. Now you’ve seen the code with the unknowable license, you are at grave risk of reproducing aspects of that code, thereby pulling in the unknowable license. Your resulting code, even if you have rewritten it - cannot be asserted free of the original (unknowable) license). And therefore you cannot assert your own copyright, or freedom from other people’s copyright. Cheers, Matthew", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/17"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00018", "post": 18, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00017", "depth": 2, "who": "P10", "op": false, "date": "9 May 2025", "body": "Those who were following this thread would probably be interested to learn that there’s now an issue to start to draft a SPEC that was discussed here", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/18"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00019", "post": 19, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00015", "depth": 2, "who": "P11", "op": false, "date": "1 Oct 2025", "body": "Those interested in prior art may be interested in Fedora’s draft policy and the discussion it has spawned . A quick summary of their proposed rules on contributions: AI tools are not banned, but low-quality AI use is. AI use must be disclosed. AI use for translation is explicitly allowed. Limit AI Tools for reviewing contributions. Of course, SciPy and Fedora are not precisely comparable as Fedora is mostly re-distributing existing software.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/19"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00020", "post": 20, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P12", "op": false, "date": "4 Oct 2025", "body": "I’d also like to propose a zero-tolerance policy for lying about AI use. For example, if you ask a contributor if they used are using AI, and you get an AI-generated answer that claims that they’re human.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/20"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "post": 21, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P13", "op": false, "date": "26 Oct 2025", "body": "I am increasingly seeing vibe-coded PRs from novice contributors and I started a discussion on the SymPy mailing list . Right now there are not too many of these but they are quite blatant and I think that this is definitely going to become worse over time. Really these are just low quality PRs but I don’t think it is reasonable to engage with them in the same way as a low quality PR that was actually written by a human. For example if you explain in detail what the problems are with the code then I think that there is a good chance that the “author” is going to type your feedback into the LLM and have it try to vibe-code an improved PR. I have tried it and I really don’t enjoy talking to LLMs if actually trying to do anything and talking to an LLM via something like GitHub comments with someone who is just typing those comments into an LLM is soul-destroying. When reviewing novice PRs the situation is generally that it would be far easier to just write the code without the novice so there is a net loss in terms of effort on the maintainer side. The flipside though is that the novice hopefully learns something from the experience and improves over time. This model is predicated on there being a certain effort-exchange ratio though e.g. that the novice does not open the PR in the first place without putting in some effort beforehand to understand the codebase, the workflow, the issue they are trying to fix and so on. I think it is pretty much possible now to say “Claude, write some code and open a PR to fix issue #12345 ” so any kind of technical barrier is gone and you can open a spam PR having spent zero time trying to understand anything. I also don’t think that this is helpful to novices because in the PR process the AI is helping them with all of the wrong things like writing the code rather than thinking about how to write the code. A student at my University recently asked what is “the purpose of Maths” given that computers (e.g. SymPy, Maple etc) can do all of the exercises we ask them to do by hand in their entry-level Maths exam. My answer was that you have to understand how to do some things manually before you can learn how to make effective use of a computer for more complex problems. Having the computer do your homework is like getting a robot to lift weights for you at the gym. I don’t think that the vibe-code PR authors are malicious. People want to contribute to open source and why wouldn’t a novice believe the hype that this is how code is written in the age of AI? Although unintentional though the resulting spam is effectively abusive to open source projects and maintainers. We need some kind of guidance or policy so that contributors understand what is reasonable. I don’t know how to write any policy/guidance about “responsible use of AI” though without first addressing the basic questions from above like whether AI-generated code is allowed at all and what it means for copyright.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/21"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00022", "post": 22, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "27 Oct 2025", "body": "Thanks for this useful write-up. I guess anyone who is using PRs to certify experience in contribution is going to run into Cambell’s law pretty soon. It still seems to me that we can avoid these problems by saying that the contributor has to take full responsibility for copyright, and therefore, they should avoid AI unless the contribution is so trivial in intellectual terms, that copyright is not an issue. Something like: I have not used AI for the code in this contribution OR I have personally reviewed the content of this PR in detail for potential copyright violations, and confirm that no such violation has occurred. I suppose we could then add advice not to use LLMs for PR summaries, because of the problems that you’ve pointed out. Perhaps we could use some heuristic - such as screening for signs of AI slop, and sticking a label on the PR to say slop is suspected, and asking the contributor to confirm that AI was not used, or that they have carefully checked the content for accuracy and made it concise. Reject the PR (for a contributor who has not shown previous bona-fides) if slop remains.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/22"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00023", "post": 23, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00022", "depth": 1, "who": "P14", "op": false, "date": "29 Oct 2025", "body": "I like this idea! Perhaps a useful addition would be instructions of what to look out for? And to ask to also confirm that the user reviewed the AI-generated code? I imagine to many of the new users that seem to be a big part of this discussion it may be not clear at all what are the possible pitfalls of using a fully automatically generated PR.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/23"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00024", "post": 24, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00021", "depth": 1, "who": "P03", "op": false, "date": "29 Oct 2025", "body": "That assumes that people are interested in learning. Lot of people don’t really care. They just want to get something merged as fast as possible for x/y/z, it does not really matter. What matters to me is to know so that I can approach the PR with a totally different mindset. If the PR gives me a small value and I want this now, I spend the review time to fix what I want and just merge. Otherwise I close and we all move on fast.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/24"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00025", "post": 25, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00023", "depth": 2, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "29 Oct 2025", "body": "One problem with this is it’s really not clear how to review the content for potential violations. For handwritten code, it’s pretty clear: don’t look into GPL licensed code (GSL is out, R is out etc). With LLMs though: how am I, as a contributor, supposed to review what LLM gave me? Comments upthread seem to indicate that if I touched LLMs at all, it’s “dirty” right away. If it’s more nuanced than that, great, then a contributor needs some more specific guidance, I’d think.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/25"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00026", "post": 26, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00025", "depth": 3, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "29 Oct 2025", "body": "We could unpack more, but basically, right, if the LLM is generating substantial code, that might be subject to copyright, you have the unpleasant job of going to check whether it should be subject to copyright. But for the kind of tasks that LLMs do well, like automatic refactoring of imports, swapping single for double quotes in strings and so on, the question doesn’t arise. So yes, the practical upshot is that we throw the burden on the contributor for substantial code, but I think that’s reasonable, given the copyright risks.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/26"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00027", "post": 27, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00026", "depth": 4, "who": "P01", "op": false, "date": "29 Oct 2025", "body": "Please do unpack more! Basically, I honestly don’t understand 1) how can a contributor check for potential copyright violations, and 2) how can an OSS project check the contributor’s check? Consider a well-meaning, diligent and competent contributor, and consider three situations: a contributor asks an LLM to translate a BSD-licensed Matlab code to Python, and submits it to SciPy (this was the original case which triggered the OP— Matt H did just this for some Alan Genz code); a contributor uses an autocomplete functionality of their IDE to write a patch; a contributor gives an LLM the link to an issue on Github and asks it to write a fix; Suppose, further, that in all cases a contributor checks the generated code, modifies it as necessary, and can in full confidence check the box “I verified all code, I understand what it does, and I believe it fixes the issue/adds an enhancement asked”. Suppose the resulting patch is good, is of high quality, and is otherwise mergeable. Questions: What actions does a contributor need to take in order to check for copyright violations? If the problem is that the LLM training set could have contained license-incompatible code (which is the problem discussed upthread IIUC), then the answer seems to be “there is no way, it’s a black box”. If the contributor says basically “I pinky swear it’s compatible”, how does this help the OSS project? again, if the problem is with the LLM training set, no actions of a contributor shield the OSS project from being in violation of the copyright? In other words, I honestly fail to see how moving the burden of a copyright check on a contributor helps to either weed out low-quality submissions, or help with LLM-assisted submissions which are of otherwise high quality.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/27"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00028", "post": 28, "parent": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00027", "depth": 5, "who": "P07", "op": false, "date": "30 Oct 2025", "body": "Let me reply with some questions, followed by the promised unpacking. Do we care about copyright? That is - is it acceptable to us, as open-source authors, that using an LLM can easily generate code to which copyright should apply, so that, if we do nothing, in due course, copyright will become very difficult to enforce, and will be practically void? Let’s say we do care about copyright - what cost are we prepared to pay, in order to defend ourselves from voiding copyright? Clearly replies here will vary from high cost to low cost. I feel strongly about copyright, and am prepared to pay a high cost. Others may feel differently. If one is engaged in that trade-off, copyright against code quality and volume, then one has to ask what benefit we expect from AI, or conversely, what benefit will we lose by constraining the use of AI. That’s a difficult question, with many facets. Could the code have been written in a similar time without AI, other than for fairly trivial and mechanical processing that is unlikely to violate copyright? (The evidence so far suggests yes ). Will contributors using AI benefit in the same way from feedback and training, our previous model of onboarding contributors? Is it in fact true that the committer of AI code understands the code and its context to the same degree as a contributor not using AI? If we are flexible and welcoming of vibe-coding and autocomplete - what kind of programmers will we encourage? And what will be the value / cost ratio of merging those contributions? And supporting those contributors? And so on. These are the kinds of questions that Oscar is alluding to in his post that set off the current discussions. At the top of your email: I’ll try and cover the first point below. For the second, as we’ve discussed before, we have to ask whether it is worth having a policy that may be effective, but is difficult to enforce. For example, it may be that contributors do change their behavior, in their desire to adapt to our norms, but that it would be hard to detect whether they have done this. I would argue that having the norm is worthwhile, even so. In general, it seems to me that we have a strong set of communities, and strong norms, which are, in fact, seldom broken by serious contributors. Anyway, back to the personal copyright check. Let’s take the example you gave: In that case, I would look carefully at the Matlab code, and at the Python code, and confirm that it was a faithful port, with no substantial code in it, that did not come directly from the Matlab code. I would state that in my PR to confirm I had done the work. If I did find substantial code that didn’t come from the original, I’d do a Github search for that code, and I’d do a Google search for potential code that might have been used. I’d then check the results to see if they matched the AI output, and report the outcome on the PR. I’m not quite sure what you mean here - if you mean a simple one or two-line fix, then I think the contributor would be reasonable in asserting something like “code is trivial, copyright unlikely to apply”. If the code is not trivial, then I’d do the Github / Google search as above, and report the results. In asking the contributor to report their findings on potential copyright violation, we make it easier for maintainers to confirm. I suspect we’ll gradually build expertise in detecting copyright violation. I suppose the underlying question is the obvious one - is it time to throw up our hands and accept copyright has become moot in the age of coding LLMs? I would argue no - copyright is too important, and LLM coders are not good enough, to make us do that.", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/28"}, {"id": "scientific-python-ai-policy-discourse-p00029", "post": 29, "parent": null, "depth": 0, "who": "P15", "op": false, "date": "30 Jan 2026", "body": "Given that this thread stalled, I have opened a new thread with a concrete proposal- Proposal for SciPy to adopt the same AI policy as SymPy . Please feel free to comment there!", "url": "https://discuss.scientific-python.org/t/1702/29"}], "participants": 15}}};
